{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"I've got a good grasp of the overall picture now. Let me also confirm the location of the public Babylon.js package (packages/public), and note that Lite is a *separate codebase* — \"we do not copy Babylon.js code\" — which is a key difference: it's a rewrite, not a fork/subset.\n\nAlso, the session's ground rules state that the dev server uses port 5182, but the README says the lab dev server is on port 5174. This is a discrepancy worth pointing out — when actually running the lab, we may need to override the port.\n\nLet me also peek at the Babylon.js public package listing to check the structure for comparison. Actually, I already have enough information. Let me write the answer concisely, but thoroughly.\n\nSummary of \"what's different\":\n\n**Identity**: Babylon-Lite is not a fork or subset of Babylon.js — it's a separate engine (`babylon-lite` v0.1.0, an independent pnpm workspace, a standalone `packages/babylon-lite` with its own src, about 562 TS files) that claims pixel-identical output to Babylon.js and is verified via Playwright pixel-diff parity tests against golden reference images, plus bundle-size cap tests.\n\n**Core design differences** (philosophy.md + GUIDANCE.md):\n1. WebGPU-only — no WebGL1/2 fallback, WGSL shaders, no abstraction layer. BJS supports both.\n2. Size-first philosophy: 100% tree-shakable, zero module-level side effects (no register*(), no globalThis, no module-level Maps), lazy-initialized caches, WGSL minification in production bundles. BJS prioritizes broad usability, and size was not a design constraint.\n3. Data-oriented API: factory functions (`createDirectionalLight([0,-1,0])`) instead of constructors (`new DirectionalLight(\"d\", dir, scene)`); plain data (`{x,y,z}`, `[r,g,b]`, `Float32Array`) instead of classes (Vector3, Color3, Matrix); explicit `addToScene()`/`removeFromScene()` instead of auto-registration via the scene constructor parameter; pure state interfaces with no methods — behavior via standalone functions.\n4. One-way data ownership: components never reference the scene; the scene owns everything. No circular dependencies.\n5. Materials own their shaders (self-contained material + pipeline units) vs. BJS's global effect/shader registry.\n6. Frame-graph based rendering (frame-graph directory, architecture docs 27-31) vs. BJS's render pipeline approach.\n7. No GPU handles in the public API — textures are `Texture2D` type, not `GPUTexture`.\n8. Engine lifecycle: `await createEngine(canvas)` + `await startEngine(engine)` (promise-based, resolves after the first frame) vs. `new WebGPUEngine` + `initAsync` + `runRenderLoop`.\n\n**Feature gaps** (from the feature comparison table):\n- Things Lite intentionally will not support (🚫): WebGL, BJS's InstancedMesh API (thin instances instead).\n- Things missing (—): particles, GUI, WebXR, SSAO/SSR/DOF built-ins, LOD, octree culling, follow/universal camera, area lights, point light shadows, contact hardening, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, KHR_audio, EXT_texture_avif, MSFT_lod, procedural/video/dynamic textures, asset manager, scene serialization, etc.\n- Partial (⚡): physics (subset of Havok V2), post-processing (bloom/blur/chromatic, no DOF/SSAO stack), sprites (a subset of the BJS SpriteManager API), navigation (Recast V2).\n- ★ Areas where Lite is ahead of BJS: high-precision / floating-origin matrices (eye-relative, up to 5e6 world units), 100% tree-shakability, zero side effects, ObservableVec3.\n\n**Implementation-level details**:\n- Dependencies: text-shaper (text layout), recast-navigation wasm (navmesh), manifold-3d (CSG2).\n- Shaders are in `shaders/` as WGSL, imported via `?raw`.\n- Bundle size is measured in runtime-fetched bytes, with NME payload modules and text-shaper excluded; bundle-size caps in tests must never be raised without approval.\n- Testing: vitest unit (pure Node), Playwright plumbing (WebGPU), parity pixel-diff against golden references, performance benchmarks. CI uses SwiftShader Vulkan.\n- Lab dev server: Vite on port 5174 (note: the session says to use port 5182 — mismatch to resolve before running `pnpm dev:lab`).\n\nThis is orientation phase. The user asked \"what's different?\" — that's the question. I should answer concisely but completely, then, per the ground rules, propose a phase transition… actually, the rules say \"when orientation is complete, call a phase transition via propose_phase_transition.\" I'm still in the middle of orientation — the user's intent hasn't yet been stated (they just asked what's different). This question is probably a warm-up / context setup for what they want to do. I should answer the question, then ask what they want to build/do, and once the intent is clarified, propose a transition. Hmm, but the ground rules say to propose a transition when orientation is complete.\n\nI think the right move is: answer thoroughly, then ask what they want to do with this (that's the intent part of orientation). I shouldn't call propose_phase_transition yet — orientation isn't complete without knowing their intent. Actually, wait — the user asked a specific question, and that's the task. Once I've answered, it might be reasonable to propose a transition… but to what phase? The phase after orient is presumably \"plan\" or \"implement.\" I don't know the phase names. propose_phase_transition takes a `to` string. I could propose \"plan.\" But their intent is still unclear. Let me answer the question, ask what they want to do, and hold off on the transition proposal until I have the intent. That seems most faithful to \"discuss the user's intent, consider approaches, and provide multiple options with tradeoffs when possible.\"\n\nLet me structure the answer to be concise and organized. Short paragraphs, bullet points where they help. Name files: philosophy.md, GUIDANCE.md, docs/lite/02-feature-comparison.md, docs/lite/03-porting-guide.md, packages/babylon-lite/src.\n","seq":2885,"t":106983,"text":"\n\nI've read the repo. Here's what differs from the Babylon.js I know — in four layers:\n\n**1. It's a rewrite, not a fork.** `packages/babylon-lite` (v0.1.0, ~562 TS files under `src/`) is a standalone engine in its own pnpm workspace, deliberately not copied from BJS (`GUIDANCE.md`: \"We do NOT copy Babylon.js code. We understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels\"). Parity with BJS is enforced, not inherited: Playwright pixel-diff tests against golden reference screenshots, plus runtime-bundle-size ceiling tests.\n\n**2. The philosophy inverts BJS's.** `philosophy.md` puts **size first, performance second, simplicity third** — and explicitly makes backward compatibility *not* a goal. Concrete consequences in `GUIDANCE.md`:\n- **WebGPU-exclusive**: no WebGL fallback, no abstraction layer; shaders are WGSL.\n- **100% tree-shakable, zero module-level side effects**: no `register*()` at import, no `globalThis`, no module-level `Map`/`Set` (lazy-init only), WGSL minified in prod bundles.\n- **Data-oriented API**: factory functions instead of constructors (`createDirectionalLight([0,-1,0])` vs `new DirectionalLight(\"d\", dir, scene)`), plain data instead of classes (`{x,y,z}`, `[r,g,b]`, `Float32Array` vs `Vector3`/`Color3`/`Matrix`), explicit `addToScene()`/`removeFromScene()` instead of auto-registration, and pure state interfaces with **no methods** — behavior lives in standalone functions.\n- **One-way data ownership**: components never reference the scene; the scene owns everything.\n- **Materials own their shaders** (self-contained pipeline units) instead of BJS's global effect registry, and rendering is **frame-graph based** (`src/frame-graph/`, architecture docs 27–31).\n- **No raw GPU handles in the public API** — `Texture2D` instead of `GPUTexture`.\n\n**3. Feature surface — narrower by design** (`docs/lite/02-feature-comparison.md`):\n- **Won't support (🚫)**: WebGL 1/2, BJS `InstancedMesh` (use thin instances).\n- **Missing (—)**: particles, GUI, WebXR, SSAO/SSR/DOF stack, LOD, octree culling, area lights, point-light shadows, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, KHR_audio, AVIF textures, procedural/video/dynamic textures, asset manager, scene serialization.\n- **Partial (⚡)**: physics (Havok V2 subset), post-processing (bloom/blur/chromatic only), sprites (subset of BJS SpriteManager), navmesh (Recast V2).\n- **★ Ahead of BJS**: eye-relative floating-origin high-precision matrices (stable at ~5e6 world units — BJS has `useHighPrecisionMatrix` but not that path), `ObservableVec3`, multi-canvas via `createSurface`.\n\n**4. Infrastructure**: Vite-native throughout, `lab/` scene gallery as the dev playground, vitest unit tests (pure Node) + Playwright plumbing/parity/perf suites (CI runs SwiftShader Vulkan), and three notable runtime deps: `text-shaper`, `@recast-navigation/wasm`, `manifold-3d`.\n\nOne flag before we go further: the README says the lab dev server runs on **port 5174**, but this session's ground rules say the dev server uses **port 5182** (5173/5180/5181 taken). I'd plan to override the port when we launch it.\n\nSo — what do you want to *do* with this? E.g., port a BJS scene to Lite, add a feature, run the parity suite, or something else?","timings":{"cache_n":15616,"draft_n":5201,"draft_n_accepted":1533,"predicted_ms":39340.0,"predicted_n":2276,"prompt_ms":3210.0,"prompt_n":2991},"to_request":1428,"usage":{"cached_tokens":15616,"completion_tokens":2276,"prompt_tokens":18607}}
