{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to list the constraints that were revealed or committed to in the immediately preceding step (exploring Babylon-Lite and its guidance documentation). Let me look at what I learned in that step:\n\nFrom GUIDANCE.md (read in the immediately preceding step):\n1. WebGPU exclusive — no WebGL fallback, no legacy wrappers, no abstraction layers.\n2. Modern TypeScript, 100% tree-shakable.\n3. Vite as the foundation for infrastructure, dev server, and production bundling.\n4. Maximum performance and minimum footprint — \"We do not copy Babylon.js code. We understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels.\"\n5. Bundle size = runtime bytes only, excluding local NME payload modules and the text-shaper vendor dependency.\n6. WGSL minification in production bundles — never parse the emitted WGSL string for structured data (use typed interfaces).\n7. Zero module-level side effects — no module-level `new Map()`/`new Set()`/`new WeakMap()`, no `register*()` at import time, no globalThis mutation. Use lazy initialization. Caches must auto-invalidate on device change.\n8. One-way data ownership — only the scene knows its contents. Components never reference the scene. Factories do not take a scene parameter. The caller adds via addToScene().\n9. Pure state interfaces — all public interfaces are pure state, no attached methods. Behavior lives in standalone functions.\n10. Do not split types into public Foo + FooInternal companion types. Instead, put internal members on Foo and tag them with @internal.\n11. No GPU internals in the public API — never expose raw WebGPU handles. Use the Texture2D type.\n12. The lab/lite/src scene setup code must read like a high-level API demo, not a WebGPU tutorial.\n13. Philosophy: size > performance > simplicity. Backward compatibility is not a first-class goal.\n14. README/troubleshooting: always use `pnpm exec` for Playwright commands (not npx/n