ison marks WebGL 1/2 as 🚫 \"won't support\").\nCONSTRAINT: 100% tree-shakable, modern TypeScript. Unused features must cost zero bytes.\nCONSTRAINT: Vite is the foundation for dev server, build, and bundling.\nCONSTRAINT: Do not copy Babylon.js code — understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels.\nCONSTRAINT: Zero module-level side effects — no code that runs at import time (no register*() calls, no globalThis mutation, no module-level new Map/Set/WeakMap). Use lazy initialization. Caches must auto-invalidate on device change.\nCONSTRAINT: One-way data ownership — only the scene knows its contents. Components (lights, cameras, meshes) are plain data and never reference the scene. Factories don't take a scene parameter. The caller uses addToScene().\nCONSTRAINT: Pure state interfaces — all public interfaces are pure state with no attached methods. Behavior lives in standalone functions that take the interface as the first argument.\nCONSTRAINT: Do not split a type into a public Foo + FooInternal companion type. Put internal members on the same type and tag them with @internal (the d.ts trim pass strips them).\nCONSTRAINT: The public API must never expose raw WebGPU handles (GPUTexture, GPUTextureView, GPUSampler, GPUBuffer, GPUDevice). Use the Texture2D type.\nCONSTRAINT: lab/lite/src scene code must read like a high-level API demo, not a WebGPU tutorial.\nCONSTRAINT: Bundle size is measured as runtime JS bytes fetched (Playwright network interception), excluding local *-nme.ts payload modules and the text-shaper vendor dependency. WGSL is minified in production.\nCONSTRAINT: Never parse the emitted WGSL string for structured data (minification rewrites it) — use typed interfaces for structured data.\nCONSTRAINT: Bundle size caps in tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts must never be raised without approval.\nCONSTRAINT: Always use `pnpm exec` for Playwright commands (not npx/npm). npx can recreate a rogue node_modules/playwright di