{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user wants to turn \"their example\" into a Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain. \"Their example\" — probably refers to the Babylon-Lite repo's lab examples/scenes. But wait — we're in the orient phase. We should discuss intent, consider approaches, present multiple options with tradeoffs, and when done, propose a phase transition.\n\nBut first, I need to understand what \"their example\" is. The Babylon-Lite repo has a `lab/` scene gallery with sceneN.ts files. The user said \"turn their example into a Quake-style FPS\" — maybe there's a specific example they're referring to. Hmm, \"their example\" might refer to something in the repo — maybe a scene in lab/lite/src, or maybe an example from the getting-started docs. Let me look at the lab scenes and the getting-started docs to find \"the example.\"\n\nAlso, I need to assess what Lite supports for an FPS:\n- Camera: FreeCamera with WASD/arrow key controls (scenes 18, 24, 25) — good for FPS movement.\n- Picking/raycasting (scenes 113-115) — for shooting.\n- Physics: Havok V2 subset (scene 40) — but is it enough for a player controller? Quake-style FPS usually has custom movement (air control, etc.), but destruction needs physics for debris.\n- Textures: 2D textures, heightmap ground (\"Ground from Heightmap — GPU texture → vertex displacement\") — relevant to destructible terrain!\n- Meshes: box, plane, etc. Thin instances for bullets/debris.\n- No particles! Quake-style FPS without particles… bullets, muzzle flash, and debris need particles. This is a gap: \"Particle System — ❌ Not supported.\" So debris effects would have to be done with thin instances or meshes.\n- Audio: no KHR_audio, and probably no audio support at all. Quake-style needs weapon sound. Gap.\n- No GUI: health/ammo HUD needs a DOM overlay or text rendering (text renderer scenes 180-181 — GPU text with editor!). Text rendering exists, so HUD via text is possible.\n- Terrain destruction: how? Options:\n  a) Heightmap-based ground with vertex displacement — can we modify the heightmap at runtime? Need to check if the heightmap ground supports dynamic updates. Probably static.\n  b) Destructible terrain as a voxel/brick approach: terrain made of many small blocks (thin instances or instanced meshes), destroyed on hit — Quake-style \"destructible wall\" is actually per-poly deletion. Quake itself had \"destructible\" floors via vertex explosion… actually classic Quake didn't have fully destructible terrain. Quake 2/3 had it. The user says \"Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain\" — meaning bullets dig into the ground.\n  c) A grid of quads/tiles that get removed or displaced on impact.\n\nKey question: does babylon-lite's API allow runtime modification of geometry (updating vertex buffers)? I need to check the mesh API — `mesh/` directory, heightmap ground. Let me investigate.\n\nAlso, let me find \"their example\" — probably the getting-started example (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md) — a simple sphere/box scene. \"Turn their example into\" — the example probably refers to the getting-started example. Let me read it.\n\nLet me explore:\n1. docs/lite/01-getting-started.md — find the example code.\n2. lab/lite/src/ — see what scenes exist. Especially the free camera scene (18), physics (40), picking (113-115), heightmap ground, text (180-181).\n3. mesh API — check vertex buffer update support.\n4. physics API — what's in the Havok V2 subset.\n\nThis is the orient phase, so I should explore enough to give grounded options with tradeoffs, then present a plan with options, and finally propose a phase transition. Actually, per the ground rules: \"Discuss the user's intent, consider possible approaches, and where possible present multiple options with tradeoffs. When orientation is complete, call a phase transition via propose_phase_transition.\"\n\nSo my job right now: explore the repo, identify what \"their example\" is, evaluate feasibility, present options (e.g., three approaches to terrain destruction), and then propose a transition to the planning phase.\n\nLet me start exploring.\n","seq":4700,"t":182160,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":19712,"draft_n":2744,"draft_n_accepted":727,"predicted_ms":15700.0,"predicted_n":1119,"prompt_ms":1500.0,"prompt_n":1196},"to_request":4327,"usage":{"cached_tokens":19712,"completion_tokens":1119,"prompt_tokens":20908}}
{"approval":{"scope":"off"},"arguments":"{\"command\": \"sed -n 1,100p ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/docs/lite/01-getting-started.md\"}","argv":["bash","-c","sed -n 1,100p ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/docs/lite/01-getting-started.md"],"confined":["/usr/bin/sandbox-exec","-D","WORKTREE=~/git/experiments/t1-floor-r3/babylon-lite-minecraft-fps","-D","READABLE_0=/usr","-D","READABLE_1=/private/etc","-D","READABLE_2=~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3","-D","WRITABLE_0=~/git/reference/Babylon","-D","SECRET_0=~/.ssh","-D","SECRET_1=~/.aws","-D","SECRET_2=~/.config/gh","-D","SECRET_3=~/Library/Keychains","-D","SECRET_4=~/.npmrc","-D","SECRET_5=~/.netrc","-D","SECRET_6=~/.docker/config.json","-D","SECRET_7=~/.gnupg","-D","SECRET_8=~/.config/discipline/t1.auth","-p","(version 1)\n(deny default)\n(define (ancestors path)\n  (let loop ((i (- (string-length path) 1)) (found (list (literal \"/\"))))\n    (cond ((<= i 0) (apply require-any found))\n          ((char=? (string-ref path i) #\\/)\n           (loop (- i 1) (cons (literal (substring path 0 i)) found)))\n          (else (loop (- i 1) found)))))\n(allow process-exec process-fork)\n(allow signal (target same-sandbox))\n(allow sysctl-read)\n(allow mach-lookup (global-name \"com.apple.system.opendirectoryd.libinfo\"))\n(allow file-read-metadata\n  (literal \"/private\") (literal \"/private/var\")\n  (subpath \"/private/var/select\")\n  (ancestors (param \"WORKTREE\"))\n  (ancestors (param \"READABLE_0\"))\n  (ancestors (param \"READABLE_1\"))\n  (ancestors (param \"READABLE_2\"))\n  (ancestors (param \"WRITABLE_0\")))\n(allow file-read*)\n(allow file-write*\n  (subpath (param \"WORKTREE\"))\n  (subpath (param \"WRITABLE_0\"))\n  (literal \"/dev/null\"))\n(allow file-ioctl (literal \"/dev/null\"))\n(allow network-outbound (remote ip \"*:*\"))\n(allow network-bind (local ip \"*:*\"))\n(allow network-inbound (local ip \"*:*\"))\n(allow network-outbound (literal \"/private/var/run/mDNSResponder\"))\n(deny file-read* file-write*\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_0\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_1\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_2\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_3\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_4\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_5\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_6\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_7\"))\n  (subpath (param \"SECRET_8\")))\n(deny file-read* file-write* (require-all (regex #\"/\\.env(\\.[^/]*)?$\") (require-not (regex #\"/\\.env\\.(example|sample|template|dist)$\")) (require-not (subpath (param \"WORKTREE\")))))\n","--","bash","-c","sed -n 1,100p ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/docs/lite/01-getting-started.md"],"cwd":"~/git/experiments/t1-floor-r3/babylon-lite-minecraft-fps","exit":0,"id":"call_2ff21a294ac6438c8e18eaaf","isolation":"sandbox","kind":"tool_call","name":"bash","network":"host","outcome":"ran","request":4327,"seq":4701,"shown":"# Getting Started with Babylon Lite\n\nThis guide gets you from `npm install` to a rendered 3D scene, and — just as importantly — gives you the **mental model** for how Babylon Lite is designed so the rest of the API feels predictable.\n\n> New here? Read **[Welcome](00-welcome.md)** first for the big picture and the \"which engine should I use?\" decision tree. This page assumes you've decided to build with Lite.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Install\n\nBabylon Lite ships as a single package:\n\n```bash\nnpm install @babylonjs/lite\n```\n\nThat's the whole runtime. There's no plugin registration, no global side effects, and no separate loaders package — every optional feature is a normal `import` that the bundler includes **only if you use it**.\n\n**Requirements:**\n\n- **WebGPU.** Lite is WebGPU-exclusive — Chrome/Edge 113+, and recent Firefox and Safari. There is no WebGL fallback by design.\n- **A bundler.** Lite is built on and designed around [Vite](https://vitejs.dev/), which is the smoothest path, but any modern ESM bundler works.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mental model (read this first)\n\nBabylon Lite looks familiar to Babylon.js, but it is built on a few deliberate rules. Internalize these four ideas and the entire API becomes predictable.\n\n### Plain data, not classes\n\nCameras, lights, meshes, and materials are **plain state objects** — no methods, no hidden references. You create them with factory functions:\n\n```typescript\nconst light = createHemisphericLight([0, 1, 0], 1.0); // just returns data\n```\n\nBehavior lives in **standalone functions** that take the object as their first argument:\n\n```typescript\naddToScene(scene, light);\ngetViewMatrix(camera);\n```\n\nThis is what makes Lite **tree-shakable**: a function you never call is stripped from your bundle entirely. There's no class dragging along methods you don't use.\n\n### The scene is the sole owner\n\nA light doesn't know about the scene. A mesh doesn't know about the scene. **Only the scene knows its contents.** You build components as standalone data, then hand them to the scene:\n\n```typescript\nconst light = createHemisphericLight([0, 1, 0], 1.0);\naddToScene(scene, light); // the scene now owns it\n```\n\nThis one-way ownership means zero circular references, trivial serialization, and predictable lifetimes — if it's not in the scene, it isn't rendered.\n\n### An explicit lifecycle\n\nEvery Lite app follows the same four phases, in order:\n\n1. **Create the engine** — `await createEngine(canvas)` (acquires the GPU device).\n2. **Build the scene** — create a `SceneContext`, then `addToScene()` your camera, lights, meshes, and loaded assets.\n3. **Register** — `await registerScene(scene)` does the deferred GPU work (builds pipelines, partitions renderables). Call this **after** everything is added.\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\n\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\n\n### You pay only for what you touch\n\nEvery optional feature — a glTF extension, clearcoat, HDR loading, shadows — is an isolated module, dynamically imported the moment a scene actually needs it. A trivial scene ships a few KB; a full PBR + IBL scene only pays for the paths it exercises. You don't manage this; just write the scene you want and the bundler does the rest.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Your first scene\n\nHere is a complete, minimal app: an engine, a camera, a light, and a sphere with a PBR material.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n    createEngine,\n    createSceneContext,\n    createArcRotateCamera,\n    attachControl,\n    createHemisphericLight,\n    createSphere,\n    createPbrMaterial,\n    addToScene,\n    registerScene,\n    startEngine,\n} from \"@babylonjs/lite\";\n\nconst canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n\n// 1. Engine\nconst engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n\n// 2. Scene\nconst scene = createSceneContext(engine);\n\n","stderr":"","stderr_bytes":0,"stdout":"# Getting Started with Babylon Lite\n\nThis guide gets you from `npm install` to a rendered 3D scene, and — just as importantly — gives you the **mental model** for how Babylon Lite is designed so the rest of the API feels predictable.\n\n> New here? Read **[Welcome](00-welcome.md)** first for the big picture and the \"which engine should I use?\" decision tree. This page assumes you've decided to build with Lite.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Install\n\nBabylon Lite ships as a single package:\n\n```bash\nnpm install @babylonjs/lite\n```\n\nThat's the whole runtime. There's no plugin registration, no global side effects, and no separate loaders package — every optional feature is a normal `import` that the bundler includes **only if you use it**.\n\n**Requirements:**\n\n- **WebGPU.** Lite is WebGPU-exclusive — Chrome/Edge 113+, and recent Firefox and Safari. There is no WebGL fallback by design.\n- **A bundler.** Lite is built on and designed around [Vite](https://vitejs.dev/), which is the smoothest path, but any modern ESM bundler works.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mental model (read this first)\n\nBabylon Lite looks familiar to Babylon.js, but it is built on a few deliberate rules. Internalize these four ideas and the entire API becomes predictable.\n\n### Plain data, not classes\n\nCameras, lights, meshes, and materials are **plain state objects** — no methods, no hidden references. You create them with factory functions:\n\n```typescript\nconst light = createHemisphericLight([0, 1, 0], 1.0); // just returns data\n```\n\nBehavior lives in **standalone functions** that take the object as their first argument:\n\n```typescript\naddToScene(scene, light);\ngetViewMatrix(camera);\n```\n\nThis is what makes Lite **tree-shakable**: a function you never call is stripped from your bundle entirely. There's no class dragging along methods you don't use.\n\n### The scene is the sole owner\n\nA light doesn't know about the scene. A mesh doesn't know about the scene. **Only the scene knows its contents.** You build components as standalone data, then hand them to the scene:\n\n```typescript\nconst light = createHemisphericLight([0, 1, 0], 1.0);\naddToScene(scene, light); // the scene now owns it\n```\n\nThis one-way ownership means zero circular references, trivial serialization, and predictable lifetimes — if it's not in the scene, it isn't rendered.\n\n### An explicit lifecycle\n\nEvery Lite app follows the same four phases, in order:\n\n1. **Create the engine** — `await createEngine(canvas)` (acquires the GPU device).\n2. **Build the scene** — create a `SceneContext`, then `addToScene()` your camera, lights, meshes, and loaded assets.\n3. **Register** — `await registerScene(scene)` does the deferred GPU work (builds pipelines, partitions renderables). Call this **after** everything is added.\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\n\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\n\n### You pay only for what you touch\n\nEvery optional feature — a glTF extension, clearcoat, HDR loading, shadows — is an isolated module, dynamically imported the moment a scene actually needs it. A trivial scene ships a few KB; a full PBR + IBL scene only pays for the paths it exercises. You don't manage this; just write the scene you want and the bundler does the rest.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Your first scene\n\nHere is a complete, minimal app: an engine, a camera, a light, and a sphere with a PBR material.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n    createEngine,\n    createSceneContext,\n    createArcRotateCamera,\n    attachControl,\n    createHemisphericLight,\n    createSphere,\n    createPbrMaterial,\n    addToScene,\n    registerScene,\n    startEngine,\n} from \"@babylonjs/lite\";\n\nconst canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n\n// 1. Engine\nconst engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n\n// 2. Scene\nconst scene = createSceneContext(engine);\n\n","stdout_bytes":3948,"t":182191,"turn":3}
