{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to reflect on the materials I've just ingested: the first 100 lines of the getting-started document, and the listing of lab scenes. Let me answer the two questions.\n\nQuestion 1: Was there a specific part that forms the foundation of what I did or am about to do?\n\nThe getting-started document describes the mental model of Babylon Lite: plain data rather than classes, scene being the sole owner, explicit lifecycle (createEngine → build scene → registerScene → startEngine), tree-shakability.\n\nWhat am I doing? I'm in the orientation phase, planning a Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain. The load-bearing parts for that plan are:\n\n- The **explicit lifecycle** section — specifically `registerScene()` being called after everything has been added, and the warning \"if something doesn't show up, it's almost always because it was added after `registerScene()`\". This is very important for the FPS: dynamically adding/removing debris meshes, bullets, and destroyed terrain tiles at runtime. If runtime additions after `registerScene()` are problematic, then the destruction architecture (adding debris at runtime) hinges on how the runtime re-registration works. This is genuinely load-bearing.\n\n- The \"scene is the sole owner\" + `addToScene()`/`removeFromScene()` — the destruction plan relies on removing destroyed tiles and adding debris at runtime. This is also load-bearing.\n\nThe question asks to quote VERBATIM at most about 15 lines as EXCERPT. Let me pick the most load-bearing part. The lifecycle warning:\n\n```\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\n\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\n```\n\nThis is the single most load-bearing line for the FPS plan, because the game requires runtime object addition/removal (bullets, debris, destroyed