disk name is `babylon-lite`. A small pointer.\n- \"Every optional feature — glTF extensions, clearcoat, HDR loading, shadows — is an isolated module, dynamically imported the moment a scene actually needs it\" — dynamic imports are important for the bundle-size ceiling, and might matter when I add features (physics, shadows). Pointer.\n- \"Requirements: WebGPU... no WebGL fallback\" — general context, already known.\n- The 4-phase lifecycle — that's the load-bearing part, not a pointer.\n\nFrom the second result (lab scene listing):\n- There are 189 scene files; specific relevant ones: scene18 (free camera), scene40 (physics), scene113-115 (picking), scene142-144 (post-processing), scene180-181 (text), scene200-209 (large world). The listing itself was truncated at 40 lines. Pointer: the lab scene files are reference implementations for FPS-related features (free camera scene18, physics scene40, picking scene113-115, text scene180-181) — I should read these when I plan the FPS.\n- The getting-started document itself continues past line 100 (I only read the first 100 lines) — the rest of the document (from \"3. Your first scene\" onward) has a complete sample app and probably further mental-model content. Pointer: I've only read the first 100 lines of 01-getting-started.md; the rest has a complete minimal app example.\n\nAlso, the lab directory has 189 files, and my listing was truncated — pointer: I only saw the first 40 of 189 scene files.\n\nLet me be careful: the instructions say \"each on its own line as POINTER: <what it is and its purpose> (<where it is>)\". So each pointer is one line, with the location noted.\n\nLet me draft:\n\nEXCERPT: (the lifecycle block, verbatim, about 11 lines)\n\nPOINTER lines:\n- POINTER: The document continues past line 100 with a complete minimal sample app (engine → scene → light → sphere) — I'll need the full example code for the FPS scaffold (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, from line 100 onward)\n- POINTER: Optional features a