{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to reflect on the material I just ingested (the opening of character-controller.ts). There are two questions:\n\n1. Was there a specific part that played a critical role? Quote it VERBATIM, up to about 15 lines maximum, prefixed with EXCERPT.\n2. Is there anything else that isn't needed right now but might become important later? For each item, prefix with POINTER and indicate where it is.\n\nLet me think about what I read:\n\nThe file header states the following:\n- A character controller for Havok Physics V2 — a kinematic character that performs collision and sliding.\n- A standalone, tree-shakeable module.\n- The code example shows the following:\n```ts\nconst cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\nonPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\n    cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\n    const p = cc.getPosition();\n    displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\n});\n```\n- The CharacterSupportedState enum: UNSUPPORTED, SLIDING, SUPPORTED.\n- PhysicsCharacterControllerOptions: capsuleHeight default 1.8, capsuleRadius default 0.6.\n- CharacterCollisionEvent: collider, impulse, impulsePosition.\n- CharacterCollisionObservable class (a minimal single-event observable).\n\nFor my current task (an orient-phase FPS with destructible terrain), the load-bearing part is the API example showing how to drive the character controller: `createPhysicsCharacterController(world, position, {capsuleHeight, capsuleRadius})` + `onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => cc.moveWithCollisions(...))` + `cc.getPosition()`. This is the critical API surface for implementing the player controller — I can confirm it exists and know the signature. The `CharacterSupportedState` enum (SUPPORTED/SLIDING/UNSUPPORTED) is also load-bearing for jump detection (whether on the ground).\n\nVerbatim quote, up to about 15 lines maximum. Let me pick the code example:\n\n```\n * ```ts\n *   const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\n *   onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\n *       cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\n *       const p = cc.getPosition();\n *       displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\n *   });\n * ```\n```\n\nThat's 8 lines — verbatim from the file. This is the load-bearing part: it's the player controller API pattern I'm planning to use.\n\nPOINTER items — things not needed right now but that might become important later:\n- `CharacterSupportedState` enum (UNSUPPORTED/SLIDING/SUPPORTED) — will be needed later for ground detection / jump gating. (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/character-controller.ts)\n- `CharacterCollisionEvent` / `onTriggerCollisionObservable` — collision callbacks with impulse, useful for detecting when the player pushes debris (destructible terrain interaction) or for knockback. (same file)\n- This module only becomes active when imported (`_stepWorld` in havok.ts does not reference it) — a tree-shaking note. (same file)\n- `moveWithCollisions` receives an explicit displacement vector (e.g., {0, -0.05, 0.02}) — that is, per-frame velocity-driven stepping, not velocity setting. This is a design detail for movement coding.\n- This mirrors Babylon.js's PhysicsCharacterController (Apache-2.0), left-handed, +Y up.\n\nLet me format the answer concisely. The user said \"pause\" — so I should answer these two questions and then presumably resume. I'll answer the questions directly and then briefly state that I'll continue the orientation work after that.\n","seq":6598,"t":261430,"text":"\n\nEXCERPT:\n```\n * ```ts\n *   const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\n *   onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\n *       cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\n *       const p = cc.getPosition();\n *       displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\n *   });\n * ```\n```\n\nThat doc example is load-bearing: it confirms the player-controller API exists and gives me the exact pattern I'd build the FPS movement on — create the controller from the Havok world, drive it per physics step with an explicit displacement vector, read position back. It also tells me the controller is stepped via `onPhysicsAfterStep`, i.e. movement is coupled to the fixed physics timestep, not the render loop.\n\nPOINTER: `CharacterSupportedState` enum (UNSUPPORTED / SLIDING / SUPPORTED) — I'll need this later for ground detection (jump gating, Quake-style air-control rules). (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/character-controller.ts)\nPOINTER: `CharacterCollisionEvent` + `onTriggerCollisionObservable` — per-contact impulse callbacks; likely how the player would knock or push destructible debris, and possibly how destruction events propagate. (same file)\nPOINTER: `moveWithCollisions` takes a displacement vector, not a velocity — movement code must integrate velocity × dt each physics step; the header example (`{ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 }`) is that idiom. (same file)\nPOINTER: the controller is a standalone tree-shakeable module — `_stepWorld` in `havok.ts` never references it, so it only costs bytes if imported. (same file)\nPOINTER: it mirrors Babylon.js' `PhysicsCharacterController` (Apache-2.0), left-handed, +Y up — BJS experience transfers 1:1 when debugging behavior. (same file)\n\nResuming orientation: I still need to confirm mouse-look/pointer-lock support in `free-camera-controls.ts`, the picking API (scenes 113–115), and whether mesh vertex buffers can be updated at runtime — that last one decides which terrain-destruction approach is viable.","timings":{"cache_n":27136,"draft_n":2744,"draft_n_accepted":1047,"predicted_ms":28100.0,"predicted_n":1439,"prompt_ms":480.0,"prompt_n":315},"to_request":5807,"usage":{"cached_tokens":27136,"completion_tokens":1439,"prompt_tokens":27451}}
