{"kind":"delta","request":7230,"seq":9647,"t":364099,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"to\": \"plan\", \"reason\": \"Intent is clear (turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain in the lab), feasibility is verified against the source (character controller, GPU picking, runtime geometry surgery, thin instances, GPU text all exist), and three terrain-destruction approaches with trade-offs are on the table. Ready to write the implementation plan once the user picks an approach.\"}","id":"call_0d6a18b02fc243f1b76a3320","index":0,"name":"propose_phase_transition"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"All confirmed:\n- `createFlatGroundData` provides a raw grid (positions/normals/uvs/indices) — can generate a dense grid, modify it, and call `uploadMeshToGPU` — the full path to runtime geometry editing exists.\n- `HavokPhysics.wasm` is served from `lab/public/` — physics works in the lab.\n- Scenes 113-115 (picking), 180-181 (text) exist as reference implementations.\n\nI have enough info to present a well-grounded orientation. Following the orient-phase ground rules: discuss intent, consider approaches, present multiple options with tradeoffs, then propose a phase transition.\n\n**Intent**: Transform the getting-started example (engine → camera → light → PBR sphere) into a Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain.\n\n**Key design decisions to present:**\n\n1. **Player controller**:\n   - Option A: Havok character controller (`createPhysicsCharacterController`) — real collisions, ground detection via `CharacterSupportedState`, collision observables. Tradeoff: pulls in the Havok wasm (large but cached), fixed timestep.\n   - Option B: Custom Quake-style movement (velocity accumulation, air control, jump) + `physicsRaycast`/`shapeCast` for ground/wall checks. Tradeoff: more code, but genuine Quake feel (accel/friction/air control), lighter.\n   - Recommendation: A for robustness (BJS-style, proven in this codebase) — but Quake feel is mostly about the movement math. The character controller gives us collisions + ground state, and the movement math (accel, air control) is applied to the input passed to `moveWithCollisions`. Actually, in scene 40's usage, `moveWithCollisions` takes a displacement. We can compute a Quake-style velocity and feed it in per step. That gets us both.\n\n2. **Aiming/shooting**: pointer lock + mouse delta → yaw/pitch (custom, since `attachFreeControl` is drag-based); `createGpuPicker` + `createPickingRay` for hit detection. Note: GPU picking covers meshes; terrain = 1 mesh so picking works.\n\n3. **Destructible terrain — 3 options:**\n   - **A. Tile grid (remove tiles)**: terrain = dense grid rendered as 1 mesh; on hit, remove tiles (index surgery: mark quads as destroyed by removing from index buffer) + physics via per-tile static bodies or heightfield. Simplest to get working, but \"tile\" destruction is coarse.\n   - **B. Vertex cratering (Quake 1/2 style)**: single dense mesh; on hit, crater vertices within a radius (flatten/delete), recompute normals with `computeNormals`, re-upload via `uploadMeshToGPU`. Most faithful to \"destructible terrain\"; hardest: physics mismatch (heightfield doesn't follow the crater) → either periodically rebuild the heightfield, or use a per-tile static box that gets re-cast.\n   - **C. Hybrid**: flat arena floor (static heightfield, player-safe) + destructible brick walls/pillars as individual blocks (thin instances for rendering + physics aggregate per block or group). Destroyed blocks → dynamic debris. Classic Quake CTF feel; simplest and most robust physics; but the floor itself is not destructible.\n   - Recommendation: A or B as core (user explicitly said terrain). I'd recommend B with a \"crater\" that removes vertices, and for physics either (i) keep a coarse static collider grid that removes per-tile bodies when a tile is destroyed, or (ii) skip physics holes (player can't fall through — Quake-style floors were solid). Honestly, the simplest and most robust is: A (tile removal) + debris via thin instances. Present tradeoffs and let the user choose.\n\n4. **Debris/effects**: no particle system → debris = thin instances of rock chunks with manual ballistic motion (gravity integration in onBeforeRender) — cheap, no physics cost; or a few Havok dynamic bodies for big chunks. Muzzle flash = short-lived emissive mesh or sprite. Sparks = tiny thin instances.\n\n5. **HUD**: GPU text (`createTextLayer`/`createTextRenderable`, scene 180-181) for health/ammo/score; crosshair = DOM overlay or thin-instance quads.\n\n6. **Audio**: no engine audio → Web Audio API (DOM) for gunshots/impacts. Outside the engine, no problem.\n\n7. **Infrastructure**: new lab scene entry (scene252+.ts or a dedicated `fps.html`), port 5182 for dev server (README says 5174 — need override; need to check how to override the port — vite config). Also bundle size: physics wasm + text + picking are opt-in imports — the lab bundle will be bigger than the parity scenes, but that's fine (the bundle cap tests only apply to parity scenes; check whether the new scene needs a cap added — per README, bundle-size caps are added per scene; but that's for parity scenes. An FPS demo isn't a parity scene — it's a lab demo. Should check whether lab scenes need parity entries — not needed for this).\n\nWait — should this be a parity scene? No. This is a demo/game, not a BJS parity scene. It lives in `lab/lite/` as a new entry (like the \"Offscreen demo\" mentioned in the feature table). So no golden reference, no parity spec needed. But `lab/vite.config.ts` rollup inputs need the new entry added.\n\n8. **What to test**: dev server on 5182, pointer lock look, WASD movement + jump, firing with GPU picking, terrain destruction visuals, HUD updates, debris. Also `pnpm lint` (ESLint + tsc).\n\nNow — how many options to present? The ground rules say \"present multiple options with tradeoffs where possible.\" Main option set:\n\n**Approach 1 — Tile-removal terrain (recommended)**: single dense grid mesh; destroy = remove quads within radius via index surgery; per-tile static physics boxes (or heightfield for floor + per-block for walls); debris via thin instances with manual ballistic motion.\n**Approach 2 — Vertex cratering**: crater = displace/delete vertices + recompute normals + re-upload; most faithful to Quake 1; physics is coarse (per-tile static body rebuild); riskiest.\n**Approach 3 — Block world (Quake CTF)**: arena of destructible brick blocks as thin instances + physics aggregates; floor is solid; simplest, most robust, fastest to working state; \"terrain\" = destructible walls rather than the floor.\n\nPlayer controller: character controller (recommended) vs custom movement + raycast.\nMovement feel: Quake-style velocity math on top of either.\n\nAdditional things to mention: audio via Web Audio (DOM), HUD via GPU text, crosshair via DOM overlay, port 5182.\n\nShould also mention risks/unknowns to verify in implementation:\n- Whether `removeFromScene` + re-`registerScene` is needed when removing meshes at runtime (does the render system handle removal without re-registration? `removeFromScene` exists, and the plumbing tests have \"material-swap\" and \"dispose\" — so runtime removal is supported).\n- Whether GPU picking works against the terrain mesh (of course it does — scene 113).\n- Cost of index buffer rewriting (re-upload per hit — fine at FPS fire rate? Each hit re-uploads the whole grid — 64×64 grid = 4096 vertices, trivial).\n- Whether the character controller can handle per-tile removal (physics bodies are static and removed on tile destruction — `removePhysicsBody` exists).\n\nI think orientation is done after presenting this. Following the ground rules, I should call propose_phase_transition when orientation is complete. But do I have full intent clarity? The user said \"turn their example into a Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain\" — that's clear enough intent. I'll present the options, ask which terrain approach they prefer, and then propose the transition. Actually, the ground rules say \"when orientation is complete, call propose_phase_transition\" — the proposal itself ends my turn. I could present the options and propose a transition to the planning phase (probably \"plan\"). Hmm, but I don't know the phase names. In the orient phase I \"discuss user intent, consider possible approaches, present multiple options with tradeoffs where possible. When orientation is complete, call propose_phase_transition.\"\n\nThe user needs to pick an approach before planning is finalized, so I could propose a transition now with a reason that says \"pending user choice of terrain approach.\" The proposal is advisory and ends my turn. Or I could wait for the user's response. I think presenting the options + proposing a transition to \"plan\" is correct — the user can accept or adjust. If they want a different approach, they'll say so and I'll adjust.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: propose_phase_transition with `to: \"plan\"` — the reason should summarize: intent clear (FPS demo in the lab), approaches presented, awaiting user's pick of the terrain approach; propose moving to planning once confirmed. That seems like the intended flow.\n\nKeep the response concise — short paragraphs, bullets, name files/functions. Don't dump everything I found; focus on: what's feasible (grounded), the 3 terrain options with tradeoffs, the recommended stack, open questions, and the transition proposal.\n","seq":9648,"t":364100,"text":"\n\nI've verified what Lite gives us for this. Good news: almost everything needed exists in the engine. Bad news: a few gaps shape the design.\n\n**What's in hand (grounded in source):**\n- **Movement**: `createFreeCamera` + `attachFreeControl` (WASD, scene 18) — but look is *drag-based*, so FPS pointer-lock mouse-look is a small custom handler (pointer lock + `mousemove` deltas → yaw/pitch).\n- **Physics**: full Havok V2 path — `createHavokWorld`, `createPhysicsCharacterController` (capsule with `moveWithCollisions`, `CharacterSupportedState` ground detection, collision observables — `src/physics/character-controller.ts`), `physicsRaycast`, `createHeightFieldShape`, `removePhysicsBody`. `HavokPhysics.wasm` is already served from `lab/public/`.\n- **Shooting**: `createGpuPicker` + `createPickingRay` (scenes 113–115) give hits + face normals.\n- **Destructible terrain**: the load-bearing finding — `createFlatGroundData` returns a raw grid (`positions/normals/uvs/indices`), meshes keep CPU copies (`_cpuPositions` etc.), `computeNormals` recomputes normals, and `uploadMeshToGPU` re-uploads. **Runtime geometry surgery is possible**, and `removeFromScene` handles removals (there are plumbing tests for dispose/swap).\n- **Debris**: no particle system, but thin instances (`addThinInstance`/`setThinInstanceMatrix`/`removeThinInstance`) are the natural substitute — ballistic chunks integrated in `onBeforeRender`.\n- **HUD**: GPU text (`createTextLayer`, scenes 180–181) for health/ammo; audio is absent from the engine → Web Audio API (DOM) for gunshots.\n\n**The core design choice — how the terrain breaks. Three options:**\n\n1. **Tile removal (my recommendation).** Terrain is one dense grid mesh; a hit removes all quads inside a radius by index surgery + re-upload; physics is a coarse grid of static tile bodies, `removePhysicsBody` on destroyed tiles. Debris = thin-instance chunks. *Trade-off:* destruction is tile-granular (reads as \"floors crumble\" rather than \"smooth craters\"), but it's robust, cheap, and physics stays consistent — player can actually fall through holes, very Quake-1.\n2. **Vertex cratering (Quake 1/2 faithfulness).** Hit displaces/destroys vertices in a radius, recomputes normals, re-uploads the buffer. *Trade-off:* looks most like real cratering, but the physics collider can't follow the deformed surface without periodic heightfield rebuilds; you'd get visual holes the player can't fall through unless you rebuild — riskiest option.\n3. **Block world (Quake CTF).** Solid arena floor + destructible brick walls/pillars as blocks (thin instances + physics aggregates); destroyed blocks become dynamic debris. *Trade-off:* fastest to a working game, most robust physics, but the *floor* isn't destructible — only the structures.\n\n**Common stack either way:** character controller for collisions/ground state, with Quake-style velocity math (accel, friction, air control, jump) feeding `moveWithCollisions`; pointer-lock look; GPU picking for hits; thin-instance debris; GPU-text HUD; Web Audio sounds; new lab entry in `lab/lite/` (a demo, not a parity scene — no golden ref needed); dev server on **port 5182** per session rules (README says 5174, so we'll override).\n\nI'd test by: `pnpm dev:lab` on 5182, pointer-lock look + movement, fire → pick hit → terrain changes + debris flies, HUD updates, `pnpm lint` clean.\n\nOne question before we lock the plan: **which terrain approach — 1, 2, or 3?** (I'd start with 1; 3 is the fallback if tile physics gets fiddly.)\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":36608,"draft_n":8491,"draft_n_accepted":1843,"predicted_ms":76820.0,"predicted_n":3056,"prompt_ms":4030.0000000000005,"prompt_n":3251},"to_request":7230,"usage":{"cached_tokens":36608,"completion_tokens":3056,"prompt_tokens":39859}}
