{"ask":"audit_q","at":12069,"kind":"fork","lane":"audit","of_turn":4,"question":"We're at a phase boundary, so I want to audit our working notes (working notes). For each note below, reply with EXACTLY one line, each starting with its number:\n<n>. KEEP\nor <n>. UPDATE: <the corrected one-line note>\nor <n>. REMOVE — <one-clause reason>\nor <n>. REMOVE — dup of #<k>  (when it duplicates another note here)\nJudge against everything that has actually happened in this session. Output 69 lines and nothing else.\n\n1. gotcha: The working directory is completely empty — no package.json, no Cargo.toml, no source files at all — IMPLICATION: there is no repo code to ground the \"Babylon\" question in, so I must say plainly that I haven't seen any code here and ask which Babylon is meant (e.g. Babylon.js) rather than assume.\n2. constraint: WebGPU-exclusive — no WebGL fallback, no legacy wrappers or abstraction layers for older APIs; WebGL 1/2 are marked \"won't support\" by design.\n3. gotcha: The repo's dev server (`pnpm dev:lab`) launches Vite on port 5174, while this session's ground rules say the dev server uses 5182 — IMPLICATION: check `lab/vite.config.ts` for the port binding before starting anything; if 5182 is required, override the port rather than assuming `pnpm dev:lab` will land there.\n4. constraint: Bundle-size ceilings in `tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts` must never be raised without approval; golden reference screenshots in `reference/lite/` are immutable.\n5. constraint: Priority order when trade-offs conflict: size > performance > simplicity; Babylon.js backward compatibility is not a first-class goal (e.g., classic `InstancedMesh` API is deliberately replaced by thin instances).\n6. constraint: `lab/lite/src` scene code is the user-facing reference and must read like a high-level API demo, never a WebGPU tutorial.\n7. constraint: `GUIDANCE.md` is immutable unless the user explicitly requests changes, and must be re-read at session start and after every compaction.\n8. constraint: README says the lab dev server runs on port 5174, which conflicts with this session's port 5182 rule — the lab config must be reconciled before running `pnpm dev:lab`.\n9. gotcha: Playwright must be invoked via `pnpm exec`; using `npx`/`npm` can create a rogue `node_modules/playwright` that shadows the pnpm-managed copy and breaks test runs (\"did not expect test() to be called here\") — IMPLICATION: always use `pnpm exec playwright ...`; if it misbehaves, `rm -rf node_modules/playwright` and reinstall.\n10. gotcha: `babylon-lite`'s package.json points `main`/`types`/`exports` at raw `src/index.ts` (with `sideEffects: false`); `dist` only exists for publishing — IMPLICATION: dev/test consumes source directly with no build step; a publishable artifact requires `pnpm build` (vite + vite-plugin-dts).\n11. gotcha: GUIDANCE.md forbids module-level side effects — a module-level `new Map()/Set()/WeakMap()` kills tree-shaking, and caches must be lazy-init and invalidated on device change — IMPLICATION: any new engine code needs the lazy-cache pattern (`let cache: Map | null = null`) and no import-time registration or `globalThis` mutation.\n12. gotcha: production bundles minify inline WGSL template literals (strip comments, newlines, leading whitespace) — IMPLICATION: never parse emitted WGSL strings (split on `\\\\n`, regex); structured data must live in typed interfaces (`UboField[]`, `BindingDecl[]`), WGSL strings are shader code only.\n13. gotcha: public API must never expose raw WebGPU handles, and public types are pure state with no methods — behavior via standalone functions, factories take no `scene`, registration is explicit `addToScene()` — IMPLICATION: new public types must be plain data + functions; GPU handles stay in internal modules only.\n14. gotcha: Lite intentionally omits WebGL (WebGPU-only), the classic `InstancedMesh` API (thin instances instead), particles, GUI, WebXR, LOD/octree, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, asset manager and scene serialization — IMPLICATION: porting a BJS scene can hit hard stops; use the documented alternatives or stay on Babylon.js for those features.\n15. gotcha: the session working directory is empty; the repo lives at `~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite` — IMPLICATION: all file access needs absolute paths, and I should confirm where any working copy lives before writing code.\n16. constraint: Never parse emitted WGSL strings for structured data — the minifier rewrites them; use typed interfaces (`UboField[]`, `BindingDecl[]`) instead.\n17. constraint: 100% tree-shakable with zero module-level side effects — no code at import time (no `register*()` calls, no `globalThis` mutation, no module-level `new Map()/Set()/WeakMap()`); use lazy-init and auto-invalidate caches on device change.\n18. constraint: One-way data ownership — only the scene knows its contents; components (lights, cameras, meshes) are plain data that never reference the scene; factories don't take a scene parameter, callers use `addToScene()`.\n19. constraint: Pure state interfaces — all public interfaces have no attached methods; behavior lives in standalone functions taking the interface as first argument (methods can't be tree-shaken).\n20. constraint: Do not split a type into public `Foo` + `FooInternal` companions — put internal members on the same type tagged `@internal` (d.ts trim pass strips them).\n21. constraint: Public API must never expose raw WebGPU handles (`GPUTexture`, `GPUTextureView`, `GPUSampler`, `GPUBuffer`, `GPUDevice`) — use `Texture2D` and similar abstractions.\n22. constraint: Do not copy Babylon.js code — understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels.\n23. constraint: Vite is the foundation for infrastructure, dev server, and production bundling; use `pnpm exec` for Playwright (never `npx`/`npm`, which can recreate a rogue `node_modules/playwright`).\n24. constraint: Bundle size = runtime fetched JS bytes only (Playwright network interception), excluding local `*-nme.ts` payload modules and the `text-shaper` vendor dependency; WGSL is minified in production.\n25. evidence: ```\\n * ```ts\\n *   const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\\n *   onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\\n *       cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\\n *       const p = cc.getPosition();\\n *       displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\\n *   });\\n * ```\\n```\\n\\nThat doc example is load-bearing: it confirms the player-controller API exists and gives me the exact pattern I'd build the FPS movement on — create the controller from the Havok world, drive it per physics step with an explicit displacement vector, read position back. It also tells me the controller is stepped via `onPhysicsAfterStep`, i.e. movement is coupled to the fixed physics timestep, not the render loop.\n26. evidence: ```\\nEvery Lite app follows the same four phases, in order:\\n\\n1. **Create the engine** — `await createEngine(canvas)` (acquires the GPU device).\\n2. **Build the scene** — create a `SceneContext`, then `addToScene()` your camera, lights, meshes, and loaded assets.\\n3. **Register** — `await registerScene(scene)` does the deferred GPU work (builds pipelines, partitions renderables). Call this **after** everything is added.\\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\\n\\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\\n```\\n\\n2. POINTERS:\n27. pointer: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` — per-shape friction, for Quake-style ground slide (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:971)\n28. pointer: `getPhysicsBodyDebugGeometry` — collider debug visualization while tuning the arena (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:1246)\n29. pointer: the doc continues past line 100 with the complete minimal example app (engine → scene → light → sphere) — I only read the first 100 lines; the full scaffold code is still unread (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §3 onward)\n30. pointer: optional features (glTF exts, clearcoat, HDR, shadows) are isolated modules dynamically imported on first use — matters when adding physics/shadows to the FPS under the bundle-size ceilings (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §2 \"You pay only for what you touch\")\n31. pointer: lab gallery has 189 scene files but I only saw the first 40; the FPS-relevant reference implementations are scene18 (free camera), scene40 (physics), scene113–115 (picking), scene180–181 (text/HUD) (lab/lite/src/lite/)\n32. pointer: published package name is `@babylonjs/lite` but the on-disk workspace package is `babylon-lite` — lab imports use the workspace name, which matters if I scaffold new lab scenes (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md §1 vs packages/babylon-lite/package.json)\n33. pointer: `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask` / `setPhysicsShapeFilterCollideMask` — collision layer filtering; needed so bullets/debris/player don't collide with each other (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:578/589)\n34. pointer: `applyHeightmap` is private and runs only at creation — heightmap ground is static, no runtime modification API (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/create-ground.ts:110)\n35. pointer: no CCD / continuous-collision export visible in the physics API — fast Quake-style bullets may tunnel through thin terrain; worth checking before committing to a bullet model (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts)\n36. pointer: `csg.ts` / `csg2.ts` in src/mesh — Manifold-backed boolean ops; candidate for actually carving craters out of a terrain mesh (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/csg2.ts)\n37. pointer: `CharacterSupportedState` enum (UNSUPPORTED / SLIDING / SUPPORTED) — I'll need this later for ground detection (jump gating, Quake-style air-control rules). (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/character-controller.ts)\n38. pointer: `CharacterCollisionEvent` + `onTriggerCollisionObservable` — per-contact impulse callbacks; likely how the player would knock or push destructible debris, and possibly how destruction events propagate. (same file)\n39. pointer: `moveWithCollisions` takes a displacement vector, not a velocity — movement code must integrate velocity × dt each physics step; the header example (`{ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 }`) is that idiom. (same file)\n40. pointer: the controller is a standalone tree-shakeable module — `_stepWorld` in `havok.ts` never references it, so it only costs bytes if imported. (same file)\n41. pointer: it mirrors Babylon.js' `PhysicsCharacterController` (Apache-2.0), left-handed, +Y up — BJS experience transfers 1:1 when debugging behavior. (same file)\n42. evidence: ```\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh.ts:136:export function initMeshTransform(mesh: Mesh, px = 0, py = 0, pz = 0, rx = 0, ry = 0, rz = 0, sx = 1, sy = 1, sz = 1): void {\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh.ts:187:export function uploadMeshToGPU(\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/compute-normals.ts:9:export function computeNormals(positions: number[], indices: number[]): number[] {\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh-dispose.ts:4:export function disposeMeshGpu(mesh: Mesh): void {\\n```\\nThis is load-bearing: it's the entire `export function` surface of the mesh module, and it contains **no runtime vertex-update API** (no `updatePositions`/`updateVertexData`). That constrains the destructible-terrain options — deforming terrain vertices in place is off the table unless we go through dispose/rebuild (`uploadMeshToGPU`) or tile deletion. Caveat: my grep only matched `export function`, so an exported class method or arrow-function const could still exist — I haven't verified that yet.\n43. pointer: `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` — the bullet-hit test against the physics world (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-queries.ts:207)\n44. pointer: `createHeightFieldShape(world, options)` — Havok heightfield collider so terrain physics can match a heightmap (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-heightfield.ts:59)\n45. pointer: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` is a pure CPU function — the hook for recomputing normals after we hand-edit terrain positions before re-upload (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/compute-normals.ts:9)\n46. pointer: `uploadMeshToGPU(...)` — the rebuild seam for a \"destroy-then-rebuild\" terrain approach (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh.ts:187)\n47. pointer: `applyPhysicsImpulse` / `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` / `removePhysicsBody` — spawn, kick, and kill debris bodies (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:518/1063/1112)\n48. decision: Physics in the lab is wired through the Havok V2 wasm asset at `lab/public/HavokPhysics.wasm` — WHY: scene 40 imports `@babylonjs/havok` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"` and `scripts/build-lab-site.ts` copies that file into the lab site, so the lab can serve it as-is.\n49. decision: Destructible terrain is implementable without engine changes, via CPU geometry edit + re-upload — WHY: `mesh.ts` exposes `uploadMeshToGPU(engine, positions, normals, indices, ...)` plus `@internal` `_cpuPositions`/`_cpuNormals`/`_cpuIndices` copies and `computeNormals(positions, indices)`, giving a complete mutate-and-reupload path.\n50. decision: The terrain base mesh can be a dense subdivided grid — WHY: `createFlatGroundData`/`createGround` accept `subdivisions`, so a vertex-level digging mesh is available from the existing ground factory.\n51. decision: Any HUD will use the in-engine GPU text path rather than a GUI package — WHY: Lite has no GUI by design, and this step confirmed the text module is present and demonstrated in lab scenes 180/181.\n52. gotcha: the \"fact\" record (Lite has no particle system or audio → debris via thin instances, sounds via Web Audio DOM) was dropped by the groundedness gate even though it was sourced from docs/lite/02-feature-comparison.md — IMPLICATION: that constraint is not in the working object, so it must be re-stated in the plan (or re-recorded in different wording) before it is treated as settled.\n53. evidence: ```\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:192:export function createNavMesh(plugin: NavigationPlugin, meshes: Mesh[], params: NavMeshParameters): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:493:export function getClosestPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:500:export function computePath(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): Vec3[];\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:528:export function createNavCrowd(plugin: NavigationPlugin, maxAgents: number, maxAgentRadius: number): NavCrowd;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:536:export function addAgent(crowd: NavCrowd, position: Vec3, params: AgentParameters): number;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:555:export function getAgentPosition(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:561:export function getAgentVelocity(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:567:export function agentGoto(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number, destination: Vec3): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:572:export function updateNavCrowd(crowd: NavCrowd, dt: number): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:583:export function findRandomPointAroundCircle(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3, radius: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:590:export function findRandomPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:615:export function raycast(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): { hit: boolean; hitPoint?: Vec3 };\\n```\\n\\n(`createNavMesh` at :192 is what makes navmesh buildable from the generated noise-terrain mesh; `computePath`/`findRandomPoint`/`raycast` are patrol/seek/line-of-sight; the crowd functions give bot-vs-bot steering.)\\n\\n2. Not needed now, may matter later:\n54. pointer: `createNavMeshFromSources` builds a navmesh from `NavMeshSource[]` instead of raw meshes — may matter if the noise-terrain mesh needs filtering (e.g., excluding holes/steep faces) before navmesh generation (navigation.ts:200)\n55. pointer: `createDebugNavMeshGeometry` returns positions/normals/indices for rendering the navmesh — useful for debugging bot paths on generated terrain (navigation.ts:404)\n56. pointer: `setNavigationRandomSeed`/`getNavigationRandomSeed` — seedable RNG for deterministic bot behavior, useful for reproducible AI tests (navigation.ts:597, 602)\n57. pointer: `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle`/`removeObstacle`/`updateNavMeshObstacles` — dynamic navmesh obstacles; relevant when destructible terrain opens holes or debris blocks paths (navigation.ts:647–681)\n58. plan: I'll record the findings (port conflict, scope update, API anchors) and then continue with the scope response and the transition proposal.\n59. decision: The lab dev server will run on port 5182, overriding the README's 5174 — WHY: the session's ground rules reserve 5182 for the dev server.\n60. decision: The FPS is built as a new lab demo entry under `lab/lite/`, not as a parity scene (no golden reference, no pixel-diff spec, no bundle-ceiling entry) — WHY: it's a game demo, not a Babylon.js parity target.\n61. decision: AI bots are in scope for this iteration and multiplayer is deferred — WHY: the user directed \"bots first, multiplayer later.\"\n62. constraint: Dev server must run on port 5182 (override the lab's default 5174; 5173/5180/5181 are taken).\n63. constraint: Babylon-Lite has no particle system and no audio — debris must be done with thin instances and sounds with Web Audio on the DOM side.\n64. constraint: Scope for this iteration is noise-generated destructible terrain with AI bots; multiplayer is deferred and must not be built now.\n65. files: listed .\n66. Babylon-Lite API anchors for the FPS (all verified in packages/babylon-lite/src): player/bot controller = createPhysicsCharacterController (physics/character-controller.ts, capsule, moveWithCollisions, CharacterSupportedState); hits = createGpuPicker + createPickingRay (picking/); runtime terrain surgery = createFlatGroundData + computeNormals + uploadMeshToGPU (mesh/), meshes keep _cpuPositions/_cpuNormals copies; debris = thin-instance API (mesh/thin-instance.ts: addThinInstance/setThinInstanceMatrix/removeThinInstance); HUD = createTextLayer/createTextRenderable (text/, scenes 180-181); bots = navigation module (createNavMesh, computePath, createNavCrowd/agentGoto/updateNavCrowd, addBoxObstacle/removeObstacle — built on @recast-navigation/wasm, scenes 170-175); physics world = createHavokWorld + createHeightFieldShape, HavokPhysics.wasm served from lab/public/.\n67. Scope: turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS as a new lab demo (lab/lite/, not a parity scene). Terrain is procedurally generated from noise and destructible. AI bots are in scope for this iteration; multiplayer is explicitly deferred (\"bots first, multiplayer later\").\n68. User has not yet chosen the terrain-destruction approach. Options presented: (1) tile removal — index surgery on one dense grid mesh + per-tile static physics bodies [my recommendation], (2) vertex cratering — displace/delete vertices + recompute normals + re-upload, riskier physics sync, (3) block world — solid floor + destructible brick blocks. Noise-generated terrain favors (1) or (2).\n69. Dev server must run on port 5182 (session rule; 5173/5180/5181 taken). Babylon-Lite's lab README says `pnpm dev:lab` launches Vite on port 5174 — the port must be overridden when running the lab for this project.","seq":14669,"t":601019,"why":"meditation"}
