9964500b9163c74226d451924651611342db20002e56b71f79238b5d093e0\n- turn 3: propose_phase_transition args={\"to\": \"plan\", \"reason\": \"Intent is clear (turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS with destructible terrain in the lab), feasibility is verified against the source (character controll… (228 more characters): 98 bytes, sha256 8c231d4a67a4939faef2f6343ea72df7c142ef1c4ebf6f2fb0658b6b224fde6e, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/8c231d4a67a4939faef2f6343ea72df7c142ef1c4ebf6f2fb0658b6b224fde6e\n- turn 4: bash args={\"command\":\"ls ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/; echo ===; grep -rn \\\"export function\\\" ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigat… (21 more characters): 4046 bytes, sha256 7654be86d8ee4ac844dbc23fa9333810018b66b0b1386245bad014857d4ffe84, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/7654be86d8ee4ac844dbc23fa9333810018b66b0b1386245bad014857d4ffe84\n- turn 4: update_record args={\"field\": \"constraint\", \"content\": \"Dev server must run on port 5182 (session rule; 5173/5180/5181 taken). Babylon-Lite's lab README says `pnpm dev:lab` launches Vite on port 5174 — the port must be o… (50 more characters): 57 bytes, sha256 2430eda1ba7b58964a6fc8cb1263477873b55082537991576c455cc76fac73e8, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/2430eda1ba7b58964a6fc8cb1263477873b55082537991576c455cc76fac73e8\n- turn 4: update_record args={\"field\": \"decision\", \"content\": \"Scope: turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS as a new lab demo (lab/lite/, not a parity scene). Terrain is procedurally generated from noise and des… (127 more characters): 55 bytes, sha256 e91cfbe8db9cd7fc53ee1c58b1fd54b1a017d6ee3b52dc69664e231306b5c0a1, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/e91cfbe8db9cd7fc53ee1c58b1fd54b1a017d6ee3b52dc69664e231306b5c0a1\n- turn 4: update_record args={\"field\": \"map\", \"content\": \"Babylon-Lite API anchors for the FPS (all verified in packages/babylon-lite/src): player/bot controller = createPhysicsCharacterController (physics/character-controller.ts… (707 more characters): 50 bytes, sha256 c3edb8337ecf1c5fe65217ab16b81a09571b9f86aec1374bff17ae4ad8973945, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/c3edb8337ecf1c5fe65217ab16b81a09571b9f86aec1374bff17ae4ad8973945\n- turn 4: update_record args={\"field\": \"fact\", \"content\": \"Lite has no particle system and no audio (per docs/lite/02-feature-comparison.md). Plan accordingly: debris/sparks = thin instances with manual ballistic integration in o… (90 more characters): 49 bytes, sha256 61265c8ece9ab06bb1927c5d8b60738ab0afdc413a9e807e6c3b44838b0fb84d, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/61265c8ece9ab06bb1927c5d8b60738ab0afdc413a9e807e6c3b44838b0fb84d\n- turn 4: update_record args={\"field\": \"open\", \"content\": \"User has not yet chosen the terrain-destruction approach. Options presented: (1) tile removal — index surgery on one dense grid mesh + per-tile static physics bodies [my … (223 more characters): 51 bytes, sha256 f748e1e08a439bd42635ce91845f7888c809e7752f5bfd0ecb253f0769c97d6d, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/f748e1e08a439bd42635ce91845f7888c809e7752f5bfd0ecb253f0769c97d6d\n- turn 4: propose_phase_transition args={\"to\": \"plan\", \"reason\": \"Orientation is complete: intent confirmed (Quake-style FPS lab demo with noise-generated destructible terrain, AI bots now, multiplayer deferred), feasibility verified agains… (244 more characters): 98 bytes, sha256 8c231d4a67a4939faef2f6343ea72df7c142ef1c4ebf6f2fb0658b6b224fde6e, saved at ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/files/8c231d4a67a4939faef2f6343ea72df7c142ef1c4ebf6f2fb0658b6b224fde6e\n","phase":{"from":"orient","to":"plan"},"phase_document":{"digest":"c3c3afe17657994b1403ed749e7352bdf4ad1eb47c688b6e60bb0e122db95e8e","ground_rules":"Turn the chosen approach into a plan: the steps, the files each one touches, and how each will be checked. Record the plan and its decisions with update_record. Change no files yet. When the plan is agreed, call for a phase transition with propose_phase_transition.","name":"plan","transitions":[{"kind":"suggested","to":"build"},{"kind":"allowed","to":"orient"}]},"placement":"message","prefix_hash_after":"a6fe8a90a208c3b48f38a0344f56c6ea465502af86ed1ea43ef992fa0434e804","prefix_hash_before":"0ef00642675ef70914402e0b7fc56fdbd95cd62fda888134c18a635d72b69769","reason":"phase","render":"# regime\narm: t1-phased-floor-r3\nsubstrate: accel24-tabbyapi-exl3-qwen38-27b-3p00\ndogma_version: 0\nphase: plan\nframe_version: placeholder\n\n# working set\naudit-14669-35\tNo CCD/continuous-collision export visible in the havok.ts, havok-queries.ts, and havok-heightfield.ts greps — fast Quake-style bullets may tunnel through thin terrain; remaining physics files (havok-collision.ts, character-controller.ts, etc.) are unchecked.\naudit-14669-42\tNo runtime vertex-update export found, but the grep only covered export-function lines in mesh.ts, compute-normals.ts, and mesh-dispose.ts (3 of ~25 mesh/*.ts files) — in-place deformation is off the table unless via uploadMeshToGPU rebuild or tile deletion, and non-function exports in other mesh files remain unchecked.\naudit-14669-47\tapplyPhysicsImpulse (havok.ts:1043) / setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity (:1063) / removePhysicsBody (:1112) — spawn, kick, and kill debris bodies; line 518 is actually applyPhysicsBodyImpulse.\naudit-14669-52\tThe \"fact\" record on no particles/audio was dropped by the groundedness gate, but the constraint now survives in the working object as #63 — treat it as settled; no re-recording needed.\ninterview-t2-0\tconstraint: WebGPU-exclusive — no WebGL fallback, no legacy wrappers or abstraction layers for older APIs; WebGL 1/2 are marked \"won't support\" by design.\ninterview-t2-1\tgotcha: The repo's dev server (`pnpm dev:lab`) launches Vite on port 5174, while this session's ground rules say the dev server uses 5182 — IMPLICATION: check `lab/vite.config.ts` for the port binding before starting anything; if 5182 is required, override the port rather than assuming `pnpm dev:lab` will land there.\ninterview-t2-10\tconstraint: Bundle-size ceilings in `tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts` must never be raised without approval; golden reference screenshots in `reference/lite/` are immutable.\ninterview-t2-11\tconstraint: Priority order when trade-offs conflict: size > performance > simplicity; Babylon.js backward compatibility is not a first-class goal (e.g., classic `InstancedMesh` API is deliberately replaced by thin instances).\ninterview-t2-12\tconstraint: `lab/lite/src` scene code is the user-facing reference and must read like a high-level API demo, never a WebGPU tutorial.\ninterview-t2-13\tconstraint: `GUIDANCE.md` is immutable unless the user explicitly requests changes, and must be re-read at session start and after every compaction.\ninterview-t2-2\tgotcha: Playwright must be invoked via `pnpm exec`; using `npx`/`npm` can create a rogue `node_modules/playwright` that shadows the pnpm-managed copy and breaks test runs (\"did not expect test() to be called here\") — IMPLICATION: always use `pnpm exec playwright ...`; if it misbehaves, `rm -rf node_modules/playwright` and reinstall.\ninterview-t2-3\tgotcha: `babylon-lite`'s package.json points `main`/`types`/`exports` at raw `src/index.ts` (with `sideEffects: false`); `dist` only exists for publishing — IMPLICATION: dev/test consumes source directly with no build step; a publishable artifact requires `pnpm build` (vite + vite-plugin-dts).\ninterview-t2-4\tgotcha: GUIDANCE.md forbids module-level side effects — a module-level `new Map()/Set()/WeakMap()` kills tree-shaking, and caches must be lazy-init and invalidated on device change — IMPLICATION: any new engine code needs the lazy-cache pattern (`let cache: Map | null = null`) and no import-time registration or `globalThis` mutation.\ninterview-t2-5\tgotcha: production bundles minify inline WGSL template literals (strip comments, newlines, leading whitespace) — IMPLICATION: never parse emitted WGSL strings (split on `\\\\n`, regex); structured data must live in typed interfaces (`UboField[]`, `BindingDecl[]`), WGSL strings are shader code only.\ninterview-t2-7\tgotcha: Lite intentionally omits WebGL (WebGPU-only), the classic `InstancedMesh` API (thin instances instead), particles, GUI, WebXR, LOD/octree, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, asset manager and scene serialization — IMPLICATION: porting a BJS scene can hit hard stops; use the documented alternatives or stay on Babylon.js for those features.\ninterview-t2-8\tgotcha: the session working directory is empty; the repo lives at `~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite` — IMPLICATION: all file access needs absolute paths, and I should confirm where any working copy lives before writing code.\ninterview-t2-f3661-2\tconstraint: One-way data ownership — only the scene knows its contents; components (lights, cameras, meshes) are plain data that never reference the scene; factories don't take a scene parameter, callers use `addToScene()`.\ninterview-t2-f3661-3\tconstraint: Pure state interfaces — all public interfaces have no attached methods; behavior lives in standalone functions taking the interface as first argument (methods can't be tree-shaken).\ninterview-t2-f3661-4\tconstraint: Do not split a type into public `Foo` + `FooInternal` companions — put internal members on the same type tagged `@internal` (d.ts trim pass strips them).\ninterview-t2-f3661-5\tconstraint: Public API must never expose raw WebGPU handles (`GPUTexture`, `GPUTextureView`, `GPUSampler`, `GPUBuffer`, `GPUDevice`) — use `Texture2D` and similar abstractions.\ninterview-t2-f3661-6\tconstraint: Do not copy Babylon.js code — understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels.\ninterview-t2-f3661-7\tconstraint: Vite is the foundation for infrastructure, dev server, and production bundling; use `pnpm exec` for Playwright (never `npx`/`npm`, which can recreate a rogue `node_modules/playwright`).\ninterview-t2-f3661-8\tconstraint: Bundle size = runtime fetched JS bytes only (Playwright network interception), excluding local `*-nme.ts` payload modules and the `text-shaper` vendor dependency; WGSL is minified in production.\ninterview-t3-0\tevidence: ```\\n * ```ts\\n *   const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\\n *   onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\\n *       cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\\n *       const p = cc.getPosition();\\n *       displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\\n *   });\\n * ```\\n```\\n\\nThat doc example is load-bearing: it confirms the player-controller API exists and gives me the exact pattern I'd build the FPS movement on — create the controller from the Havok world, drive it per physics step with an explicit displacement vector, read position back. It also tells me the controller is stepped via `onPhysicsAfterStep`, i.e. movement is coupled to the fixed physics timestep, not the render loop.\ninterview-t3-1\tevidence: ```\\nEvery Lite app follows the same four phases, in order:\\n\\n1. **Create the engine** — `await createEngine(canvas)` (acquires the GPU device).\\n2. **Build the scene** — create a `SceneContext`, then `addToScene()` your camera, lights, meshes, and loaded assets.\\n3. **Register** — `await registerScene(scene)` does the deferred GPU work (builds pipelines, partitions renderables). Call this **after** everything is added.\\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\\n\\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\\n```\\n\\n2. POINTERS:\ninterview-t3-10\tpointer: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` — per-shape friction, for Quake-style ground slide (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:971)\ninterview-t3-11\tpointer: `getPhysicsBodyDebugGeometry` — collider debug visualization while tuning the arena (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:1246)\ninterview-t3-2\tpointer: the doc continues past line 100 with the complete minimal example app (engine → scene → light → sphere) — I only read the first 100 lines; the full scaffold code is still unread (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §3 onward)\ninterview-t3-3\tpointer: optional features (glTF exts, clearcoat, HDR, shadows) are isolated modules dynamically imported on first use — matters when adding physics/shadows to the FPS under the bundle-size ceilings (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §2 \"You pay only for what you touch\")\ninterview-t3-4\tpointer: lab gallery has 189 scene files but I only saw the first 40; the FPS-relevant reference implementations are scene18 (free camera), scene40 (physics), scene113–115 (picking), scene180–181 (text/HUD) (lab/lite/src/lite/)\ninterview-t3-5\tpointer: published package name is `@babylonjs/lite` but the on-disk workspace package is `babylon-lite` — lab imports use the workspace name, which matters if I scaffold new lab scenes (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md §1 vs packages/babylon-lite/package.json)\ninterview-t3-6\tpointer: `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask` / `setPhysicsShapeFilterCollideMask` — collision layer filtering; needed so bullets/debris/player don't collide with each other (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:578/589)\ninterview-t3-7\tpointer: `applyHeightmap` is private and runs only at creation — heightmap ground is static, no runtime modification API (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/create-ground.ts:110)\ninterview-t3-9\tpointer: `csg.ts` / `csg2.ts` in src/mesh — Manifold-backed boolean ops; candidate for actually carving craters out of a terrain mesh (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/csg2.ts)\ninterview-t3-f5806-1\tpointer: `CharacterSupportedState` enum (UNSUPPORTED / SLIDING / SUPPORTED) — I'll need this later for ground detection (jump gating, Quake-style air-control rules). (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/character-controller.ts)\ninterview-t3-f5806-2\tpointer: `CharacterCollisionEvent` + `onTriggerCollisionObservable` — per-contact impulse callbacks; likely how the player would knock or push destructible debris, and possibly how destruction events propagate. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f5806-3\tpointer: `moveWithCollisions` takes a displacement vector, not a velocity — movement code must integrate velocity × dt each physics step; the header example (`{ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 }`) is that idiom. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f5806-4\tpointer: the controller is a standalone tree-shakeable module — `_stepWorld` in `havok.ts` never references it, so it only costs bytes if imported. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f5806-5\tpointer: it mirrors Babylon.js' `PhysicsCharacterController` (Apache-2.0), left-handed, +Y up — BJS experience transfers 1:1 when debugging behavior. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f6606-1\tpointer: `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` — the bullet-hit test against the physics world (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-queries.ts:207)\ninterview-t3-f6606-2\tpointer: `createHeightFieldShape(world, options)` — Havok heightfield collider so terrain physics can match a heightmap (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-heightfield.ts:59)\ninterview-t3-f6606-3\tpointer: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` is a pure CPU function — the hook for recomputing normals after we hand-edit terrain positions before re-upload (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/compute-normals.ts:9)\ninterview-t3-f6606-4\tpointer: `uploadMeshToGPU(...)` — the rebuild seam for a \"destroy-then-rebuild\" terrain approach (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh.ts:187)\ninterview-t3-f9898-0\tdecision: Physics in the lab is wired through the Havok V2 wasm asset at `lab/public/HavokPhysics.wasm` — WHY: scene 40 imports `@babylonjs/havok` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"` and `scripts/build-lab-site.ts` copies that file into the lab site, so the lab can serve it as-is.\ninterview-t3-f9898-1\tdecision: Destructible terrain is implementable without engine changes, via CPU geometry edit + re-upload — WHY: `mesh.ts` exposes `uploadMeshToGPU(engine, positions, normals, indices, ...)` plus `@internal` `_cpuPositions`/`_cpuNormals`/`_cpuIndices` copies and `computeNormals(positions, indices)`, giving a complete mutate-and-reupload path.\ninterview-t3-f9898-2\tdecision: The terrain base mesh can be a dense subdivided grid — WHY: `createFlatGroundData`/`createGround` accept `subdivisions`, so a vertex-level digging mesh is available from the existing ground factory.\ninterview-t3-f9898-3\tdecision: Any HUD will use the in-engine GPU text path rather than a GUI package — WHY: Lite has no GUI by design, and this step confirmed the text module is present and demonstrated in lab scenes 180/181.\ninterview-t4-1\tevidence: ```\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:192:export function createNavMesh(plugin: NavigationPlugin, meshes: Mesh[], params: NavMeshParameters): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:493:export function getClosestPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:500:export function computePath(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): Vec3[];\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:528:export function createNavCrowd(plugin: NavigationPlugin, maxAgents: number, maxAgentRadius: number): NavCrowd;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:536:export function addAgent(crowd: NavCrowd, position: Vec3, params: AgentParameters): number;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:555:export function getAgentPosition(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:561:export function getAgentVelocity(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:567:export function agentGoto(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number, destination: Vec3): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:572:export function updateNavCrowd(crowd: NavCrowd, dt: number): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:583:export function findRandomPointAroundCircle(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3, radius: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:590:export function findRandomPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:615:export function raycast(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): { hit: boolean; hitPoint?: Vec3 };\\n```\\n\\n(`createNavMesh` at :192 is what makes navmesh buildable from the generated noise-terrain mesh; `computePath`/`findRandomPoint`/`raycast` are patrol/seek/line-of-sight; the crowd functions give bot-vs-bot steering.)\\n\\n2. Not needed now, may matter later:\ninterview-t4-2\tpointer: `createNavMeshFromSources` builds a navmesh from `NavMeshSource[]` instead of raw meshes — may matter if the noise-terrain mesh needs filtering (e.g., excluding holes/steep faces) before navmesh generation (navigation.ts:200)\ninterview-t4-3\tpointer: `createDebugNavMeshGeometry` returns positions/normals/indices for rendering the navmesh — useful for debugging bot paths on generated terrain (navigation.ts:404)\ninterview-t4-4\tpointer: `setNavigationRandomSeed`/`getNavigationRandomSeed` — seedable RNG for deterministic bot behavior, useful for reproducible AI tests (navigation.ts:597, 602)\ninterview-t4-5\tpointer: `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle`/`removeObstacle`/`updateNavMeshObstacles` — dynamic navmesh obstacles; relevant when destructible terrain opens holes or debris blocks paths (navigation.ts:647–681)\ninterview-t4-f13394-1\tdecision: The FPS is built as a new lab demo entry under `lab/lite/`, not as a parity scene (no golden reference, no pixel-diff spec, no bundle-ceiling entry) — WHY: it's a game demo, not a Babylon.js parity target.\ninterview-t4-f14169-1\tconstraint: Babylon-Lite has no particle system and no audio — debris must be done with thin instances and sounds with Web Audio on the DOM side.\nr11177/call_3e0014035cb94159a810d363/map\tBabylon-Lite API anchors for the FPS (all verified in packages/babylon-lite/src): player/bot controller = createPhysicsCharacterController (physics/character-controller.ts, capsule, moveWithCollisions, CharacterSupportedState); hits = createGpuPicker + createPickingRay (picking/); runtime terrain surgery = createFlatGroundData + computeNormals + uploadMeshToGPU (mesh/), meshes keep _cpuPositions/_cpuNormals copies; debris = thin-instance API (mesh/thin-instance.ts: addThinInstance/setThinInstanceMatrix/removeThinInstance); HUD = createTextLayer/createTextRenderable (text/, scenes 180-181); bots = navigation module (createNavMesh, computePath, createNavCrowd/agentGoto/updateNavCrowd, addBoxObstacle/removeObstacle — built on @recast-navigation/wasm, scenes 170-175); physics world = createHavokWorld + createHeightFieldShape, HavokPhysics.wasm served from lab/public/.\nr11177/call_83149a63ed4b48e18cc9a43c/decision\tScope: turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS as a new lab demo (lab/lite/, not a parity scene). Terrain is procedurally generated from noise and destructible. AI bots are in scope for this iteration; multiplayer is explicitly deferred (\"bots first, multiplayer later\").\nr11177/call_a78853d904634d32a2e0e01d/open\tUser has not yet chosen the terrain-destruction approach. Options presented: (1) tile removal — index surgery on one dense grid mesh + per-tile static physics bodies [my recommendation], (2) vertex cratering — displace/delete vertices + recompute normals + re-upload, riskier physics sync, (3) block world — solid floor + destructible brick blocks. Noise-generated terrain favors (1) or (2).\nr11177/call_b04dd22925274eb0b6579989/constraint\tDev server must run on port 5182 (session rule; 5173/5180/5181 taken). Babylon-Lite's lab README says `pnpm dev:lab` launches Vite on port 5174 — the port must be overridden when running the lab for this project.\n","render_budget_tokens":40000,"render_fits":true,"render_over_budget":"tier","render_reduced":0,"render_tokens":4524,"seq":19451,"t":853683,"tail_tokens":4000,"tool_outputs":"reference","warm":{"cache_n":0,"draft_n":7,"draft_n_accepted":0,"predicted_ms":40.0,"predicted_n":1,"prompt_ms":12110.0,"prompt_n":14585}}
