{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user has approved tile deletion as the terrain destruction approach. Next, I need to create a plan for the Quake-style FPS lab demo.\n\nLet me organize what I know:\n\n**Scope:**\n- Turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS as a new lab demo under `lab/lite/` (not a parity scene)\n- Terrain: procedurally generated from noise, destructible via tile deletion\n- AI bots are in scope; multiplayer is deferred\n- Lab scene code must read like a high-level API demo\n\n**Constraints:**\n- WebGPU-only, no WebGL fallback\n- Module-level side effects forbidden (lazy cache pattern)\n- One-way data ownership; pure state interfaces\n- Don't copy Babylon.js code\n- Bundle size > performance > simplicity\n- No particles/audio — debris = thin instances + Web Audio on the DOM side\n- Dev server on port 5182 (lab's default is 5174 — override the port)\n- `pnpm exec playwright` for Playwright, never npx/npm\n- lab/lite/src is user-facing reference — must read like a high-level API demo\n\n**Terrain destruction — tile deletion:**\n- Index surgery on a single dense grid mesh + per-tile static physics bodies\n- `createFlatGroundData`/`createGround` accept `subdivisions`\n- Meshes hold `_cpuPositions`/`_cpuNormals`/`_cpuIndices` copies\n- `computeNormals(positions, indices)` is a pure CPU function\n- `uploadMeshToGPU(engine, positions, normals, indices, ...)` is the rebuild seam\n- `createHeightFieldShape(world, options)` for heightfield colliders\n- Per-tile static physics bodies for destruction: when a tile is destroyed, remove its physics body\n\nWait — actually, tile deletion means: terrain is a dense grid; tiles are subregions (e.g., 4x4 or 8x8 vertex patches). When a tile is destroyed:\n1. Remove its triangles from the index buffer (index surgery on `_cpuIndices`)\n2. Recompute normals\n3. Re-upload the mesh to the GPU\n4. Remove/modify the physics collider\n\nPhysics sync options:\n- One big heightfield collider for the whole terrain — but tile deletion wouldn't be reflected. Actually, the heightfield is a Havok shape created only at creation time; there's no runtime modification API.\n- Per-tile static box colliders: each tile gets a static physics body (a box at its max height). On destruction, `removePhysicsBody`. This is the \"per-tile static physics bodies\" option the user approved.\n\nSo terrain physics = grid of static box bodies (one per tile). Player standing on a tile: character controller interacts with the box. Tile destroyed → remove body → player falls through. This is simpler than a heightfield and matches the approved approach.\n\nHmm, but with per-tile boxes with noise height, the top of the box is the max height of the tile, and the visual mesh is the per-vertex heightmap surface. There will be a visual mismatch (player standing slightly above/below the surface). Alternative: per-tile physics with the tile's average height, or a thin box per tile with height = max height within the tile, and a flat top. Acceptable for the demo. Actually, for Quake-style gameplay, a simpler approach: keep the terrain relatively gentle, and use per-tile static boxes.\n\nActually wait — maybe even simpler: one static box per tile at the tile's average/max height. That's the plan.\n\n**Player:**\n- `createPhysicsCharacterController(world, position, { capsuleHeight, capsuleRadius })`\n- `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` per physics step, via `onPhysicsAfterStep`\n- Integrate velocity × dt each step\n- Quake-style: ground slide via `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` (low friction), air control\n- `CharacterSupportedState` for ground detection, jump gating\n- `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask`/`setPhysicsShapeFilterCollideMask` for collision layers\n\n**Camera:**\n- FPS camera following the character controller. No pointer lock in the engine — need to check. Scene 18 has a free camera. For FPS, need mouse look via Pointer Lock API on the DOM side + `createFreeCamera` or a manual camera. The camera must be driven manually: position = character position + eye offset, rotation from mouse yaw/pitch.\n- Actually, there's `createFreeCamera` and `free-camera-controls.ts`. The FPS camera is probably: `createFreeCamera` with manual position/rotation updates (no input controls), or maybe a `Camera` with `position`/`rotation` set per frame.\n- Pointer lock: DOM side, no problem (it's a lab demo, so DOM events are acceptable? Hmm — the constraint says lab/lite/src scene code must read like a high-level API demo, never a WebGPU tutorial. Pointer lock and keyboard input are DOM events; since there's no engine input system, DOM events are unavoidable. The scene code should be organized so that input handling is cleanly separated).\n\nWait, does the engine have an input module? Let me check the src directory listing I got. From the file listing (f4490faf...), I saw the directory listing but haven't re-verified. I should check whether there's an input/ or events module. Haven't verified. The plan should include a verification step before implementation: check the input module.\n\n**Shooting:**\n- `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` for hit testing (havok-queries.ts:207)\n- Collision masks: bullets only hit player/bots/terrain? Bullets vs bots: bot = character controller (kinematic) — does a raycast hit character controllers? Character controllers are typically kinematic bodies... need to verify. Alternative: raycast in mesh space via `createGpuPicker` + `createPickingRay` (picking module) — pick against the bot's display mesh. Actually, for bullets, doing a physics raycast against the world and then manually checking the bots' capsule proximity is simpler. Or use the GPU picker against the bot mesh.\n\nHmm, let me think about the cleanest:\n- Bullets vs terrain: `physicsRaycast` against terrain tiles.\n- Bullets vs bots: bots are character controllers with a display mesh. A `physicsRaycast` might not hit kinematic character controllers. Use the GPU picker's `createPickingRay` against the bot's display mesh. This requires a picker and picking ray per frame — no problem.\n\nDebris from terrain: `applyPhysicsImpulse` / `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` / `removePhysicsBody` for debris bodies; debris rendered as thin instances of a rock/box mesh with ballistic integration... wait, if debris is a physics body (dynamic), its position comes from the physics world (`getPhysicsBodyPosition`? need to verify — the map notes `applyPhysicsImpulse (havok.ts:1043) / setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity (:1063) / removePhysicsBody (:1112)` for \"spawning, kicking, and killing debris bodies\"). So debris = dynamic physics bodies rendered as thin instances, reading positions from the physics world each frame.\n\n**HUD:**\n- `createTextLayer`/`createTextRenderable` (text module, scenes 180-181) for score/health/HUD\n\n**Bots:**\n- Navigation module: `createNavMesh(plugin, meshes, params)`, `createNavCrowd`/`addAgent`/`agentGoto`/`updateNavCrowd`, `computePath`, `findRandomPoint`\n- Bot = character controller + nav crowd agent? Or bot = character controller with a path from `computePath`. The nav plugin is `NavigationPlugin` — need to check how it's created (probably `createNavigationPlugin(world)`?). Scenes 170-175 have the reference implementation.\n- Bots pick random points, move, and shoot the player on line of sight (`raycast(plugin, start, end)` for LOS, or a physics raycast).\n\nHmm wait — there's a subtlety: the navmesh is built from the terrain mesh at startup. When a tile is destroyed, `addBoxObstacle`/`removeObstacle`/`updateNavMeshObstacles` handle dynamic obstacles. But removing terrain (holes) — is there a \"remove obstacle\" equivalent? `removeObstacle` exists. For a hole, you can add a box obstacle in place of the hole. Actually, the simplest: treat destroyed tiles as obstacles (addBoxObstacle), and bots won't walk over holes.\n\n**Audio:**\n- Web Audio on the DOM side: gunshots, impacts. Simple oscillator/noise-based sounds.\n\n**Files to create (lab demo):**\n- `lab/lite/src/lite/scene<NNN>.ts` — the FPS scene entry. What number? There are 189 scene files (scene1..scene189?). Need to check the highest number. Or a directory-based scene? Lab scenes are flat files `scene18.ts` etc. Need to find the next available number. Verification step.\n- The lab site must register the scene — check how scenes are registered (scripts/build-lab-site.ts copies HavokPhysics.wasm; there may be a manifest). Verification step.\n\nStructure: the scene file must read like a high-level API demo. Probably split into a few modules:\n- `scene190.ts` (or whatever) — entry: engine/scene setup, terrain, player, bots, HUD, input\n- Maybe helper modules under `lab/lite/src/lite/` for terrain destruction, bullets, bots. But the constraint says scene code must read like a high-level API demo — game logic is fine, but should use the API cleanly. A few small modules are OK.\n\nActually, \"never a WebGPU tutorial\" means: no raw GPU API, no low-level shader plumbing. DOM events for input are fine.\n\n**Plan steps:**\n\n1. **Re-verify the API surface** (read-only):\n   - Check the input module / pointer lock usage (is there an engine input helper?)\n   - Check the character controller API details (moveWithCollisions, getPosition, velocity access, supported state)\n   - Check the nav plugin creation (scenes 170-175)\n   - Check the text module API (scenes 180-181)\n   - Check the picking API (createGpuPicker, createPickingRay)\n   - Check the physics body query functions (getPhysicsBodyPosition etc.) for debris\n   - Check how lab scenes are registered (site manifest / build script) and find the next scene number\n   - Check lab/vite.config.ts for the port configuration\n   - Read scene40.ts in full (physics reference), scene18.ts (camera reference)\n\n2. **Scaffold the scene**: new lab scene file with the engine/scene four-phase scaffold (createEngine → build scene → registerScene → startEngine), a free camera for FPS, a noise-generated terrain via `createFlatGroundData` + subdivision + `uploadMeshToGPU` (or `createGround` with custom positions? need to check whether createGround accepts custom heights — `applyHeightmap` is private; so use `createFlatGroundData` and modify positions myself).\n\n   Terrain generation: value/simplex noise implemented on the CPU side in the scene file (small, no external dependency). Grid: e.g., 128x128 tiles? Tile = 1 unit? Let me think: a 64x64 tile arena, each tile 4x4 vertices (so a 256x256 vertex grid). Per-tile physics boxes.\n\n3. **Terrain destruction (tile deletion)**:\n   - Build index surgery: each tile owns a range of indices in the flat grid's index buffer. Destroy = zero out / remove those indices, recompute normals (`computeNormals`), `uploadMeshToGPU`.\n   - Physics: one static box body per tile (top face at the tile's max height). On destruction, `removePhysicsBody` + `addBoxObstacle` for the navmesh (hole).\n   - Performance: re-upload the entire index buffer on each destruction — no problem for the demo (throttled).\n\n4. **Player controller**: character controller, Quake-style movement (slide friction, air control, jump gated by `CharacterSupportedState`), camera follow, pointer lock mouse look, WASD, jump.\n\n5. **Shooting**: pointer lock + click → raycast (physics raycast for terrain, GPU picker ray for bots), spawn hit effects; damage; kill.\n\n6. **Debris**: on tile destruction, spawn a few dynamic physics bodies (boxes) with `applyPhysicsImpulse`, render as thin instances of a debris mesh, read positions from the physics world each frame, despawn after a lifetime.\n\n7. **Bots**: character controller + navmesh (built from the terrain mesh), pick random points with `findRandomPoint`, `agentGoto` or `computePath` for path following, LOS check, shoot the player with some accuracy, take damage, die → respawn.\n\n8. **HUD**: text layer for health/ammo/score, crosshair.\n\n9. **Audio**: Web Audio DOM side: gunshot, impact, tile destruction.\n\n10. **Dev server**: run on port 5182 (override lab's 5174). Test: manually verify the scene loads, move around, shoot, destroy tiles, bots move.\n\n11. **Check**: run type check (`pnpm tsc`?), the lab build, and Playwright smoke tests via `pnpm exec playwright` if any exist for the lab.\n\nThe plan phase says: \"Turn the chosen approach into a plan: the steps, the files each step touches, and how each will be verified. Record the plan and its decisions with update_record. Don't modify any files yet. Once the plan is agreed, propose a phase transition with propose_phase_transition.\"\n\nSo I should:\n1. Do a little more verification (read-only) to firm up the plan? The phase is \"plan\" — the ground rules say don't modify files. Reading is fine. A few targeted reads would make the plan more concrete: check the next scene number, the vite config port, the nav plugin creation, the text module API, and whether there's an input module.\n\nLet me be efficient: a few bash/grep calls in parallel.\n\nActually, the plan phase ground rules say don't modify files — reading is fine. A few targeted reads will make the plan more concrete:\n- Next scene number: `ls lab/lite/src/lite/ | tail`\n- Vite config: `cat lab/vite.config.ts`\n- Nav plugin creation: `grep -n \"createNavigationPlugin\\|NavigationPlugin\" src/navigation/*.ts | head`\n- Text module exports: `grep -n \"export function\" src/text/*.ts | head -30`\n- Input: `ls src/` to check for an input directory\n- Physics body position query: `grep -n \"export function get\" src/physics/havok.ts | head -30`\n- Character controller details: `grep -n \"export\" src/physics/character-controller.ts`\n- Scene registration in the lab: check `lab/vite.config.ts` or the build script for how scenes are discovered.\n\nLet me run these in parallel.\n\nThen record the plan with update_record (field: plan), record the decisions, and propose a phase transition.\n\nLet me first run the verification batch.\n","seq":20757,"t":910313,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":14336,"draft_n":9667,"draft_n_accepted":2463,"predicted_ms":53160.0,"predicted_n":3844,"prompt_ms":390.0,"prompt_n":265},"to_request":19454,"usage":{"cached_tokens":14336,"completion_tokens":3844,"prompt_tokens":14601}}
