{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Excellent. Now I have everything I need:\n\n1. **Scene registration**: `readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` — the lab site auto-discovers scene files inside `lab/lite/` (HTML pages) — I need to understand how the scene HTML works. Let me check: the lab has HTML files inside `lab/lite/` (e.g., `scene18.html`?). Let me quickly check what's in `lab/lite/`.\n\nActually, line 120: `const liteHtml = readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` — this is reading the `lab/lite/` directory. Let me look at that section of vite.config.ts to understand how new scenes get registered in the gallery.\n\n2. **Physics body creation**: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` — a static body is tied to a SceneNode. Interesting — bodies are attached to scene nodes. So per-tile static bodies need a SceneNode per tile. That's 64x64 = 4096 nodes... hmm, that might be heavy. Alternative: fewer, larger tiles (e.g., 32x32 = 1024 tiles), or use a different approach.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider. Per-tile static physics bodies via `createPhysicsAggregate` require a SceneNode per tile. 1024–4096 static bodies in Havok — probably fine (static bodies are cheap), but creating 4096 scene nodes just for physics... The node is a SceneNode — is a minimal node cheap? Let me check what the minimum SceneNode is (probably `createTransformNode` or similar). Also `createPhysicsBody(world, node, motionType)` needs a node.\n\nAlternatively, use a heightfield shape for the entire terrain for walking (createHeightFieldShape exists — havok-heightfield.ts:59), and on tile destruction... the heightfield can't be modified at runtime. Hmm.\n\nBut wait — the approved approach is \"tile removal — index surgery on a single dense grid mesh + per-tile static physics bodies.\" So per-tile static bodies it is. To keep the node count reasonable, use medium-sized tiles: e.g., a 128x128 unit arena with 4-unit tiles → 32x32 = 1024 tiles. The terrain mesh grid is dense: 128x128 vertices (each tile = 4x4 vertices). 1024 static box bodies — acceptable for a demo.\n\nActually, let me check the heightfield options once more — a heightfield shape takes the heightmap directly. If we use a heightfield for the terrain, physics would match the mesh exactly (better for Quake-style movement). But when a tile is destroyed, the heightfield still has that tile → you'd walk into a hole. So per-tile boxes are necessary for the destruction to work. Per-tile boxes it is (approved).\n\nTo reduce mismatch: make tile height uniform (flat top = max height of the tile), and generate the noise so adjacent heights are close (low-frequency noise). Or make the per-tile box top the tile's average height. Either way, with gentle noise the visual/physics mismatch is small.\n\n3. **Character controller**: `getPosition()`, `getVelocity()`, `moveWithCollisions(displacement)`, `supportedState` (via the surface info from moveWithCollisions' return value? Line 480: `if (info.supportedState === ...)` — moveWithCollisions probably returns a surface info). Let me check the return type of moveWithCollisions. Lines 384–480 suggest that moveWithCollisions computes an `info` with a supportedState and probably returns it. Let me quickly verify.\n\n4. **Debris**: dynamic physics body via `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 1 })`, position read via... the node's position (body→node sync is done by the engine: \"after body→node sync\"). So debris rendering reads the node's position — no need for a physics getter. Thin instances of a debris mesh, updated from node positions each frame.\n\n5. **Picking for bullets vs bots**: `createGpuPicker(scene)` + pick per click. But bullets are fast — better to use `physicsRaycast` for terrain and a manual capsule-vs-ray test for bots (bots are character controllers, not physics bodies, so a physics raycast probably won't hit them). Actually, let me check whether physicsRaycast hits character controllers... character controllers are probably kinematic bodies inside the Havok world (BJS's PhysicsCharacterController creates a kinematic body). If kinematic, a physics raycast might hit them. But more simply and deterministically: for bullets, do a manual ray vs capsule (a sphere segment) test against each bot. Bots are only a handful. And use physicsRaycast for terrain hits (to get the hit point/normal for debris and damage).\n\nThis is clean: bullet = ray from camera; terrain via physicsRaycast; bots via manual ray-capsule intersection (pick whichever is closer).\n\n6. **Navmesh for bots**: `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` — the wasm exists in lab/public. Build the navmesh from the terrain mesh. Bots: character controller + navmesh path following (computePath + per-step moveWithCollisions along the path). Use NavCrowd for steering, or more simply: pick a random point, computePath, follow waypoints. NavCrowd with `agentGoto` gives bot-vs-bot separation automatically — use the crowd (createNavCrowd, addAgent, agentGoto, updateNavCrowd, getAgentPosition/Velocity). The bot's character controller moves toward the agent's target/velocity.\n\nActually, the simplest and most robust approach: each bot = character controller + crowd agent. Each physics step: `updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt)`, get the agent's velocity, `moveWithCollisions(velocity * dt)`. That's exactly what the nav scenes do.\n\n7. **Scene registration**: I need to check how a new scene file becomes a gallery entry. Let me look at lines 100–160 of vite.config.ts and the structure of lab/lite/. Also, check whether there's a manifest file (a JSON listing scenes) that I need to update.\n\nLet me check the structure of lab/lite/ and the HTML pages.\n","seq":22470,"t":972603,"text":"\n\nAlmost there. Two last structural questions: how lab scenes are registered in the gallery, and the exact shape of `moveWithCollisions`' return value