{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking about gotchas I discovered in the step I just completed (the verification batch). Let me think about what I learned in that step and what differs from my prior expectations:\n\n1. **Vite config port**: The lab README says `pnpm dev:lab` runs on 5174, and I had recorded that the port needs to be overridden. Now confirmed: `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174` — the override mechanism is the environment variable `LAB_DEV_PORT`, not a CLI flag. So to run on 5182, use `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`. This is a refinement of the prior gotcha (interview-t2-1), and it's the concrete mechanism discovered in this step. Worth listing.\n\n2. **Scene numbers**: Latest is scene251 — the next scene will be scene252. Nothing surprising.\n\n3. **`DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL`** points to `lab/lite/src/demos/` — this is the GitHub URL used for source links on the gallery cards, but the actual scenes are in `lab/lite/src/lite/`. Hmm, does this mean the demos directory exists? I only grepped vite.config for \"scene|lite|demos\" with filters. Line 120: `const liteHtml = readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` — this reads the `lab/lite` directory. This suggests the lab has a `lab/lite/` directory containing HTML files. The demos URL in the GitHub repo may be stale, or the directory may have been renamed. This is a potential pitfall when registering a new scene: I need to understand how the scene list is built (readdirSync of `lab/lite`? or of `lite/src/lite`?). I haven't fully resolved this yet — it's an open question, not yet a discovered gotcha. Actually, I've discovered that scene discovery isn't a simple glob: vite.config does `readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` at line 120 — meaning the lab root `lab/lite/` directory contains something (HTML?) and the scene list is built from that. Not fully resolved, so I should note it as something to verify during implementation rather than as a solid gotcha.\n\n4. **Navigation plugin**: `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` — async, and requires the wasm asset. `lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm` exists (confirmed in the listing). So nav works the same way as Havok. Not a gotcha.\n\n5. **Character controller**: I confirmed that `getPosition()`/`getVelocity()` are methods on the controller instance (a class with methods — fine since it's an object returned from a factory). The controller exposes `supportedState`... actually, `supportedState` is on the `CharacterSurfaceInfo` interface (line 114) — i.e., the state is read per-step from the surface info, not directly from the controller. Hmm, line 113-114: \"Whether the character is unsupported, sliding, or supported. supportedState: CharacterSupportedState.\" is inside `CharacterSurfaceInfo`. So ground detection is: read `supportedState` from the surface info — how do you get that outside of `moveWithCollisions`? Perhaps via the `onTriggerCollisionObservable` event, or maybe there's a `getSurfaceInfo()` method. I didn't confirm how to read the supported state after a step. This is an open detail for implementation, not a discovered contradiction.\n\n6. **PhysicsBody has no position field**: The `PhysicsBody` interface (lines 156-167) has `node: SceneNode` — so you read the body's position via its `node` (SceneNode position), not via a `getPhysicsBodyPosition` function. I had been expecting a `getPhysicsBodyPosition` function. Discovery: body transform is read through `body.node.position`. This is a real API-shape discovery: there is no `getPhysicsBodyPosition` export, the body wraps a `SceneNode`. IMPLICATION: debris/bot position reads go through `body.node.position`.\n\nActually wait — is this \"different from expected\"? In the working-set map I had recorded `applyPhysicsImpulse`/`setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity`/`removePhysicsBody` for debris. I was assuming I could read positions via a physics function. The interface shows `node: SceneNode` — the physics system syncs body→node each step (scene40's comment says \"after the body→node sync\"), so `node.position` is the source of truth for rendering. That's the discovery.\n\n7. **`createPhysicsAggregate`** — scene40 shows the pattern: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, mesh, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` for static bodies, `{ mass: 1, restitution: 0.75 }` for dynamic. So bodies are created from existing scene nodes (meshes) — a physics body is attached to a SceneNode. Important implication for per-tile static physics: you don't create a bare box body in world space — you create an aggregate tied to a node. So each tile needs a SceneNode (e.g., an invisible mesh, or a Mesh node) to attach the static box body to. Hmm — is there a way to create a physics body without a visible mesh? `createPhysicsBody(world, node, motionType)` requires a node. So per-tile static bodies require per-tile nodes. This is a design discovery: the tile physics approach needs one node per tile (e.g., a thin box mesh, or an empty node?). Is there a \"create empty node\" API? I don't know. Maybe you can use a Box mesh with a material, or there might be a node factory. This is an important design implication for the tile-removal approach, but I haven't verified the empty-node API yet. It's a real discovery that the physics body is node-anchored (which I already knew from the `node: SceneNode` field, but its implications for 256+ per-tile bodies are a design concern).\n\nActually, this was partially known: `createPhysicsBody(world, node, ...)` — the node parameter was visible in the export list. But the specific pattern from scene40 (`createPhysicsAggregate(world, ground, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })`) is the static-body idiom. I'd already recorded that aggregates exist. Not a big surprise.\n\n8. **`DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL` and `lab/lite` readdir** — the lab structure: scenes in `lab/lite/src/lite/`, but vite reads `lab/lite` (line 120). I still don't know how a new scene file gets registered in the gallery. This is an open question, not a gotcha yet.\n\n9. **`hasCpuDeformation`/`computeDeformedPositions` in picking/deformed-geometry.ts** — interesting: the picking module has CPU deformation support! This suggests meshes can be deformed on the CPU and picking accounts for it. Relevant to terrain editing, but not a contradiction.\n\n10. **Text API**: `createTextLayer(data, options)` — takes `TextData`, not a string directly. And `createTextRenderer(surface, opts)` requires a `SurfaceContext`. So the HUD text path is: font → glyph storage → text data → text layer → text renderer on a surface. That's a fairly multi-step API. Scene 180/181 show the usage. The discovery is that the text API is more elaborate than \"createTextLayer('hello')\" — it requires a TextData object built from glyph storage, and a renderer attached to a SurfaceContext. I noted scenes 180-181 as references, so that was known. But the specific chain (TextData from GlyphStorage, TextRenderer on SurfaceContext) is more elaborate than my \"createTextLayer/createTextRenderable\" note. Worth listing as a minor gotcha: the HUD needs a full text-renderer chain, and I should copy the scene180/181 pattern verbatim.\n\n11. **Vite config comment**: \"The default interactive port is 5174. Playwright test runs pass LAB_DEV_PORT to spin up an ISOLATED server on a dedicated port (so test traffic never...)\" — meaning LAB_DEV_PORT is the documented override. This confirms the mechanism. Good — this is a concrete refinement: the override is the env var `LAB_DEV_PORT`, and the Playwright tests use it.\n\nSo, the gotchas I should report (discoveries from this step only):\n\n1. GOTCHA: The lab's dev-server port override is the env var `LAB_DEV_PORT` (vite.config.ts:743, `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174`), not a CLI flag — IMPLICATION: start the lab with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`.\n\n2. GOTCHA: `PhysicsBody` has no position accessor; it wraps a `SceneNode` (`node` field, havok.ts:163), and the world syncs body→node each step — IMPLICATION: read debris/bot positions via `body.node.position`, there is no `getPhysicsBodyPosition` function.\n\n3. GOTCHA: Physics bodies are node-anchored — `createPhysicsBody(world, node, ...)` requires a SceneNode, and the static idiom is `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` (scene40.ts) — IMPLICATION: per-tile static colliders require one node per tile, and I need to check whether an empty/invisible node can be created, or else I must create per-tile box meshes.\n\n4. GOTCHA: The text/HUD API is a multi-step chain — `createTextLayer` takes a `TextData` (built from GlyphStorage/font), and rendering requires a `TextRenderer` created on a `SurfaceContext` (`addTextRendererLayer`) — IMPLICATION: the HUD must copy the full setup chain from scene180/181 rather than assuming a simple `createTextLayer(\"...\")`.\n\n5. GOTCHA: The nav plugin is async and wasm-backed — `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` (scene170.ts:53), and the asset is at `lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm` — IMPLICATION: bot setup must await the plugin, and the wasm is already served so no build-script changes are needed.\n\nHmm, is #5 a gotcha or just confirmation? It confirms my map entry (scenes 170-175, @recast-navigation/wasm). It also confirms that lab/public already has recast-navigation.wasm — so no asset-copy step is needed. This is a useful discovery (removes a step from the plan). Might be worth listing as a minor positive discovery. The format asks for \"things that behave differently than expected, errors and their actual causes, limitations or unsupported features discovered.\" #5 is not a deviation from expectation — it's confirmation. I'll skip it or include it as minor. I'll include the strongest ones.\n\n6. Scene discovery: `vite.config.ts:120` does `readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` — the lab's HTML pages are read from the `lab/lite` directory, while scene TS files are in `lab/lite/src/lite/`. The `DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL` points to `lab/lite/src/demos/` (stale?). I don't yet know how a new scene gets registered in the gallery. This is an open question — not a gotcha yet, but it is a discovery that registration is not automatic from a simple glob and I need to read vite.config's scene-list construction. Actually, is it a \"limitation discovered\"? It's an unresolved unknown. The instructions say \"only discoveries from this step.\" I discovered that vite.config builds the gallery from `readdirSync(\"lab/lite\")` and a `liteHtml` variable — meaning there may be per-scene HTML files in `lab/lite/`. I haven't looked at `lab/lite/` itself yet. Let me list this as a discovered open item: the scene gallery is built by a custom vite plugin (readdirSync of `lab/lite`), so I must verify how scene252 gets listed before implementation.\n\nLet me be careful not to over-report. The user wants deviations/discoveries. Strong candidates:\n\n1. LAB_DEV_PORT env var (concrete mechanism for the port override — refinement of a known gotcha, discovered this step).\n2. PhysicsBody.node — no position getter; read via node (API-shape discovery).\n3. Node-anchored bodies — per-tile colliders need per-tile nodes (design implication).\n4. Text chain is multi-step (TextData + TextRenderer on SurfaceContext) — copy scene180/181.\n5. Gallery/scene registration is a custom vite plugin reading `lab/lite` — need to verify registration before assuming a new scene file just works.\n\nAlso: character controller's `supportedState` lives on `CharacterSurfaceInfo` (returned per-step / via event), not as a direct getter on the controller — ground detection requires reading surface info. I confirmed `supportedState` at line 114 inside `CharacterSurfaceInfo`. How do you get surface info? I didn't see a `getSurfaceInfo` method in the grep. Methods I saw: getPosition, getVelocity, moveWithCollisions, onTriggerCollisionObservable. Hmm — the `CharacterSurfaceInfo` interface is at line 110, and lines 413-480 are internal logic (`this.maxSlopeCosine` etc.). There may be a public way to read the last surface info that I didn't see. This is an open item for implementation: confirm how to read the supported state (probably via the onTriggerCollisionObservable event or a method I haven't seen). Worth listing as a discovered open question.\n\nI'll list these concisely, one per line. Keep it to this step's discoveries only.\n","seq":24077,"t":1023688,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: The lab port override is an env var, not a CLI flag — `lab/vite.config.ts:743` is `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174` — IMPLICATION: start the lab with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`; there is no `--port` path.\nGOTCHA: `PhysicsBody` has no position accessor — the interface (havok.ts:156-167) wraps a `SceneNode` (`node` field) and the world syncs body→node each step — IMPLICATION: read debris/bot positions via `body.node.position`; there is no `getPhysicsBodyPosition` to plan around.\nGOTCHA: Physics bodies are node-anchored — `createPhysicsBody(world, node, ...)` requires a SceneNode, and the static idiom is `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` (scene40.ts) — IMPLICATION: per-tile static colliders need one node per tile; I must verify an empty/invisible node can be created before assuming the tile-removal physics approach is cheap.\nGOTCHA: The text/HUD API is a multi-step chain, not a one-liner — `createTextLayer` takes a `TextData` (built from `GlyphStorage`/font), and it only renders once a `TextRenderer` is created on a `SurfaceContext` and the layer is added to it — IMPLICATION: the HUD must copy the full setup chain from scene180/181 verbatim; my earlier \"createTextLayer a string\" mental model was wrong.\nGOTCHA: The lab gallery is built by a custom Vite plugin (`readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` at vite.config.ts:120), and `DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL` points at `lab/lite/src/demos/` while scenes actually live in `lab/lite/src/lite/` — IMPLICATION: I must read the plugin's scene-list construction and check how a new scene file gets registered in the gallery before assuming dropping `scene252.ts` in is enough.\nGOTCHA: The character controller's ground state lives on `CharacterSurfaceInfo` (character-controller.ts:110-114), not as a direct controller getter — the public surface I verified is only `getPosition`/`getVelocity`/`moveWithCollisions`/`onTriggerCollisionObservable` — IMPLICATION: I need to find how to read the last `supportedState` (event or method) for jump gating before implementing movement; I won't assume one exists.","timings":{"cache_n":26368,"draft_n":8708,"draft_n_accepted":2409,"predicted_ms":75020.0,"predicted_n":3653,"prompt_ms":520.0,"prompt_n":306},"to_request":21645,"usage":{"cached_tokens":26368,"completion_tokens":3653,"prompt_tokens":26674}}
