{"kind":"delta","request":22627,"seq":25855,"t":1086307,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"field\": \"plan\", \"content\": \"FPS plan (Quake-style, tile-removal terrain) — new lab demo, NO engine changes:\\n\\nFILES (all new, under ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite):\\n1. lab/lite/scene252.html — copy scene18.html pattern (canvas + /loader.js + module script); auto-discovered by getHtmlInputs() in lab/vite.config.ts. Next free number after scene251.\\n2. lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts — main scene: 4-phase scaffold (createEngine → build → registerScene → startEngine), Havok world (locateFile \\\"/HavokPhysics.wasm\\\"), player, bots, shooting, HUD, game loop.\\n3. lab/lite/src/lite/fps-terrain.ts — noise terrain + tile removal: build grid (~256×256 verts over 128×128u), tiles 4×4u → 32×32 = 1024 tiles; per-tile static box bodies (createPhysicsAggregate mass 0, top at tile max height); destroy = remove tile's index range from _cpuIndices, computeNormals, uploadMeshToGPU, removePhysicsBody, addBoxObstacle on navmesh.\\n4. lab/lite/src/lite/fps-input.ts — pointer lock + keyboard state (DOM-side; no engine input module exists).\\n5. lab/lite/src/lite/fps-audio.ts — Web Audio SFX (gunshot, thud, hit), lazy-init on first gesture, no module-level side effects.\\n\\nSTEPS:\\nS1 Verify remaining API details (read-only): frame-observable name (scene18), physicsRaycast query shape (havok-queries.ts:207), NavMeshParameters usage (scene170), whether controller exposes its shape for friction, bundle-size spec doesn't cover lab scenes.\\nS2 fps-terrain.ts: seeded value-noise heights, low-frequency (amp ~5u); createFlatGroundData → mutate positions → computeNormals → uploadMeshToGPU; per-tile static boxes + membership/collide masks; destroyTile() index surgery + re-upload + body removal + nav obstacle.\\nS3 scene252.ts: engine/scene/light/camera scaffold; createHavokWorld; terrain via fps-terrain; player = createPhysicsCharacterController (capsule 1.8/0.5), Quake movement in onPhysicsAfterStep (slide friction, air control, jump gated by checkSupport supportedState); camera follow w/ pointer-lock yaw/pitch.\\nS4 Shooting: click → ray from eye; physicsRaycast for terrain (hit → destroyTile), manual ray-capsule vs bots (closest wins); fire-rate limit; hit/kill feedback.\\nS5 Bots: createNavigationPluginAsync (recast-navigation.wasm) + createNavMesh from terrain mesh; N=4 bots = character controller + NavCrowd agent (agentGoto/findRandomPoint, updateNavCrowd per step); display = thin instances; bots LOS-check player (physicsRaycast) and shoot with distance-based accuracy; damage/respawn both sides.\\nS6 HUD: createTextLayer/createTextRenderer (pattern from scenes 180/181) — health, kills, tiles destroyed; crosshair.\\nS7 fps-audio.ts wired to events.\\nS8 Dev server: LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab (vite config reads LAB_DEV_PORT, default 5174); manual playtest checklist: move/slide/jump, mouse look, shoot holes, fall through → respawn, debris, bots patrol+shoot, HUD, audio. Add canvas.dataset.ready/error like scene40 for scriptability.\\n\\nCHECKS: typecheck (repo script), manual playtest on :5182/lite/scene252.html, confirm bundle-size.spec.ts unaffected, no module-level side effects, no raw GPU handles in scene code.\\n\\nRISKS: 1024 static bodies init cost (fallback: 8×8u tiles → 256 bodies); navmesh from dense mesh (tune NavMeshParameters); controller shape access for friction (fallback: low friction on terrain tile shapes).\"}","id":"call_731f2ddafab24ab79ace0962","index":0,"name":"update_record"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I have everything I need to write a concrete plan. Let me put it together:\n\n**Scene registration**: Copy the `scene18.html` pattern → `lab/lite/scene252.html` + `lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts`. Auto-detected by the vite config's `getHtmlInputs()`. No manifest to update.\n\n**Final key API anchors:**\n- `createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, gravity?)` — scene40 uses `await createHavokWorld(scene)` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"` (need to double-check the exact scene40 setup lines 1–80 — from the earlier head -80 I confirmed the world setup; the wasm path is /HavokPhysics.wasm, and it exists in lab/public)\n- `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` — static body tied to a SceneNode\n- Character controller: `createPhysicsCharacterController(world, pos, { capsuleHeight, capsuleRadius })`, `getPosition()`, `getVelocity()`, `setVelocity()`, `moveWithCollisions(displacement)`, `checkSupport(dt, dir) → CharacterSurfaceInfo { supportedState, ... }`, `setPosition()` (for respawn)\n- Navigation: `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })`, `createNavMesh(plugin, meshes, params)`, `createNavCrowd(plugin, maxAgents, maxAgentRadius)`, `addAgent`, `agentGoto`, `updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt)`, `getAgentPosition/Velocity`, `findRandomPoint(plugin)`, `addBoxObstacle(plugin, ...)` (navigation.ts:647+)\n- Text: `createTextLayer(data, options)`, `setTextLayerPosition`, `createTextRenderer(surface, opts)`, `addTextRendererLayer` — scenes 180/181 for the working pattern\n- Terrain: `createFlatGroundData(subdivisions)` → modify positions with noise → `computeNormals(positions, indices)` → `uploadMeshToGPU(engine, positions, normals, indices, ...)`; the mesh holds `_cpuPositions/_cpuNormals/_cpuIndices`\n- Debris: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, BOX, { mass: 1 })` + `applyPhysicsImpulse` / `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` + `removePhysicsBody`; body→node sync is automatic; thin instances for rendering\n- Bullets: `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` (havok-queries.ts:207) for terrain; bots via manual ray-capsule test (a few bots, per-frame or per-shot is cheap)\n- Collision mask: `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask` / `setPhysicsShapeFilterCollideMask` (havok.ts:578/589)\n- Friction: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` (havok.ts:971)\n\n**Structure of the scene code** (must read like a high-level API demo):\n- `scene252.ts` — entry: the 4-phase scaffold, world, terrain, player, bots, HUD, input, game loop\n- It'll get big (about 800–1200 lines). Should I split it? The constraint is that scene code should read like a high-level API demo — a single file might be fine, but a few small sibling modules would be cleaner. The lab convention seems to be one file per scene (scene18.ts is 2.5KB; scene40 is probably bigger). I'd propose one scene file + a few small helper modules in the same directory if needed (e.g., `fps-terrain.ts` for tile removal, `fps-noise.ts` for noise). Actually — to keep it simple and demo-like, I'll go with a single `scene252.ts` that's well-organized with section comments, and pull out the noise generator + tile terrain manager if it's getting too big. Hmm. Let me not over-commit: single file, with a note that if it exceeds about 1000 lines, I'll split out `fps-terrain.ts`.\n\nActually, on second thought: the tile removal logic (index surgery + physics body management + navmesh obstacles) is the meatiest part. A separate module `fps-terrain.ts` keeps the scene file focused on game flow. Plan: `scene252.ts` (game) + `fps-terrain.ts` (noise terrain + tile removal) + `fps-input.ts` (pointer lock + keyboard). This reads like a demo while staying clean.\n\nWait, but the constraint says \"the scene code in lab/lite/src is the user-facing reference and must read like a high-level API demo\" — DOM input (pointer lock, keyboard) is unavoidable since there's no engine input module. Fine — I'll organize it as a small input module.\n\n**Arena design:**\n- Arena: 128×128 units, tiles of 4×4 units → 32×32 = 1024 tiles\n- Terrain mesh: 128×128 vertices (1 vertex per unit), so each tile = 4×4 vertices (16 vertices, 6 triangles... actually a 4×4 vertex patch is 3×3 quads = 9 quads = 18 triangles). Hmm, let me redo: if the grid is 129×129 vertices covering 128×128 units with 1-unit spacing, a 4×4 unit tile spans 5×5 vertices, 4×4 quads = 8 triangles...\n\nSimpler: pick the vertex grid first. A 256×256 vertex grid over 128×128 units (2 vertices per unit). Tile = 4×4 units = 8×8 vertices = 7×7 quads = 14 triangles per tile. 32×32 tiles = 1024 tiles. Total triangles ≈ 1024 × 14 = 14,336 — light. 256×256 = 65,536 vertices — no problem.\n\nActually, for a Quake-style game a smaller arena might play better: 96×96 units, 4-unit tiles → 24×24 = 576 tiles, mesh 192×192 vertices. Hmm, or 128. I'll leave the exact numbers as tunable and note the design: tile size 4u, arena 128×128u, mesh = arena/0.5+1... I'll state: \"Mesh grid ≈ 256×256 vertices, tiles 4×4 units → 32×32 = 1024 tiles; final numbers tuned during implementation.\"\n\n- Noise: low-frequency value noise (2–3 octaves), amplitude ~4–6 units, plus a slight bowl to keep the arena together. CPU-side, seeded.\n\n**Physics per tile**: 1024 static boxes. Each needs a SceneNode. Check: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, BOX, { mass: 0 })` — the node provides position/size. So per tile: create a transform node (hidden, no mesh) at the tile center, box dimensions (4, height, 4), top surface at the tile's max height. 1024 nodes — is that OK? It's a demo; static bodies are cheap. Alternative: a single heightfield + per-tile boxes only for destroyed tiles... no, the heightfield can't be modified. Going with 1024 static boxes; if the world init is slow, I'll reduce to 2×2-unit tiles. I'll note it as a risk.\n\nActually wait — is there a way to create a static body without a SceneNode? `createPhysicsBody(world, node, motionType)` requires a node. OK, going with nodes.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: 1024 boxes vs. a single box per tile — the character controller will slide across box seams just fine.\n\n**Player (Quake-style):**\n- `createPhysicsCharacterController(world, spawn, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.5 })`\n- Low-friction material on the character shape for sliding: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction ~0.01, restitution 0)` — wait, how do I get the character's shape? The controller might expose it... in BJS, `controller.physicsShape`? I haven't verified the shape getter on the Lite controller. If not exposed, alternative: set the friction on the terrain's tile boxes (low friction = slide). Terrain friction is the same for bots too — Quake-style slide affects everything; that's fine/consistent. I'll plan: set low friction on the terrain tile shapes (via the shape from createPhysicsAggregate). If the controller exposes its shape, I'll set it there instead. Note: verify during implementation.\n- Movement each physics step (`onPhysicsAfterStep`): input direction × speed, acceleration, ground friction decay, air control (reduced acceleration when `checkSupport` is unsupported), jump: set upward velocity when supported.\n- Camera: a free camera manually driven to `cc.getPosition()` + eye height, yaw/pitch from pointer-lock mouse deltas. Use `createFreeCamera`? Or the minimal camera from scene18. I'll use a camera created the scene18 way and set position/rotation each frame (render loop via onBeforeRenderObservable? Check the scene's frame event — there's `startEngine`; frame loop hook: `onFrameObservable`? I haven't verified the name. Scene18 probably shows the pattern for camera animation. I'll note: verify the frame-observable name from scene18/scene40 during implementation.)\n\n**Shooting:**\n- On click (while pointer-locked): fire — a ray from the camera's eye in the camera-forward direction\n- Terrain: `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` → hit point/normal → damage to tile → tile removal\n- Bots: manual ray-capsule test per bot (capsule = bot's position from the controller, radius/height) → closest hit wins\n- Muzzle/hit feedback: thin-instance hit sparks? Keep it simple: no tracers (no particles); flash + sound. Debris from destroyed tiles.\n- Ammo: infinite? Quake-style: a single weapon, unlimited ammo, or clip-based. Keep it simple: unlimited ammo, fire rate limited.\n\n**Bots:**\n- N = 4 bots (tunable)\n- Each: a character controller + a navmesh crowd agent + a display mesh (a capsule/box thin instance or an actual small mesh; the simplest: a scaled box mesh, or a thin instance of one box mesh with a per-bot matrix)\n- Navmesh: `createNavMesh(plugin, [terrainMesh], params)` after terrain creation; on tile destruction, `addBoxObstacle` over the hole (so bots avoid it)\n- Each physics step: `updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt)`; bot movement = the agent's velocity × dt via `moveWithCollisions`; occasionally pick a new `findRandomPoint`\n- Bots shoot the player: line-of-sight check (a `physicsRaycast` from the bot's eye to the player's eye; if the terrain doesn't block → fire; a small chance to hit based on distance/aim); the player takes damage → health HUD; death → respawn\n- Bot death: fade out / remove, respawn after a delay\n- Bots also shoot each other? Keep it simple: bots only target the player (and maybe each other if it's cheap — defer; bots attack the player).\n\n**HUD:**\n- Text layer: health, kills, tile-destruction count; crosshair: a small DOM element or a text layer. Use `createTextLayer`/`createTextRenderer` following scenes 180/181.\n\n**Audio:**\n- Web Audio on the DOM side: gunshot (noise burst), tile destruction (low thud), hit (click). A small `fps-audio.ts` module, lazily initialized on the first user gesture (browser autoplay policy).\n\n**File list:**\n1. `lab/lite/scene252.html` — new (copy the scene18.html pattern, title \"Quake-style FPS — Destructible Terrain\")\n2. `lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts` — main scene/game code\n3. `lab/lite/src/lite/fps-terrain.ts` — noise terrain generation + tile removal (index surgery, normal recomputation, re-upload, per-tile static bodies, navmesh obstacle hooks)\n4. `lab/lite/src/lite/fps-input.ts` — pointer lock + keyboard state\n5. `lab/lite/src/lite/fps-audio.ts` — Web Audio SFX (lazy init)\n\nNo engine (`packages/babylon-lite`) changes — the whole thing uses the existing API. This matches the \"don't copy Babylon.js code\" and tree-shaking constraints; no module-level side effects (lazy audio context, no module-level Maps).\n\n**Verification steps:**\n- `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit` or the repo's typecheck script (verify the name) — check `pnpm -r typecheck` etc.\n- Dev server: `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` in the background; open `http://localhost:5182/lite/scene252.html`\n- Manual test checklist: move (WASD), slide on flat terrain, jump, mouse look, shoot → tile hole appears, fall through the hole (spawn a floor below? or respawn), debris flies, bots patrol + shoot, HUD updates, sound plays\n- Optional: Playwright smoke via `pnpm exec playwright` if the lab has a test harness for scenes (there's a pattern with `canvas.dataset.ready` like scene40 — I can add `canvas.dataset.ready` + error reporting so it's scriptable).\n- Check the bundle-size spec isn't affected (it's not a parity scene — no ceiling entry; confirm that `tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts` doesn't include lab scenes... the constraint says bundle size = runtime JS bytes for lite; lab scenes are demos — verify the spec doesn't pick up scene252. A quick check during implementation.)\n\n**Risks / open items:**\n- 1024 static box bodies: world-creation cost; if slow, coarsen the tiles (2×2u → 4096... no, that's more; 8×8u → 256 bodies). Actually 8×8-unit tiles → 16×16 = 256 bodies, but the tile holes get big. Compromise: keep 4u; test performance; the fallback is 8u tiles.\n- Character-controller shape exposure for friction — verify; fallback is to set friction on the terrain shapes.\n- Navmesh built from a dense 256×256 mesh — recast may be slow or produce a coarse mesh; use `NavMeshParameters` (tileSize etc.) — verify from the scene170 usage.\n- Physics raycast query parameters (havok-queries.ts:207) — check the `query` shape at implementation time.\n- Frame-observable name for per-frame camera/HUD updates — verify from scene18.\n\nOK — I'll record the plan with update_record, and then propose a phase transition. Per the ground rules: \"Record the plan and its decisions with update_record. Don't modify any files yet. Once the plan is agreed, call for a phase transition with propose_phase_transition.\"\n\nThe user said \"tile removal is fine. Plan it and proceed.\" — so the plan is basically agreed on, and after I record it I can propose the transition to the next phase (implementation/build). What's the name of the next phase? The regime says \"phase: plan\". The phase is probably \"build\" or \"implement\". I'll propose \"build\" — hmm, I don't know the exact phase name. From the arm name \"t1-phased-floor-r3\" and the earlier transition (\"to\": \"plan\"), the phase names are probably: orient → plan → build → verify? I'll propose \"build\" with a reason. If the operator knows a different name, they'll correct me.\n\nLet me write the plan record. It should be comprehensive but concise. I'll record:\n1. Plan (steps + files + verification)\n2. Decisions (tile-removal design parameters, no engine changes, file layout)\n\nAnd mark the \"open\" record (user hasn't chosen an approach) as resolved → resolve_entry? That entry is `r11177/call_a78853d904634d32a2e0e01d/open` — \"User has not yet chosen a terrain-destruction approach.\" The user chose tile removal. I can resolve_entry with verdict \"done\". And update the decision record.\n\nLet me write the plan record first.\n","seq":25856,"t":1086308,"text":"\n\nAll anchors verified. The scene registration is auto-discovery (HTML in `lab/lite/` → `getHtmlInputs()` in `lab/vite.config.ts`), so no manifest editing needed. Let me record the plan.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":29952,"draft_n":12264,"draft_n_accepted":2990,"predicted_ms":104240.0,"predicted_n":4742,"prompt_ms":760.0,"prompt_n":577},"to_request":22627,"usage":{"cached_tokens":29952,"completion_tokens":4742,"prompt_tokens":30529}}
