ly constraints that surfaced in this step.\"\n\nIs \"the lab's dev server port is configurable via the LAB_DEV_PORT environment variable, defaulting to 5174\" a constraint? It's an environmental fact, not a rule. But combined with the session rule, it becomes: \"The lab must be launched with LAB_DEV_PORT=5182, not plain `pnpm dev:lab`.\" That's a requirement that must be honored for the rest of the project (any dev server invocation). This did surface in this step. I think it's reasonable to list it as a constraint — it refines the existing one with the exact mechanism.\n\nOther things that could arguably be constraints:\n- \"New lab scenes go in lab/lite/src/lite/ with a unique sceneN.ts number (the most recent is 251)\" — this is a naming convention, not really a \"must never do\" constraint.\n- The physics pattern `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` — a fact.\n\nHonestly, the main constraint-like thing in this step is the LAB_DEV_PORT mechanism. The rest are API facts/pointers.\n\nShould I say NONE? The prompt says \"Did you learn or commit to any constraints the rest of the project must honor.\" The LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 requirement definitely is something the rest of the project must honor (every time I start the dev server). And it surfaced in this step (the env var mechanism was learned here). I'll list it.\n\nAnything else? Let me think about \"must never do\" items:\n- Nothing about copying Babylon.js (already recorded).\n- Nothing about the golden reference.\n- Scene numbering: not a must-never.\n\nOne more: the text renderer needs a `SurfaceContext` — `createTextRenderer(surface, opts)` — the HUD's text layer is added to the text renderer, which is registered... `registerTextRenderer(tr)`. A fact, not a constraint.\n\nI'll list just the one constraint about the dev server port mechanism. Actually, wait — should I re-list the port 5182 constraint itself? It was recorded in the previous step (turn 4: \"Dev server must run on