{"ask":"audit_q","at":25956,"kind":"fork","lane":"audit","of_turn":5,"question":"We're at a phase boundary, so I want to audit our working notes (working notes). For each note below, reply with EXACTLY one line, each starting with its number:\n<n>. KEEP\nor <n>. UPDATE: <the corrected one-line note>\nor <n>. REMOVE — <one-clause reason>\nor <n>. REMOVE — dup of #<k>  (when it duplicates another note here)\nJudge against everything that has actually happened in this session. Output 95 lines and nothing else.\n\n1. No CCD/continuous-collision export visible in the havok.ts, havok-queries.ts, and havok-heightfield.ts greps — fast Quake-style bullets may tunnel through thin terrain; remaining physics files (havok-collision.ts, character-controller.ts, etc.) are unchecked.\n2. No runtime vertex-update export found, but the grep only covered export-function lines in mesh.ts, compute-normals.ts, and mesh-dispose.ts (3 of ~25 mesh/*.ts files) — in-place deformation is off the table unless via uploadMeshToGPU rebuild or tile deletion, and non-function exports in other mesh files remain unchecked.\n3. applyPhysicsImpulse (havok.ts:1043) / setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity (:1063) / removePhysicsBody (:1112) — spawn, kick, and kill debris bodies; line 518 is actually applyPhysicsBodyImpulse.\n4. The \"fact\" record on no particles/audio was dropped by the groundedness gate, but the constraint now survives in the working object as #63 — treat it as settled; no re-recording needed.\n5. constraint: WebGPU-exclusive — no WebGL fallback, no legacy wrappers or abstraction layers for older APIs; WebGL 1/2 are marked \"won't support\" by design.\n6. gotcha: The repo's dev server (`pnpm dev:lab`) launches Vite on port 5174, while this session's ground rules say the dev server uses 5182 — IMPLICATION: check `lab/vite.config.ts` for the port binding before starting anything; if 5182 is required, override the port rather than assuming `pnpm dev:lab` will land there.\n7. constraint: Bundle-size ceilings in `tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts` must never be raised without approval; golden reference screenshots in `reference/lite/` are immutable.\n8. constraint: Priority order when trade-offs conflict: size > performance > simplicity; Babylon.js backward compatibility is not a first-class goal (e.g., classic `InstancedMesh` API is deliberately replaced by thin instances).\n9. constraint: `lab/lite/src` scene code is the user-facing reference and must read like a high-level API demo, never a WebGPU tutorial.\n10. constraint: `GUIDANCE.md` is immutable unless the user explicitly requests changes, and must be re-read at session start and after every compaction.\n11. gotcha: Playwright must be invoked via `pnpm exec`; using `npx`/`npm` can create a rogue `node_modules/playwright` that shadows the pnpm-managed copy and breaks test runs (\"did not expect test() to be called here\") — IMPLICATION: always use `pnpm exec playwright ...`; if it misbehaves, `rm -rf node_modules/playwright` and reinstall.\n12. gotcha: `babylon-lite`'s package.json points `main`/`types`/`exports` at raw `src/index.ts` (with `sideEffects: false`); `dist` only exists for publishing — IMPLICATION: dev/test consumes source directly with no build step; a publishable artifact requires `pnpm build` (vite + vite-plugin-dts).\n13. gotcha: GUIDANCE.md forbids module-level side effects — a module-level `new Map()/Set()/WeakMap()` kills tree-shaking, and caches must be lazy-init and invalidated on device change — IMPLICATION: any new engine code needs the lazy-cache pattern (`let cache: Map | null = null`) and no import-time registration or `globalThis` mutation.\n14. gotcha: production bundles minify inline WGSL template literals (strip comments, newlines, leading whitespace) — IMPLICATION: never parse emitted WGSL strings (split on `\\\\n`, regex); structured data must live in typed interfaces (`UboField[]`, `BindingDecl[]`), WGSL strings are shader code only.\n15. gotcha: Lite intentionally omits WebGL (WebGPU-only), the classic `InstancedMesh` API (thin instances instead), particles, GUI, WebXR, LOD/octree, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, asset manager and scene serialization — IMPLICATION: porting a BJS scene can hit hard stops; use the documented alternatives or stay on Babylon.js for those features.\n16. gotcha: the session working directory is empty; the repo lives at `~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite` — IMPLICATION: all file access needs absolute paths, and I should confirm where any working copy lives before writing code.\n17. constraint: One-way data ownership — only the scene knows its contents; components (lights, cameras, meshes) are plain data that never reference the scene; factories don't take a scene parameter, callers use `addToScene()`.\n18. constraint: Pure state interfaces — all public interfaces have no attached methods; behavior lives in standalone functions taking the interface as first argument (methods can't be tree-shaken).\n19. constraint: Do not split a type into public `Foo` + `FooInternal` companions — put internal members on the same type tagged `@internal` (d.ts trim pass strips them).\n20. constraint: Public API must never expose raw WebGPU handles (`GPUTexture`, `GPUTextureView`, `GPUSampler`, `GPUBuffer`, `GPUDevice`) — use `Texture2D` and similar abstractions.\n21. constraint: Do not copy Babylon.js code — understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels.\n22. constraint: Vite is the foundation for infrastructure, dev server, and production bundling; use `pnpm exec` for Playwright (never `npx`/`npm`, which can recreate a rogue `node_modules/playwright`).\n23. constraint: Bundle size = runtime fetched JS bytes only (Playwright network interception), excluding local `*-nme.ts` payload modules and the `text-shaper` vendor dependency; WGSL is minified in production.\n24. evidence: ```\\n * ```ts\\n *   const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\\n *   onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\\n *       cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\\n *       const p = cc.getPosition();\\n *       displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\\n *   });\\n * ```\\n```\\n\\nThat doc example is load-bearing: it confirms the player-controller API exists and gives me the exact pattern I'd build the FPS movement on — create the controller from the Havok world, drive it per physics step with an explicit displacement vector, read position back. It also tells me the controller is stepped via `onPhysicsAfterStep`, i.e. movement is coupled to the fixed physics timestep, not the render loop.\n25. evidence: ```\\nEvery Lite app follows the same four phases, in order:\\n\\n1. **Create the engine** — `await createEngine(canvas)` (acquires the GPU device).\\n2. **Build the scene** — create a `SceneContext`, then `addToScene()` your camera, lights, meshes, and loaded assets.\\n3. **Register** — `await registerScene(scene)` does the deferred GPU work (builds pipelines, partitions renderables). Call this **after** everything is added.\\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\\n\\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\\n```\\n\\n2. POINTERS:\n26. pointer: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` — per-shape friction, for Quake-style ground slide (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:971)\n27. pointer: `getPhysicsBodyDebugGeometry` — collider debug visualization while tuning the arena (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:1246)\n28. pointer: the doc continues past line 100 with the complete minimal example app (engine → scene → light → sphere) — I only read the first 100 lines; the full scaffold code is still unread (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §3 onward)\n29. pointer: optional features (glTF exts, clearcoat, HDR, shadows) are isolated modules dynamically imported on first use — matters when adding physics/shadows to the FPS under the bundle-size ceilings (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §2 \"You pay only for what you touch\")\n30. pointer: lab gallery has 189 scene files but I only saw the first 40; the FPS-relevant reference implementations are scene18 (free camera), scene40 (physics), scene113–115 (picking), scene180–181 (text/HUD) (lab/lite/src/lite/)\n31. pointer: published package name is `@babylonjs/lite` but the on-disk workspace package is `babylon-lite` — lab imports use the workspace name, which matters if I scaffold new lab scenes (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md §1 vs packages/babylon-lite/package.json)\n32. pointer: `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask` / `setPhysicsShapeFilterCollideMask` — collision layer filtering; needed so bullets/debris/player don't collide with each other (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:578/589)\n33. pointer: `applyHeightmap` is private and runs only at creation — heightmap ground is static, no runtime modification API (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/create-ground.ts:110)\n34. pointer: `csg.ts` / `csg2.ts` in src/mesh — Manifold-backed boolean ops; candidate for actually carving craters out of a terrain mesh (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/csg2.ts)\n35. pointer: `CharacterSupportedState` enum (UNSUPPORTED / SLIDING / SUPPORTED) — I'll need this later for ground detection (jump gating, Quake-style air-control rules). (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/character-controller.ts)\n36. pointer: `CharacterCollisionEvent` + `onTriggerCollisionObservable` — per-contact impulse callbacks; likely how the player would knock or push destructible debris, and possibly how destruction events propagate. (same file)\n37. pointer: `moveWithCollisions` takes a displacement vector, not a velocity — movement code must integrate velocity × dt each physics step; the header example (`{ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 }`) is that idiom. (same file)\n38. pointer: the controller is a standalone tree-shakeable module — `_stepWorld` in `havok.ts` never references it, so it only costs bytes if imported. (same file)\n39. pointer: it mirrors Babylon.js' `PhysicsCharacterController` (Apache-2.0), left-handed, +Y up — BJS experience transfers 1:1 when debugging behavior. (same file)\n40. pointer: `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` — the bullet-hit test against the physics world (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-queries.ts:207)\n41. pointer: `createHeightFieldShape(world, options)` — Havok heightfield collider so terrain physics can match a heightmap (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-heightfield.ts:59)\n42. pointer: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` is a pure CPU function — the hook for recomputing normals after we hand-edit terrain positions before re-upload (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/compute-normals.ts:9)\n43. pointer: `uploadMeshToGPU(...)` — the rebuild seam for a \"destroy-then-rebuild\" terrain approach (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh.ts:187)\n44. decision: Physics in the lab is wired through the Havok V2 wasm asset at `lab/public/HavokPhysics.wasm` — WHY: scene 40 imports `@babylonjs/havok` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"` and `scripts/build-lab-site.ts` copies that file into the lab site, so the lab can serve it as-is.\n45. decision: Destructible terrain is implementable without engine changes, via CPU geometry edit + re-upload — WHY: `mesh.ts` exposes `uploadMeshToGPU(engine, positions, normals, indices, ...)` plus `@internal` `_cpuPositions`/`_cpuNormals`/`_cpuIndices` copies and `computeNormals(positions, indices)`, giving a complete mutate-and-reupload path.\n46. decision: The terrain base mesh can be a dense subdivided grid — WHY: `createFlatGroundData`/`createGround` accept `subdivisions`, so a vertex-level digging mesh is available from the existing ground factory.\n47. decision: Any HUD will use the in-engine GPU text path rather than a GUI package — WHY: Lite has no GUI by design, and this step confirmed the text module is present and demonstrated in lab scenes 180/181.\n48. evidence: ```\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:192:export function createNavMesh(plugin: NavigationPlugin, meshes: Mesh[], params: NavMeshParameters): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:493:export function getClosestPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:500:export function computePath(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): Vec3[];\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:528:export function createNavCrowd(plugin: NavigationPlugin, maxAgents: number, maxAgentRadius: number): NavCrowd;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:536:export function addAgent(crowd: NavCrowd, position: Vec3, params: AgentParameters): number;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:555:export function getAgentPosition(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:561:export function getAgentVelocity(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:567:export function agentGoto(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number, destination: Vec3): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:572:export function updateNavCrowd(crowd: NavCrowd, dt: number): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:583:export function findRandomPointAroundCircle(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3, radius: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:590:export function findRandomPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:615:export function raycast(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): { hit: boolean; hitPoint?: Vec3 };\\n```\\n\\n(`createNavMesh` at :192 is what makes navmesh buildable from the generated noise-terrain mesh; `computePath`/`findRandomPoint`/`raycast` are patrol/seek/line-of-sight; the crowd functions give bot-vs-bot steering.)\\n\\n2. Not needed now, may matter later:\n49. pointer: `createNavMeshFromSources` builds a navmesh from `NavMeshSource[]` instead of raw meshes — may matter if the noise-terrain mesh needs filtering (e.g., excluding holes/steep faces) before navmesh generation (navigation.ts:200)\n50. pointer: `createDebugNavMeshGeometry` returns positions/normals/indices for rendering the navmesh — useful for debugging bot paths on generated terrain (navigation.ts:404)\n51. pointer: `setNavigationRandomSeed`/`getNavigationRandomSeed` — seedable RNG for deterministic bot behavior, useful for reproducible AI tests (navigation.ts:597, 602)\n52. pointer: `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle`/`removeObstacle`/`updateNavMeshObstacles` — dynamic navmesh obstacles; relevant when destructible terrain opens holes or debris blocks paths (navigation.ts:647–681)\n53. decision: The FPS is built as a new lab demo entry under `lab/lite/`, not as a parity scene (no golden reference, no pixel-diff spec, no bundle-ceiling entry) — WHY: it's a game demo, not a Babylon.js parity target.\n54. constraint: Babylon-Lite has no particle system and no audio — debris must be done with thin instances and sounds with Web Audio on the DOM side.\n55. evidence: ```\\n        // Default interactive port is 5174. Playwright test runs pass LAB_DEV_PORT\\n        // to spin up an ISOLATED server on a dedicated port (so test traffic never\\n        // test server so the exact port Playwright waits on is guaranteed; the\\n        port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174,\\n```\\n\\nThat settles the port question: no config edit needed — `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` (or `pnpm exec vite` with the env var) lands the lab on 5182 per the session rule.\n56. pointer: - The vite config embeds a custom plugin that generates the gallery HTML and links demo sources to `lab/lite/src/demos/` (`DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL`) — how a new scene gets listed/served on the lab site is defined here, worth reading when we register the FPS scene (lab/vite.config.ts, top + `renderPagesDemoCard`)\\n- `public/bundle/demos-manifest.json` holds per-demo raw/gzip KB measured by the build; gallery cards and `window.__DEMO_ENGINE_KB` read it — the same data the bundle-size ceilings track (lab/vite.config.ts, `injectDemoEngineSize`)\\n- A `?compat` query-marker plugin redirects `@babylonjs/core` imports to the compat barrel for the BJS oracle scenes under `lab/lite/src/bjs/` — irrelevant for a pure Lite scene, but explains the repo's dual-scene layout (lab/vite.config.ts, lines 607–656)\\n- The config sets the `babylon-lite` alias to `packages/babylon-lite/src` plus a directory alias for sub-path imports — that's how lab scenes import engine modules (lab/vite.config.ts, ~line 733)\n57. gotcha: Physics bodies are node-anchored — `createPhysicsBody(world, node, ...)` requires a SceneNode, and the static idiom is `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` (scene40.ts) — IMPLICATION: per-tile static colliders need one node per tile; I must verify an empty/invisible node can be created before assuming the tile-removal physics approach is cheap.\n58. gotcha: The text/HUD API is a multi-step chain, not a one-liner — `createTextLayer` takes a `TextData` (built from `GlyphStorage`/font), and it only renders once a `TextRenderer` is created on a `SurfaceContext` and the layer is added to it — IMPLICATION: the HUD must copy the full setup chain from scene180/181 verbatim; my earlier \"createTextLayer a string\" mental model was wrong.\n59. gotcha: The lab gallery is built by a custom Vite plugin (`readdirSync(resolve(__dirname, \"lite\"))` at vite.config.ts:120), and `DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL` points at `lab/lite/src/demos/` while scenes actually live in `lab/lite/src/lite/` — IMPLICATION: I must read the plugin's scene-list construction and check how a new scene file gets registered in the gallery before assuming dropping `scene252.ts` in is enough.\n60. gotcha: The character controller's ground state lives on `CharacterSurfaceInfo` (character-controller.ts:110-114), not as a direct controller getter — the public surface I verified is only `getPosition`/`getVelocity`/`moveWithCollisions`/`onTriggerCollisionObservable` — IMPLICATION: I need to find how to read the last `supportedState` (event or method) for jump gating before implementing movement; I won't assume one exists.\n61. decision: Bullet-vs-bot hits use the GPU picker (`createGpuPicker`) rather than `physicsRaycast` — WHY: character controllers are kinematic and I have not verified `physicsRaycast` hits them, while the picker reliably hits display meshes.\n62. decision: HUD uses `createTextLayer` + `createTextRenderer`/`addTextRendererLayer` — WHY: verified those exports in the text module; scenes 180–181 are the working references.\n63. decision: Terrain heights from seeded low-frequency value noise, amplitude ~5u — WHY: gentle noise keeps the flat-topped tile-box physics close to the mesh surface.\n64. decision: Add `canvas.dataset.ready`/`dataset.error` like scene40 — WHY: it makes the scene scriptable for a later Playwright smoke test via `pnpm exec playwright`.\n65. gotcha: The lab port override is an env var, not a CLI flag — `lab/vite.config.ts:743` is `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174` — IMPLICATION: start the lab with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`; there is no `--port` path.\n66. gotcha: `PhysicsBody` has no position accessor — the interface (havok.ts:156-167) wraps a `SceneNode` (`node` field) and the world syncs body→node each step — IMPLICATION: read debris/bot positions via `body.node.position`; there is no `getPhysicsBodyPosition` to plan around.\n67. decision: Dev server runs as `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` — WHY: lab/vite.config.ts already honors `LAB_DEV_PORT` (falls back to 5174), so the 5182 session rule needs no config edit.\n68. decision: The FPS scene lands at `lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts` — WHY: existing scene files end at scene251, so 252 is the next free number.\n69. decision: Bots use `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` — WHY: scene170/171 confirm it as the established lab idiom and `lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm` already exists, served like HavokPhysics.wasm.\n70. decision: Per-tile terrain physics = static boxes via `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` — WHY: scene40 confirms this is the Lite idiom for static bodies, and per-tile bodies can be individually removed (`removePhysicsBody`) on destruction.\n71. decision: Player uses `moveWithCollisions` + `getPosition`/`getVelocity`, with ground detection via `supportedState` — WHY: verified those exact members on `PhysicsCharacterController` (character-controller.ts:360/371/384/114).\n72. decision: Debris bodies are rendered as thin instances by reading `body.node.position` each frame — WHY: `PhysicsBody` exposes `node: SceneNode` (havok.ts:156-167), so no extra position-query API is needed.\n73. constraint: The lab dev server port is set via the `LAB_DEV_PORT` env var (default 5174 per `lab/vite.config.ts`), so every dev-server start for this project must be `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` — plain `pnpm dev:lab` would land on 5174 and violate the session's port 5182 rule.\n74. gotcha: `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` returns void — it does not return the surface/ground state I'd assumed from the doc example; ground state comes from a separate `checkSupport(deltaTime, direction): CharacterSurfaceInfo` call (and there's a `checkSupport` + `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` pair as the BJS-style stepping path) — IMPLICATION: S3 player stepping should call `checkSupport` each physics step for jump gating (one extra support cast per step) and then `moveWithCollisions` or `integrate`, not rely on a return value.\n75. gotcha: the exported `createHavokWorld` is `createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, gravity?)` — non-async, requiring a pre-loaded Havok module handle; the doc comment's `await createHavokWorld(scene)` does not match the real signature — IMPLICATION: S3 must first load the wasm module (scene40 pattern: `@babylonjs/havok` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"`) and pass that handle in; verify the exact load call from scene40's first 80 lines before writing code.\n76. gotcha: there is no `getPhysicsBodyPosition` — `PhysicsBody` exposes `node: SceneNode` and the engine syncs body→node each step, so positions are read off `body.node.position` — IMPLICATION: debris thin-instance matrices come from node positions, and every per-tile static box needs its own transform node (1024 extra nodes in the scene graph).\n77. gotcha: `PhysicsCharacterController` is a class with public methods (`getPosition`, `setPosition`, `getVelocity`, `setVelocity`, `moveWithCollisions`, `integrate`, `checkSupport`, `onTriggerCollisionObservable`) — an exception to the pure-state-interface dogma from the interview constraints — IMPLICATION: call the methods directly; don't hunt for functional wrappers or assume the controller can be reconstructed from plain data.\n78. gotcha: the text module is multi-stage — `createTextLayer`/`createTextRenderable` both take a `TextData`, which itself needs a font buffer + glyph storage (`createFontFromBuffer`, `createDefaultTextData`/`createTextData`), and layers render through a `TextRenderer` bound to a `SurfaceContext` — IMPLICATION: S6 should copy the scene180/181 flow verbatim (including font loading from `lab/public/fonts`) rather than improvising a simpler call path.\n79. decision: New scene is `scene252.ts` + `scene252.html` under `lab/lite/` — WHY: scene251 is the highest existing number and `getHtmlInputs()` auto-discovers HTML, so no manifest edits.\n80. decision: Split the demo into `scene252.ts` (game flow) + `fps-terrain.ts` + `fps-input.ts` + `fps-audio.ts` — WHY: the entry must read like a high-level API demo, and terrain surgery, DOM input, and audio don't belong in the game-flow file.\n81. decision: Zero changes to `packages/babylon-lite`; the FPS is built purely on existing APIs — WHY: every needed capability (character controller, raycast, navmesh, text, thin instances) was verified present, and engine edits would fight the tree-shaking/pure-state constraints.\n82. decision: Arena 128×128u with 4×4u tiles → 1024 per-tile static box bodies, ~256×256-vertex mesh, box top at tile max height — WHY: concrete realization of tile removal + per-tile static bodies, with 8×8u tiles (256 bodies) as the fallback if world init is slow.\n83. decision: Bullet hits terrain via `physicsRaycast`, hits bots via manual ray-capsule test with closest-hit wins — WHY: character controllers aren't reliable physics-raycast targets, and a manual test against ~4 bots is cheap and deterministic.\n84. decision: Bots are character controllers driven by NavCrowd agents, targeting only the player — WHY: the crowd gives bot-vs-bot separation for free, and bot-vs-bot combat is out of scope this iteration.\n85. decision: Quake-style slide via low friction on the terrain tile shapes, with controller-shape friction as the preferred target if it's exposed — WHY: the controller's shape getter is unverified, and terrain friction applies consistently to player and bots.\n86. decision: Dev server started as `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` — WHY: `lab/vite.config.ts` honors `LAB_DEV_PORT` (default 5174), satisfying the session's port-5182 rule without editing config.\n87. constraint: New lab scenes must be registered as an HTML page in `lab/lite/` following the scene18 pattern (`sceneN.html` with canvas id `renderCanvas`, `/loader.js`, and a module script pointing at `/lite/src/lite/sceneN.ts`) — the gallery auto-discovers scenes via `getHtmlInputs()` in `lab/vite.config.ts`, so a scene file without its HTML page won't exist in the lab.\n88. constraint: The dev server must be started with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182` — `lab/vite.config.ts` defaults to port 5174 and only honors the `LAB_DEV_PORT` env var, so plain `pnpm dev:lab` would violate the session's port-5182 rule.\n89. constraint: The FPS is built entirely from existing Babylon-Lite APIs — no changes to `packages/babylon-lite`; all new files live under `lab/lite/` (scene252.html, scene252.ts, fps-terrain.ts, fps-input.ts, fps-audio.ts).\n90. constraint: Physics and navigation wasm must be loaded via `locateFile` from the lab root paths `/HavokPhysics.wasm` and `/recast-navigation.wasm` (both confirmed present in `lab/public/`) — don't introduce other asset locations.\n91. Babylon-Lite API anchors for the FPS (all verified in packages/babylon-lite/src): player/bot controller = createPhysicsCharacterController (physics/character-controller.ts, capsule, moveWithCollisions, CharacterSupportedState); hits = createGpuPicker + createPickingRay (picking/); runtime terrain surgery = createFlatGroundData + computeNormals + uploadMeshToGPU (mesh/), meshes keep _cpuPositions/_cpuNormals copies; debris = thin-instance API (mesh/thin-instance.ts: addThinInstance/setThinInstanceMatrix/removeThinInstance); HUD = createTextLayer/createTextRenderable (text/, scenes 180-181); bots = navigation module (createNavMesh, computePath, createNavCrowd/agentGoto/updateNavCrowd, addBoxObstacle/removeObstacle — built on @recast-navigation/wasm, scenes 170-175); physics world = createHavokWorld + createHeightFieldShape, HavokPhysics.wasm served from lab/public/.\n92. Scope: turn the getting-started example into a Quake-style FPS as a new lab demo (lab/lite/, not a parity scene). Terrain is procedurally generated from noise and destructible. AI bots are in scope for this iteration; multiplayer is explicitly deferred (\"bots first, multiplayer later\").\n93. Dev server must run on port 5182 (session rule; 5173/5180/5181 taken). Babylon-Lite's lab README says `pnpm dev:lab` launches Vite on port 5174 — the port must be overridden when running the lab for this project.\n94. FPS plan (Quake-style, tile-removal terrain) — new lab demo, NO engine changes:\\n\\nFILES (all new, under ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite):\\n1. lab/lite/scene252.html — copy scene18.html pattern (canvas + /loader.js + module script); auto-discovered by getHtmlInputs() in lab/vite.config.ts. Next free number after scene251.\\n2. lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts — main scene: 4-phase scaffold (createEngine → build → registerScene → startEngine), Havok world (locateFile \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"), player, bots, shooting, HUD, game loop.\\n3. lab/lite/src/lite/fps-terrain.ts — noise terrain + tile removal: build grid (~256×256 verts over 128×128u), tiles 4×4u → 32×32 = 1024 tiles; per-tile static box bodies (createPhysicsAggregate mass 0, top at tile max height); destroy = remove tile's index range from _cpuIndices, computeNormals, uploadMeshToGPU, removePhysicsBody, addBoxObstacle on navmesh.\\n4. lab/lite/src/lite/fps-input.ts — pointer lock + keyboard state (DOM-side; no engine input module exists).\\n5. lab/lite/src/lite/fps-audio.ts — Web Audio SFX (gunshot, thud, hit), lazy-init on first gesture, no module-level side effects.\\n\\nSTEPS:\\nS1 Verify remaining API details (read-only): frame-observable name (scene18), physicsRaycast query shape (havok-queries.ts:207), NavMeshParameters usage (scene170), whether controller exposes its shape for friction, bundle-size spec doesn't cover lab scenes.\\nS2 fps-terrain.ts: seeded value-noise heights, low-frequency (amp ~5u); createFlatGroundData → mutate positions → computeNormals → uploadMeshToGPU; per-tile static boxes + membership/collide masks; destroyTile() index surgery + re-upload + body removal + nav obstacle.\\nS3 scene252.ts: engine/scene/light/camera scaffold; createHavokWorld; terrain via fps-terrain; player = createPhysicsCharacterController (capsule 1.8/0.5), Quake movement in onPhysicsAfterStep (slide friction, air control, jump gated by checkSupport supportedState); camera follow w/ pointer-lock yaw/pitch.\\nS4 Shooting: click → ray from eye; physicsRaycast for terrain (hit → destroyTile), manual ray-capsule vs bots (closest wins); fire-rate limit; hit/kill feedback.\\nS5 Bots: createNavigationPluginAsync (recast-navigation.wasm) + createNavMesh from terrain mesh; N=4 bots = character controller + NavCrowd agent (agentGoto/findRandomPoint, updateNavCrowd per step); display = thin instances; bots LOS-check player (physicsRaycast) and shoot with distance-based accuracy; damage/respawn both sides.\\nS6 HUD: createTextLayer/createTextRenderer (pattern from scenes 180/181) — health, kills, tiles destroyed; crosshair.\\nS7 fps-audio.ts wired to events.\\nS8 Dev server: LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab (vite config reads LAB_DEV_PORT, default 5174); manual playtest checklist: move/slide/jump, mouse look, shoot holes, fall through → respawn, debris, bots patrol+shoot, HUD, audio. Add canvas.dataset.ready/error like scene40 for scriptability.\\n\\nCHECKS: typecheck (repo script), manual playtest on :5182/lite/scene252.html, confirm bundle-size.spec.ts unaffected, no module-level side effects, no raw GPU handles in scene code.\\n\\nRISKS: 1024 static bodies init cost (fallback: 8×8u tiles → 256 bodies); navmesh from dense mesh (tune NavMeshParameters); controller shape access for friction (fallback: low friction on terrain tile shapes).\n95. Terrain destruction = tile removal (user-approved): index surgery on one dense grid mesh + per-tile static box physics bodies; destroy = drop tile's indices, recompute normals, re-upload, removePhysicsBody, addBoxObstacle on navmesh. No engine changes — everything via existing public API. Arena 128×128u, tiles 4×4u (1024 tiles), mesh ~256×256 verts; fallback to 8×8u tiles if static-body init is slow.","seq":28769,"t":1214580,"why":"meditation"}
