{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"This is a really important finding: `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs?, ...)` is the perfect seam for terrain destruction — it replaces the mesh's GPU geometry in place, keeps the Mesh object valid, bumps the renderable version so cached bundles get re-recorded, and retains the CPU geometry for picking. This is better than a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap.\n\nWait — but there's a subtle point: resizeMeshGeometry allocates new buffers and destroys the old ones. The comment says \"The old GPU buffers are destroyed to free device memory,\" but it also says destruction is deferred until the GPU drains. OK.\n\nBut, does the scene's renderable partition (which happens in registerScene) cache the mesh's index count for draw calls? The comment says that bumping `_renderableVersion` triggers re-recording of cached bundles. Let me check how the main opaque renderer determines index count — probably it reads `mesh._gpu.indexBuffer`'s size every frame, or it's cached. The `_renderableVersion` bump mechanism seems designed exactly for this (the \"ever-growing bridge\" use case is mentioned). So resizeMeshGeometry is the intended path.\n\nHmm, but wait — one issue: when we delete a tile, the number of indices decreases. If the renderable partition caches the index count per mesh... the version bump should handle it. Let me quickly check how the index count is determined in the main render path.\n\nActually, let me not go too deep down this rabbit hole. The comment explicitly says this function is designed for dynamically changing mesh geometry (\"dynamically generated meshes whose size keeps growing\"). Shrinking should be equally fine. I'll verify with a playtest (S8).\n\nNow, about the per-tile static box: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node: Mesh, type, options)` — takes a Mesh node. Each tile needs a node. Options:\n1. Create 1024 small invisible meshes (a thin plane or a small box per tile) and attach a static box aggregate to each. That's 1024 meshes in the scene — might be heavy, but meshes are cheap in Lite? Each mesh gets partitioned into a renderable... that would add 1024 draw calls (if visible). If I make them invisible... is there an `isVisible` flag? Let me check.\n\n2. Alternative: use `createPhysicsBody(world, node, PhysicsMotionType.STATIC)` + `createPhysicsShape` + `setPhysicsBodyShape` — this also needs a node.\n\n3. Better: can I skip per-tile bodies and use one static body for the entire terrain? But then tile deletion wouldn't remove physics. Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider. Do we really need per-tile physics bodies? The terrain mesh is one dense grid. If I delete a tile's indices, the mesh no longer has a triangle there — but the physics body (if one big box) would still block. The plan (user-approved) says per-tile static box bodies so we can remove them individually on destruction.\n\nAlternative approach: use one static body per tile via `createPhysicsBody` with a TransformNode (not a Mesh!). `createPhysicsBody(world, node: SceneNode, motionType)` — takes any SceneNode. And `createTransformNode(name, x, y, z)` creates a TransformNode (the character controller uses it). So I can create 1024 TransformNodes (invisible, not added to the scene at all — no renderable), attach a static body + box shape to each. The node doesn't need to be in the scene for physics to work... let me verify: does the physics world sync nodes to the world? The body is anchored to the node, and its position comes from the node's transform. A TransformNode that's not added to the scene should still work — the controller creates one without adding it to the scene.\n\nSo: per tile, `createTransformNode(\"tile\", cx, topY, cz)` + `createPhysicsBody(world, node, STATIC)` + `createPhysicsShape(world, {type: BOX, parameters: {extents: {x: 2, y: h/2, z: 2}}})` + `setPhysicsBodyShape(world, body, shape)`.\n\nWait, but does createPhysicsAggregate auto-size the box from the node's bounding box? `_buildShapeParams(node, type, options)` — auto-sizes from the bounding box if necessary. Let me check the PhysicsAggregateOptions interface — it probably takes an explicit `boxSize` or extents. Let me look at PhysicsAggregateOptions.\n\nAlso, I need to check whether a TransformNode's position is synced to the physics body (static bodies don't move, so this is fine).\n\nActually — the simpler thing is: use createPhysicsAggregate with a real (invisible) mesh? No — a TransformNode is cleaner. But createPhysicsAggregate takes a `Mesh` type. So I have to use createPhysicsBody + createPhysicsShape + setPhysicsBodyShape directly. That's fine — all public APIs.\n\nLet me check:\n1. The full definition of the PhysicsAggregateOptions interface\n2. The parameters for createPhysicsShape (the shape of the BOX parameters)\n3. Whether a TransformNode works with a physics body (the controller proves it)\n4. How the renderer determines the index count per mesh (to confirm that shrinking works)\n5. The signature of `createTransformNode`\n6. `isVisible` on Mesh\n7. `findRandomPointAroundCircle` / `findRandomPoint` — already confirmed in the earlier navigation.ts grep.\n8. The full flow of scene170 (addAgent parameters, updateNavCrowd).\n\nAlso for the camera: I need a first-person camera. The plan uses FreeCamera + manual yaw/pitch with pointer lock. Let me check the FreeCamera API — what fields does it have? scene18 uses `createFreeCamera(position, target)`. For FPS, I want the camera at the player's eye position, and yaw/pitch controlled by the mouse. FreeCamera has `position` and... rotation? Let me check the camera types. There might be a `rotation` field. Let me look at the FreeCamera interface.\n\nBatch:\n1. The PhysicsAggregateOptions interface\n2. The parameters for createPhysicsShape (BOX)\n3. The signature of createTransformNode\n4. The FreeCamera interface\n5. The rest of scene170 (the crowd portion)\n","seq":36778,"t":1610266,"text":"\n\nImportant finding: `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) is the intended in-place seam for changing geometry, and it's better than raw `uploadMeshToGPU`. Currently checking the remaining API details: aggregate options, shape parameters, transform nodes, camera, and the rest of scene170.\n\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":39936,"draft_n":4242,"draft_n_accepted":1136,"predicted_ms":39520.0,"predicted_n":1742,"prompt_ms":4070.0000000000005,"prompt_n":3421},"to_request":35609,"usage":{"cached_tokens":39936,"completion_tokens":1742,"prompt_tokens":43357}}
