{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me look back on what I found in the previous step (S1 verification round 2) and check whether there were any surprises compared to my expectations from the plan.\n\nExpectations from the plan/working set:\n1. S1 item: \"frame observable name (scene18)\" — found `onBeforeRender(scene, cb)` in scene-core.ts:236. This matches expectations. No surprise.\n2. physicsRaycast query shape — found RaycastQuery with membership/collideWith/shouldHitTriggers. This matches the plan. No surprise.\n3. NavMeshParameters usage in scene170 — confirmed. No surprise.\n4. Controller shape access for friction — surprise! The controller has public fields `staticFriction` and `dynamicFriction` (default 0 and 1). That is, I can directly set `cc.staticFriction` / `cc.dynamicFriction` on the controller. The plan said \"low friction on terrain tile shapes, with controller shape friction as the preferred target if exposed.\" The plan assumed the controller might not expose its shape — and in fact `_shape` is private. But the friction fields themselves are public, so I can control the controller's friction directly. This is a positive surprise: friction can be set on the controller fields, no need to go through shape material.\n\n   Wait — is this \"behaved differently than expected\"? The plan said \"controller shape friction as the preferred target if exposed.\" The shape is not exposed (private `_shape`), but the friction fields themselves are public. This is a meaningful discovery: instead of `setPhysicsShapeMaterial` on the tile shape, set `cc.staticFriction`/`cc.dynamicFriction`. Actually, both matter: the controller's staticFriction default is 0 and dynamicFriction default is 1. Quake-style slide wants low dynamic friction on the ground. Setting the controller's dynamicFriction low (e.g., 0.2) achieves the slide. This is a different approach than the plan's \"low friction on terrain tile shapes.\" It's a positive discovery worth recording.\n\n5. bundle-size spec doesn't cover lab scenes — confirmed: SCENES = scenes in scene-config.json that have maxRawKB. Our scene is not in scene-config.json. No surprise.\n\n6. `resizeMeshGeometry` — big discovery! The plan said \"destroy = delete tile's index range from _cpuIndices, computeNormals, uploadMeshToGPU, removePhysicsBody, addBoxObstacle.\" The plan assumed raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap. But there's a public `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs?, ...)` that does exactly this: reallocates GPU buffers in place, bumps renderable version so cached bundles re-record, recomputes bounds, retains CPU geometry, updates the DLR. This is the correct seam — not raw uploadMeshToGPU + manual `_gpu` assignment. Using raw uploadMeshToGPU and manually setting `mesh._gpu` would leak old buffers and skip the renderable version bump (cached draw bundles would keep binding the old buffers) — this is a real pitfall: \"Buffer used in submit while destroyed\" or stale geometry.\n\n   In other words: the plan's \"uploadMeshToGPU\" step is wrong in its details — the correct API is `resizeMeshGeometry`. This is a genuine GOTCHA: the in-place swap path requires the version bump + deferred destruction that resizeMeshGeometry handles, and the raw uploadMeshToGPU + manual `_gpu` assignment would break cached draw bundles.\n\n7. scene180 text flow — no surprise, matches the working set.\n8. scene181 — `createTextRenderable` + `addTextRenderable(scene, text)` — for 3D scenes, text is added as a renderable in the scene, not as a standalone TextRenderer. The plan said \"HUD: createTextLayer/createTextRenderer (pattern from scenes 180/181).\" Scene 180 uses TextRenderer (standalone 2D), scene 181 uses createTextRenderable + addTextRenderable (in-scene 3D). For a HUD in a 3D scene... hmm, actually for a HUD (screen-space overlay), a standalone TextRenderer (scene 180 pattern) might be better, because a TextRenderable is in world space. Wait — does TextRenderer render on top of a 3D scene? Scene 180 is \"standalone 2D text path: no scene, no camera.\" When there's a 3D scene and you registerTextRenderer a TextRenderer — does it composite on top? I haven't verified that yet. This is an open question, not a discovery in this step.\n\n   Actually, in this step I discovered that scene 181 shows `createTextRenderable(data, {opacity})` + `addTextRenderable(scene, text)` — text as a scene renderable in world space. The plan's \"createTextLayer/createTextRenderer\" was based on scene 180 (standalone). For the HUD, either path might work, but I haven't yet verified whether TextRenderer composites on top of a 3D scene. Not a discovery, more of an open item.\n\n9. scene40 Havok load — confirmed: `const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" }); const world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -9.8, z: 0 });` — matches working set expectations. No surprise.\n\n10. `createFlatGroundData` returns GroundData {positions, normals, uvs, indices} — matches. No surprise.\n\n11. `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, type, options)` — takes a Mesh node. For per-tile static boxes, I need one node per tile. interview-t5-2 said \"must verify that empty/invisible nodes can be created before assuming the tile-removal physics approach is cheap.\" Not yet verified in this step. Open item.\n\n12. Character controller class — confirmed public methods. Matches the working set.\n\n13. `checkSupport(deltaTime, direction)` — confirmed. Matches.\n\n14. Controller has public `staticFriction`/`dynamicFriction` fields — as above, a positive discovery.\n\n15. Thin instance API — `setThinInstances(mesh, matrices, count)`, `setThinInstanceMatrix`, `removeThinInstance`, `flushThinInstances`, `setThinInstanceColors` — matches the plan. No surprise.\n\n16. `addBoxObstacle(plugin, position, halfExtents, angle)` — confirmed. Matches.\n\nSo the genuine discoveries from this step:\n\nGOTCHA 1: `resizeMeshGeometry` exists as the correct in-place geometry swap — the plan's raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `mesh._gpu` assignment is wrong. resizeMeshGeometry bumps `_renderableVersion` (so cached draw bundles re-record) and defers destruction of old buffers (avoids \"Buffer used in submit while destroyed\"). Using raw uploadMeshToGPU would leave cached bundles binding freed buffers.\n\nGOTCHA 2: The controller's friction is directly exposed as public fields `staticFriction`/`dynamicFriction` (defaults 0/1) — no need for `setPhysicsShapeMaterial` on tile shapes, and no access to the shape is needed. Quake slide = set `cc.dynamicFriction` low. The plan's fallback \"low friction on terrain tile shapes\" is unnecessary for the player, though the bot (which is also a controller) gets the same treatment.\n\nWait, is GOTCHA 2 a \"difference from expectation\"? The plan said \"Quake-style slide via low friction on terrain tile shapes, with controller shape friction as the preferred target if exposed.\" The shape itself is not exposed (private `_shape`), but the friction coefficients are public fields. So the plan's framing (\"controller shape friction\") was slightly off — it's not via shape, it's via the controller's fields. This is a discovery worth recording because it changes the implementation approach.\n\nAnything else?\n\n- scene181 vs scene180 text: for in-scene HUD, the 3D path is `createTextRenderable` + `addTextRenderable(scene, text)`; scene 180's `createTextRenderer` is a standalone 2D overlay. Whether the TextRenderer composites on top of a 3D scene is unverified. This is an open item, not a discovery. I could mention it as an unresolved question, but the prompt asks for \"behaved differently than expected.\" The plan said \"HUD: createTextLayer/createTextRenderer (pattern from scenes 180/181)\" — I discovered that scene 181 uses a different pattern (createTextRenderable + addTextRenderable), and that pattern is for in-scene 3D text. The HUD might need the scene 180 pattern (screen-space) — unverified whether it overlays on top of a 3D scene. I'll record this as a partial discovery: the two text paths differ, and I must choose one before writing the HUD.\n\nHmm, the prompt says \"list each item on a separate line... only discoveries from this step.\" Let me be strict about what I actually discovered in this step (the last tool round):\n\n1. resizeMeshGeometry exists and is the correct in-place swap seam (mesh-factories.ts:104). The plan said uploadMeshToGPU. This is a discovery that changes what to do.\n\n2. Controller friction fields are public (staticFriction=0, dynamicFriction=1 defaults); `_shape` is private — so friction is via the controller's fields, not via shape material. Changes the plan's approach.\n\n3. Text: scene 181 shows `createTextRenderable` + `addTextRenderable(scene, text)` as the in-scene path, while scene 180's `createTextRenderer` is standalone. The plan lumped both together as \"createTextLayer/createTextRenderer.\" Discovery: there are two distinct text paths, and the HUD approach needs a decision.\n\n4. `onBeforeRender(scene, cb)` confirmed — as expected, no surprise.\n\n5. `createPhysicsAggregate` requires a Mesh node — I still need to verify that invisible nodes can be created. Not yet discovered, open item.\n\nI think 1–3 are the genuine discoveries. Let me write them up concisely.\n\nActually, one more: `updateMeshPositions` also exists (in-place position write, no reallocation) — but since tile removal changes the index count, I need resizeMeshGeometry rather than updateMeshPositions. That's part of discovery 1.\n\nOne more: in the previous step (turn 5) it was noted that \"havok-collision.ts is unverified\" — that's still open, not a discovery.\n\nI'll answer with the GOTCHA lines. Should I also record them with update_record? The ground rules say \"record decisions and learnings with update_record.\" The pause prompt asks for a list. I'll list them and also record the key ones. Actually, the prompt specifically asks to \"list each item on a separate line as GOTCHA: ...\". I'll do that, and also record them with update_record since they're important learnings. Let me do both — record first, then respond.\n\nActually, let me be careful: the response should be the list. Recording is good practice. I'll record the main discoveries and then present the list.\n","seq":37336,"t":1638580,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":43264,"draft_n":6398,"draft_n_accepted":1999,"predicted_ms":66160.0,"predicted_n":2913,"prompt_ms":590.0,"prompt_n":178},"to_request":35651,"usage":{"cached_tokens":43264,"completion_tokens":2913,"prompt_tokens":43442}}
