{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"S1 is fully confirmed. Key decisions:\n\n1. **Camera**: FreeCamera — set `cam.position` (ObservableVec3, auto-marks dirty) and `cam._yaw`/`cam._pitch` (internal, push-based dirty). World matrix is built from `mat4LookAtLH(position, target, up)` — wait, the local world matrix uses `cam.position` and `cam.target`! Not yaw/pitch. So `_yaw`/`_pitch` are only used by `attachFreeControl` for mouse manipulation, and the actual matrix comes from position+target.\n\nSo for FPS camera: set `cam.position` = player's eye position, `cam.target` = eye + forward(yaw, pitch). Both are ObservableVec3 with onDirty → matrix rebuild.\n\n2. **HUD**: `registerTextRenderer` registers a *rendering context* to the surface — `registerRenderingContext(tr._surface, tr)`. Multiple rendering contexts on the same canvas? A 3D scene is also a rendering context on the same canvas. How are they ordered? Let me check `registerRenderingContext` — there might be an ordering issue. The 3D scene clears the canvas and draws; then the text renderer draws on top (with `clear: false` so it doesn't wipe the 3D).\n\nThis is a clean HUD path: a screen-space text layer with `positionPx`, updated every frame (health, kills, tiles destroyed) + a static crosshair layer. Set `clear: false` in `createTextRenderer(engine, { layers, clear: false })`? Options: `opts.clear ?? true` — so pass `clear: false`. Let me verify that TextRendererOptions has `clear`. Line 300: `_clear: opts.clear ?? true`. Yes.\n\nBut wait — does the engine's render loop render all registered contexts (scene + text renderer) on the same canvas? scene180 is standalone (no scene). If both are registered on the same canvas, both get rendered every frame — the order depends on registration order. The scene clears the depth/color buffers, and the text renderer with clear:false draws on top. It should work if the order is scene first, text after. I need to verify the ordering of `registerRenderingContext`. Let me take a quick look.\n\nAlternatively, use scene181's 3D `createTextRenderable` + `addTextRenderable(scene, text)` — text in the scene, positioned each frame in front of the camera. This is definitely supported (same scene, depth-sorted). For a crosshair, place a small text at camera position + forward*2. For HUD stats, place text in front of the camera every frame.\n\nHmm, but `TextRenderable` has depth testing — the crosshair might get hidden by terrain. Does TextRenderable have a `depthTest` option? Let me check. If it can be rendered on top (depth-write off, depth-test off), that would be ideal.\n\nLet me quickly check both: the ordering of registerRenderingContext, and the TextRenderable options.\n\n3. **createMeshFromData(engine, name, positions, normals, indices, uvs?, ...)** — for terrain. ✓\n\n4. **Terrain destruction**: `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs)` — replaces geometry in place. For tile removal: remove the tile's indices from `_cpuIndices`, build a new index array (excluding the tile), recompute normals? No — removing a tile doesn't change the positions of the remaining vertices, so normals don't change either (except for the boundary vertices adjacent to the hole, but that's a minor visual issue). Actually, computeNormals computes per-vertex normals from the index list — removing a tile slightly changes the normals of the boundary vertices (smoothing). It's fine to recompute: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` returns the new normals. Cheap on CPU (256×256 = 65k vertices, 255×255×6 = 390k indices — fine even per destruction, probably ~ms).\n\nSo destroyTile(tileIndex):\n- newIndices = _cpuIndices minus the tile's 6 indices (filter)\n- newNormals = computeNormals(_cpuPositions, newIndices)\n- resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, _cpuPositions, newNormals, newIndices, _cpuUvs)\n- removePhysicsBody(world, tileBody)\n- addBoxObstacle(nav, tileCenter, halfExtents, 0) — so bots don't walk into the hole\n\nWait, does the navmesh's addBoxObstacle actually work for making holes? It adds an obstacle (a box that blocks paths). A box in the air at the hole position — the navmesh is built from the terrain surface; the obstacle blocks the area. OK, good enough.\n\n5. **Per-tile static bodies**: TransformNode per tile + createPhysicsBody(world, node, STATIC) + createPhysicsShape(BOX, extents) + setPhysicsBodyShape. But wait — does the world sync a static body's transform from the node? Static bodies are set at creation time (from the node's transform). Fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: 1024 bodies × shape creation at init time. The plan has a fallback (8×8u tiles → 256 bodies). Start with 4×4u tiles (1024) and measure init time via `canvas.dataset.initMs`.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider tile size. Arena 128×128u, tile 4×4u → 32×32 = 1024 tiles. Mesh: 256×256 vertices means 255 subdivisions (cols = subdivisions+1 = 256 → subdivisions = 255). Hmm, 255 subdivisions → tile size = 128/255 ≈ 0.502u. That doesn't align with 4u tiles!\n\nLet me redo: for a 4×4u tile in a 128×128u arena, we need subdivisions = 128/4 = 32 tiles → 32 subdivisions → 33×33 vertices. That's way sparser than 256×256. The plan said \"mesh ~256×256 vertices\" — that was for vertex-level digging, but we chose tile removal (user-approved), so we don't need 256×256. 33×33 vertices (1089 vertices, 6144 indices) is plenty for tile removal: each tile = 1 quad = 2 triangles = 6 indices.\n\nHmm wait, but the noise terrain: low-frequency value noise at 33×33 vertices is fine (amp ~5u).\n\nBut with tile removal on a 32×32 grid, the hole is a full 4×4u square — that's the \"tile removal\" aesthetic. Good.\n\nSo: `createFlatGroundData({ width: 128, height: 128, subdivisions: 32 })` → 33×33 vertices. Then displace Y with seeded value noise. Then computeNormals. Then createMeshFromData.\n\nTiles: tile (tx, tz) covers columns tx..tx+1 (vertex indices), and the 6 indices start at index:\nrow = tz, col = tx in the quad grid:\nindices for quad (row, col): topLeft = row*cols + col (cols=33), etc. From createFlatGroundData:\n```\ntopLeft = row * cols + col\ntopRight = topLeft + 1\nbottomLeft = (row + 1) * cols + col\nbottomRight = bottomLeft + 1\nindices: bottomRight, topRight, topLeft, bottomLeft, bottomRight, topLeft\n```\nSo tile (tx, tz) has 6 indices at position `(tz * 32 + tx) * 6` in the index array.\n\nTile center: x = -64 + tx*4 + 2, z = -64 + tz*4 + 2 (z = -height/2 + (1 - row/subdivisions)*height → row 0 is z=-64, row 32 is z=+64; tile tz spans z from -64+tz*4 to -64+(tz+1)*4).\n\nTile top height: max Y of the 4 vertices of the tile (physics box top is at tile max height, so the character doesn't clip through on slopes). Box: center (x, topY - boxH/2, z), extent (2, boxH/2, 2), boxH = maxTileHeight - minTileHeight… simpler: box from y = tileMinY (or a bit below) to tileMaxY. Let box height = (tileMaxY - tileMinY) + 2 (a bit below the lowest point to prevent gaps).\n\nBox physics: `createPhysicsBody(world, node, STATIC)` where node = createTransformNode at the box center; `createPhysicsShape(world, { type: BOX, parameters: { extents: {x: 2, y: h/2, z: 2} } })`; `setPhysicsBodyShape(world, body, shape)`.\n\nFriction: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` — low friction (e.g. 0.1) for Quake-style sliding. Plus the controller's own staticFriction/dynamicFriction fields — the controller multiplies its own friction with the surface friction (BJS-style). Setting the controller's dynamicFriction low gives slide. Actually, in BJS the character controller's friction is `min(characterFriction, surfaceFriction)`? I'll just set both: surface friction 0.1, controller staticFriction 0, dynamicFriction 0.1.\n\nWait — the controller's default is staticFriction=0, dynamicFriction=1. Hmm, that's the opposite of BJS (BJS: staticFriction=1, dynamicFriction=0.05?). For Quake-style slide: I want low friction on horizontal movement → set controller dynamicFriction low (0.05?), and surface friction low. I'll tune during playtest.\n\n6. **Shooting**: mouse click → ray from camera position along forward (from yaw/pitch). `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, { membership: TERRAIN_MASK })` for terrain; hit → find the tile from hitPoint x/z → destroyTile. For bots: manual ray-capsule test against bot positions (capsule height 1.8, radius 0.5), closest hit wins. Fire rate limit (e.g. 0.15s).\n\n7. **Bots**: NavCrowd agent + character controller. Each bot: controller (capsule 1.8/0.5) + nav agent. In onPhysicsAfterStep: agentGoto to a random point (or the player if there's LOS), updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt), read getAgentPosition, and move the controller toward it… hmm, how do you drive a character controller from a nav agent?\n\nApproach: use the nav agent's velocity: `getAgentVelocity(crowd, index)` → set the controller's velocity toward the target, then `moveWithCollisions(velocity * dt)`. The nav agent computes the path; the controller handles the collision. But the nav agent's position doesn't match the controller's actual position (collisions). Simplest robust approach:\n- Give the nav agent the bot's actual position each step (does the API have a way to reset the agent's position? Let me check — maybe there's an `agentSetPosition`?). Let me grep for the agent functions in navigation.ts.\n\nIf there's no agent reposition, alternative: drive the controller toward the nav agent's destination with velocity: the nav agent's velocity direction is the path direction; the controller follows that direction at some speed; if the controller deviates too far from the agent, re-target with agentGoto to the controller's position + direction… actually, the simplest and most robust:\n- Bot behavior: target = player (if LOS within 30u) or a random point. Use `computePath(plugin, start, end)` to get waypoints; steer the controller toward the next waypoint (velocity = normalize(waypoint - pos) * speed); on collision/stall, advance the waypoint.\n\nThis avoids the complexity of syncing NavCrowd. But the plan says NavCrowd agent. Hmm. NavCrowd provides separation between bots. Let me check what agent functions exist — `getAgentVelocity` gives a velocity you can apply directly: `cc.moveWithCollisions(vel * dt)`. And to keep the agent aligned with the bot, re-target with `agentGoto(crowd, idx, botPos)` each step? That's weird.\n\nLet me check if there's an agent teleport/set-position function. Grep the exports in navigation.ts.\n\nActually — even simpler: skip NavCrowd entirely and use `computePath` + waypoint following for each bot. 4 bots, cheap. Bot-vs-bot separation: skip (per the plan, bot-vs-bot combat is out of scope; just spread spawn points).\n\nBut the plan (S5) says \"character controller + NavCrowd agent (agentGoto/findRandomPoint, updateNavCrowd per step)\". The plan is the user-approved path… the plan also says the nav plugin is established idiom. Using NavCrowd is fine if the sync works. Let me check the agent API.\n\n8. **Bot display**: thin instances — one capsule mesh (createCapsule exists!) with 4 thin instances; each frame, set the matrix from the controller's position (bots face their movement direction).\n\n9. **Debris**: thin instances — small box mesh, N=64 instances, ring buffer; on tile destruction, spawn 4-8 debris with `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity`… wait, the debris bodies: dynamic boxes (mass 1) with box shape; each frame, copy body.node.position + orientation into the instance matrix. `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` is at havok.ts:1056 (audit-28769-3). `applyPhysicsImpulse` also exists.\n\nDebris lifetime: despawn after ~10s or when it comes to rest; recycle the slot.\n\n10. **Respawn**: player falls below y < -20 → respawn at spawn point, damage/invulnerability. Bots are the same.\n\n11. **Audio**: fps-audio.ts — Web Audio, lazy init on first pointerdown. Gunshot (noise burst), hit, thud.\n\nOK — remaining items to check:\n- registerRenderingContext ordering (text renderer on top of scene)\n- TextR