{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to reflect on the last tool output (the grep for registerTextRenderer/createTextRenderer).\n\nThe key part: `createTextRenderer(surface: SurfaceContext, opts: TextRendererOptions)` — the first argument is `SurfaceContext`, not `EngineContext`. But scene180 passes `engine`. So is EngineContext a SurfaceContext, or does it have canvas? Also `registerTextRenderer(tr)` → `registerRenderingContext(tr._surface, tr)` — it registers the rendering context on a surface. The question is whether the TextRenderer is drawn on top of the 3D scene (composited on the same canvas), or on a separate canvas. This is the decisive question for the HUD: if a registered text renderer on the same canvas renders after the scene, then screen-space HUD layers will work.\n\nThe excerpt shows `registerTextRenderer(tr): void { registerRenderingContext(tr._surface, tr); }` — this suggests that the engine's rendering loop picks up all registered rendering contexts for each surface/canvas and draws them in order. If the scene is also a rendering context on the same canvas, then the TextRenderer registered on the same canvas will be drawn alongside the scene — the order matters (whether it's drawn after the scene determines whether the HUD is visible).\n\nAlso, `_clear: opts.clear ?? true` — clearing the target by default is true. In the HUD case, I'd want `clear: false` (otherwise it would wipe out the 3D scene). This is actually a decisive detail: `TextRendererOptions` has `clear`, and by default it clears with `clearValue`. If the TextRenderer renders on the same canvas after the scene, then unless we set `clear: false`, the scene would get erased. Hmm — but wait, scene180 is standalone (no scene), so the default clear=true makes sense there.\n\nBut how does the ordering work? I need to check the engine's render loop: how the rendering contexts on a surface are ordered. That's the \"next thing to look at\" — POINTER territory.\n\nSo:\n1. Decisive part: the signature line of `createTextRenderer` + `registerTextRenderer` → `registerRenderingContext(tr._surface, tr)`, plus `_clear: opts.clear ?? true`. This tells me the following: (a) the TextRenderer is registered per-surface (canvas), so a renderer registered on the FPS canvas should be composited with the scene within the engine's frame loop; (b) I have to verify the drawing order and set `clear: false` so the HUD doesn't wipe out the 3D scene.\n\n2. Pointers:\n- `unregisterTextRenderer` / `disposeTextRenderer` exist — for cleanup (text-renderer.ts:441-456).\n- `TextRendererOptions.clear` defaults to true — for the HUD overlay I need to verify whether the option exists and set it to false (around text-renderer.ts:300).\n- The engine's frame loop that orders the registered rendering contexts per surface — I need to find where registerRenderingContext is consumed to confirm that the TextRenderer is drawn after the scene (engine's rendering context registry).\n- `createTextRenderer` takes a `SurfaceContext` — engine must be a SurfaceContext (scene180 passes engine) — a minor point.\n\nI'll quote the decisive part verbatim, at most about 15 lines.\n","seq":44433,"t":1917739,"text":"\n\nEXCERPT:\n```\nexport function createTextRenderer(surface: SurfaceContext, opts: TextRendererOptions): TextRenderer {\n    const canvas = surface.canvas;\n...\n        _clear: opts.clear ?? true,\n...\nexport function registerTextRenderer(tr: TextRenderer): void {\n    registerRenderingContext(tr._surface, tr);\n}\n```\n\nThat's load-bearing: it answers the open HUD question from S1. `registerTextRenderer` registers the renderer as a rendering context *on its surface (canvas)* — so a TextRenderer created from the FPS engine lands on the same canvas as the 3D scene and is driven by the engine's frame loop, which is exactly the screen-space HUD path I want (health/kills/tiles + crosshair without per-frame world-space placement). And `_clear: opts.clear ?? true` tells me the default would wipe the 3D scene, so I must pass `clear: false` (or verify the option name) when wiring the HUD.\n\nPOINTER: the actual frame-loop ordering — i.e. where the engine consumes registered rendering contexts per surface and decides draw order (scene vs. text renderer) — is still unverified; I need to confirm the TextRenderer draws *after* the scene on the same canvas (engine render-context registry, referenced from `registerRenderingContext` in text-renderer.ts:437)\nPOINTER: `unregisterTextRenderer` / `disposeTextRenderer` exist for HUD teardown/disposal if the scene is ever re-registered or the device is lost (text-renderer.ts:441-456)\nPOINTER: `createTextRenderer` takes a `SurfaceContext`, not `EngineContext` — scene180 passes `engine` directly, so EngineContext evidently satisfies SurfaceContext; worth keeping in mind if I ever pass a non-engine surface (text-renderer.ts:289)","timings":{"cache_n":54016,"draft_n":2611,"draft_n_accepted":749,"predicted_ms":28240.0,"predicted_n":1122,"prompt_ms":780.0,"prompt_n":381},"to_request":43709,"usage":{"cached_tokens":54016,"completion_tokens":1122,"prompt_tokens":54397}}
