{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"All confirmed. Summary of final API decisions:\n\n**HUD**: Two options:\n- TextRenderer (screen space, `registerTextRenderer`, `clear: false`) — registered as an additional rendering context on the same surface. The scene is also a rendering context. Order = registration order in `surface._renderingContexts`. If we register the scene first and the text renderer later, the text will be drawn after the scene (on top). This is the clean HUD approach.\n- TextRenderable (`ignoreDepth: true` option! — perfect for crosshair/HUD), `addTextRenderable(scene, text)` — inside the scene, can be placed in front of the camera every frame.\n\nEither works. The TextRenderer screen-space approach is simpler for HUD (no per-frame camera-relative placement needed), and `clear: false` guarantees it won't wipe out the 3D. Going with TextRenderer + layers:\n- Health layer (bottom-left)\n- Kill/tile layer (top-left)\n- Crosshair layer (center)\n\nUpdate text every frame (or when values change): `updateDefaultTextData(data, newText)`.\n\nWait — does the engine render multiple contexts on the same surface every frame? Let me check how the engine's render loop iterates over contexts… scene180 works standalone, so at least single context works. For two contexts on one surface — engine.ts:512 `const ctxs = surface._renderingContexts;` suggests the loop iterates over all contexts. Good enough; I'll verify with a playtest.\n\n**Nav agents**: no agent reposition API. Decision: use NavCrowd for separation + steering, and drive the character controller with the agent's velocity:\n- Each physics step: `updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt)` → for each bot: `vel = getAgentVelocity(crowd, i)` → `cc.moveWithCollisions(vel * dt)`.\n- Bot targeting: LOS check (navmesh raycast or physicsRaycast) → if player is visible within range: `agentGoto(crowd, i, playerPos)`; otherwise: random point.\n- Risk: agent position drifts from controller position (because collisions move the controller). Mitigation: if the agent is far from the bot (agent has reached destination, or path is stale), re-target: `agentGoto(crowd, i, getAgentPosition(...))` hmm no.\n\nActually, a simpler and more robust pattern that matches \"bot targets player\":\n- Keep the nav agent as a path source: agentGoto(target). The agent moves along the path inside the navmesh. The bot's controller chases the agent's position: velocity = normalize(agentPos - botPos) * speed. The controller handles collisions (sliding along walls). If the bot gets stuck (velocity ~0 for 2 seconds), re-pick a target / nudge with a random offset.\n\nHmm, but then the nav agent and the bot drift apart — the agent walks over holes (the navmesh doesn't know about holes until an obstacle is added — we add a box obstacle on destruction, good).\n\nHonestly, the cleanest is: use NavCrowd purely for steering, apply the agent's velocity to the controller, and periodically (every ~0.5 seconds) snap the agent to the bot's actual position via agentGoto… no wait, agentGoto sets a destination, not the agent's position.\n\nLet me look at the AgentParameters docs in navigation.ts — maybe there's a way. No `setAgentPosition` export. OK.\n\nDecision: bot = controller + NavCrowd agent, apply agent velocity to controller every step. If the bot's controller deviates more than 3u from the agent's position (collision detour), re-target the agent to the bot's current position (agentGoto to bot position → agent re-paths from there… hmm, does agentGoto re-path from the agent's current position to the destination? Yes — that's how goto works. If the agent is 3u away from the bot, re-targeting to the bot's position makes the agent path to the bot's position, and then… the bot keeps chasing the agent. This ping-pongs.\n\nBetter: re-target the agent to a point past the bot: `agentGoto(crowd, i, botPos + dir*2)`? Over-engineering.\n\nAlternative decision: drop NavCrowd, use `computePath` waypoint following. Each bot:\n- Every 0.5–1 second (or on reaching a waypoint): `path = computePath(nav, botPos, target)` (target = player if LOS, otherwise a random point)\n- Steer the controller: velocity = normalize(nextWaypoint - botPos) * speed; advance the waypoint within 1u.\n- If the path is empty (no route), re-pick the target.\n\nThis is deterministic, simple, robust, and uses the navmesh correctly. Bot-to-bot separation: not needed (they're at different spawn points, target the player, 4 of them).\n\nThe plan says NavCrowd, but the plan also says \"the crowd gives bot-to-bot separation for free\" — that was the justification. With computePath the bots might occasionally cross, but that's fine for a demo. Hmm. The plan is the user-approved one; if it proves wrong I should say so rather than drift (ground rules). I'll try NavCrowd first (it's in the plan, and updateNavCrowd + getAgentVelocity is a simple one-liner), and if the agent/bot drift gets annoying, fall back to computePath and record the reason.\n\nActually, let me think about what's most likely to work: NavCrowd agents don't know the bot's collisions. In an open arena with gentle noise terrain, the navmesh is a single big walkable region; the agent walks roughly straight to the target; the controller follows the agent's velocity — the controller also slides along the same terrain (the navmesh is built from that terrain), so drift should be small. Holes: obstacle added → agent detours; controller slides around the edge of the hole. Drift stays bounded. I think NavCrowd will work fine. If a bot gets stuck on a hole edge, re-targeting the agent to a random point every few seconds solves it.\n\nDecision: NavCrowd per the plan; re-target the agent every 2–4 seconds (or on reaching destination); if the bot and agent are more than 5u apart, re-target the agent to the bot's position + forward offset… I'll implement the simple version first: apply agent velocity, re-target every 3 seconds or on reaching destination.\n\n**PhysicsWorld**: `_timestep` is @internal — use `dt` from the `onPhysicsAfterStep(world, (timestep) => ...)` callback instead.\n\n**Camera**: set `cam.position` and `cam.target` every frame (ObservableVec3 → auto dirty). Forward from yaw/pitch:\nforward = (sin(yaw)*cos(pitch)… let me define: yaw around Y, pitch around X.\n- forward.x = sin(yaw) * cos(pitch)\n- forward.y = sin(pitch)\n- forward.z = cos(yaw) * cos(pitch)\n(Babylon left-handed, +Y up, -Z forward is default look-at. mat4LookAtLH(position, target, up) — the camera looks at target. So target = position + forward. Consistent.)\n\nWait, in createFreeCamera: `_yaw = Math.atan2(dx, dz)`, `_pitch = Math.atan2(dy, sqrt(dx²+dz²))` (d = target - position). So target = position + (sin(yaw)*cos(pitch), sin(pitch), cos(yaw)*cos(pitch)) * dist. Yes, matches.\n\nMouse look: on pointermove (while pointer-locked): yaw -= movementX * sens; pitch -= movementY * sens; clamp pitch to ±1.5.\n\n**Shooting ray**: from eye (cam.position) to eye + forward * 100. `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, { membership: TERRAIN_MASK })`. Hmm — membership filtering: ray query's `membership` filters bodies by the shape's membership mask. Per-tile static bodies need membership = TERRAIN_MASK. And the character controller's capsule — what's its membership? Probably default ~0. If I raycast with membership TERRAIN_MASK, I only hit terrain tiles.\n\nBut note: `findBodyById` returns the hit body — I need to map body → tile index. Store in a Map<PhysicsBody, tileIndex>… no, no module-level Map (GUIDANCE zero-side-effects). Lazy map or just an array indexed by tile index (bodies[i] corresponds to tile i). Simple array: `tileBodies: (PhysicsBody | null)[]`.\n\nFrom hitPoint: tile tx = floor((x + 64) / 4), tz = floor((z + 64) / 4). Even simpler — use hitPoint, no body→tile map needed. But the membership mask guarantees I only hit terrain. Both: use the hitPoint's x/z to compute the tile.\n\nHmm wait — subtle point: the physics box extends up to the tile's max height; a ray hitting the box's side (below the terrain surface, e.g. hitting the side of a neighboring raised tile) still maps to that tile — fine, destroying it is acceptable (that's the tile the box belongs to).\n\n**Collision masks**:\n- TERRAIN = 1, PLAYER = 2, BOT = 4, DEBRIS = 8.\n- Terrain tile: membership TERRAIN, collide with PLAYER|BOT|DEBRIS (so the player/bots stand on it, debris bounces).\n- Player controller capsule: membership PLAYER, collide with TERRAIN|DEBRIS? (debris pushes the player? character controller's characterStrength is huge — pushing the player with debris… keep it simple: collide with TERRAIN only).\n- Bot: membership BOT, collide with TERRAIN.\n- Debris: membership DEBRIS, collide with TERRAIN|PLAYER|BOT (bounces off tiles, gets pushed by character… but the character controller handles dynamics via its own query — onTriggerCollisionObservable fires for dynamic bodies the character pushes. Debris colliding with the player capsule gives the \"knockback\" feel — optional polish per audit-28769-36).\n\nWhere do I set the controller's membership? The controller creates its own shape internally (`createPhysicsShape` in the constructor) — I can't get it (private `_shape`). So I can't set the controller's membership mask! Hmm.\n\nSo the controller's capsule has the default membership (0? or ~0?). The terrain tiles have membership TERRAIN (1). The raycast query filters by the body's membership mask: the query's `membership` is ANDed with the body's membership. If the terrain tile's membership = 1, a raycast with `membership: 1` only hits tiles.\n\nBut do the controller's capsule and the tile boxes collide? Collision uses the shape's collide mask + the other shape's membership. Default collide mask is probably ~0 (collide with everything). So the controller collides with the tiles — good.\n\nDebris dynamic body: can I create the shape via createPhysicsShape and set the filter mask (createPhysicsBody + setPhysicsBodyShape + setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask)? createPhysicsAggregate takes options.shape (a pre-built shape) — I can build the shape, set the mask, then pass it to the aggregate.\n\nOK, this is workable. I'll set the masks on the terrain tiles + debris; the controller is default (collides with everything) — the debris collides with the controller's capsule (the character's strength pushes the debris). Good.\n\nOne more: **navmesh from the terrain mesh** — `createNavMesh(nav, [terrainMesh], params)`. Terrain mesh = the dense grid mesh. NavMeshParameters: from scene170 {cs: 0.2, ch: 0.2, walkableSlopeAngle: 90, walkableHeight: 1, walkableClimb: 1, walkableRadius: 1, maxEdgeLen: 12, maxSimplificationError: 1.3, minRegionArea: 8, mergeRegionArea: 20, maxVertsPerPoly: 6, detailSampleDist: 6, detailSampleMaxError: 1}. For a 128×128u arena, cs=0.2 might be heavy (640×640 cells). Use cs=0.5, ch=0.5 → 256×256 cells. walkableSlopeAngle ~45 (gentle noise). walkableRadius 0.5 (bot radius). I'll tune in the playtest.\n\n**Scene setup**: `scene.fixedDeltaMs = 1000/60` (like scene40).\n\n**Player movement (Quake-style)** in onPhysicsAfterStep:\n```\nconst dt = timestep;\n// input direction (WASD relative to yaw)\n// ground check: surfaceInfo = cc.checkSupport(dt, velocityDir)\n// if on ground: apply input acceleration with slide (low friction); if space: vy = jumpSpeed\n// in air: reduced control\n// velocity *= friction\ncc.moveWithCollisions({x: vx*dt, y: vy*dt, z: vz*dt})\n```\nQuake-style: ground slide — velocity decays slowly (no friction while sliding), input adds acceleration. In-air control: weaker acceleration. Jump: vy = 8 (tuned).\n\nController friction fields: staticFriction=0, dynamicFriction=0.1 → slide feel. Surface friction also low (0.1).\n\n**Respawn**: if cc.getPosition().y < -30 → damage 25, respawn at spawn point, invulnerable for 2 seconds. Bots: same, respawn at random spawn point.\n\n**Damage**: player health 100; bot hit → 10 damage (or instant kill? Quake-style: 100hp, hits do 25–35). Bots shoot the player: distance-based accuracy, 10 damage per hit, fire interval ~1 second, only with LOS.\n\n**Kill/respawn**: on bot death: kills++, respawn at random spawn point after 3 seconds, brief invulnerability.\n\nOK — the S1 verification is done. Record the findings and start S2 (fps-terrain.ts).\n\nFile plan (from the recorded plan):\n1. `lab/lite/scene252.html`\n2. `lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts` — main scene\n3. `lab/lite/src/lite/fps-terrain.ts` — terrain + tile removal\n4. `lab/lite/src/lite/fps-input.ts` — pointer lock + keyboard\n5. `lab/lite/src/lite/fps-audio.ts` — Web Audio SFX\n\nWrite fps-terrain.ts first (S2).\n\nfps-terrain.ts design:\n\n```ts\n// Terrain for the FPS demo: seeded value-noise heights on a dense grid,\n// one static box collider per tile, and tile removal via index surgery.\n\nimport { createFlatGroundData, createMeshFromData, computeNormals, resizeMeshGeometry, createTransformNode, createPhysicsBody, createPhysicsShape, createPhysicsAggregate, setPhysicsShapeMaterial, removePhysicsBody, PhysicsShapeType, PhysicsMotionType, ... } from \"babylon-lite\";\nimport type { EngineContext, SceneContext, Mesh, PhysicsWorld, PhysicsBody, NavigationPlugin } from \"babylon-lite\";\n\nexport const ARENA_SIZE = 128;\nexport const TILE_SIZE = 4;\nexport const TILE_COUNT = ARENA_SIZE / TILE_SIZE; // 32\n\nexport interface TileInfo { tx, tz, centerX, centerZ, topY, body: PhysicsBody | null, obstacle: ObstacleHandle | null, alive: boolean }\n\nexport interface FpsTerrain {\n    mesh: Mesh;\n    tiles: TileInfo[];\n    destroyTile(terrain, world, tx, tz): void;  // hmm — needs engine\n}\n```\n\nBetter API:\n```ts\nexport function createFpsTerrain(engine, scene, world): FpsTerrain\nexport function destroyTerrainTile(terrain, engine, world, nav, tx, tz): void\n```\n\nWait — the scene is not needed (the mesh is added by the caller). Terrain module:\n\n```ts\nexport interface FpsTerrain {\n    mesh: Mesh;\n    tileAt(tx: number, tz: number): TileInfo;\n    tileFromPoint(x: number, z: number): TileInfo | null;\n    destroyedCount: number;\n}\n\nexport function createFpsTerrain(engine: EngineContext, world: PhysicsWorld): FpsTerrain\nexport function destroyTerrainTile(terrain: FpsTerrain, engine: EngineContext, world: PhysicsWorld, nav: NavigationPlugin | null, tx: number, tz: number): void\n```\n\nNoise: seeded value noise, low frequency. Implementation:\n```ts\nfunction mulberry32(seed: number): () => number  // seeded PRNG\n// value noise: grid of random heights, bilinear interp with smoothstep\n```\nLow frequency: sample noise on a coarse grid (e.g. 8×8 random values over the arena), bilinear interp → smooth undulations. Amplitude ~5u.\n\nHeights: for each vertex (x, z): h = noise(x, z) * 5. Keep the spawn area relatively flat? Keep it simple — gentle noise everywhere.\n\nPer-tile physics: topY = max of the 4 corner vertex heights; bottomY = min of the 4 corners. Box: extents (TILE_SIZE/2, (topY-bottomY)/2 + 1, TILE_SIZE/2), center y = (topY+bottomY)/2… actually, extend the box below the bottom to avoid gaps between tiles: boxBottom = bottomY - 2 (below the local ground; neighboring tiles might be lower, but with a 5u amplitude and 4u tiles the neighbor's bottom is at worst ~1–2u lower… to be safe, extend down to bottomY - 4 (well below any neighbor's surface). This makes a thicker box — no problem for a static body.\n\nHmm wait, actually — if a box extends 4u below its tile, the player walks on the neighboring tile and… the box's top is only at this tile's topY; the player on the neighbor is on the neighbor's box (higher or lower). If the neighbor is lower, the player is inside this box's volume (which extends down to bottomY-4). Penetration → Havok pushes the player out upward (toward the nearest surface = this tile's top). That would make stepping between tiles of different heights bumpy.\n\nBetter: box from bottomY (the tile's own lowest corner) to topY, no extension. Gaps between a tile and a lower neighbor: the surface between them is the mesh (the triangles interpolate). A character sliding down a slope into a lower tile: the capsule dips below the current tile's box bottom → briefly unsupported → falls a little → hits the lower tile's box. A small dip of at most (tileHeightRange) — with 5u amplitude, adjacent 4u tiles differ by at most ~1–2u. Acceptable for a demo; the character controller has keepDistance=0.05.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: make the box from (bottomY - 1) to topY. A 1u extension below the tile's lowest corner covers most of the inter-tile dip without the player getting trapped in a neighbor's box (the neighbor's box top is at most 1u above… hmm).\n\nKeep it simple: box bottom = bottomY - 1, top = topY. I'll tune in the playtest.\n\nNavmesh: built from the terrain mesh in scene252 (S5), not in the terrain module.\n\nIndex surgery for destroyTile:\n```ts\nconst cols = TILE_COUNT + 1; // 33\n// tile (tx, tz) quad indices at position (tz * TILE_COUNT + tx) * 6\n// new indices = all indices except that range\nconst newIndices = new Uint32Array(old.length - 6);\n// copy before + after\nconst newNormals = computeNormals(positions, newIndices);\nresizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, newNormals, newIndices, uvs);\nremovePhysicsBody(world, tile.body);\ntile.obstacle = addBoxObstacle(nav, center, {x:2, y:2, z:2}, 0);\n```\n\nWait — positions stays the same (we're only removing indices). computeNormals(positions, newIndices) → boundary normals update.\n\nOne concern: `resizeMeshGeometry` reallocates GPU buffers on every destruction. With many rapid shots, that's many buffer allocations + deferred destruction. Fine for a demo (Havok raycast is limited by fire rate).\n\nAlso, `_cpuIndices` is updated by resizeMeshGeometry (it sets mesh._cpuIndices = indices). So I should read from mesh._cpuIndices every time (not cache my own copy).\n\nNow — the `createFlatGroundData` winding order and my tile index math: from the code above:\n```\nfor row in 0..subdivisions:\n  for col in 0..subdivisions:\n    topLeft = row*cols + col\n    ...\n    indices[ii++] = bottomRight; topRight; topLeft; bottomLeft; bottomRight; topLeft;\n```\nSo tile (tx, tz) → indices at offset `((tz * TILE_COUNT) + tx) * 6`. ✓\n\nVertex layout: row 0 = z = -height/2 (from `z = -height/2 + (1 - row/subdivisions)*height`). col = x from -width/2. So tile (tx, tz): x from -64 + tx*4 to -64 + (tx+1)*4; z from -64 + tz*4 to -64 + (tz+1)*4. Center: (-64 + tx*4 + 2, ·, -64 + tz*4 + 2). ✓\n\ntileFromPoint(x, z): tx = floor((x + 64) / 4), tz = floor((z + 64) / 4), clamped to 0..31, null if out of range.\n\nOK, let me record the S1 findings and write fps-terrain.ts.\n\nAlso need to double-check that `computeNormals`'s signature is `computeNormals(positions, indices)` → returns Float32Array (normals). From interview-t3-f6606-3: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` is a pure CPU function at compute-normals.ts:9. ✓\n\nAnd check that `createPhysicsAggregate` works with a TransformNode — it takes `node: Mesh` (type). A TransformNode is not a Mesh. For a static tile box, use createPhysicsBody(world, node: SceneNode, STATIC) + createPhysicsShape + setPhysicsBodyShape. The TransformNode works (SceneNode). ✓\n\nActually wait — for static bodies, does the body's transform come from the node at creation time? The world syncs body→node every step (body to node), and node→body for ANIMATED bodies. For STATIC, the transform is set at creation time (from the node's transform). Let me verify that createPhysicsBody sets the initial transform from the node… the controller uses ANIMATED. In the static case: let me quickly check createPhysicsBody — I only saw the first few lines. Let me check whether it reads the node's transform at creation time.\n","seq":44715,"t":1939480,"text":"\n\nS1 verification is complete. Recording the results, then checking one last detail (static body initial transform from node) before writing code.\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":57344,"draft_n":15785,