{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking about the unexpected findings from the immediately preceding step (the S1 verification batch and the S2 write). Let me try to recall what I actually discovered that contradicted expectations:\n\n1. **`resizeMeshGeometry` exists** — the plan called for using raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + `mesh._gpu` swapping for terrain destruction, but `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) turned out to be the intended in-place seam: it reallocates the GPU buffers, bumps `_renderableVersion` on all registered scenes so cached draw bundles get re-recorded, and updates the `_cpu*` copies. Raw `_gpu` swapping could leave cached render bundles binding to the old buffers. → Use `resizeMeshGeometry` for tile destruction. This has already been implemented in fps-terrain.ts.\n\n2. **Controller friction is a public field** — the plan (interview-t5-f27685-6) said \"if the controller's shape getter is exposed, that's the preferred target\" and it was unverified. Discovered: `PhysicsCharacterController` has public `staticFriction`/`dynamicFriction` fields (defaults 0/1) — no shape access needed at all. → Set the controller's friction fields for Quake-style sliding; the fallback (terrain shape friction) is not needed (though low terrain friction is still being set).\n\n3. **`moveWithCollisions` does not apply gravity** — the plan assumed a `moveWithCollisions` + `checkSupport` pattern. Confirmed: `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` just integrates the given displacement; gravity must be added manually (or use `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` which applies gravity internally). → Manually apply gravity in the per-step movement.\n\n4. **FreeCamera's world matrix is derived from position+target, not from `_yaw`/`_pitch`** — I assumed that by setting `_yaw`/`_pitch` (internal fields) I could control the camera orientation, but it turned out the local world matrix is computed as `mat4LookAtLH(position, target, up)`; `_yaw`/`_pitch` are only used by `attachFreeControl`. → For the FPS camera, set `cam.position` and `cam.target` every frame from the player's eye position + forward vector (yaw/pitch).\n\n5. **Nav agents have no reposition API** — there's no `setAgentPosition`; NavCrowd agents will drift from the character controller when collisions push the bot off the path. → Mitigation: periodically re-target the agent / re-sync when the agent and bot diverge (e.g., when they're more than 4u apart, agentGoto to the bot's position).\n\n6. **The `applyPhysicsImpulse` name** — audit-28769-3 had recorded `applyPhysicsImpulse`, but the actual export is `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (line 610 of index.ts). Also, `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` is at line 1056, `getPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` at line 1063 — the audit had flagged line drift. → Use `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse`.\n\n7. **`tileFromPoint` placeholder bug** — in fps-terrain.ts I wrote a placeholder `return null` and then fixed it. This was my own bug, not an API discovery. Might be worth mentioning? It's more of a mistake than a gotcha about the API. I could include it as a self-inflicted note, but the question is about API/behavior discoveries. I'll skip it or touch on it briefly.\n\n8. **Static bodies get their transform from the node at creation time** — `createPhysicsBody` reads `node.position`/`node.rotationQuaternion` at creation; static bodies don't track the node afterward. → Place the TransformNode at the box center before creating the body (which is what I did). This is more a confirmation than a surprise, but it does carry implications for ordering.\n\n9. **TextRenderer HUD path** — `registerTextRenderer` registers a rendering context on the surface; render order = registration order (scene first, text second → text on top); use `clear: false` so the 3D frame isn't wiped. This is more a plan-confirmation than a gotcha. The plan said \"copy the scene180/181 flow as-is\" — but the scene180 path (TextRenderer) is for standalone use, and I determined that it works even with a 3D scene present by registering after the scene. A subtle finding: ordering matters.\n\n10. **`physicsRaycast` membership filter** — the query's `membership` defaults to `~0`; to hit only terrain, set `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER`. The character controller's capsule has a default membership (0?) and its shape is private (`_shape`), so you can't set the controller's mask — meaning the controller's capsule will have default mask behavior. Implication: bullet raycasts use the terrain mask; the controller collides by default. No problem.\n\n11. **Bundle size spec scope** — confirmed that only scenes in scene-config.json with maxRawKB are covered; scene252 is unaffected. Plan confirmation.\n\n12. **`createFlatGroundData` winding order / index layout** — the quad (row,col) indices sit at offset (row*subdiv + col)*6, row 0 is at z=-height/2 — this is needed for the tile index calculation. This is a discovery that's critical to the correctness of the tile calculation (I used it in fps-terrain.ts).\n\nThe question says \"only discoveries from this step.\" The immediately preceding step was the batch of verification greps (character-controller's integrate/checkSupport, mat4 exports, createBox, registerScene) — plus the whole S1 phase before that. The user is asking after the \"immediately preceding step\" — probably meaning the most recent work block (S1 verification + S2 write). Let me enumerate the real discoveries that would change what I do going forward:\n\n- resizeMeshGeometry is the seam (the plan called for uploadMeshToGPU)\n- Controller friction is a public field (the plan was uncertain)\n- moveWithCollisions doesn't apply gravity (integrate does)\n- FreeCamera orientation comes from position+target; _yaw/_pitch are control-only\n- No nav agent reposition API\n- applyPhysicsBodyImpulse is the name (audit had applyPhysicsImpulse)\n- Static bodies are snap to the node at creation (order matters)\n- registerScene grep returned nothing — wait, my grep for `export function registerScene\\b` against scene-core.ts returned nothing (the last grep produced no output — the box grep was shown but there were no lines for registerScene). So registerScene is exported from somewhere else (probably scene-register.ts). Not really a gotcha, just that my grep pattern was too narrow. Don't include.\n\nAlso: the `_yaw`/`_pitch` fields are @internal — scene code reads internal fields \"with full TypeScript typing\" per GUIDANCE (internal code reads fields directly). But since the matrix comes from position+target, they're not needed in the first place.\n\nThe reply should be concise, one per line in GOTCHA format. Let me pick the ones that actually matter:\n\n1. resizeMeshGeometry vs uploadMeshToGPU (already recorded in evidence, but the user is asking — it's a correction to the plan)\n2. Controller friction is a public field\n3. moveWithCollisions: no gravity\n4. FreeCamera: matrix from position+target, _yaw/_pitch are control-only\n5. No nav agent reposition\n6. applyPhysicsBodyImpulse name\n7. Static bodies snap to the node at creation\n8. Text renderer order: registration order = render order; clear:false needed\n\nLet me condense these to the essential ones. The instruction says \"list each on its own line as GOTCHA: ... — IMPLICATION: ...\". Let me do that.\n","seq":53342,"t":2355062,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: the plan's tile-removal path called for raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + `mesh._gpu` swap, but `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) is the real in-place seam — it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record, and refreshes the `_cpu*` copies; a raw `_gpu` swap would leave cached bundles binding the old buffers — IMPLICATION: fps-terrain.ts uses `resizeMeshGeometry` (already written that way); index surgery reads `mesh._cpuIndices` each time since the function updates it.\nGOTCHA: `PhysicsCharacterController` exposes friction as plain public fields (`staticFriction` default 0, `dynamicFriction` default 1) — no shape getter needed, settling the plan's open question — IMPLICATION: Quake slide is tuned via `cc.staticFriction`/`cc.dynamicFriction` in scene252, not via controller-shape mask access.\nGOTCHA: `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` integrates only the given displacement — it does NOT apply gravity (only `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` does) — IMPLICATION: player and bot stepping must add `vel.y -= g*dt` manually before each `moveWithCollisions` call.\nGOTCHA: FreeCamera's world matrix is computed from `position` + `target` (`mat4LookAtLH`), not from `_yaw`/`_pitch` — those internal fields are only used by `attachFreeControl` — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera is driven by setting `cam.position` (eye) and `cam.target` (eye + forward(yaw,pitch)) each frame; don't touch `_yaw`/`_pitch`.\nGOTCHA: the navigation API has no agent reposition/teleport — NavCrowd agents will drift from the character controller when collisions push a bot off its path — IMPLICATION: re-sync the agent (agentGoto to the bot's actual position) when agent/bot distance exceeds ~4u, plus periodic re-targeting.\nGOTCHA: the impulse export is `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse`, not `applyPhysicsImpulse` as the audit note had — IMPLICATION: debris knockback uses `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (also `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` at havok.ts:1056 is confirmed).\nGOTCHA: `createPhysicsBody` copies the node's transform into the Havok body at creation time; STATIC bodies do not track the node afterwards — IMPLICATION: the per-tile TransformNode must be placed at the box center BEFORE `createPhysicsBody` (fps-terrain.ts does this); never move a tile node after creation.\nGOTCHA: multiple rendering contexts on one surface render in registration order, and `createTextRenderer` clears by default — IMPLICATION: register the 3D scene first, then `registerTextRenderer` with `clear: false`, or the HUD wipes the frame (or vice versa).","timings":{"cache_n":84736,"draft_n":5509,"draft_n_accepte