{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking about constraints that surfaced specifically in the step I just completed (the last tool call batch and its results). Let me think about what I learned in that step:\n\n1. `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` — applies gravity internally, projects velocity against the surface.\n2. `checkSupport(deltaTime, direction)` — \"direction to probe, usually the gravity direction\" — probes along the direction (usually gravity) to classify support. Important: direction should be the gravity direction (0,-1,0), not the velocity direction.\n3. `mat4Translation` and `mat4Compose` are exported — for thin instance matrices.\n4. `createBoxData(size = 1)` — the box factory takes a uniform size.\n5. The grep for `registerScene` returned nothing in that output — actually, the output of the 3rd grep was cut off: only mat4, createBoxData were shown... the registerScene grep produced no output lines in the result (=== followed by nothing). So `registerScene` is not exported from scene-core.ts — it must be exported from somewhere else. This is an unresolved item, not a constraint.\n\nConstraints that surfaced in this step that the rest of the project must respect:\n\n- `checkSupport` must be probed along the gravity direction (0,-1,0), not the movement direction — that's the documented usage (\"usually the gravity direction\"). This is a rule for my movement code.\n- `integrate` applies gravity internally — so if I use integrate, I must not double-apply gravity. But that's more of a note than a constraint.\n- `createBox` only supports uniform size (size parameter) — the debris boxes are cubes, not non-uniform boxes. This is a constraint on debris design.\n- `mat4Compose`/`mat4Translation` are the available helpers for composing thin instance matrices — more of a pointer than a constraint.\n\nIs \"checkSupport uses the gravity direction\" a constraint? It's a requirement for how to correctly call the API — yes, it's a requirement that the rest of the project (my movement code) must respect.\n\n\"createBox only takes a uniform size\" — a constraint on the debris visual (must be a cube; for non-uniform, use scaling on the mesh).\n\nAnything else? The fact that registerScene is not exported from scene-core is unresolved — it's not a constraint.\n\nThe user's question is specifically: \"In the step I just completed, did I learn or commit to any constraints that the rest of the project must respect?\"\n\nSo the list:\n- CONSTRAINT: checkSupport's direction parameter must be the gravity direction (0,-1,0) — not the velocity/movement direction — otherwise the support classification is wrong.\n- CONSTRAINT: integrate() applies gravity internally; must not add gravity separately when using it (avoid double application).\n- CONSTRAINT: createBox only creates uniform cubes (single size parameter); for non-uniform debris, apply mesh scaling, and the physics box shape has its own extent specification.\n\nThese are the constraints that surfaced in this step. The mat4 exports are pointers, not constraints. The fact that registerScene is not in scene-core is a missing piece, not a constraint.\n\nI should answer concisely, listing each item on its own line.\n","seq":54438,"t":2404367,"text":"\n\nCONSTRAINT: `checkSupport(deltaTime, direction)` must be probed along the gravity direction (0, -1, 0) — the docs say \"usually the gravity direction\" — not the movement/velocity direction, or the SUPPORTED/SLIDING classification is wrong.\nCONSTRAINT: `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` applies gravity internally to the character — if the player/bot stepping uses `integrate`, gravity must not be added again by the scene code (double application).\nCONSTRAINT: `createBox`/`createBoxData` only produce uniform cubes (single `size` parameter) — non-uniform debris visuals need mesh `scaling` (physics box extents are separate via shape parameters).","timings":{"cache_n":84736,"draft_n":1750,"draft_n_acce