{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"OK, `registerScene` is at scene-core.ts:400. It probably registers the scene's rendering context. If we call `registerScene(scene)` first and then `registerTextRenderer(tr)`, the text context gets pushed after the scene → the text is drawn on top.\n\nNow I have everything I need. Let me write the remaining files:\n\n1. `fps-input.ts`\n2. `fps-audio.ts`\n3. `scene252.ts`\n4. `scene252.html`\n\nFirst, I'll write fps-input.ts:\n\n```ts\n// DOM input for the FPS demo: pointer-lock mouse look + WASD/space keyboard state.\n// The engine has no input module; the demo owns its DOM listeners.\n\nexport interface FpsInput {\n    /** True while the pointer is locked to the canvas. */\n    locked: boolean;\n    /** Move keys. */\n    forward: boolean;\n    back: boolean;\n    left: boolean;\n    right: boolean;\n    jump: boolean;\n    /** Accumulated mouse-look delta in pixels since the last read; resets on read. */\n    consumeLookDelta(): { dx: number; dy: number };\n    /** Subscribe to a shot request (mouse-down while locked). Returns a disposer. */\n    onFire(cb: () => void): () => void;\n    /** Subscribe to pointer-lock changes. Returns a disposer. */\n    onLockChange(cb: (locked: boolean) => void): () => void;\n    /** Remove all listeners. */\n    dispose(): void;\n}\n\nexport function createFpsInput(canvas: HTMLCanvasElement): FpsInput {\n    const state = { forward: false, back: false, left: false, right: false, jump: false };\n    let locked = false;\n    let dx = 0;\n    let dy = 0;\n    const fireSubs: (() => void)[] = [];\n    const lockSubs: ((locked: boolean) => void)[] = [];\n\n    const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent): void => {\n        switch (e.code) {\n            case \"KeyW\": case \"ArrowUp\": state.forward = true; break;\n            case \"KeyS\": case \"ArrowDown\": state.back = true; break;\n            case \"KeyA\": case \"ArrowLeft\": state.left = true; break;\n            case \"KeyD\": case \"ArrowRight\": state.right = true; break;\n            case \"Space\": state.jump = true; e.preventDefault(); break;\n        }\n    };\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nWait — \"curly: error — always use braces\" — does `case \"KeyW\": case \"ArrowUp\": state.forward = true; break;` count as a single-statement case without braces? ESLint's curly rule with \"all\" requires braces for if/for/while/do, but doesn't apply to switch cases. In fact, `curly` applies to if/else/for/while/do. Switch cases are fine. But Prettier will format them. Let me use the simple form.\n\nAlso no-floating-promises: `canvas.requestPointerLock()` returns a Promise in modern browsers — must handle it: `void canvas.requestPointerLock().catch(() => {})` — hmm, the TS lib might type it as Promise<void>. I'll use `canvas.requestPointerLock().catch(() => undefined)` — this handles it.\n\nfps-audio.ts:\n\n```ts\n// Web Audio SFX for the FPS demo. Lazy-initialized on the first user gesture;\n// all sounds are synthesized (no assets). No module-level side effects.\n\nexport interface FpsAudio {\n    /** Must be called from a user gesture (pointer lock) to unlock the AudioContext. */\n    unlock(): void;\n    gunshot(): void;\n    hit(): void;\n    thud(): void;\n    damage(): void;\n}\n\nexport function createFpsAudio(): FpsAudio {\n    let ctx: AudioContext | null = null;\n\n    const ensure = (): AudioContext | null => {\n        if (!ctx) {\n            try {\n                ctx = new AudioContext();\n            } catch {\n                return null;\n            }\n        }\n        if (ctx.state === \"suspended\") {\n            void ctx.resume();\n        }\n        return ctx;\n    };\n\n    const noiseBuffer = (c: AudioContext, seconds: number): AudioBuffer => {\n        const buf = c.createBuffer(1, Math.floor(c.sampleRate * seconds), c.sampleRate);\n        const data = buf.getChannelData(0);\n        for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\n            data[i] = Math.random() * 2 - 1;\n        }\n        return buf;\n    };\n\n    const gunshot = (): void => {\n        const c = ensure();\n        if (!c) return;\n        const t = c.currentTime;\n        const src = c.createBufferSource();\n        src.buffer = noiseBuffer(c, 0.15);\n        const filter = c.createBiquadFilter();\n        filter.type = \"lowpass\";\n        filter.frequency.setValueAtTime(3000, t);\n        filter.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(200, t + 0.12);\n        const gain = c.createGain();\n        gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0.5, t);\n        gain.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.001, t + 0.15);\n        src.connect(filter).connect(gain).connect(c.destination);\n        src.start(t);\n        src.stop(t + 0.15);\n    };\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm — `connect()` returns an AudioNode, so chaining works.\n\nhit: a short 880Hz sine wave, 0.08s.\nthud: 90Hz sine wave, 0.2s, fast decay.\ndamage: 220Hz sawtooth wave, 0.15s.\n\nNext, scene252.ts. Let me write it carefully. Structure:\n\n```ts\n// Scene 252: Quake-style FPS — destructible noise terrain, tile-removal shooting,\n// navmesh bots, GPU-text HUD, Web Audio SFX. Built purely on the public Lite API.\n\nimport HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\";\nimport {\n    addToScene,\n    agentGoto,\n    addAgent,\n    addTextRenderable?? no — text renderer path\n    createBox,\n    createCapsule,\n    createDefaultTextData,\n    createEngine,\n    createFpsInput... no — local import\n    createFreeCamera,\n    createHavokWorld,\n    createHemisphericLight,\n    createNavigationPluginAsync,\n    createNavCrowd,\n    createNavMesh,\n    createPhysicsCharacterController,\n    createSceneContext,\n    createStandardMaterial,\n    createTextLayer,\n    createTextRenderer,\n    findRandomPointAroundCircle,\n    getAgentPosition,\n    getAgentVelocity,\n    loadFont,\n    mat4Compose,\n    onBeforeRender,\n    onPhysicsAfterStep,\n    physicsRaycast,\n    raycast,  // navmesh raycast — exported as `raycast` from navigation\n    registerScene,\n    registerTextRenderer,\n    removePhysicsBody,\n    setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity,\n    setThinInstances,\n    startEngine,\n    updateDefaultTextData,\n    updateNavCrowd,\n    CharacterSupportedState,\n    PhysicsShapeType,\n    createPhysicsBody,\n    createPhysicsShape,\n    setPhysicsBodyShape,\n    setPhysicsBodyMass,\n    setPhysicsShapeMaterial,\n} from \"babylon-lite\";\nimport type { ... } from \"babylon-lite\";\nimport { ARENA_SIZE, TERRAIN_LAYER, createFpsTerrain, destroyTerrainTile, terrainHeightAt, tileFromPoint } from \"./fps-terrain.js\";\nimport { createFpsInput } from \"./fps-input.js\";\nimport { createFpsAudio } from \"./fps-audio.js\";\n```\n\nWait — `raycast` is exported from navigation (navigation.ts:615 `export function raycast(plugin, start, end)`). Check whether it's in the index barrel... the earlier grep showed the navigation exports block at lines 662-679: createNavigationPluginAsync, createNavMesh, createNavMeshFromSources, ..., getClosestPoint, ..., addBoxObstacle. The grep output was truncated (head -60). `raycast` might be in there. Let me check the exact name in the index.\n\nAlso `setPhysicsBodyMass` — exported? Let me check.\n\nGame state object (local to main(), no module-level state):\n\n```ts\ninterface Bot {\n    cc: PhysicsCharacterController;\n    agentIndex: number;\n    hp: number;\n    dead: boolean;\n    respawnAt: number;   // game time seconds\n    nextRetarget: number;\n    nextShot: number;\n    heading: number;\n}\n```\n\nTime management: accumulate game time from the physics step's dt (physics time, deterministic).\n\nPlayer state: hp, invulnerableUntil, lastShotAt.\n\nMain flow:\n\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    const scene = createSceneContext(engine);\n    scene.fixedDeltaMs = 1000 / PHYSICS_FPS;\n    scene.clearColor = { r: 0.05, g: 0.07, b: 0.1, a: 1 };\n\n    // Camera (position + target driven per frame from the player's view)\n    const cam = createFreeCamera({ x: 0, y: 5, z: -10 }, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 });\n    cam.nearPlane = 0.1;\n    cam.farPlane = 500;\n    cam.fov = 1.1;\n    scene.camera = cam;\n\n    const light = createHemisphericLight([0, 1, 0]);\n    light.intensity = 0.7;\n    addToScene(scene, light);\n\n    // Physics\n    const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\n    const world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -9.8, z: 0 });\n\n    // Terrain\n    const terrain = createFpsTerrain(engine, world);\n    const terrainMat = createStandardMaterial();\n    terrainMat.diffuseColor = [0.45, 0.5, 0.42];\n    terrainMat.specularColor = [0.05, 0.05, 0.05];\n    terrain.mesh.material = terrainMat;\n    addToScene(scene, terrain.mesh);\n\n    // Player\n    const spawn = { x: 0, z: -40 };\n    const player = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: spawn.x, y: terrainHeightAt(terrain, spawn.x, spawn.z) + 2, z: spawn.z }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.5 });\n    player.staticFriction = 0;\n    player.dynamicFriction = 0.1;\n    player.maxSlopeCosine = 0.7; // hmm — default 0.5 (60°). Our slopes are gentle (~10°). Keep the default.\n\n    // Navigation\n    const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n    createNavMesh(nav, [terrain.mesh], { cs: 0.5, ch: 0.5, walkableSlopeAngle: 45, walkableHeight: 1, walkableClimb: 1, walkableRadius: 0.5, maxEdgeLen: 12, maxSimplificationError: 1.3, minRegionArea: 8, mergeRegionArea: 20, maxVertsPerPoly: 6, detailSampleDist: 6, detailSampleMaxError: 1 });\n\n    // Bots: NavCrowd + character controllers, displayed as thin instances\n    const crowd = createNavCrowd(nav, BOT_COUNT, 0.5);\n    const botMesh = createCapsule(engine, { height: 1.8, radius: 0.5, tessellation: 12 });\n    const botMat = createStandardMaterial();\n    botMat.diffuseColor = [0.9, 0.4, 0.2];\n    botMat.specularColor = [0.1, 0.05, 0.05];\n    botMesh.material = botMat;\n    addToScene(scene, botMesh);\n    const botMatrices = new Float32Array(BOT_COUNT * 16);\n    setThinInstances(botMesh, botMatrices, BOT_COUNT);\n    const bots: Bot[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < BOT_COUNT; i++) {\n        const p = findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 40 }, 10);  // hmm — better to spread spawns around the arena\n        ...\n    }\n```\n\nSpawning bots: findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, position, radius) — a random point on the navmesh within radius. Position: spread them around the arena: e.g. (±30, ±30). Use findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, {x: sx, y: 5, z: sz}, 8). The returned point's y is the navmesh height.\n\nBot controller position: (p.x, p.y + 1, p.z) — the capsule center is 0.9 above the ground; the navmesh point is at surface level; the controller center should be ~0.9 above.\n\nAgent position: p (surface level). The agent walks at surface level; the controller is at center height. getAgentVelocity's y is ~0. No problem.\n\nDebris:\n```ts\nconst DEBRIS_COUNT = 32;\nconst debrisMesh = createBox(engine, 0.6);\nconst debrisMat = createStandardMaterial();\ndebrisMat.diffuseColor = [0.55, 0.5, 0.45];\ndebrisMesh.material = debrisMat;\naddToScene(scene, debrisMesh);\nconst debrisMatrices = new Float32Array(DEBRIS_COUNT * 16);\nsetThinInstances(debrisMesh, debrisMatrices, DEBRIS_COUNT);\ninterface Debris { body: PhysicsBody; alive: boolean; expiresAt: number; }\nconst debris: Debris[] = [];\nlet debrisNext = 0;\n```\n\nspawnDebris(x, y, z):\n- Take a slot (recycle the oldest), createPhysicsBody(world, node=TransformNode at pos, DYNAMIC) — wait, dynamic bodies are synced node→body via prestep TELEPORT... a dynamic body with prestep TELEPORT every step would be teleported to the node's position — that breaks the simulation!\n\nHmm. How does scene40 handle a dynamic sphere? `createPhysicsAggregate(world, sphere, SPHERE, { mass: 1 })` — it creates the body and shape; for DYNAMIC bodies, what's the prestep default? `createPhysicsBody` sets `_preStep: false` by default. The controller sets `setPhysicsBodyPreStep(this._body, true)` for ANIMATED. For DYNAMIC, prestep stays false → the body is simulated freely, and the world syncs body→node each step (`_syncBodyToNode`). So the node follows the body.\n\nSo for debris: createTransformNode at the spawn position, createPhysicsBody(world, node, DYNAMIC), a box shape with extents 0.3, mass 1. After that, the node tracks the body. Read `body.node.position` + `body.node.rotationQuaternion` for the instance matrix.\n\nTo remove: removePhysicsBody(world, body).\n\n- setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity(world, body, {random}) for the initial pop.\n\nHUD:\n```ts\nconst font = await loadFont(\"/fonts/Inter.ttf\");\nconst hudData = createDefaultTextData(font, 28, \"HP 100   KILLS 0   TILES 0\", [1, 1, 1, 1], { maxWidth: 1000, align: \"left\" });\nconst hudLayer = createTextLayer(hudData, { positionPx: { x: 24, y: 24 }, scale: 1, rotationRad: 0, opacity: 1 });\nconst crossData = createDefaultTextData(font, 20, \"+\", [1, 1, 1, 1], {});\nconst crossLayer = createTextLayer(crossData, { positionPx: { x: canvas.width / 2 - 8, y: canvas.height / 2 - 10 }, scale: 1, rotationRad: 0, opacity: 0.9 });\nconst tr = createTextRenderer(engine, { layers: [hudLayer, crossLayer], clear: false });\n```\n\nWait — the createTextRenderer options: `{ layers, clearValue, clear }`? From the code: `opts.layers.slice()`, `opts.clear ?? true`, `opts.clearValue ?? ...`. So `clear: false` is valid. Let me confirm that TextRendererOptions has `clear`. I'll check the interface.\n\nThen `registerTextRenderer(tr)` — after registerScene.\n\nOrdering issue: registerScene(scene) must be called after everything is added (meshes, text renderables — though the text renderer is a separate context). So:\n1. Add all meshes to the scene\n2. await registerScene(scene)\n3. registerTextRenderer(tr)\n4. await startEngine(engine)\n\nThen the game loop:\n\nonPhysicsAfterStep(world, (dt) => {\n    game.time += dt;\n    stepPlayer(dt);\n    stepBots(dt);\n    stepDebris();  // expire\n});\n\nonBeforeRender(scene, () => {\n    updateCamera();\n    updateBotMatrices();\n    updateDebrisMatrices();\n    updateHud();\n});\n\nPlayer step:\n```ts\nconst playerVel = { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 };\nfunction stepPlayer(dt: number): void {\n    const info = player.checkSupport(dt, { x: 0, y: -1, z: 0 });\n    const grounded = info.supportedState === CharacterSupportedState.SUPPORTED || info.supportedState === CharacterSupportedState.SLIDING;\n    // input direction in world space\n    const cy = Math.cos(yaw), sy = Math.sin(yaw);\n    let ix = 0, iz = 0;\n    if (input.forward) { ix += sy; iz += cy; }\n    if (input.back) { ix -= sy; iz -= cy; }\n    if (input.right) { ix += cy; iz -= sy; }\n    if (input.left) { ix -= cy; iz += sy; }\n    const il = Math.hypot(ix, iz);\n    if (il > 0) { ix /= il; iz /= il; }\n    const accel = grounded ? GROUND_ACCEL : AIR_ACCEL;\n    playerVel.x += ix * accel * dt;\n    playerVel.z += iz * accel * dt;\n    if (input.jump && grounded) {\n        playerVel.y = JUMP_SPEED;\n    }\n    playerVel.y -= GRAVITY * dt;\n    // slide friction: decay horizontal velocity when not on ground\n    if (grounded && il === 0) {\n        const damp = Math.max(0, 1 - SLIDE_FRICTION * dt);\n        playerVel.x *= damp;\n        playerVel.z *= damp;\n    }\n    // clamp horizontal speed\n    const hs = Math.hypot(playerVel.x, playerVel.z);\n    if (hs > MAX_SPEED) {\n        const s = MAX_SPEED / hs;\n        playerVel.x *= s;\n        playerVel.z *= s;\n    }\n    player.moveWithCollisions({ x: playerVel.x * dt, y: playerVel.y * dt, z: playerVel.z * dt });\n    // fell out of the world?\n    const p = player.getPosition();\n    if (p.y < -20) {\n        damagePlayer(25);\n        respawnPlayer();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — Quake-style slide: in Quake, the ground friction is low so you keep sliding; input adds acceleration. My damp only applies when there's no input. When there is input, velocity keeps growing up to MAX_SPEED. Good enough.\n\nAlso — `checkSupport` before moving: it validates the manifold from the previous step. The first call is UNSUPPORTED (no manifold yet) — no problem.\n\nOne concern: the controller's default maxSlopeCosine is 0.5 (60°). Our terrain is gentle. Keep the default.\n\nShooting:\n```ts\nfunction tryFire(): void {\n    if (game.time - lastShot < FIRE_INTERVAL) return;\n    lastShot = game.time;\n    audio.gunshot();\n    const eye = eyePosition();\n    const fwd = forwardDir();\n    const from = { x: eye.x, y: eye.y, z: eye.z };\n    const to = { x: eye.x + fwd.x * 100, y: eye.y + fwd.y * 100, z: eye.z + fwd.z * 100 };\n    // terrain\n    const terr = physicsRaycast(world, from, to, { membership: TERRAIN_LAYER });\n    let bestDist = Infinity;\n    let hitBot: Bot | null = null;\n    for (const bot of bots) {\n        if (bot.dead) continue;\n        const d = rayVsCapsule(from, fwd, botPos(bot), BOT_CAP_H, BOT_CAP_R);\n        if (d !== null && d < bestDist) { bestDist = d; hitBot = bot; }\n    }\n    if (terr.hasHit && (!hitBot || terr.hitDistance < bestDist)) {\n        const tile = tileFromPoint(terrain, terr.hitPoint.x, terr.hitPoint.z);\n        if (tile) {\n            destroyTerrainTile(terrain, engine, world, nav, tile);\n            spawnDebrisAt(terr.hitPoint.x, terr.hitPoint.y, terr.hitPoint.z);\n            audio.thud();\n        }\n    } else if (hitBot) {\n        damageBot(hitBot, 25);\n        audio.hit();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait — physicsRaycast's hitDistance: computed as the distance from `from` to hitPos. OK.\n\nNote: shooting a tile also damages... no, it's just terrain.\n\nAlso, when the player shoots a tile and falls into the hole — the player can fall through (the body is removed). That's the Quake-style \"shoot the floor\" mechanic.\n\nBots:\n```ts\nfunction stepBots(dt: number): void {\n    updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt);\n    for (let i = 0; i < bots.length; i++) {\n        const bot = bots[i]!;\n        if (bot.dead) {\n            if (game.time >= bot.respawnAt) respawnBot(bot);\n            continue;\n        }\n        // retarget\n        if (game.time >= bot.nextRetarget) {\n            bot.nextRetarget = game.time + 2 + Math.random() * 2;\n            const bp = bot.cc.getPosition();\n            const pp = player.getPosition();\n            const dist = Math.hypot(pp.x - bp.x, pp.z - bp.z);\n            const los = raycast(nav, botEye(bp), playerEye(pp));\n            if (!playerInvulnerable() && dist < 40 && los.hit) {\n                agentGoto(crowd, bot.agentIndex, pp);\n            } else {\n                const target = findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, bp, 20);\n                agentGoto(crowd, bot.agentIndex, target);\n            }\n        }\n        // follow the agent's velocity\n        const av = getAgentVelocity(crowd, bot.agentIndex);\n        const bp = bot.cc.getPosition();\n        const ap = getAgentPosition(crowd, bot.agentIndex);\n        if (Math.hypot(ap.x - bp.x, ap.z - bp.z) > 4) {\n            agentGoto(crowd, bot.agentIndex, bp);  // re-sync the agent to the bot\n        }\n        bot.vel.y -= GRAVITY * dt;\n        bot.cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: av.x * dt, y: bot.vel.y * dt, z: av.z * dt });\n        // heading for display\n        if (av.x !== 0 || av.z !== 0) bot.heading = Math.atan2(av.x, av.z);\n        // shooting\n        if (game.time >= bot.nextShot) {\n            bot.nextShot = game.time + 1.2;\n            const dist = ...;\n            const los = raycast(nav, botEye(bp), playerEye(pp));\n            if (los.hit && dist < 40 && !playerInvulnerable()) {\n                const accuracy = Math.max(0.15, 1 - dist / 50);\n                if (Math.random() < accuracy) damagePlayer(10);\n            }\n        }\n        if (bp.y < -20) respawnBot(bot);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm — botEye: the navmesh's raycast is surface-to-surface; both are at navmesh height. botEye(bp) = (bp.x, bp.y - 0.9, bp.z) — surface level. playerEye = (pp.x, pp.y - 0.9, pp.z).\n\nraycast(nav, start, end) returns {hit, hitPoint} — hit=true means... let me check the semantics: does hit=true mean blocked, or that it reached the end? Let me check the raycast implementation in navigation.ts.\n\nAlso note: retargeting every 2–4 seconds means the bot periodically loses its target (patrols) even with LOS — that's fine (keeps it from swarming).\n\nBot damage to the player is gated by invulnerability (after respawn).\n\nPlayer damage: hp -= n; if hp <= 0 → \"respawn\": hp = 100, damage count... the plan says \"damage/respawn on both sides\". Player death → full respawn (hp 100) + penalty? Keep it simple: player death → respawn at spawn point, hp 100, 3-second invulnerability. Track deaths? The HUD shows HP, KILLS, TILES.\n\nKills: bot death → kills++.\n\nHUD update:\n```ts\nlet lastHudText = \"\";\nfunction updateHud(): void {\n    const text = `HP ${Math.max(0, Math.round(playerHp))}   KILLS ${kills}   TILES ${terrain.destroyedCount}`;\n    if (text !== lastHudText) {\n        lastHudText = text;\n        updateDefaultTextData(hudData, text);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBot matrix update (per render frame):\n```ts\nfunction updateBotMatrices(): void {\n    for (let i = 0; i < bots.length; i++) {\n        const bot = bots[i]!;\n        const m = botMatrices.subarray(i * 16, i * 16 + 16);\n        if (bot.dead) {\n            // hide with scale 0\n            m.set(HIDDEN_MATRIX);\n        } else {\n            const p = bot.cc.getPosition();\n            mat4Compose(m, p.x, p.y, p.z, 0, bot.heading, 0, 1, 1, 1);\n        }\n    }\n    setThinInstances(botMesh, botMatrices, BOT_COUNT);\n}\n```\n\nWait — mat4Compose signature: (result, x, y, z, qx, qy, qz, qw, sx, sy, sz)? Let me check mat4-compose.ts. The quaternion for a Y rotation: qy = sin(heading/2), qw = cos(heading/2).\n\nActually, let me compute the quaternion components directly:\n```ts\nconst hy = bot.heading / 2;\nmat4Compose(m, p.x, p.y, p.z, 0, Math.sin(hy), Math.cos(hy), 1, 1, 1);\n```\n\nNeed to verify the mat4Compose signature. Let me check.\n\nDebris matrix update:\n```ts\nfunction updateDebrisMatrices(): void {\n    for (let i = 0; i < debris.length; i++) {\n        const d = debris[i]!;\n        const m = debrisMatrices.subarray(i * 16, i * 16 + 16);\n        if (!d.alive) { m.set(HIDDEN_MATRIX); continue; }\n        const n = d.body.node;\n        mat4Compose(m, n.position.x, n.position.y, n.position.z, n.rotationQuaternion.x, n.rotationQuaternion.y, n.rotationQuaternion.z, n.rotationQuaternion.w, 1, 1, 1);\n    }\n    setThinInstances(debrisMesh, debrisMatrices, DEBRIS_COUNT);\n}\n```\n\nHmm — setThinInstances every frame — does it have dirty tracking? Looking at the code: it sets `_dirtyMin/_dirtyMax`... I need to check what setThinInstances does after initialization — it probably writes to the buffer per frame (writeBuffer). 32+4 instances × 64 bytes — trivial.\n\nActually wait — is there a better API? `setThinInstanceMatrix(mesh, index, matrix)` for per-index updates. But setting them all every frame is fine (small).\n\nCamera update:\n```ts\nfunction updateCamera(): void {\n    const p = player.getPosition();\n    const eyeY = p.y + 1.5;\n    cam.position.set(p.x, eyeY, p.z);\n    const fwd = forwardDir();\n    cam.target.set(p.x + fwd.x * 10, eyeY + fwd.y * 10, p.z + fwd.z * 10);\n}\n```\n\nforwardDir:\n```ts\nfunction forwardDir(): Vec3 {\n    const cp = Math.cos(pitch);\n    return { x: Math.sin(yaw) * cp, y: Math.sin(pitch), z: Math.cos(yaw) * cp };\n}\n```\n\nYaw/pitch from input (in onBeforeRender or the mousemove handler?):\n- Mouse look is applied in the mousemove handler (accumulate into dx/dy, apply in the render frame to keep it frame-consistent):\n```ts\nconst delta = input.consumeLookDelta();\nyaw -= delta.dx * MOUSE_SENS;\npitch -= delta.dy * MOUSE_SENS;\npitch = Math.max(-1.5, Math.min(1.5, pitch));\n```\nMOUSE_SENS ~ 0.002.\n\nFiring: input.onFire(() => tryFire()) — but tryFire uses game.time (physics time) for rate limiting. The fire event fires on mousedown (DOM). That's fine.\n\nPointer lock: the user must click the canvas to lock the pointer. Add a hint: \"click to play\" text — put it in the HUD layer? The crosshair is always visible. Keep it simple: the HUD shows the status.\n\nAlso, the scene's canvas.dataset.ready/error pattern (from scene40) for later Playwright scripting.\n\nInit-time measurement: canvas.dataset.initMs.\n\nNow — bot velocity state: `bot.vel = {x:0,y:0,z:0}`.\n\nPlayer's initial yaw: facing the arena center: spawn (0, z=-40) → yaw = atan2(0, 40) = 0? forward = (sin(0)*cp, 0, cos(0)*cp) = (0,0,1) → +Z direction → toward the center (0,0). Good: yaw=0.\n\nEdge case: `createNavCrowd(nav, maxAgents, maxAgentRadius)` — maxAgentRadius 0.5.\n\n`addAgent(crowd, position, params)` — params from scene170: { radius: 0.5, height: 1.8, maxAcceleration: 20, maxSpeed: 8, collisionQueryRange: 4, pathOptimizationRange: 0, separationWeight: 1 }.\n\nHmm — maxSpeed 8 m/s for the bot. Player's MAX_SPEED is ~10. The bot is slightly slower — the player can escape. Good.\n\nNow let me verify the remaining signatures:\n1. mat4Compose\n2. raycast(nav) semantics\n3. TextRendererOptions (clear)\n4. setPhysicsBodyMass export\n5. nav's `raycast` export in the index barrel\n6. createBox(engine, size)\n\nThen write the files.\n","seq":54753,"t":2424275,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":85248,"draft_n":14063,"draft_n_accepted":5661,"predicted_ms":165170.0,"predicted_n":7670,"prompt_ms":830.0,"prompt_n":202},"to_request":51113,"usage":{"cached_tokens":85248,"completion_tokens":7670,"prompt_tokens":85450}}
