{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Next up is the main scene: scene252.ts. This is the big one. Let's write it carefully.\n\nImportant details to nail down:\n- Four-phase scaffold: createEngine → build → registerScene → startEngine.\n- scene.fixedDeltaMs = 1000/60.\n- Havok world with gravity.\n- Terrain + material.\n- Player controller + Quake-style movement.\n- Nav plugin + navmesh + crowd + 4 bots.\n- Thin instances for bots and debris.\n- HUD via TextRenderer (clear: false).\n- Audio.\n- canvas.dataset.ready / error / initMs.\n\nType imports: Vec3, Mat4, PhysicsWorld, NavigationPlugin, PhysicsBody, PhysicsCharacterController, EngineContext, SceneContext, TextData, TextLayer, TextRenderer, ObstacleHandle (not needed here).\n\nNow let's write the code. Things to watch out for:\n- No module-level side effects (no top-level `new Map` etc.). Constants are fine.\n- All promises: awaited or voided.\n- `import type` for type-only imports.\n- 4-space indentation, 180 width.\n\nRay-capsule test:\n\n```ts\n/** Ray vs vertical capsule (axis Y, center at `center`, half-height hh, radius r). Returns distance along ray or null. */\nfunction rayVsCapsule(o: Vec3, d: Vec3, center: Vec3, hh: number, r: number): number | null {\n    // Segment A..B along the capsule axis\n    const ax = center.x, ay = center.y - hh, az = center.z;\n    const bx = center.x, by = center.y + hh, bz = center.z;\n    const wx = o.x - ax, wy = o.y - ay, wz = o.z - az;\n    const a = d.x * d.x + d.y * d.y + d.z * d.z;\n    const b = d.x * (bx - ax) + d.y * (by - ay) + d.z * (bz - az);\n    const c = d.x * wx + d.y * wy + d.z * wz;\n    const dd = (bx - ax) * (bx - ax) + (by - ay) * (by - ay) + (bz - az) * (bz - az);\n    const e = (bx - ax) * wx + (by - ay) * wy + (bz - az) * wz;\n    const det = a * dd - b * b;\n    if (det < 1e-8) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    const t = (b * e - c * dd) / det;\n    const u = (a * e - b * c) / det;\n    if (t < 0 || u < 0 || u > 1) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    const px = o.x + d.x * t - (ax + (bx - ax) * u);\n    const py = o.y + d.y * t - (ay + (by - ay) * u);\n    const pz = o.z + d.z * t - (az + (bz - az) * u);\n    if (px * px + py * py + pz * pz > r * r) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    return Math.sqrt(t) * 100; // d is normalized; ray length 100 → distance = t * 100\n}\n```\n\nWait — if d is normalized, then |d| = 1, and the ray endpoint is o + d*100. t is in [0,1] → distance = t * 100. So `return t * RAY_RANGE`.\n\nd is normalized — a = 1. OK.\n\nBot structure and game state — all inside main().\n\nOne thing: `player.getPosition()` returns a Vec3 owned by the controller — read it but don't retain it.\n\nPhysics step and render frame:\n- onPhysicsAfterStep: player/bot/debris logic (deterministic, 60Hz).\n- onBeforeRender: camera, matrices, HUD (visual only).\n\nDebris expiration: inside the physics step.\n\nGame time: `let gameTime = 0;` inside main, incremented in the physics step.\n\nHUD: TextRenderer. One concern — `createTextRenderer(engine, ...)` — engine is the SurfaceContext. registerTextRenderer(tr) registers on the engine's surface. Registration order: registerScene first (the scene context is pushed first), then the text renderer.\n\nHmm wait — does registerScene push the scene onto surface._renderingContexts? Let me verify quickly... registerScene at scene-core.ts:400. I'll check while writing. Actually, the engine.ts:512 loop iterates surface._renderingContexts — both the scene and the text renderer are RenderingContexts registered on the surface. registerScene probably calls registerRenderingContext(scene.surface, scene). Since the engine is the primary surface, scene.surface === engine. So the order: registerScene → scene is pushed; registerTextRenderer → tr is pushed. Render order: scene, then text.\n\nBut wait — does the engine's render loop run all contexts per frame? Let me check around engine.ts:512 while writing. Actually, let me just check quickly.\n","seq":56011,"t":2506409,"text":"\n\nQuick verification that the engine render loop runs all registered contexts per frame (needed for the HUD overlay), then writing scene252.ts:\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":94208,"draft_n":2660,"draft_n_accepted":924,"predicted_ms":34910.0,"predicted_n":1304,"prompt_ms":3430.0,"prompt_n":2089},"to_request":55253,"usage":{"cached_tokens":94208,"completion_tokens":1304,"prompt_tokens":96297}}
