{"kind":"delta","request":57027,"seq":57315,"t":2641019,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\", \"new_string\": \"main().catch((err) => {\\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\\\"renderCanvas\\\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\\n    if (canvas) {\\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\\n    }\\n    console.error(err);\\n});\", \"old_string\": \"// Lazily-created transform node for debris bodies (keeps the debris ring buffer\\n// allocation-free; the node is only a physics anchor, never added to the scene).\\nlet debrisNodePool: unknown[] = [];\\nfunction createTransformNodeLazy(): { position: { x: number; y: number; z: number }; rotationQuaternion: { x: number; y: number; z: number; w: number } } {\\n    // Imported here to avoid a top-level import cycle risk; in practice createTransformNode\\n    // is already imported above — this indirection just defers allocation.\\n    return null as never;\\n}\\n\\nmain().catch((err) => {\\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\\\"renderCanvas\\\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\\n    if (canvas) {\\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\\n    }\\n    console.error(err);\\n});\"}","id":"call_1879f0f8f72a4c92aae4a187","index":2,"name":"edit"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"I made a mistake at the end — I wrote a broken `createTransformNodeLazy` placeholder and a module-level `let debrisNodePool: unknown[] = []` (a module-level side effect — actually, `let ... = []` is a module-level allocation, which violates the zero-side-effects rule... well, an empty array literal `[]` — is `new Array()` a side effect? An empty array literal is a value. `let x: unknown[] = []` allocates an array at module evaluation time — this is a module-level allocation, and it's exactly the kind of thing GUIDANCE prohibits (module-level `new Map()`/`new Set()`/`new WeakMap()` are listed; `[]` is a typed array constant... no wait, `[]` is a regular array). Typed array constants are safe, but regular arrays are not. And besides, that whole lazy node thing is bogus — I should just use `createTransformNode` directly, which is already imported... no wait, I didn't import createTransformNode in scene252.ts!\n\nLet me fix this: import `createTransformNode` from babylon-lite and replace the broken lazy function with a direct call.\n","seq":57316,"t":2641019,"text":"\n\nI left a broken placeholder at the bottom of scene252.ts — fixing it with a direct `createTransformNode` import:\n\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":104448,"draft_n":1309,"draft_n_accepted":721,"predicted_ms":17600.0,"predicted_n":908,"prompt_ms":2370.0,"prompt_n":579},"to_request":57027,"usage":{"cached_tokens":104448,"completion_tokens":908,"prompt_tokens":105027}}
