{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Start the dev server in the background. Wait for it to come up, then check whether the scene page loads. Write a small Playwright smoke script that does the following:\n1. Open http://localhost:5182/lite/scene252.html\n2. Wait for canvas[data-ready] or canvas[data-error] (timeout ~30 seconds — wasm loading + navmesh construction + 1024 static bodies can take a while)\n3. Print the dataset values and console errors\n\nPer the gotcha, use `pnpm exec playwright`. Write the script to /tmp or the working directory... the session's working directory is empty, and the repo is at ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite. Put the temporary script in /tmp so it doesn't pollute the repo (and remember to clean it up later — actually, files in /tmp are fine and don't need to be deleted from the repo).\n\nScript (plain node + playwright):\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require(\"playwright\");\n(async () => {\n    const browser = await chromium.launch();\n    const page = await browser.newPage();\n    const errors = [];\n    page.on(\"console\", (msg) => { if (msg.type() === \"error\") errors.push(msg.text()); });\n    page.on(\"pageerror\", (err) => errors.push(String(err)));\n    await page.goto(\"http://localhost:5182/lite/scene252.html\", { waitUntil: \"load\" });\n    const result = await page.waitForFunction(\n        () => {\n            const c = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\");\n            return c && (c.dataset.ready || c.dataset.error) ? { ready: c.dataset.ready, error: c.dataset.error, initMs: c.dataset.initMs } : null;\n        },\n        null,\n        { timeout: 60000 }\n    ).catch(() => null);\n    console.log(JSON.stringify({ result, errors: errors.slice(0, 10) }, null, 2));\n    await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\nWhere to run it from? `pnpm exec playwright` is for the CLI. For scripts, I need node with playwright resolvable. The repo has playwright in its devDependencies (pnpm-managed). If I run `node /tmp/smoke.js` from the repo directory, require(\"playwright\") gets resolved via the repo's node_modules. Let me confirm that playwright is in the repo's node_modules.\n","seq":61585,"t":2918854,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":112640,"draft_n":1260,"draft_n_accepted":508,"predicted_ms":17670.0,"predicted_n":688,"prompt_ms":1650.0,"prompt_n":493},"to_request":61237,"usage":{"cached_tokens":112640,"completion_tokens":688,"prompt_tokens":113133}}
