{"kind":"delta","request":63755,"seq":65258,"t":3182244,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"content\": \"Operational gotchas this session (sandbox + tooling): (1) `pnpm dev:lab` EPERMs (tsx IPC pipe in system temp) — use `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite` from lab/ instead; (2) sandbox blocks /tmp and /var/folders T — scratch files go in-repo (delete before commit), node needs TMPDIR in-repo; (3) Playwright default launch fails (cached browser build 1243 vs playwright 1.58.2 expecting 1208) — launch `channel: \\\"chrome\\\"` + `--enable-unsafe-webgpu` (repo idiom); (4) computeNormals is number[]-based and createFlatGroundData/computeNormals need subpath imports (babylon-lite/mesh/*.js), not the barrel.\", \"field\": \"gotcha\"}","id":"call_be86e39b5d5c4e11ad52368a","index":0,"name":"update_record"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to reflect on the step I just completed — specifically, anything that behaved differently than expected, errors and their actual causes, or limitations I discovered. They want it listed in the following format:\n\nGOTCHA: <what is true> — IMPLICATION: <what needs to be done differently>\n\nOnly discoveries from this step. Let me look back at what happened in this session (the \"step\" here — probably referring to the whole thing since compaction, i.e., the build phase so far).\n\nLet me enumerate the discoveries from this session (post-compaction, build phase):\n\n1. **`computeNormals` takes plain `number[]`, not typed arrays** — I expected it to take Float32Array/Uint32Array (like the interview note suggested: \"computeNormals(positions, indices) is a pure CPU function\"). Actually it's `computeNormals(positions: number[], indices: number[]): number[]`. Implication: wrap with Array.from() and convert the result back to Float32Array.\n\n2. **`createFlatGroundData` and `computeNormals` are not exported from the `babylon-lite` barrel** — I expected them to be at the top level. They need subpath imports: `babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js`, `babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js`. The lab idiom for non-barrel exports is subpath imports (scene114, scene17, etc.).\n\n3. **The `dev:lab` script fails under the sandbox** — `pnpm dev:lab` runs `tsx scripts/build-bundle-scenes.ts` first, and tsx creates an IPC pipe under /var/folders/.../T → EPERM under the sandbox. Implication: run `pnpm exec vite` directly inside `lab/` with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182` to skip the bundle build step. The bundle build isn't needed for a dev playtest.\n\n4. **The sandbox blocks writes to /tmp and the system temp directory** — Playwright's `chromium.launch()` tries to mkdtemp under /var/folders/.../T → EPERM. Implication: set TMPDIR to a directory inside the repo for node; even `tmpDir` in launch options wasn't enough (Playwright's artifact directory still used the system temp dir).\n\n5. **The cached Playwright browser build doesn't match the repo's playwright version** — `~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright` has chromium-1243, but the repo's playwright 1.58.2 expects build 1208. So `chromium.launch()` with the default (bundled) browser fails with \"Executable doesn't exist\". Implication: use `channel: \"chrome\"` (system Chrome) — this is the repo's own idiom (playwright.config.ts uses `channel: \"chrome\"`).\n\n6. **WebGPU requires `--enable-unsafe-webgpu`** — the repo's playwright launch args include this. Headless smoke tests need it too.\n\n7. **`resizeMeshGeometry` exists** — this is a discovery, but it's a positive discovery (better than expected), not a gotcha. The plan said \"rebuild via uploadMeshToGPU\", but actually there's a purpose-built `resizeMeshGeometry` that handles buffer disposal timing and renderable version bumping. This is \"the API behaves differently than expected\" — the plan's approach (raw uploadMeshToGPU + manual _gpu swap) would have missed the _renderableVersion bump needed to re-record cached draw bundles. Implication: use resizeMeshGeometry.\n\n8. **FreeCamera's world matrix is derived from position+target, not yaw/pitch** — `_yaw`/`_pitch` are only used by attachFreeControl. I had planned to set cam._yaw/_pitch directly; actually I need to set position + target every frame.\n\n9. **NavCrowd has no agent reposition API** — the plan assumed NavCrowd agents would track bots; actually there's no way to snap an agent to a bot's position, so I need drift mitigation (re-target when they're more than 4u apart).\n\n10. **The controller's friction is exposed as public fields** (staticFriction/dynamicFriction) — this resolved an open question; positive discovery.\n\n11. **TextRenderer compositing**: registerTextRenderer registers an additional rendering context on the same surface; rendering order = registration order; `clear: false` is designed exactly for HUD overlays (\"set to false for HUD overlays so the text pass preserves existing scene color\"). This is confirmation, mostly as expected.\n\n12. **physicsRaycast needs at least one physics step before querying** (broadphase) — from the doc comment. Minor.\n\n13. **`applyPhysicsImpulse` is actually `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse`** — noted as an old name in the audit note. I used setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity instead.\n\n14. **tsc typecheck runs `tsc -p lab/tsconfig.typecheck.json`** — catches unused type imports (TS6196) — so `import type` must be exactly what's used.\n\n15. **`pnpm run lint` = eslint + 3 tsc projects** — full typecheck is part of lint.\n\nNow, the question says \"in the step you just completed\" — singular. The most recent step is… I was in the middle of setting up the Playwright smoke test. But I think the intent is: discoveries from this session/step (the build work I've done so far). Given the format and the working set of existing GOTCHA entries (the interview-t2-2 gotcha format), they want me to record discoveries in that style.\n\nI should list the real discoveries from this build step. Let me be careful to only include genuine discoveries (things that differed from expectations or blocked me), not just \"facts I learned\".\n\nMain ones:\n1. computeNormals signature (number[] not typed arrays) — actual error (TS2345).\n2. createFlatGroundData/computeNormals not in the barrel — actual error (TS2305); subpath import idiom.\n3. `pnpm dev:lab` fails under the sandbox (tsx IPC pipe EPERM) — actual error; workaround is direct vite.\n4. Playwright bundled browser build mismatch (1208 vs cached 1243) — actual error; use channel: \"chrome\".\n5. Sandbox blocks /tmp and system temp — actual error; TMPDIR inside the repo.\n6. resizeMeshGeometry is the proper seam (plan said uploadMeshToGPU) — plan correction.\n7. FreeCamera matrix from position+target, not _yaw/_pitch — API behaves differently than the plan assumed.\n8. NavCrowd has no agent reposition — limitation discovered.\n\nThe user said \"only discoveries from this step.\" I'll interpret \"this step\" as the work I've done since compaction (the build phase up to now). I'll list each on its own line in the exact format.\n\nI'll also record these with update_record so they persist. Actually — the user asked for a reply in the listing format. Should I also call update_record? The system prompt says update_record is \"offered, never required.\" Given the working set format, recording these gotchas is valuable. But the user explicitly asked to \"list each on its own line\" — they want a text reply. I'll reply with the list, and also record the most important ones with update_record so they persist. Actually, the instruction format \"GOTCHA: ... — IMPLICATION: ...\" matches the interview-t2-2 working set entry style, so they probably want me to both reply and record. Let me do both: update_record with a consolidated gotcha entry, and reply with the list.\n\nHmm, but over-recording is a waste of turns. The user explicitly asked for a list — I'll provide the list as the reply. Recording one consolidated entry seems reasonable and useful for the next step (playtest). Let me do a single update_record capturing the operational gotchas (dev server + playwright under the sandbox), since those will affect the rest of the session, and include the API discoveries in the reply.\n\nActually, let me just answer concisely with the list. Recording: I'll add one entry for the sandbox/tooling gotchas (needed for the remaining playtest step) — that's the one that will matter going forward. And the API-level discoveries are already in the S1 evidence entry I recorded earlier… wait, no — that evidence entry was discarded (\"dropped: the groundedness gate kept nothing of it\"). Hmm, interesting — my big S1 evidence record was dropped by the groundedness gate. So those findings aren't recorded. The user's current question is specifically about behavioral differences/gotchas, not all the API facts.\n\nLet me reply with the list, and record the operational gotchas (which will matter for the remaining steps) with update_record. I'll keep the reply concise.\n","seq":65259,"t":3182245,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: `computeNormals` takes/returns plain `number[]`, not typed arrays (mesh/compute-normals.ts) — IMPLICATION: wrap with `Array.from(...)` and convert the result back to `Float32Array` at both call sites in fps-terrain.ts.\nGOTCHA: `createFlatGroundData` and `computeNormals` are NOT in the `babylon-lite` barrel — IMPLICATION: lab scenes import them via subpath (`babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js`, `babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js`), the same idiom scene114/scene17 use.\nGOTCHA: `pnpm dev:lab` fails in this sandbox — the script's `tsx scripts/build-bundle-scenes.ts` step EPERMs on an IPC pipe in the system temp dir — IMPLICATION: start the lab with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite` from `lab/` directly (skips the bundle build, which a dev playtest doesn't need).\nGOTCHA: the sandbox blocks writes to `/tmp` and the system temp dir (`/var/folders/.../T`) — IMPLICATION: scratch files must live inside the repo (delete before commit per GUIDANCE 0b), and node/Playwright need `TMPDIR` pointed at a repo-local dir.\nGOTCHA: the cached Playwright browsers (build 1243) don't match the repo's playwright 1.58.2 (expects 1208), so default `chromium.launch()` fails with \"Executable doesn't exist\" — IMPLICATION: launch with `channel: \"chrome\"` (system Chrome), which is the repo's own idiom (playwright.config.ts), plus `--enable-unsafe-webgpu` for WebGPU.\nGOTCHA: the plan's \"uploadMeshToGPU rebuild\" seam is actually `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) — raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap would skip the `_renderableVersion` bump that cached draw bundles key off — IMPLICATION: tile destruction must call `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs)`; it also refreshes `_cpuIndices` for the next surgery.\nGOTCHA: FreeCamera's world matrix is built from `position` + `target` (mat4LookAtLH), not from `_yaw`/`_pitch` (those are only used by `attachFreeControl`) — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera is driven by setting `cam.position` and `cam.target` each frame from eye + (yaw, pitch); don't poke `_yaw`/`_pitch`.\nGOTCHA: NavCrowd has no agent reposition API (only `agentGoto` destinations) — IMPLICATION: nav agents drift from collision-driven controllers; mitigate by re-targeting the agent to the bot's position when they separate >4u, else bots oscillate.\nGOTCHA: `pnpm run lint` = eslint + three `tsc --noEmit` projects (packages, lab, tests) and flags unused type imports (TS6196) — IMPLICATION: `import type` lists must be pruned to exactly what's used; lint is the full typecheck gate, no separate step needed.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":120320,"draft_n":6223,"draft_n_accepted":1968,"predicted_ms":100090.0,"predicted_n":2857,"prompt_ms":910.0,"prompt_n":303},"to_request":63755,"usage":{"cached_tokens":120320,"completion_tokens":2857,"prompt_tokens":120623}}
