{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Static review — potential issues:\n\n1. **Bot `checkSupport`/grounding**: bots rely on `bot.velY` + gravity + `moveWithCollisions`. When the bot is grounded, `velY` accumulates `-g*dt` each step, and `moveWithCollisions` pushes downward, and the capsule is held up by the floor — but `velY` keeps going negative every step (it's never reset on grounding). So `velY` becomes a large negative value, and the downward displacement per step grows. The controller pushes it into the floor every step; Havok resolves the penetration; net effect: the bot stays on the floor (the controller clamps it). But `velY` could drift to, say, -20, giving a downward displacement of -0.33u per step — the controller absorbs it (can't go through the floor). In practice, character controllers handle this: the move is clamped at the floor. The bot stays on the floor. No issue, but a bit wasteful. Bigger risk: bot falling through a destroyed tile — no problem, respawns.\n\n   Actually, wait — there's a subtle issue: the player's `playerVel.y` is never zeroed on grounding either! `playerVel.y -= GRAVITY*dt` every step; on grounding, `moveWithCollisions` clamps the downward motion, but `playerVel.y` keeps decreasing: -9.8*0.0167 = -0.163/step → after 1 second of standing still, vel.y = -9.8. Then when the player walks off a ledge, they start falling at -9.8 immediately (correct — that's free fall from rest... no wait, free fall from rest starts at 0 and accelerates). Walking off a ledge with vel.y = -9.8 (the accumulated value) means the fall starts at terminal-ish velocity. Also, the jump sets vel.y = +7 — good.\n\n   This is the classic \"velocity doesn't reset on grounding\" bug. The proper fix: when grounded (support state is SUPPORTED/SLIDING), set `playerVel.y = Math.min(playerVel.y, 0)` and snap to 0? In Quake, vel.z = 0 on ground contact. Standard: after moveWithCollisions, if grounded → vel.y = 0 (or leave it slightly negative). Let's add: after the move, if grounded, `playerVel.y = 0`. Hmm, but `grounded` was computed before the move (checkSupport at the current position). Close enough: after moveWithCollisions, re-check? Simpler: if support was grounded, then vel.y = 0 after the move. Slight edge case: running off a ledge — the previous step's support was grounded, so vel.y gets zeroed — no problem (falls from rest).\n\n   Actually, even simpler and more robust: clamp `playerVel.y` to a minimum of -30 (terminal velocity), and zero it when grounded. Same for the bot's velY.\n\n2. **`checkSupport` cost**: one extra support cast per step per player+bot — acceptable.\n\n3. **Bot LOS raycast with nav's `raycast`** — nav raycast is 2D (x/z plane). The bot's bp.y is the capsule's center; the player's pp2.y is... the ray is projected onto the navmesh plane; destroyed tiles add box obstacles — the ray is blocked when passing through a hole. Good enough.\n\n4. **`findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, bp, 20)`** — bp includes the bot's y; the API probably projects to the navmesh. OK.\n\n5. **Bot firing uses the player's `dist` computed before the retarget block — no problem.\n\n6. **`damagePlayer` invulnerability — after respawn, invulnerable for 3 seconds. Good.\n\n7. **HUD `updateDefaultTextData(hudData, hudText)` — need to confirm this exists and works on `TextData` created by `createDefaultTextData`. I confirmed the `updateDefaultTextData` export in the barrel (grep listed it at index.ts:474). Its semantics — probably re-renders the text to the texture and updates `data`. Since the layer references the same TextData object, the renderer should pick up the updated texture. Reasonable.\n\n8. **`canvas.width/2`** for the crosshair — the canvas's default width is 300×150 (HTML default!) — the canvas has no explicit size in the HTML! The canvas in scene252.html has no width/height attributes. `createEngine(canvas)` — does the engine resize the canvas to the window? If the engine resizes the canvas, `canvas.width` at the time of HUD creation (before startEngine) is 300 → the crosshair ends up at (142, 65) instead of the center. Need to check how scene180 positions its text layer... scene180 probably uses the same pattern. Let's check whether the engine resizes the canvas and whether scene180's crosshair-style positioning exists.\n\n   In fact, in scene180, the text layer's positionPx is computed from the canvas size — let's see how. If the engine sets the canvas size at registerScene/startEngine time, then HUD creation happens before that. Better: use `window.innerWidth/2`. Let's check how the engine handles canvas sizing.\n\n9. **`createTextRenderer(engine, { layers, clear: false })`** — need to re-check the option name `clear` in TextRendererOptions (the grep at text-renderer.ts:289 shows `opts.clear` — already confirmed in a previous turn: `clear?: boolean` in options; default is true). ✓ (confirmed earlier: `clear: opts.clear ?? true`).\n\n10. **Physics raycast before the first step** — firing only happens in the game loop after startup; the world has already stepped. ✓\n\n11. **`rayVsCapsule`'s `t * RAY_RANGE`** — t is normalized to [0,1] along a direction scaled by RAY_RANGE... wait: `to = eye + fwd * RAY_RANGE`, so the ray is eye→to. In rayVsCapsule I pass `d = fwd` (unit vector) and use `t` as a parameter along the unit direction — t is the actual distance (because d is unit). And I multiply by RAY_RANGE?? Bug: `return t * RAY_RANGE` — t is already a distance (since d is unit). Multiplying by RAY_RANGE makes the distance 100× too large → bot hits always \"behind\" terrain hits → bots are never hit. This is a real bug!\n\n    Let me double-check: `const to: Vec3 = { x: eye.x + fwd.x * RAY_RANGE, ... }` — yes, `to` is scaled. But `rayVsCapsule(from, fwd, ...)` uses the unit `fwd` and returns `t` (distance along the unit direction) * RAY_RANGE. Wrong. Fix: return `t` (and ensure t ≤ RAY_RANGE).\n\n12. **Player self-hit guard** — decided in an open item; currently no guard. If the default shape mask is ~0 (all bits), then a raycast with `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER` might hit the player's capsule (if the player's shape's collide mask includes bit 0). Then the shot would destroy the tile under the player's feet. Risk: annoying but not fatal. The plan was to add a guard: if terr.hitDistance < 2, ignore the terrain hit. Add it: `if (terr.hasHit && terr.hitDistance > 2 && ...)`.\n\n13. **`player.setPosition` on respawn** — exists (confirmed in a previous grep: character-controller.ts has setPosition). ✓\n\n14. **Debris node \"fpsDebris\" ×32** — no problem.\n\n15. **Bot's `respawnBot` uses `findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, {x:0,y:0,z:0}, ARENA_SIZE/3)`** — a point on the navmesh; y is from the navmesh — the bot is placed at p.y + CAP_HEIGHT/2. If p.y is the ground height — OK.\n\n16. **`input.consumeLookDelta()`** — returns accumulated {dx, dy}; yaw -= dx*sens: moving the mouse right (dx>0) → yaw decreases. Forward = (sin yaw, 0, cos yaw): yaw=0 → +z. Moving the mouse right should turn the view right (clockwise from above = negative rotation around +Y in a left-handed system...?). Sign convention — hard to verify statically; the user will tell me during playtest if it's inverted. The camera target = eye + fwd*10 ensures the view follows. If it's inverted, a one-line fix (flip the sign).\n\n17. **`cam.position.set(...)`** — `position` is an ObservableVec3 with .set ✓.\n\n18. **`mat4Compose` for debris uses the node's quaternion** — the node's rotation is updated by the physics world every step (the world syncs body→node for DYNAMIC). ✓\n\n19. **`setThinInstances` re-upload per frame** — a 64×16 float upload per frame — trivial.\n\n20. **HUD text font**: `/fonts/Inter.ttf` — check that it exists in lab/public/fonts (scene180 uses it). The previous turn confirmed the font is loaded from lab/public/fonts — Inter.ttf exists (scene180 pattern). ✓\n\n21. **`createNavMesh(nav, [terrain.mesh], params)`** — NavMeshParameters fields: cs, ch, walkableSlopeAngle, walkableHeight, walkableClimb, walkableRadius, maxEdgeLen, maxSimplificationError, minRegionArea, mergeRegionArea, maxVertsPerPoly, detailSampleDist, detailSampleMaxError — copied from scene170. ✓ (scene170 was the source; trust it.)\n\n22. **Player spawn**: `terrainHeightAt(terrain, 0, -40) + 2` — capsule center; no problem.\n\n23. **`scene.fixedDeltaMs`** — is this a real field on SceneContext? I confirmed it exists earlier (scene40 pattern). ✓\n\n24. **`onBeforeRender` import** — from the barrel; scene-core.ts:236 ✓.\n\n25. **`registerTextRenderer(tr)`** — registers a context on the engine surface; scene is registered first → drawn second. ✓\n\n26. **Engine resize and crosshair position** — the main open item (#8). Let's check how the engine sizes the canvas and how scene180 positions text. If the engine resizes the canvas at registerScene time, `canvas.width` at HUD creation time (before registerScene) is the HTML default 300×150 → the crosshair ends up at (142, 65) instead of the center. Fix: position the crosshair after startEngine, or use window.innerWidth/innerHeight. Simplest: compute it after `await startEngine(engine)` — but the layers are already created... TextLayer's positionPx — is it mutable? Check the TextLayer interface — `positionPx` is probably a plain field; the renderer reads it every frame? If so, setting it after start is enough.\n\nLet's check: (a) how the engine sizes the canvas, (b) whether TextLayer.positionPx is mutable/read per frame, (c) scene180's crosshair-like usage (if any).\n","seq":67503,"t":3376990,"text":"\n\nStatic review found 2 actual bugs and 1 risk. First, I'll verify the details of the canvas size specification (the crosshair uses `canvas.width` before the engine resizes it):\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":0,"draft_n":7483,"draft_n_accepted":1666,"predicted_ms":52160.0,"predicted_n":2735,"prompt_ms":60760.0,"prompt_n":60417},"to_request":66144,"usage":{"cached_tokens":0,"completion_tokens":2735,"prompt_tokens":60417}}
