{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me think about what was revealed in the most recent step (the step where I read havok-queries.ts and verified the raycast filter semantics).\n\nIn the step just completed, I read `physicsRaycast` in havok-queries.ts. What I learned:\n\n1. The default values for the raycast query's `membership`/`collideWith` are `~0` (all bits).\n2. The query passes `[membership, collideWith]` to Havok's `HP_World_CastRayWithCollector`.\n\nThe key question was: does a raycast with `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER` hit the player's capsule?\n\nIn Havok, the raycast filter works as follows: the query has a filter `[membership, collideWith]`. A shape is hit if the query's membership matches the shape's collide mask AND the query's collideWith matches the shape's membership mask. Specifically, Havok's filter semantics: a collision occurs when `(query.membership & shape.collideMask) != 0 && (query.collideMask & shape.membership) != 0`.\n\nIn other words, for a raycast with `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER` (bit 0), `collideWith: ~0`:\n- Against a terrain tile shape (membership=TERRAIN_LAYER=bit 0, collideMask=~0 default):\n  - query.membership (bit 0) & shape.collideMask (~0) = bit 0 ≠ 0 ✓\n  - query.collideWith (~0) & shape.membership (bit 0) = bit 0 ≠ 0 ✓\n  - → Hit. Good.\n- Against the player's capsule (membership=~0 default, collideMask=~0 default):\n  - query.membership (bit 0) & shape.collideMask (~0) = bit 0 ≠ 0 ✓\n  - query.collideWith (~0) & shape.membership (~0) = all bits ≠ 0 ✓\n  - → Hit!\n\nSo the raycast with `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER` will also hit the player's own capsule (because the player's default collideMask is ~0, which includes bit 0). This confirms the self-hit concern: shots that pass near the player's own capsule will register a terrain-layer hit against the player's body.\n\nWait, but the ray originates from the eye (inside/above the player's capsule). The first hit along the ray would be the player's own capsule (since the ray starts inside or very close to it). So `terr.hasHit` would be true, and `terr.hitDistance` would be very small (the distance to the player's own capsule surface). That's exactly why I added the `terr.hitDistance > 2` guard — to ignore self-hits near the muzzle.\n\nSo the constraint/learning that has surfaced: the physics raycast with a membership filter will also hit the player's own character controller capsule, because Havok's default shape filter is `~0` (all bits) for both membership and collide, and Lite does not expose a way to set the controller's private `_shape` filter mask. Therefore, the self-hit guard (`terr.hitDistance > 2`) is not optional but essential.\n\nNow, is this a \"constraint that the rest of the project must comply with\"? It's more of a learning/gotcha than a project-wide constraint. But it does impose a requirement: any future raycast-based shooting/detection logic must account for self-hits, because the character controller's capsule cannot be excluded from raycasts via the filter mask (the shape is private, and its default filter is ~0).\n\nLet me think about whether there's a real \"constraint\" here in the sense the question is asking — \"a requirement, an environmental limitation, a compatibility rule, something that must never be done.\"\n\nWhat I've learned in this step:\n- Havok's default shape filter membership/collide mask is `~0` (all bits), and `physicsRaycast`'s default query mask is also `~0`.\n- The character controller's capsule shape is private (`_shape`), so its filter mask cannot be changed via the public API.\n- Therefore, a `physicsRaycast` with `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER` will also hit the player's own capsule (and the bots' capsules) — because their default collideMask (~0) includes TERRAIN_LAYER.\n\nThis is a genuine constraint/gotcha that the rest of the project (shooting logic, any raycast-based detection) must comply with: raycasts cannot cleanly separate \"terrain only\" from \"character controllers\" via filter masks alone, because controller shapes have the default all-bits filter and are not publicly addressable. Self-hit guards or manual capsule tests (like the one I did for bots) are necessary.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My design already handles this: I use `physicsRaycast` with `membership: TERRAIN_LAYER` for terrain, and use a manual `rayVsCapsule` for bots. The self-hit guard (`hitDistance > 2`) handles the case where the player's own capsule registers as a terrain-layer hit.\n\nSo the constraint that has surfaced: raycasts filtered by membership will still hit character controller capsules (default ~0 filter, private shape), so self-hit guards are mandatory for any ray-based hit detection near the player.\n\nLet me formulate this as a constraint. Actually, I should be careful — the question asks for constraints that \"the rest of the project must comply with.\" This is really a gotcha/learning. But it does carry an imperative: do not assume that `physicsRaycast` with a terrain membership filter will exclude the player/bot capsules; you must guard against self-hits.\n\nLet me record this as a constraint line. I think the honest answer is that there is one real constraint/gotcha that has surfaced.\n\nLet me reconsider the exact semantics to make sure I'm stating it correctly. In Havok, the collision filter for a ray query: the query filter is `[membership, collideMask]`. For a shape with filter `[shapeMembership, shapeCollideMask]`, a hit occurs when:\n`(query.membership & shapeCollideMask) != 0 && (query.collideMask & shapeMembership) != 0`\n\nLet me verify against the code: `hkQuery = [[from...], [to...], [membership, collideWith], ...]`. So query membership = query.membership, query collide = query.collideWith.\n\nFor a terrain raycast: query.membership = TERRAIN_LAYER (bit 0), query.collideWith = ~0 (default).\nPlayer capsule shape: shapeMembership = ~0 (default), shapeCollideMask = ~0 (default).\n- query.membership & shapeCollideMask = bit0 & ~0 = bit0 ≠ 0 ✓\n- query.collideWith & shapeMembership = ~0 & ~0 = ~0 ≠ 0 ✓\n→ Hit. Confirmed.\n\nSo yes, the player's own capsule will be hit by a terrain-layer raycast. The self-hit guard is mandatory.\n\nNow, is this \"a constraint that the rest of the project must comply with\"? In the sense that: any future feature that raycasts against the physics world (e.g., bot line-of-sight using physicsRaycast, aiming assist, etc.) must account for the fact that character controllers will be hit because their default filter is ~0 and their shape is private. So the constraint is: you cannot rely on membership/collide filters to exclude character controller capsules from raycasts — a manual exclusion or distance guard is required.\n\nI'll present this as a constraint. I'll also note the confirmed environmental facts (default ~0, private shape) as part of it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should just answer NONE. The question specifically asks about constraints that \"the rest of the project must comply with.\" Let me think about whether this is really project-wide or just something I've already handled.\n\nThe shooting logic is one part of the project. But bots' line-of-sight (bot shooting) currently uses the navmesh `raycast` (2D nav raycast), not physicsRaycast. So physicsRaycast is only used for player→terrain shooting. The self-hit guard has already been added.\n\nBut the learning is still valuable for the rest of the project: if I later add any physicsRaycast-based detection (e.g., bullet tracers, bot physics-based LOS, wall checks), I need to know that character controllers will be hit and cannot be filtered out. That's a genuine constraint on future work.\n\nI'll state it as a constraint. Let me keep it concise and precise.\n\nI think the cleanest way to state it is:\n\nCONSTRAINT: A `physicsRaycast` filtered by `membership` (e.g., TERRAIN_LAYER) will still hit character controller capsules, because Havok/Lite default shape filter masks are `~0` (all bits) and the controller's capsule `_shape` is private (its mask cannot be set via the public API) — so any ray-based hit detection near the player/bots must guard against self-hits (e.g., a minimum-distance guard like `hitDistance > 2`) rather than relying on filter masks to exclude controllers.\n\nThat's the constraint. Let me also double-check: did I confirm in this step that the default is ~0? I confirmed that the raycast query's default is `~0` (from the code `query.membership ?? ~0`). For the shape's default filter, I saw the `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask`/`CollideMask` functions but did not explicitly read the default value of the shape's filter in this step. I inferred ~0 from Havok conventions. So I should be careful not to overclaim that I verified the shape's default is ~0 in this step.\n\nActually, in this step I read the raycast code and confirmed the query's default is `~0`. I did not definitively confirm in this step that the shape's default filter is ~0 (I inferred it). So I should phrase the constraint around what I actually confirmed and flag the inference.\n\nLet me be honest: what I confirmed in this step is that the raycast query's default mask is `~0` and that the filter is passed through to Havok. The consequence (that the player's capsule gets hit) depends on the shape's default filter being ~0, which is Havok's documented default but which I have not re-verified in this step.\n\nI'll state the constraint with appropriate caveats. The core actionable constraint: don't rely on raycast filter masks to exclude character controllers, because the controller's shape is private and the default masks are all-bits — use explicit self-hit guards / manual capsule tests.\n\nLet me write this up. I'll keep it to one line (or a few lines) as a constraint.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check: is there really a constraint here, or am I overthinking? Let me reconsider the framing. The instruction says: \"Did you learn or commit to any constraint that the rest of the project must comply with — a requirement, an environmental limitation, a compatibility rule, something that must never be done? Enumerate each on its own line as CONSTRAINT: <rule>. Only constraints that surfaced in this step.\"\n\nThe self-hit / filter-mask issue surfaced in this step (I was actively investigating it — I had an unresolved item f57025-4 about exactly this). So it's fair to record it. It's a compatibility rule: physics raycasts cannot exclude character controllers via filter masks.\n\nI'll provide that. Let me make it precise and note the part that is confirmed vs. inferred.\n\nI'll answer with the constraint.\n","seq":80870,"t":4057359,"text":"\n\nCONSTRAINT: A `physicsRaycast` filtered by `membership` (e.g. `TERRAIN_LAYER`) will still hit character-controller capsules — the raycast query defaults to `~0` for both masks (confirmed in `havok-queries.ts` this step), the controller's capsule `_shape` is private so its filter mask can't be set via public API, and Havok's default shape filter is all-bits — so any ray-based hit detection near the player/bots must use an explicit self-hit guard (e.g. `hitDistance > 2`) or a manual capsule test, never rely on filter masks to exclude controllers. (The shape-default-`~0` part is inferred from Havok convention, not re-verified in this step.)","timings":{"cache_n":93696,"draft_n":5698,"draft_n_accepted":1831,"predicted_ms":75480.0,"predicted_n":2645,"prompt_ms":810.0,"prompt_n":316},"to_request":79229,"usage":{"cached_tokens":93696,"completion_tokens":2645,"prompt_tokens":94012}}
