{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"So the capsule shape is built with pointA at `y = h*0.5 - r` and pointB at `y = -h*0.5 + r`. So the capsule's axis goes from `y = -h/2 + r` to `y = +h/2 - r` (in local space), and the full capsule (including the hemispherical caps) spans from `y = -h/2` to `y = +h/2`. And `_position` is the center of the capsule (the node is placed at `_position`).\n\nSo `getPosition()` returns the capsule center. The capsule spans from `center.y - h/2` (feet) to `center.y + h/2` (head).\n\nSo for the player:\n- `player.getPosition().y` = capsule center y.\n- Feet at `center.y - 0.9`.\n- Head at `center.y + 0.9`.\n- Eye at `center.y + EYE_HEIGHT` = `center.y + 1.5`.\n\nWait, EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5, but the head is at center + 0.9. So the eye is at center + 1.5, which is 0.6 above the head. That's a bit high (eye should be near the head, ~center + 0.7). But it's fine for a game — the eye is slightly above the capsule. Actually, having the eye above the capsule means the ray origin is outside the capsule, which is good for avoiding self-hits from the origin being inside.\n\nActually, wait — for a first-person view, the eye should be at the head, i.e., near `center + 0.9`. With EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5, the eye is 0.6 above the capsule top. That means the camera is above the player's head, which looks a bit odd but is playable. Let me reconsider — maybe EYE_HEIGHT should be relative to the feet, not the center.\n\nCommon convention: eye height is measured from the feet. A 1.8m tall character has eyes at ~1.5m from the ground. If the feet are at `center.y - 0.9`, then the eye should be at `feet.y + 1.5 = center.y - 0.9 + 1.5 = center.y + 0.6`. So EYE_HEIGHT relative to the center should be 0.6, not 1.5.\n\nCurrently I have `eyePos = player.getPosition().y + EYE_HEIGHT` = `center.y + 1.5`. That puts the eye 1.5 above the center = 0.6 above the head. That's too high.\n\nLet me fix: the eye should be at `center.y + 0.6` (i.e., 1.5 above the feet). So I should define EYE_HEIGHT as the offset from the capsule center, which is `1.5 - 0.9 = 0.6`. Or measure from the feet.\n\nLet me reconsider the constants. CAP_HEIGHT = 1.8, so half = 0.9. Feet at center - 0.9. Eye at 1.5 above feet = center + 0.6.\n\nSo `eyePos().y = player.getPosition().y + (1.5 - CAP_HEIGHT/2)` = `center.y + 0.6`.\n\nLet me define `EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5` as height above the feet, and compute `eyeY = center.y - CAP_HEIGHT/2 + EYE_HEIGHT`. That's cleaner and matches real-world convention.\n\nActually, this also affects the spawn position. The player spawns at `terrainHeightAt + 2`. The capsule center should be at `feet + 0.9` = `terrainHeight + 0.9` (standing on the ground). Spawning at `terrainHeight + 2` puts the center 2 above the ground, so the feet are at `terrainHeight + 1.1` — floating 1.1 above the ground. That's fine (they'll fall to the ground). Actually, it's better to spawn them right at standing height: `terrainHeight + 0.9` (center) so the feet are on the ground. But a small drop is fine and avoids initial penetration. Let me spawn at `terrainHeight + 1.0` (center), feet at `terrainHeight + 0.1`. Close enough, minimal drop.\n\nHmm, but actually the character controller's position — when standing on the ground, what does `getPosition().y` equal? The capsule's feet touch the ground, so center.y = ground.y + 0.9. So spawning the center at `ground.y + 0.9` = standing position. Let me use that.\n\nLet me reconsider the eye height. Let me redefine:\n- `EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5` (above the feet, real-world convention).\n- `eyeY = center.y - CAP_HEIGHT/2 + EYE_HEIGHT` = `center.y - 0.9 + 1.5` = `center.y + 0.6`.\n\nThat's correct. Let me update `eyePos()`.\n\nAlso, the bot's rayVsCapsule uses `bot.cc.getPosition()` as the capsule center, with `halfH = CAP_HEIGHT/2 = 0.9` and `r = CAP_RADIUS = 0.5`. That's correct — the capsule center is `getPosition()`, and the capsule spans center ± 0.9. Good.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider the self-hit guard distance. The eye is at `center + 0.6`. The capsule spans `center - 0.9` to `center + 0.9`. So the eye is 0.3 below the capsule top (center + 0.9). Wait, eye at center + 0.6, capsule top at center + 0.9. So the eye is 0.3 below the top, and inside the vertical extent of the capsule! And the eye's horizontal position is at the center's x/z. So the eye is inside the capsule (0.3 below the top, within the radius 0.5).\n\nUh oh. So the ray origin (eye) is inside the player's capsule. That means a raycast from the eye will immediately hit the player's own capsule (the near intersection). \n\nWait, but the eye is at center + 0.6, and the capsule's cylindrical part spans center - 0.9 + r to center + 0.9 - r = center - 0.4 to center + 0.4 (the cylindrical band), with the caps extending to center ± 0.9. So at y = center + 0.6, we're in the top cap region (above center + 0.4). The top cap is a hemisphere of radius 0.5 centered at (center.x, center + 0.4, center.z). At y = center + 0.6, the cap's cross-section radius is `sqrt(0.5^2 - (0.6-0.4)^2) = sqrt(0.25 - 0.04) = sqrt(0.21) ≈ 0.458`. The eye is at (center.x, center + 0.6, center.z), which is at the cap's axis (x/z = center). So the eye is inside the top cap (distance from cap center = 0.2 < 0.5). So yes, the eye is inside the capsule.\n\nSo a raycast from the eye will hit the player's own capsule at the near surface. This is a problem. Havok raycasts from inside a shape: the behavior depends on whether the ray origin is considered \"inside.\" Typically, a raycast returns the first intersection, which from inside would be the far side of the shape (or the near side, depending on implementation). \n\nActually, this is getting complicated. The cleanest solution: make the eye height such that the eye is above the capsule, OR accept that the ray hits the player's capsule and use the distance guard to skip it.\n\nGiven the player capsule can't be filtered out (private shape, default filter), the distance guard is the way. But if the eye is inside the capsule, the self-hit distance is small (the ray exits the capsule at ~0.5-1 unit). So a guard of `> 2` would skip the self-hit (which is at distance < 1) and allow terrain hits (which are at distance > 2). \n\nBut wait — if the ray hits the player's capsule first (at distance ~0.5), and the terrain is at distance 10, the raycast returns the capsule hit (distance 0.5), not the terrain. Because `physicsRaycast` returns the first (closest) hit. So `terr` would be the capsule hit, and `terr.hitDistance` would be ~0.5, which is `< 2`, so the guard skips it. But then we never hit the terrain behind the player! The raycast returns only the closest hit, which is the player's own capsule.\n\nThis is a real problem. The player's capsule occludes all terrain raycasts. So shooting would never destroy tiles.\n\nI need to fix this. Options:\n1. Move the eye above the capsule so the ray origin is outside. But then the first-person view is from above the head, which is odd but workable. Actually, if the eye is above the capsule, a forward ray won't hit the capsule (it's below), so the raycast would hit the terrain. But a downward ray could still hit the capsule.\n2. Use the raycast's `body` to check if the hit is the player's controller body, and if so, ignore it and... but the raycast only returns the closest hit, so if the capsule is closest, we get the capsule and can't see the terrain behind it.\n\nHmm. The fundamental issue: the player's capsule is in the physics world and the raycast hits it.\n\nWait — is the character controller's body actually in the world's broadphase and hit by raycasts? The controller creates a `PhysicsBody` with `PhysicsMotionType.ANIMATED`. Raycasts hit dynamic/animated bodies. So yes, the player's capsule would be hit.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe I should set the player capsule's filter to exclude the bullet raycast. But the shape is private. \n\nAlternatively — maybe I can get the player's body and set its filter. The controller