s above the capsule (since the capsule is only 0.5 radius and the eye is ~1 unit above the capsule top). So it should hit the tile, not the capsule. \n\nLet me set the eye height so the eye is just above the capsule top. Capsule top at center + 0.9. Set eye at center + 1.0 (0.1 above the top). That's a reasonable first-person height (just above the head).\n\nActually, for first-person, the eye should be at the head, which is the capsule top (center + 0.9). But to avoid the ray origin being inside the capsule, I'll put it slightly above: center + 1.0.\n\nHmm, but if the eye is at center + 1.0 and the capsule top is at center + 0.9, the eye is 0.1 above the top. A ray aimed straight down from the eye would hit the capsule top (at distance 0.1 + a bit). The guard `> 2` skips it. A ray aimed forward-down at a tile 5 units ahead: the ray goes from (eye) to (tile). Does it pass through the capsule? The capsule is a 0.5-radius cylinder below the eye. A ray from 0.1 above the capsule top, aimed at a point 5 units ahead and on the ground (which is ~1.5 units below the eye)... the ray's angle is mostly horizontal with a slight downward tilt. It should pass above the capsule (which is only 0.5 wide and 1.8 tall, but the top is a hemisphere). \n\nActually, this is hard to reason about precisely. Let me just go with the eye slightly above the capsule and the distance guard, and rely on playtesting to tune it.\n\nActually, you know what — let me reconsider the whole approach. The cleanest fix for the self-hit problem is to **not rely on the physics raycast for the player's own capsule**. Instead, I can:\n- Cast the ray, and if the hit body is the player's controller body, ignore it.\n\nBut the raycast only returns the closest hit. If the capsule is closest, I get the capsule and miss the terrain.\n\nUnless... I can cast the ray and get multiple hits. But `physicsRaycast` returns only the first hit.\n\nOK here's another idea: **Set the player's capsule filter to exclude the bullet raycast by using a dedicated bullet layer.** \n\nThe issue is the player capsule has default filter (~0, ~0). If I could set the player capsule's collide mask to exclude the bullet layer, then a bullet raycast with `membership = BULLET_LAYER` wouldn't hit the player capsule. But the shape is private.\n\nWait — let me check: does the character controller expose any way to set the filter? Let me search for filter-related methods on the controller.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me check if I can access the controller's body. The controller is created by `createPhysicsCharacterController`. It returns a `PhysicsCharacterController` instance. The `_body` is private. But maybe I can find it.\n\nActually — here's a cleaner idea. What if the bullet raycast uses `collideWith` to target only the terrain layer, and I set the terrain tiles' membership to `TERRAIN_LAYER`, AND I also set the player capsule's membership... no, I can't.\n\nLet me try yet another approach: **cast the ray from the eye, but if the closest hit is within a small distance (the self-hit), cast again from a point slightly further along the ray to get past the capsule.** That's hacky.\n\nActually, the simplest and most correct solution: **the eye should be positioned so that a forward ray doesn't intersect the capsule, and use the distance guard.** Let me set the eye at the capsule top + a small epsilon, and forward rays will clear the capsule.\n\nLet me reconsider: is the player capsule even a problem for forward rays? The capsule is a vertical cylinder (radius 0.5) centered at the player's x/z, spanning y from center-0.9 to center+0.9. The eye is at (center.x, center.y + eyeOffset, center.z). A forward ray goes in direction (sin(yaw)*cos(pitch), sin(pitch), cos(yaw)*cos(pitch)). For a horizontal forward ray (pitch=0), the ray stays at y = center.y + eyeOffset. If eyeOffset > 0.9 (above the capsule top), the ray is above the capsule and won't hit it. If eyeOffset < 0.9, the ray is at the capsule's height and will hit the capsule (since the ray starts at the capsule's axis).\n\nSo: to avoid self-hits on forward shots, the eye must be above the capsule top: eyeOffset > 0.9. Let me set eyeOffset = 1.0 (just above the top at 0.9).\n\nBut then the first-person view is from 1.0 above the capsule center = 0.1 above the head. That's fine — like looking from just above the head. Actually, that's a bit high for first-person (usually eye is at head height). But it's acceptable and avoids the self-hit problem.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider whether the ray really hits the capsule when the eye is at the capsule's axis height. The ray starts at the eye, which is at the capsule's axis (x/z = center.x/z). The capsule is a cylinder of radius 0.5 around that axis. A ray starting at the axis and going forward — it starts inside the cylinder (at the axis, which is inside the cylinder). So the raycast would immediately be \"inside\" the capsule. The first intersection would be where the ray exits the cylinder, at distance ~0.5 (the radius). So the self-hit distance is ~0.5.\n\nSo even with the eye at the capsule's axis height, forward rays exit the capsule at ~0.5 and then hit the terrain. But the raycast returns the closest hit = the capsule exit at 0.5, not the terrain. So the guard `> 2` skips the capsule hit, but we never get the terrain hit (because the raycast only returns the closest).\n\nSo the raycast approach fundamentally can't see past the player's capsule if the capsule is in the ray's path. The only way is to have the eye above the capsule so the ray's path doesn't intersect the capsule.\n\nTherefore: **the eye must be above the capsule top (eyeOffset > 0.9) for forward rays to clear the capsule.** Let me set eyeOffset = 1.0.\n\nBut wait, there's still the issue of downward rays. If the player aims down (pitch < 0), the ray goes downward and could hit the capsule (which is below the eye). With the eye at center + 1.0 (0.1 above the top at center + 0.9), a downward ray from the eye would hit the capsule top (the hemisphere) at a small distance. The guard `> 2` skips it. But again, the raycast returns the capsule hit, not the terrain beyond.\n\nFor shooting tiles, the player aims roughly horizontally or slightly down at tiles in front. With the eye 0.1 above the capsule top, a slightly-downward forward ray aimed at a tile a few units ahead should clear the capsule (the capsule is 0.5 radius, and the ray is aimed at a point ahead, not straight down). So it should hit the tile. \n\nI think setting the eye just above the capsule top is the right call. Let me set EYE_HEIGHT such that the eye is at center + 1.0. Since `eyePos().y = center.y + EYE_HEIGHT`, I set EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0.\n\nWait, but EYE_HEIGHT is currently 1.5 and used as `center.y + EYE_HEIGHT`. Let me just change EYE_HEIGHT to 1.0. Actually, let me reconsider — the capsule top is at center + 0.9 (halfH = 0.9). So eye at center + 1.0 is 0.1 above the top. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually, I realize the eye being above the capsule means the camera (which follows the eye) is above the player's head. In first-person, you'd see the world from slightly above where the head is. That's fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the player's \"display\" — there's no player display mesh (first-person, the player is the camera). So the eye height just determines the camera height. Good.\n\nLet me also reconsider: when the player is on the ground, center.y = ground.y + 0.9 (feet on ground). Eye at center.y + 1.0 = ground.y + 1.9. So the camera is 1.9 above the ground. That's a reasonable first-person height (a bit high, like 1.9m eye level, which is tall but OK).\n\nActually, real first-person eye height is ~1.5-1.6m. With the capsule being 1.8 tall (feet to head), the head is at 1.8, and the eye at ~1.5-1.6. So the eye should be at ground.y + 1.5 = center.y + 0.6. But that's inside the capsule (center + 0.6 < center + 0.9 top). So the ray origin would be inside the capsule.\n\nThere's a tension: realistic eye height (center + 0.6) is inside the capsule, causing self-hits. Higher eye (center + 1.0) avoids self-hits but is unrealistic.\n\nThe solution: keep the eye at a realistic height (center + 0.6) for the camera, but handle the self-hit by **ignoring hits on the player's own capsule**. \n\nTo ignore hits on the player's capsule, I need to either:\n(a) Filter it out (can't, private shape), or\n(b) Check the hit body and skip it, but the raycast only returns the closest hit.\n\nFor (b), I can cast the ray, and if the hit is the player's capsule, I need to find the next hit. But `physicsRaycast` returns only the first hit.\n\nHmm. Let me check if there's a way to get the player's body to identify it. Actually — I can find the player's body by matching the controller's node. But the node is private.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider. Maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let me check: does the character controller's body actually get hit by `physicsRaycast`? The controller's body is `ANIMATED` motion type. Let me check if ANIMATED bodies are included in raycasts. In Havok, raycasts hit all bodies unless filtered. ANIMATED bodies are hit. So yes.\n\nBut wait — maybe the character controller's body has a special filter. Let me check the controller's constructor again — it creates the body with `createPhysicsBody(world, this._node, PhysicsMotionType.ANIMATED)` and `setPhysicsBodyShape`. No filter is set, so default (~0, ~0). So it's hit by all raycasts.\n\nOK so the player capsule is definitely in the raycast's path. I need to handle it.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest solution given the constraints:\n\n**Option A: Eye above the capsule (center + 1.0).** Forward rays clear the capsule. Downward rays might hit the capsule, but the player rarely aims straight down. The distance guard handles near self-hits. This is simple and works for the common case (shooting tiles in front). Downside: camera is a bit high.\n\n**Option B: Eye at realistic height (center + 0.6), and filter out the player capsule.** Requires accessing the controller's body/shape to set a filter, which is private. Not feasible without engine changes.\n\n**Option C: Eye at realistic height, and when the raycast hits the player capsule, re-cast from a point beyond the capsule.** Hacky but works.\n\nI'll go with **Option A** (eye above the capsule) as the simplest robust solution. Let me set EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0 (eye at center + 1.0, just above the capsule top at center + 0.9).\n\nWait, but actually, let me reconsider the camera. The camera position is set to the eye. If the eye is at center + 1.0, the camera is 1.0 above the capsule center. The capsule top is at center + 0.9. So the camera is 0.1 above the head. That's fine for first-person.\n\nActually, I realize there might be an even simpler solution: **what if the bullet raycast just doesn't hit the player because I give the player's controller a filter via the world?** No, the shape is private.\n\nLet me go with Option A. Let me update EYE_HEIGHT to 1.0 and the eyePos calculation.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider once more. Let me re-examine: is the eye really going to be inside the capsule at center + 0.6? The capsule is a capsule shape (cylinder + 2 hemispheres). At y = center + 0.6, we're in the top hemisphere region (the cylinder spans center - 0.4 to center + 0.4, since r = 0.5 and the cylinder is from -h/2 + r to h/2 - r = -0.9 + 0.5 = -0.4 to 0.9 - 0.5 = 0.4). So at y = center + 0.6, we're above the cylinder (0.4) and in the top hemisphere (which spans 0.4 to 0.9). The top hemisphere is centered at (center.x, center + 0.4, center.z) with radius 0.5. At y = center + 0.6, the height above the hemisphere center is 0.2, so the cross-section radius is sqrt(0.5^2 - 0.2^2) = sqrt(0.25 - 0.04) = sqrt(0.21) ≈ 0.458. The eye is at (center.x, center + 0.6, center.z), which is at the hemisphere's axis (x/z = center). Distance from hemisphere center = 0.2 < 0.5. So the eye is inside the top hemisphere. Confirmed: eye at center + 0.6 is inside the capsule.\n\nSo for a realistic eye height, the ray origin is inside the capsule. The raycast would hit the capsule's far surface. So I must either raise the eye above the capsule or handle the self-hit.\n\nDecision: **Raise the eye to center + 1.0** (Option A). This is the simplest robust fix. Let me update.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the trade-off once more. A first-person camera 0.1 above the head is slightly high but totally playable (many games have the camera at or slightly above head height). And it cleanly avoids the self-hit problem for forward shots. I'll go with it.\n\nLet me update EYE_HEIGHT from 1.5 to 1.0. Wait, EYE_HEIGHT is used as `center.y + EYE_HEIGHT`. So EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0 gives eye at center + 1.0. Good.\n\nBut hold on — I should double check: with the eye at center + 1.0, and the capsule top at center + 0.9, is the eye really outside the capsule? The capsule top (hemisphere) reaches y = center + 0.9 at the axis. The eye is at y = center + 1.0, which is 0.1 above the top. So the eye is outside the capsule (above it). A forward ray from the eye (horizontal) stays at y = center + 1.0, which is above the capsule (top at center + 0.9). So the ray clears the capsule. \n\nFor a slightly downward ray (small negative pitch), the ray descends. It could hit the capsule top hemisphere if aimed steeply enough. But for shooting tiles a few units ahead, the pitch is small, and the ray clears the capsule. Good.\n\nLet me make the change.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I'm overthinking the self-hit. Let me reconsider whether the player capsule is really a problem. In many FPS games with physics, the player's collision body is excluded from weapon raycasts via layer masking. Since I can't mask the player capsule (private shape), the eye-above-capsule approach is the pragmatic solution.\n\nLet me go with EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0.\n\nHmm, wait, but actually there's an even cleaner solution I overlooked: **what if I make the bullet raycast originate from the eye but the player capsule simply isn't in the raycast's target because I set the terrain layer filtering correctly AND the player capsule's default filter doesn't match?**\n\nLet me reconsider the filter semantics. The raycast query is `[membership, collideWith]`. A shape is hit if it passes the filter test. In Havok V2, the filter test for a raycast is: the shape's filter `[shapeGroup, shapeMask]` passes if `(shapeGroup & collideWith) != 0` (I think the query's collideWith is ANDed with the shape's group). Let me check the exact semantics.\n\nActually, the semantics in Havok: for a query with filter `[queryGroup, queryMask]`, a shape with filter `[shapeGroup, shapeMask]` is considered if `(shapeGroup & queryMask) != 0`. So the query's `collideWith` (queryMask) is ANDed with the shape's group (shapeGroup).\n\nWait, that's one convention. Let me verify with the code. The query is `[membership, collideWith]`. So `membership` = queryGroup, `collideWith` = queryMask. A shape is hit if `(shapeGroup & collideWith) != 0`.\n\nThe terrain tiles have `shapeGroup = TERRAIN_LAYER` (I set it via `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask`). The player capsule has `shapeGroup = ~0` (default).\n\nIf I cast with `collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER`:\n- Terrain tile: `shapeGroup & collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER & TERRAIN_LAYER = TERRAIN_LAYER != 0` → hit. \n- Player capsule: `shapeGroup & collideWith = ~0 & TERRAIN_LAYER = TERRAIN_LAYER != 0` → hit. \n\nSo the player capsule is still hit (because its group is ~0, which includes TERRAIN_LAYER). So `collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER` doesn't exclude the player.\n\nWhat if I cast with `membership = BULLET_LAYER` and rely on the shape's mask? The shape's mask (collideMask) for the player is ~0 (default). The query's membership (queryGroup) is BULLET_LAYER. If the filter test is `(queryGroup & shapeMask) != 0`, then player: `BULLET_LAYER & ~0 = BULLET_LAYER != 0` → hit. Still hit.\n\nSo no matter what I do with the query, the player capsule (with default ~0, ~0 filter) will be hit. The only way to exclude it is to change the player capsule's filter, which requires access to the private shape.\n\nTherefore, the eye-above-capsule approach is the pragmatic solution. Let me go with it.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider — maybe I CAN access the player's body. Let me check if `world._bodies` is accessible and if I can find the player's body by matching... no, I don't have the node reference.\n\nWait, actually — the character controller is a class. Even though `_body` is private in TypeScript, at runtime it's just a property. But the guidance says not to access @internal/private members. And `PhysicsCharacterController` is a class (not a pure-state interface), so it has private fields. Accessing `player._body` would be accessing a private member, which violates the dogma.\n\nSo I'll go with the eye-above-capsule approach. Let me set EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the value. The capsule top is at center + 0.9. I want the eye just above it. Let me use EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0 (0.1 above the top). \n\nBut wait, there's another consideration: the bot's rayVsCapsule. The bot capsule center is `bot.cc.getPosition()`, and I pass `halfH = CAP_HEIGHT/2 = 0.9`, `r = CAP_RADIUS = 0.5`. The bot capsule spans center ± 0.9. The player's eye is at center_player + 1.0. When the player shoots at a bot, the ray goes from the player's eye to the bot's capsule. That's fine — the bot capsule is a valid target.\n\nOK let me just make the change. EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0.\n\nWait, I want to reconsider whether 1.0 is enough clearance. The capsule top hemisphere: at the axis, the top is at center + 0.9. The eye is at center + 1.0. A horizontal ray from the eye is at y = center + 1.0, which is 0.1 above the capsule top. So it clears by 0.1. Good. But if the player is on a slope or the capsule is tilted... the capsule is always vertical (character controllers don't tilt). So 0.1 clearance is fine.\n\nLet me make the change.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole eye height thing in terms of the camera feel. With the eye at center + 1.0 and the capsule being 1.8 tall (feet at center - 0.9, head at center + 0.9), the camera is at head + 0.1. When the player looks forward, they see the world from ~1.9m above the ground (if standing). That's a bit high (average eye height is 1.5-1.6m), but it's playable and avoids self-hits. \n\nAlternatively, I could keep the eye at a realistic height (center + 0.6) and accept that the player capsule blocks raycasts, and instead **cast the bullet ray and if it hits the player capsule, ignore that hit and cast again from the hit point + a small offset**. But that's more complex.\n\nI'll go with the simple approach: eye at center + 1.0. Let me update EYE_HEIGHT.\n\nLet me make the edit.\n","seq":81689,"t":4098054,"text":"\n\nThe character controller's `getPosition()` returns the capsule **center** (shape spans center ± halfH). This matters: with `EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5` the eye is 0.6 above the head, but more importantly, a realistic eye height (center + 0.6) would be *inside* the player's capsule, and since the controller's shape is private (can't be filter-masked), the player capsule would occlude every terrain raycast. Let me set the eye just above the capsule top so forward rays clear it:\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":96256,"draft_n":15925,"draft_n_accepted":5613,"predicted_ms":212710.0,"predicted_n":7888,"prompt_ms":2200.0,"prompt_n":914},"to_request":77149,"usage":{"cached_tokens":96256,"completion_tokens":7888,"prompt_tokens":97170}}
