{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me reconsider. The player's capsule has a default filter (~0, ~0), so it will always be hit by raycasts. The character controller's shape is private, so we can't set a filter mask on it.\n\nOptions:\n1. Put the eye above the capsule (EYE_HEIGHT > CAP_HEIGHT/2 = 0.9) so forward rays clear the capsule. But the raycast still returns the closest hit — if the capsule is closer than the terrain, we get the capsule hit. Wait, no — if the eye is above the capsule and the ray goes forward (horizontally), the ray doesn't intersect the capsule at all (the capsule is below the eye). So the raycast will hit the terrain, not the capsule.\n\nActually, let me think about this. The eye is at `center.y + EYE_HEIGHT`. The capsule spans `center.y - 0.9` to `center.y + 0.9`. If EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5, the eye is at `center.y + 1.5`, which is 0.6 above the capsule top (`center.y + 0.9`). A forward ray (horizontal) from the eye would pass above the capsule, so it wouldn't hit the capsule. It would hit the terrain.\n\nBut wait — what if the player aims downward? Then the ray could hit the capsule. But that's an edge case. For most forward shots, the ray clears the capsule.\n\nActually, the cleaner solution: the eye should be at a realistic height. A human's eye is about 1.5m above the ground. The capsule is 1.8m tall, centered at `center.y`. The feet are at `center.y - 0.9`, the head at `center.y + 0.9`. The eye should be near the head, so about `center.y + 0.7` (a bit below the top). So EYE_HEIGHT relative to center should be about 0.7, not 1.5.\n\nWait, I'm confusing myself. Let me redefine. `player.getPosition()` returns the capsule center. The eye should be at `center.y + eyeOffsetFromCenter`. For a 1.8m capsule, the head is at +0.9 from center. The eye is a bit below the head, so about +0.7 from center. So EYE_HEIGHT (as an offset from center) should be about 0.7.\n\nBut currently EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5, and `eyePos()` does `player.getPosition().y + EYE_HEIGHT` = `center.y + 1.5`. That's 1.5 above center, which is 0.6 above the head. That's too high — the camera would be floating above the player's head.\n\nSo I should change EYE_HEIGHT to about 0.7 (offset from center), so the eye is near the head. Let me use 0.7.\n\nBut then the eye is at `center.y + 0.7`, which is inside the capsule (the capsule spans center ± 0.9). So the ray origin is inside the player's capsule. A forward ray from inside the capsule would immediately hit the capsule's own surface (the near side) or pass through it.\n\nHmm, this is the self-hit problem. When the ray origin is inside the player's capsule, `physicsRaycast` might return the player's capsule as the first hit (the exit point of the ray from the capsule). That would cause the shot to be blocked by the player's own body.\n\nWait, but actually — does `physicsRaycast` hit the player's capsule? The player's capsule is a physics body (ANIMATED motion type). Raycasts hit physics bodies. So yes, a ray from inside the player's capsule would hit the capsule's surface.\n\nSo I need to prevent the bullet raycast from hitting the player's own capsule. Options:\n1. Use collision layer filtering: set the player capsule's filter to a \"player\" layer, and set the bullet raycast's `collideWith` to exclude the player layer. But the controller's shape is private — I can't set its filter mask.\n\nWait, can I? The controller creates its own shape internally. I don't have access to it. So I can't set the player capsule's filter.\n\n2. Set the bullet raycast's `collideWith` to only the terrain layer. Then the raycast would only hit terrain (and debris if I put debris in the terrain layer). It would NOT hit the player capsule (which is in the default layer). Let me check: the raycast query has `membership` and `collideWith`. \n\nLooking at the physicsRaycast code:\n```\nconst membership = query.membership ?? ~0;\nconst collideWith = query.collideWith ?? ~0;\nconst hkQuery = [[from], [to], [membership, collideWith], ...];\n```\n\nSo the query has `[membership, collideWith]`. In Havok, the collision filter works like this: a raycast with filter `[membership, collideWith]` hits shapes where `(shape.membership & collideWith) != 0` AND `(shape.collideMask & membership) != 0` — actually the exact semantics depend on Havok's filter implementation.\n\nLet me check how the filter works. The terrain tiles have `membership = TERRAIN_LAYER` (bit 0). The player capsule has default membership (probably `~0` or `0`?).\n\nActually, let me check what the default membership mask is. In Havok, the default filter is usually `membership = ~0` (all bits) and `collideMask = ~0`. Let me verify.\n\nActually, the key question: if I set the bullet raycast's `collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER` (bit 0), will it hit only shapes whose membership includes bit 0? The terrain tiles have membership = TERRAIN_LAYER (bit 0). The player capsule has default membership. If the default membership is `~0` (all bits including bit 0), then the player capsule would also be hit. If the default is `0`, then it wouldn't.\n\nLet me check the default. Let me look at how shapes are created and what the default filter is.\n\nActually, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about the cleanest approach.\n\nThe cleanest approach for a Quake-style FPS:\n- The bullet raycast should hit terrain and bots, but not the player.\n- Bots are handled separately via `rayVsCapsule` (manual test), so the physics raycast only needs to hit terrain.\n- So I should make the physics raycast hit ONLY terrain (and debris), not the player.\n\nTo do this, I use collision layer filtering:\n- Terrain tiles: membership = TERRAIN_LAYER (bit 0).\n- Bullet raycast: `collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER` — so it only hits shapes in the terrain layer.\n- Player capsule: default membership. If the default is NOT in the terrain layer, the raycast won't hit the player.\n\nBut I need to know the default membership. Let me check.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The simplest robust approach: make the eye height realistic (near the head), and use collision filtering so the bullet raycast only hits terrain. Let me verify the default filter membership.\n\nLet me check what membership a newly created shape has by default.\n","seq":82621,"t":4145517,"text":"\n\nLet me check the default shape filter membership — this determines whether the bullet raycast can be filtered to terrain-only (avoiding the player's own capsule):\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":104448,"draft_n":3262,"draft_n_accepted":1240,"predicted_ms":45020.0,"predicted_n":1706,"prompt_ms":1630.0,"prompt_n":819},"to_request":81692,"usage":{"cached_tokens":104448,"completion_tokens":1706,"prompt_tokens":105267}}
