{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"`createPhysicsShape` doesn't set any filter at all — so the shape ends up with Havok's default filter. In Havok V2, the default filter for a newly created shape is `[~0, ~0]` (membership = all bits, collideMask = all bits).\n\nIn other words, the player's capsule has membership `~0` and collideMask `~0`.\n\nNow, about the raycast query `[membership, collideWith]`: in Havok V2, a raycast hits a shape if `(shape.membership & query.collideWith) != 0` — I need to verify the exact semantics. Let me think about what the Havok V2 API does.\n\nActually, in Havok, the collision filter for queries is: the query has a filter `[group, mask]`. A shape is hit if `(shape.group & query.mask) != 0`. That is, the query's `collideWith` (mask) is ANDed with the shape's group (membership).\n\nSo if I cast with `collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER` (bit 0):\n- Terrain tile (group = TERRAIN_LAYER = bit 0): `bit0 & bit0 = bit0 != 0` → hit. ✓\n- Player capsule (group = ~0): `~0 & bit0 = bit0 != 0` → hit. ✗ (I don't want this)\n\nSo even if I use `collideWith = TERRAIN_LAYER`, the player capsule still gets hit. That's because its group is `~0` (all bits).\n\nHmm. So the filter can't exclude the player capsule. That's because the player's group is `~0` (it \"belongs\" to every layer).\n\nWait, that's the problem. The player capsule's membership is `~0`, meaning it belongs to every layer. So any `collideWith` mask that has any bit set will match it.\n\nTo exclude the player, I'd need to set the player capsule's membership to a specific layer (e.g., PLAYER_LAYER) that the bullet raycast's `collideWith` excludes. But the player capsule's shape is private (inside the character controller), so I can't set its membership.\n\nSo the filter approach won't work for excluding the player capsule.\n\nLet me reconsider. What are my options?\n\nOption A: Put the eye above the capsule so forward rays clear the capsule. Then the raycast hits the terrain (not the player capsule, since the ray doesn't intersect it). This works for forward shots. The self-hit guard (`hitDistance > 2`) handles the edge case of aiming down at your own feet.\n\nOption B: Cast the ray, and if the hit body is the player's controller body, ignore it. But the raycast only returns the closest hit. If the player capsule is closest, I get the capsule and miss the terrain behind it. So this doesn't work unless I can get multiple hits.\n\nOption C: Give the player capsule a filter. Not possible (private shape).\n\nSo Option A is the way. Put the eye above the capsule. Let me reconsider the eye height.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the ray from the eye really hits the player capsule. The eye is at `center.y + EYE_HEIGHT`. If EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5, the eye is at `center.y + 1.5`. The capsule top is at `center.y + 0.9`. So the eye is 0.6 above the capsule top. A forward (horizontal) ray from the eye stays at `y = center.y + 1.5`, which is above the capsule (top at `center.y + 0.9`). So the ray clears the capsule. It hits the terrain.\n\nBut if the player aims down (negative pitch), the ray descends and could hit the capsule. For example, aiming straight down, the ray goes from `center.y + 1.5` downward, passing through the capsule (which spans `center.y - 0.9` to `center.y + 0.9`). So the ray hits the top of the capsule at `center.y + 0.9`, a distance of 0.6. The self-hit guard (`hitDistance > 2`) would skip this (0.6 < 2). But then the raycast returns the capsule hit (closest), and we miss the terrain below. So aiming straight down, the player can't destroy the tile directly under their feet. That's an acceptable edge case (Quake players don't usually shoot the floor directly under their feet).\n\nFor the common case (aiming forward at tiles), the ray clears the capsule and hits the terrain.\n\nSo Option A works. Let me keep EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5 (eye 0.6 above the head) — actually, that's a bit high for first-person. Let me reconsider.\n\nHmm, actually, for a good first-person feel, the eye should be at head height. But if the eye is at head height (inside the capsule), forward rays hit the player capsule. So there's a trade-off.\n\nLet me reconsider: what if I set EYE_HEIGHT so the eye is just above the capsule top? The capsule top is at `center.y + 0.9`. So EYE_HEIGHT = 0.95 (just above the top). Then the eye is just above the head, and forward rays clear the capsule by a small margin. This gives a more realistic first-person height (eye at head level + a bit).\n\nActually, the most natural is: eye at head height. The head is at `center.y + 0.9` (top of the capsule). So EYE_HEIGHT = 0.9 puts the eye exactly at the capsule top. A forward ray from there grazes the top of the capsule. To be safe, EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0 (0.1 above the top).\n\nLet me use EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0. This puts the eye just above the head, and forward rays clear the capsule.\n\nWait, but I should double-check: is the capsule top really at `center.y + 0.9`? The capsule shape is created with pointA at `y = h*0.5 - r` and pointB at `y = -h*0.5 + r`. So the capsule's cylindrical part goes from `y = -h/2 + r` to `y = h/2 - r`, and the full capsule (including the hemispherical caps) goes from `y = -h/2` to `y = h/2`. With h = 1.8, the full capsule goes from `y = -0.9` to `y = +0.9` (relative to center). So the top is at `center.y + 0.9`. ✓\n\nSo EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0 puts the eye at `center.y + 1.0`, which is 0.1 above the capsule top. Forward rays clear the capsule. Good.\n\nLet me change EYE_HEIGHT from 1.5 to 1.0.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the camera feel once more. With EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0, the camera is 1.0 above the capsule center. The capsule center is at `feet.y + 0.9` (when standing). So the camera is at `feet.y + 1.9`. That's 1.9m above the ground — a bit high (average eye height is about 1.5–1.6m). But it's acceptable for a game, and it avoids self-hits.\n\nAlternatively, I could lower the whole capsule or adjust. But let's keep it simple: EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Actually, the standard approach in these games: the character controller's position is the center of the capsule. The camera (eye) is placed at `center.y + eyeOffset`, where eyeOffset is a bit less than the capsule's half-height (so the eye is near the head, inside the capsule). And to avoid self-hits, the weapon raycast either (a) starts from a point in front of the eye (muzzle offset), or (b) uses layer filtering.\n\nSince I can't use layer filtering (private shape), let me use a muzzle offset: start the ray from a point in front of the eye (e.g., 0.5 units forward and slightly down, like a gun barrel). This way the ray starts outside the player's capsule and doesn't hit it.\n\nActually, that's a cleaner solution! Let me offset the ray origin forward from the eye by a small amount (like a gun muzzle). Then the ray starts in front of the player's head, outside the capsule, and won't hit the player capsule.\n\nLet me do that: `from = eye + forward * 0.5` (muzzle 0.5 in front of the eye). And keep EYE_HEIGHT at a realistic value (like 0.7, near the head).\n\nWait, but if the eye is inside the capsule (EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7 → eye at `center.y + 0.7`, inside the capsule which spans ±0.9), and the muzzle is 0.5 in front of the eye, the muzzle is at `eye + forward * 0.5`. Is the muzzle outside the capsule? The capsule has radius 0.5. The eye is at the capsule's axis (x/z = center). Moving 0.5 forward (horizontally), the muzzle is 0.5 from the axis, which is exactly at the capsule's surface (radius 0.5). So the muzzle is at the capsule's surface, not outside. I need to move further, like 0.6–0.7 forward, to be clearly outside.\n\nHmm, this is getting fiddly. Let me just combine: put the eye at a realistic height (near the head) AND offset the ray origin forward (muzzle). Let me use:\n- EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7 (eye near the head, at `center.y + 0.7`).\n- Muzzle offset: `from = eye + forward * 0.8` (0.8 in front of the eye, clearly outside the capsule's radius of 0.5).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The capsule radius is 0.5. The eye is at the axis. To be outside the capsule horizontally, I need to be more than 0.5 from the axis. A muzzle 0.8 in front of the eye is 0.8 from the axis (horizontally), which is outside the capsule (radius 0.5). Good. And vertically, the muzzle is at the eye's height (`center.y + 0.7`), which is within the capsule's vertical range (±0.9), but since it's 0.8 from the axis horizontally (outside the radius of 0.5), it's outside the capsule. ✓\n\nSo the ray starts from the muzzle (outside the capsule) and goes forward. It won't hit the player capsule. It will hit the terrain or bots.\n\nBut wait — what about the bot's capsule test (`rayVsCapsule`)? I'm using `from` (the muzzle) for that too. That's fine — the muzzle is the effective ray origin.\n\nLet me implement the muzzle offset. Let me define `const MUZZLE_OFFSET = 0.8;` and in `tryFire`, compute `from = eye + fwd * MUZZLE_OFFSET`.\n\nActually, I realize the simplest and most robust solution is a combination:\n1. Realistic eye height (EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7, near the head).\n2. Muzzle offset for the ray origin (0.8 in front of the eye).\n\nThis gives a good first-person feel and avoids self-hits. Let me implement it.\n\nWait, but the camera should be at the eye (not the muzzle). So the camera position = eye, and the ray origin = muzzle. Let me keep them separate.\n\nLet me reconsider EYE_HEIGHT. For a 1.8m capsule (feet to head), the head is at the top (`center.y + 0.9`). The eye is a bit below the top, at about `center.y + 0.7`. So EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7.\n\nLet me make these changes:\n1. EYE_HEIGHT: 1.5 → 0.7\n2. Add MUZZLE_OFFSET = 0.8\n3. In tryFire, `from = eye + fwd * MUZZLE_OFFSET`\n\nLet me also reconsider the self-hit guard. With the muzzle offset, the ray starts outside the player capsule, so self-hits are unlikely. But I'll keep the guard (`hitDistance > 2`) as a safety net. Actually, with the muzzle offset, the guard is less necessary, but it doesn't hurt. Let me keep it.\n\nLet me make the edits.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I even need the muzzle offset if I put the eye above the capsule. If EYE_HEIGHT = 1.0 (eye above the capsule top), forward rays clear the capsule, and no muzzle offset is needed. But the camera would be 1.0 above the center = 1.9 above the ground, which is high.\n\nI think the muzzle offset approach is better (realistic eye height + clean ray origin). Let me go with that.\n\nLet me make the changes:\n1. EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7\n2. MUZZLE_OFFSET = 0.8\n3. tryFire: from = eye + fwd * MUZZLE_OFFSET\n\nLet me look at the current tryFire and eyePos.\n","seq":84278,"t":4231762,"text":"\n\nThe character controller's capsule shape is private (can't be filter-masked), and its default membership is `~0` (belongs to every layer), so a physics raycast can't be filtered to exclude the player's own capsule. The clean fix: keep a realistic eye height but cast the bullet ray from a **muzzle offset** in front of the eye (outside the capsule radius), so it never starts inside the player's body. Let me view the current `eyePos`/`tryFire`:\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":107520,"draft_n":5600,"draft_n_accepted":2332,"predicted_ms":77420.0,"predicted_n":3132,"prompt_ms":2000.0,"prompt_n":768},"to_request":82692,"usage":{"cached_tokens":107520,"completion_tokens":3132,"prompt_tokens":108288}}
