{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The new dev server is serving the latest code (EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7, MUZZLE_OFFSET present).\n\nNow, let me record the main findings from this session and do a final review. I'll log the following:\n1. Chrome can't be launched inside the sandbox (crashpad's Mach bootstrap is denied) — browser-based verification is impossible.\n2. The dev server's file watcher was serving stale content — needed a restart.\n3. Fixed the bugs in rayVsCapsule.\n4. Self-hit issue: the player's capsule has a default filter (~0), and since it can't be masked, I used a muzzle offset.\n\nLet me record these findings.\n\nAlso, let me do a final check on the whole file to make sure everything is consistent. Let me re-read the important parts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the bot's line-of-sight (LOS) raycast. The bot uses `raycast(nav, ...)` (the navmesh raycast) for LOS. That's a 2D raycast on the navmesh. But when terrain tiles are destroyed, holes open up in the navmesh (via `addBoxObstacle`). So the navmesh raycast accounts for the holes. Good.\n\nBut wait — the bot's LOS raycast is done on the navmesh, which is a 2D representation. The raycast from the bot to the player checks whether there's a path on the navmesh. If there's a hole (destroyed tile) between them, the raycast will be blocked. That's a reasonable approximation.\n\nHowever, there's a subtle issue: the navmesh raycast is horizontal (2D), so it doesn't account for height differences. If the bot is on a high ledge and the player is in a low valley, the navmesh raycast might say \"clear\" even though the bot can't actually see the player (due to the terrain in between). But for a game demo, this is acceptable.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the bot's shooting. The bot shoots the player if:\n- `dist < BOT_RANGE` (40 units)\n- `!los.hit` (navmesh raycast is clear)\n- `Math.random() < accuracy` (distance-based)\n\nOn a hit, `damagePlayer(10)`. This is reasonable.\n\nLet me reconsider: the bot's LOS raycast uses the bot's position `bp` and the player's position `pp2`. But `bp.y` is the bot's capsule center, and `pp2.y` is the player's capsule center. The navmesh raycast is 2D (ignores y). So the y values don't matter for the navmesh raycast. Good.\n\nWait, actually, let me double-check: does the navmesh `raycast` function use the y coordinate? Let me check. The navmesh is a 2D representation (x/z plane at various heights). The raycast function probably projects onto the navmesh. Let me verify it doesn't get confused by y.\n\nActually, I recall from the navigation API that `raycast(plugin, start, end)` does a 2D raycast on the navmesh. The y coordinate is used to find the navmesh polygon at that x/z, but the ray itself is in the x/z plane. So passing the capsule center's y is fine (it just needs to be within the navmesh's height range).\n\nOK, I think the implementation is solid. Let me do a final review of the whole file to catch any remaining issues.\n\nLet me re-read the physics step and render frame sections.\n","seq":88276,"t":4491863,"text":"\n\nThe fresh server serves the latest code. Let me do a final review of the physics-step and render sections to catch any remaining issues:\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":119296,"draft_n":1827,"draft_n_accepted":607,"predicted_ms":27400.0,"predicted_n":868,"prompt_ms":1720.0,"prompt_n":476},"to_request":87759,"usage":{"cached_tokens":119296,"completion_tokens":868,"prompt_tokens":119772}}
