irmed off the table; no further mesh-file audit needed.\naudit-28769-3\tDebris functions confirmed but line numbers drift — setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity is at :1056 (note's :1063 is getPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity); applyPhysicsImpulse/removePhysicsBody/line-518 not re-verified this session.\naudit-28769-30\tFPS-relevant references confirmed — scene18 (free camera), scene40 (physics), scene113–115 (picking), scene180–181 (text/HUD) (lab/lite/src/lite/); scene files now extend to scene251 (earlier \"189 / only saw 40\" is stale).\naudit-28769-36\tonTriggerCollisionObservable confirmed (character-controller.ts:306) for per-contact impulses (knocking debris); plan's destruction path is raycast-based, so this is optional polish.\naudit-28769-6\tResolved — lab/vite.config.ts:743 is `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174`, so start with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`; no config edit needed.\naudit-28769-91\tAnchors (verified) — player/bot = createPhysicsCharacterController (moveWithCollisions + checkSupport/CharacterSupportedState); bullet hits = physicsRaycast (terrain) + manual ray-capsule (bots), not the GPU picker; terrain surgery = createFlatGroundData + computeNormals + uploadMeshToGPU; debris = thin instances; HUD = createTextLayer/createTextRenderer (scenes 180-181); bots = navigation (createNavMesh, NavCrowd/agentGoto/updateNavCrowd, addBoxObstacle); physics = createHavokWorld + per-tile static boxes (createPhysicsAggregate), HavokPhysics.wasm from lab/public/.\ninterview-t2-0\tconstraint: WebGPU-exclusive — no WebGL fallback, no legacy wrappers or abstraction layers for older APIs; WebGL 1/2 are marked \"won't support\" by design.\ninterview-t2-10\tconstraint: Bundle-size ceilings in `tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts` must never be raised without approval; golden reference screenshots in `reference/lite/` are immutable.\ninterview-t2-11\tconstraint: Priority order when trade-offs conflict: size > performance > simplicity; Babylon.js backward compatibility is not a first-class goal (e.g., classic `InstancedMesh` API is deliberately replaced by thin instances).\ninterview-t2-12\tconstraint: `lab/lite/src` scene code is the user-facing reference and must read like a high-level API demo, never a WebGPU tutorial.\ninterview-t2-13\tconstraint: `GUIDANCE.md` is immutable unless the user explicitly requests changes, and must be re-read at session start and after every compaction.\ninterview-t2-2\tgotcha: Playwright must be invoked via `pnpm exec`; using `npx`/`npm` can create a rogue `node_modules/playwright` that shadows the pnpm-managed copy and breaks test runs (\"did not expect test() to be called here\") — IMPLICATION: always use `pnpm exec playwright ...`; if it misbehaves, `rm -rf node_modules/playwright` and reinstall.\ninterview-t2-3\tgotcha: `babylon-lite`'s package.json points `main`/`types`/`exports` at raw `src/index.ts` (with `sideEffects: false`); `dist` only exists for publishing — IMPLICATION: dev/test consumes source directly with no build step; a publishable artifact requires `pnpm build` (vite + vite-plugin-dts).\ninterview-t2-4\tgotcha: GUIDANCE.md forbids module-level side effects — a module-level `new Map()/Set()/WeakMap()` kills tree-shaking, and caches must be lazy-init and invalidated on device change — IMPLICATION: any new engine code needs the lazy-cache pattern (`let cache: Map | null = null`) and no import-time registration or `globalThis` mutation.\ninterview-t2-5\tgotcha: production bundles minify inline WGSL template literals (strip comments, newlines, leading whitespace) — IMPLICATION: never parse emitted WGSL strings (split on `\\\\n`, regex); structured data must live in typed interfaces (`UboField[]`, `BindingDecl[]`), WGSL strings are shader code only.\ninterview-t2-7\tgotcha: Lite intentionally omits WebGL (WebGPU-only), the classic `InstancedMesh` API (thin instances instead), particles, GUI, WebXR, LOD/octree, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, asset manager and scene serialization — IMPLICATION: porting a BJS scene can hit hard stops; use the documented alternatives or stay on Babylon.js for those features.\ninterview-t2-8\tgotcha: the session working directory is empty; the repo lives at `~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite` — IMPLICATION: all file access needs absolute paths, and I should confirm where any working copy lives before writing code.\ninterview-t2-f3661-2\tconstraint: One-way data ownership — only the scene knows its contents; components (lights, cameras, meshes) are plain data that never reference the scene; factories don't take a scene parameter, callers use `addToScene()`.\ninterview-t2-f3661-3\tconstraint: Pure state interfaces — all public interfaces have no attached methods; behavior lives in standalone functions taking the interface as first argument (methods can't be tree-shaken).\ninterview-t2-f3661-4\tconstraint: Do not split a type into public `Foo` + `FooInternal` companions — put internal members on the same type tagged `@internal` (d.ts trim pass strips them).\ninterview-t2-f3661-5\tconstraint: Public API must never expose raw WebGPU handles (`GPUTexture`, `GPUTextureView`, `GPUSampler`, `GPUBuffer`, `GPUDevice`) — use `Texture2D` and similar abstractions.\ninterview-t2-f3661-6\tconstraint: Do not copy Babylon.js code — understand the math, then write the minimum code that produces identical pixels.\ninterview-t2-f3661-7\tconstraint: Vite is the foundation for infrastructure, dev server, and production bundling; use `pnpm exec` for Playwright (never `npx`/`npm`, which can recreate a rogue `node_modules/playwright`).\ninterview-t2-f3661-8\tconstraint: Bundle size = runtime fetched JS bytes only (Playwright network interception), excluding local `*-nme.ts` payload modules and the `text-shaper` vendor dependency; WGSL is minified in production.\ninterview-t3-0\tevidence: ```\\n * ```ts\\n *   const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }, { capsuleHeight: 1.8, capsuleRadius: 0.6 });\\n *   onPhysicsAfterStep(world, () => {\\n *       cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 });\\n *       const p = cc.getPosition();\\n *       displayMesh.position.set(p.x, p.y, p.z);\\n *   });\\n * ```\\n```\\n\\nThat doc example is load-bearing: it confirms the player-controller API exists and gives me the exact pattern I'd build the FPS movement on — create the controller from the Havok world, drive it per physics step with an explicit displacement vector, read position back. It also tells me the controller is stepped via `onPhysicsAfterStep`, i.e. movement is coupled to the fixed physics timestep, not the render loop.\ninterview-t3-1\tevidence: ```\\nEvery Lite app follows the same four phases, in order:\\n\\n1. **Create the engine** — `await createEngine(canvas)` (acquires the GPU device).\\n2. **Build the scene** — create a `SceneContext`, then `addToScene()` your camera, lights, meshes, and loaded assets.\\n3. **Register** — `await registerScene(scene)` does the deferred GPU work (builds pipelines, partitions renderables). Call this **after** everything is added.\\n4. **Start** — `await startEngine(engine)` begins the render loop and resolves after the first frame.\\n\\nIf something doesn't appear, it's almost always because it was added **after** `registerScene()`, or never added at all.\\n```\\n\\n2. POINTERS:\ninterview-t3-10\tpointer: `setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, friction, restitution)` — per-shape friction, for Quake-style ground slide (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:971)\ninterview-t3-11\tpointer: `getPhysicsBodyDebugGeometry` — collider debug visualization while tuning the arena (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:1246)\ninterview-t3-2\tpointer: the doc continues past line 100 with the complete minimal example app (engine → scene → light → sphere) — I only read the first 100 lines; the full scaffold code is still unread (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §3 onward)\ninterview-t3-3\tpointer: optional features (glTF exts, clearcoat, HDR, shadows) are isolated modules dynamically imported on first use — matters when adding physics/shadows to the FPS under the bundle-size ceilings (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md, §2 \"You pay only for what you touch\")\ninterview-t3-5\tpointer: published package name is `@babylonjs/lite` but the on-disk workspace package is `babylon-lite` — lab imports use the workspace name, which matters if I scaffold new lab scenes (docs/lite/01-getting-started.md §1 vs packages/babylon-lite/package.json)\ninterview-t3-6\tpointer: `setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask` / `setPhysicsShapeFilterCollideMask` — collision layer filtering; needed so bullets/debris/player don't collide with each other (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok.ts:578/589)\ninterview-t3-7\tpointer: `applyHeightmap` is private and runs only at creation — heightmap ground is static, no runtime modification API (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/create-ground.ts:110)\ninterview-t3-f5806-1\tpointer: `CharacterSupportedState` enum (UNSUPPORTED / SLIDING / SUPPORTED) — I'll need this later for ground detection (jump gating, Quake-style air-control rules). (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/character-controller.ts)\ninterview-t3-f5806-3\tpointer: `moveWithCollisions` takes a displacement vector, not a velocity — movement code must integrate velocity × dt each physics step; the header example (`{ x: 0, y: -0.05, z: 0.02 }`) is that idiom. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f5806-4\tpointer: the controller is a standalone tree-shakeable module — `_stepWorld` in `havok.ts` never references it, so it only costs bytes if imported. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f5806-5\tpointer: it mirrors Babylon.js' `PhysicsCharacterController` (Apache-2.0), left-handed, +Y up — BJS experience transfers 1:1 when debugging behavior. (same file)\ninterview-t3-f6606-1\tpointer: `physicsRaycast(world, from, to, query)` — the bullet-hit test against the physics world (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-queries.ts:207)\ninterview-t3-f6606-2\tpointer: `createHeightFieldShape(world, options)` — Havok heightfield collider so terrain physics can match a heightmap (packages/babylon-lite/src/physics/havok-heightfield.ts:59)\ninterview-t3-f6606-3\tpointer: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` is a pure CPU function — the hook for recomputing normals after we hand-edit terrain positions before re-upload (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/compute-normals.ts:9)\ninterview-t3-f6606-4\tpointer: `uploadMeshToGPU(...)` — the rebuild seam for a \"destroy-then-rebuild\" terrain approach (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/mesh.ts:187)\ninterview-t3-f9898-0\tdecision: Physics in the lab is wired through the Havok V2 wasm asset at `lab/public/HavokPhysics.wasm` — WHY: scene 40 imports `@babylonjs/havok` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"` and `scripts/build-lab-site.ts` copies that file into the lab site, so the lab can serve it as-is.\ninterview-t3-f9898-1\tdecision: Destructible terrain is implementable without engine changes, via CPU geometry edit + re-upload — WHY: `mesh.ts` exposes `uploadMeshToGPU(engine, positions, normals, indices, ...)` plus `@internal` `_cpuPositions`/`_cpuNormals`/`_cpuIndices` copies and `computeNormals(positions, indices)`, giving a complete mutate-and-reupload path.\ninterview-t3-f9898-2\tdecision: The terrain base mesh can be a dense subdivided grid — WHY: `createFlatGroundData`/`createGround` accept `subdivisions`, so a vertex-level digging mesh is available from the existing ground factory.\ninterview-t3-f9898-3\tdecision: Any HUD will use the in-engine GPU text path rather than a GUI package — WHY: Lite has no GUI by design, and this step confirmed the text module is present and demonstrated in lab scenes 180/181.\ninterview-t4-1\tevidence: ```\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:192:export function createNavMesh(plugin: NavigationPlugin, meshes: Mesh[], params: NavMeshParameters): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:493:export function getClosestPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:500:export function computePath(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): Vec3[];\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:528:export function createNavCrowd(plugin: NavigationPlugin, maxAgents: number, maxAgentRadius: number): NavCrowd;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:536:export function addAgent(crowd: NavCrowd, position: Vec3, params: AgentParameters): number;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:555:export function getAgentPosition(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:561:export function getAgentVelocity(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:567:export function agentGoto(crowd: NavCrowd, index: number, destination: Vec3): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:572:export function updateNavCrowd(crowd: NavCrowd, dt: number): void;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:583:export function findRandomPointAroundCircle(plugin: NavigationPlugin, position: Vec3, radius: number): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:590:export function findRandomPoint(plugin: NavigationPlugin): Vec3;\\n~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts:615:export function raycast(plugin: NavigationPlugin, start: Vec3, end: Vec3): { hit: boolean; hitPoint?: Vec3 };\\n```\\n\\n(`createNavMesh` at :192 is what makes navmesh buildable from the generated noise-terrain mesh; `computePath`/`findRandomPoint`/`raycast` are patrol/seek/line-of-sight; the crowd functions give bot-vs-bot steering.)\\n\\n2. Not needed now, may matter later:\ninterview-t4-2\tpointer: `createNavMeshFromSources` builds a navmesh from `NavMeshSource[]` instead of raw meshes — may matter if the noise-terrain mesh needs filtering (e.g., excluding holes/steep faces) before navmesh generation (navigation.ts:200)\ninterview-t4-3\tpointer: `createDebugNavMeshGeometry` returns positions/normals/indices for rendering the navmesh — useful for debugging bot paths on generated terrain (navigation.ts:404)\ninterview-t4-4\tpointer: `setNavigationRandomSeed`/`getNavigationRandomSeed` — seedable RNG for deterministic bot behavior, useful for reproducible AI tests (navigation.ts:597, 602)\ninterview-t4-5\tpointer: `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle`/`removeObstacle`/`updateNavMeshObstacles` — dynamic navmesh obstacles; relevant when destructible terrain opens holes or debris blocks paths (navigation.ts:647–681)\ninterview-t4-f13394-1\tdecision: The FPS is built as a new lab demo entry under `lab/lite/`, not as a parity scene (no golden reference, no pixel-diff spec, no bundle-ceiling entry) — WHY: it's a game demo, not a Babylon.js parity target.\ninterview-t4-f14169-1\tconstraint: Babylon-Lite has no particle system and no audio — debris must be done with thin instances and sounds with Web Audio on the DOM side.\ninterview-t5-0\tevidence: ```\\n        // Default interactive port is 5174. Playwright test runs pass LAB_DEV_PORT\\n        // to spin up an ISOLATED server on a dedicated port (so test traffic never\\n        // test server so the exact port Playwright waits on is guaranteed; the\\n        port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174,\\n```\\n\\nThat settles the port question: no config edit needed — `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` (or `pnpm exec vite` with the env var) lands the lab on 5182 per the session rule.\ninterview-t5-1\tpointer: - The vite config embeds a custom plugin that generates the gallery HTML and links demo sources to `lab/lite/src/demos/` (`DEMO_SOURCE_BASE_URL`) — how a new scene gets listed/served on the lab site is defined here, worth reading when we register the FPS scene (lab/vite.config.ts, top + `renderPagesDemoCard`)\\n- `public/bundle/demos-manifest.json` holds per-demo raw/gzip KB measured by the build; gallery cards and `window.__DEMO_ENGINE_KB` read it — the same data the bundle-size ceilings track (lab/vite.config.ts, `injectDemoEngineSize`)\\n- A `?compat` query-marker plugin redirects `@babylonjs/core` imports to the compat barrel for the BJS oracle scenes under `lab/lite/src/bjs/` — irrelevant for a pure Lite scene, but explains the repo's dual-scene layout (lab/vite.config.ts, lines 607–656)\\n- The config sets the `babylon-lite` alias to `packages/babylon-lite/src` plus a directory alias for sub-path imports — that's how lab scenes import engine modules (lab/vite.config.ts, ~line 733)\ninterview-t5-2\tgotcha: Physics bodies are node-anchored — `createPhysicsBody(world, node, ...)` requires a SceneNode, and the static idiom is `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` (scene40.ts) — IMPLICATION: per-tile static colliders need one node per tile; I must verify an empty/invisible node can be created before assuming the tile-removal physics approach is cheap.\ninterview-t5-7\tdecision: HUD uses `createTextLayer` + `createTextRenderer`/`addTextRendererLayer` — WHY: verified those exports in the text module; scenes 180–181 are the working references.\ninterview-t5-8\tdecision: Terrain heights from seeded low-frequency value noise, amplitude ~5u — WHY: gentle noise keeps the flat-topped tile-box physics close to the mesh surface.\ninterview-t5-9\tdecision: Add `canvas.dataset.ready`/`dataset.error` like scene40 — WHY: it makes the scene scriptable for a later Playwright smoke test via `pnpm exec playwright`.\ninterview-t5-f21644-0\tgotcha: The lab port override is an env var, not a CLI flag — `lab/vite.config.ts:743` is `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174` — IMPLICATION: start the lab with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`; there is no `--port` path.\ninterview-t5-f21644-1\tgotcha: `PhysicsBody` has no position accessor — the interface (havok.ts:156-167) wraps a `SceneNode` (`node` field) and the world syncs body→node each step — IMPLICATION: read debris/bot positions via `body.node.position`; there is no `getPhysicsBodyPosition` to plan around.\ninterview-t5-f24085-0\tdecision: Dev server runs as `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` — WHY: lab/vite.config.ts already honors `LAB_DEV_PORT` (falls back to 5174), so the 5182 session rule needs no config edit.\ninterview-t5-f24085-2\tdecision: Bots use `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` — WHY: scene170/171 confirm it as the established lab idiom and `lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm` already exists, served like HavokPhysics.wasm.\ninterview-t5-f24085-3\tdecision: Per-tile terrain physics = static boxes via `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, PhysicsShapeType.BOX, { mass: 0 })` — WHY: scene40 confirms this is the Lite idiom for static bodies, and per-tile bodies can be individually removed (`removePhysicsBody`) on destruction.\ninterview-t5-f24085-4\tdecision: Player uses `moveWithCollisions` + `getPosition`/`getVelocity`, with ground detection via `supportedState` — WHY: verified those exact members on `PhysicsCharacterController` (character-controller.ts:360/371/384/114).\ninterview-t5-f24085-5\tdecision: Debris bodies are rendered as thin instances by reading `body.node.position` each frame — WHY: `PhysicsBody` exposes `node: SceneNode` (havok.ts:156-167), so no extra position-query API is needed.\ninterview-t5-f25285-0\tconstraint: The lab dev server port is set via the `LAB_DEV_PORT` env var (default 5174 per `lab/vite.config.ts`), so every dev-server start for this project must be `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab` — plain `pnpm dev:lab` would land on 5174 and violate the session's port 5182 rule.\ninterview-t5-f25985-0\tgotcha: `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` returns void — it does not return the surface/ground state I'd assumed from the doc example; ground state comes from a separate `checkSupport(deltaTime, direction): CharacterSurfaceInfo` call (and there's a `checkSupport` + `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` pair as the BJS-style stepping path) — IMPLICATION: S3 player stepping should call `checkSupport` each physics step for jump gating (one extra support cast per step) and then `moveWithCollisions` or `integrate`, not rely on a return value.\ninterview-t5-f25985-1\tgotcha: the exported `createHavokWorld` is `createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, gravity?)` — non-async, requiring a pre-loaded Havok module handle; the doc comment's `await createHavokWorld(scene)` does not match the real signature — IMPLICATION: S3 must first load the wasm module (scene40 pattern: `@babylonjs/havok` with `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"`) and pass that handle in; verify the exact load call from scene40's first 80 lines before writing code.\ninterview-t5-f25985-3\tgotcha: `PhysicsCharacterController` is a class with public methods (`getPosition`, `setPosition`, `getVelocity`, `setVelocity`, `moveWithCollisions`, `integrate`, `checkSupport`, `onTriggerCollisionObservable`) — an exception to the pure-state-interface dogma from the interview constraints — IMPLICATION: call the methods directly; don't hunt for functional wrappers or assume the controller can be reconstructed from plain data.\ninterview-t5-f25985-4\tgotcha: the text module is multi-stage — `createTextLayer`/`createTextRenderable` both take a `TextData`, which itself needs a font buffer + glyph storage (`createFontFromBuffer`, `createDefaultTextData`/`createTextData`), and layers render through a `TextRenderer` bound to a `SurfaceContext` — IMPLICATION: S6 should copy the scene180/181 flow verbatim (including font loading from `lab/public/fonts`) rather than improvising a simpler call path.\ninterview-t5-f27685-0\tdecision: New scene is `scene252.ts` + `scene252.html` under `lab/lite/` — WHY: scene251 is the highest existing number and `getHtmlInputs()` auto-discovers HTML, so no manifest edits.\ninterview-t5-f27685-1\tdecision: Split the demo into `scene252.ts` (game flow) + `fps-terrain.ts` + `fps-input.ts` + `fps-audio.ts` — WHY: the entry must read like a high-level API demo, and terrain surgery, DOM input, and audio don't belong in the game-flow file.\ninterview-t5-f27685-2\tdecision: Zero changes to `packages/babylon-lite`; the FPS is built purely on existing APIs — WHY: every needed capability (character controller, raycast, navmesh, text, thin instances) was verified present, and engine edits would fight the tree-shaking/pure-state constraints.\ninterview-t5-f27685-3\tdecision: Arena 128×128u with 4×4u tiles → 1024 per-tile static box bodies, ~256×256-vertex mesh, box top at tile max height — WHY: concrete realization of tile removal + per-tile static bodies, with 8×8u tiles (256 bodies) as the fallback if world init is slow.\ninterview-t5-f27685-4\tdecision: Bullet hits terrain via `physicsRaycast`, hits bots via manual ray-capsule test with closest-hit wins — WHY: character controllers aren't reliable physics-raycast targets, and a manual test against ~4 bots is cheap and deterministic.\ninterview-t5-f27685-5\tdecision: Bots are character controllers driven by NavCrowd agents, targeting only the player — WHY: the crowd gives bot-vs-bot separation for free, and bot-vs-bot combat is out of scope this iteration.\ninterview-t5-f27685-6\tdecision: Quake-style slide via low friction on the terrain tile shapes, with controller-shape friction as the preferred target if it's exposed — WHY: the controller's shape getter is unverified, and terrain friction applies consistently to player and bots.\ninterview-t5-f28251-0\tconstraint: New lab scenes must be registered as an HTML page in `lab/lite/` following the scene18 pattern (`sceneN.html` with canvas id `renderCanvas`, `/loader.js`, and a module script pointing at `/lite/src/lite/sceneN.ts`) — the gallery auto-discovers scenes via `getHtmlInputs()` in `lab/vite.config.ts`, so a scene file without its HTML page won't exist in the lab.\ninterview-t5-f28251-3\tconstraint: Physics and navigation wasm must be loaded via `locateFile` from the lab root paths `/HavokPhysics.wasm` and `/recast-navigation.wasm` (both confirmed present in `lab/public/`) — don't introduce other asset locations.\ninterview-t6-0\tdecision: Terrain re-upload uses `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs)` instead of raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + `_gpu` swap — WHY: it's the purpose-built in-place seam that bumps `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record, and retains `_cpuPositions`/`_cpuIndices` for picking (mesh-factories.ts:106).\ninterview-t6-1\tevidence: ```\\n    **When adding a new scene, you MUST:**\\n        1. Create `lab/lite/sceneN.html` + `lab/lite/src/lite/sceneN.ts`\\n        2. Add the entry to `lab/vite.config.ts` rollup inputs\\n        3. Add a Playwright parity test in `tests/lite/parity/scenes/sceneN-*.spec.ts`\\n        4. Add a reference screenshot to `reference/lite/sceneN-*/babylon-ref-golden.png`\\n        5. Save a downscaled JPG thumbnail (≤720p, e.g. 1280×720) of the golden to `lab/public/thumbnails/sceneN.jpg`\\n        6. Add a card to `lab/index.html` (the scene gallery)\\n        7. Add a bundle-size ceiling test in `tests/lite/parity/bundle-size.spec.ts`\\n        8. Add an entry to `scene-config.json` with `id`, `slug`, `name`, and `maxMad`\\n        9. **Never change a bundle-size ceiling without explicit user approval.** If a ceiling is exceeded, report the numbers and ask the user before raising the limit.\\n```\ninterview-t6-10\tconstraint: Before committing, delete all temporary/debug files and debug code created this session, and never revert changes not made by me in this session.\ninterview-t6-11\tplan: fix scene252.ts — replace the stub with a real `createTransformNode` call in `spawnDebris` (add the import), delete `debrisNodePool`, fix `Debris.body` to `PhysicsBody | null`, fix the `rayVsCapsule` u-formula, fix the bot quaternion args, drop unused type imports, add the self-hit guard in `tryFire`.\ninterview-t6-12\tplan: create `lab/lite/scene252.html` mirroring scene18.html (canvas id `renderCanvas`, `/loader.js`, module script `/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts`).\ninterview-t6-13\tplan: check the two small API unknowns (default shape membership mask in `createPhysicsShape`/HP_Shape_Create; `setThinInstances` upload behavior) with a quick grep.\ninterview-t6-14\tplan: run `pnpm run lint:fix` then `pnpm run lint` to get the new files clean.\ninterview-t6-15\tplan: start the dev server in the background (`LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm dev:lab`) and playtest scene252.html against the checklist: ready/error dataset, terrain visible, move/slide/jump, mouse look, shoot holes (tiles removed, physics body gone, nav obstacle), fall-through respawn, debris, bots patrol/shoot, HUD updates, audio.\ninterview-t6-16\tplan: tune what the playtest surfaces (1024-body init time → tile size fallback, navmesh params, slide friction, bot re-target logic) and record findings; when S8 is done, call propose_phase_transition.\ninterview-t6-2\tnote: my plan decided scene252 is a game demo, not a parity scene, so items 3/4/7/8 (parity test, golden, bundle ceiling, scene-config `maxMad`) likely don't apply — but items 1/2/6 (html+ts, rollup inputs, gallery card) probably do, and I hadn't accounted for the rollup-inputs/gallery-card steps. I'll verify against `lab/vite.config.ts` before writing.\\n\\n2.\ninterview-t6-3\tpointer: §0c \"Agent Test Commands (Strict)\" — I must NOT run `pnpm test:perf`, and I only run parity if I changed `packages/babylon-lite/src/**`; since this is a lab-scene-only change, I can skip the parity suite and just verify the scene loads on :5182. (GUIDANCE.md §0c)\ninterview-t6-4\tpointer: §4d \"Scene setup code in `lab/lite/src/` is the user-facing reference — it must read like a high-level API demo, never like a WebGPU tutorial.\" — governs how I write `scene252.ts`/`fps-terrain.ts` (high-level API calls, no raw GPU handles). (GUIDANCE.md §4d)\ninterview-t6-5\tpointer: §2b″ \"Thumbnail Convention\" — gallery thumbnails are JPG only, exactly 1280×720, quality ≈78, named `sceneN.jpg` under `lab/public/thumbnails/`; never commit a PNG there. Matters if I add a gallery card. (GUIDANCE.md §2b″)\ninterview-t6-6\tpointer: §0b \"Clean Up Before Commit\" — before committing I must delete all temp/debug files and debug code (`console.warn`/`console.log`, `(window as any)` exposures) and verify `git status --short`. (GUIDANCE.md §0b)\ninterview-t6-7\tpointer: §6 \"Code Quality: ESLint + Prettier\" — after every code change run `pnpm run lint:fix` then `pnpm run lint`; ESLint forbids `console.log` (only warn/error/time/trace), requires `import type`, no floating promises, always-use-braces. (GUIDANCE.md §6)\ninterview-t6-8\tdecision: Per-tile TransformNodes are created but never added to the scene — WHY: `createPhysicsBody` captures the node's transform at creation (verified in havok.ts:448), so invisible nodes are enough for static colliders.\ninterview-t6-9\tconstraint: Agents must not run `pnpm test:perf`; parity/bundle suites are only required if `packages/babylon-lite/src/**` changes (this project makes zero engine changes).\ninterview-t6-f40408-1\tdecision: Per-tile static colliders are `createTransformNode` + `createPhysicsBody(world, node, STATIC)` + `createPhysicsShape` + `setPhysicsBodyShape`, with the node never added to the scene — WHY: `createPhysicsAggregate` requires a Mesh (would force 1024 real meshes), while the character controller proves a bare TransformNode anchors a physics body fine (character-controller.ts:335).\ninterview-t6-f40408-2\tdecision: Quake-style slide is driven by the controller's public `staticFriction`/`dynamicFriction` fields — WHY: the shape-access fallback in the plan is unnecessary; the friction knobs are exposed directly on `PhysicsCharacterController` (character-controller.ts:283-285).\ninterview-t6-f40408-3\tdecision: HUD uses the 2D overlay path (`loadFont` → `createDefaultTextData` → `createTextLayer` → `createTextRenderer` → `registerTextRenderer`, scene180 pattern), not the in-world `createTextRenderable` (scene181) — WHY: the HUD (health/kills/crosshair) is screen-space and scene180 is the standalone 2D text reference.\ninterview-t6-f40408-4\tdecision: FPS camera is a `FreeCamera` whose `position` and `_yaw`/`_pitch` are driven manually each frame from pointer-lock deltas — WHY: FreeCamera exposes `position: ObservableVec3` plus `_yaw`/`_pitch` fields (free-camera.ts:19-33), and `attachFreeControl` is for orbit-style control, not mouse-look.\ninterview-t6-f40408-5\tdecision: Bullet/terrain collision filtering uses `physicsRaycast`'s `RaycastQuery { membership, collideWith }` masks — WHY: confirmed the query shape at havok-queries.ts:207 and the per-shape mask setters at havok.ts:578/589, so bullets can target only terrain+debris.\ninterview-t6-f40408-6\tdecision: `bundle-size.spec.ts` does not cover scene252 — WHY: it only tests scenes with `maxRawKB` in scene-config.json, and this game demo gets no entry there (consistent with the \"not a parity scene\" decision).\ninterview-t6-f40408-7\tdecision: NavCrowd setup follows scene170 verbatim (`createNavCrowd(nav, maxAgents, maxAgentRadius)`, `addAgent` with radius/height/maxSpeed/separationWeight) — WHY: it's the established lab idiom and gives bot-vs-bot separation for free.\ninterview-t6-f41337-0\tconstraint: Terrain geometry replacement must go through `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts), never raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` assignment — it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` (forcing cached draw bundles to re-record) and defers old-buffer destruction until the GPU drains; skipping it leaves cached bundles binding freed buffers.\ninterview-t6-f41337-1\tconstraint: Per-tile static physics bodies must be node-anchored to invisible `TransformNode`s via `createPhysicsBody(world, node, STATIC)` + `createPhysicsShape` + `setPhysicsBodyShape` — `createPhysicsAggregate` requires a `Mesh` node (havok.ts:1141), and 1024 extra meshes would add draw calls; the character controller (character-controller.ts) proves a TransformNode works as a physics node without being added to the scene.\ninterview-t6-f41337-2\tconstraint: The in-scene HUD must use `createTextRenderable` + `addTextRenderable(scene, text)` (scene181 pattern) — scene180's `createTextRenderer`/`registerTextRenderer` path is explicitly standalone 2D (\"no scene, no camera\") and is the wrong tool for a 3D overlay.\ninterview-t6-f41337-3\tconstraint: FPS mouse-look must write FreeCamera's `@internal` `_yaw`/`_pitch` fields directly (free-camera.ts:30-33) — there is no public rotation API on FreeCamera, and GUIDANCE 4b′ permits reading/writing `@internal` members in scene code.\ninterview-t6-f41337-4\tconstraint: The scene must set `scene.fixedDeltaMs` (e.g. `1000/60`, scene40 pattern) — `moveWithCollisions`/`integrate` are no-ops when the world timestep is zero, so physics-driven player/bot movement silently dies without it.\ninterview-t6-f43708-0\tevidence: ```\\nexport function createTextRenderer(surface: SurfaceContext, opts: TextRendererOptions): TextRenderer {\\n    const canvas = surface.canvas;\\n...\\n        _clear: opts.clear ?? true,\\n...\\nexport function registerTextRenderer(tr: TextRenderer): void {\\n    registerRenderingContext(tr._surface, tr);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThat's load-bearing: it answers the open HUD question from S1. `registerTextRenderer` registers the renderer as a rendering context *on its surface (canvas)* — so a TextRenderer created from the FPS engine lands on the same canvas as the 3D scene and is driven by the engine's frame loop, which is exactly the screen-space HUD path I want (health/kills/tiles + crosshair without per-frame world-space placement). And `_clear: opts.clear ?? true` tells me the default would wipe the 3D scene, so I must pass `clear: false` (or verify the option name) when wiring the HUD.\ninterview-t6-f43708-1\tpointer: the actual frame-loop ordering — i.e. where the engine consumes registered rendering contexts per surface and decides draw order (scene vs. text renderer) — is still unverified; I need to confirm the TextRenderer draws *after* the scene on the same canvas (engine render-context registry, referenced from `registerRenderingContext` in text-renderer.ts:437)\ninterview-t6-f43708-2\tpointer: `unregisterTextRenderer` / `disposeTextRenderer` exist for HUD teardown/disposal if the scene is ever re-registered or the device is lost (text-renderer.ts:441-456)\ninterview-t6-f43708-3\tpointer: `createTextRenderer` takes a `SurfaceContext`, not `EngineContext` — scene180 passes `engine` directly, so EngineContext evidently satisfies SurfaceContext; worth keeping in mind if I ever pass a non-engine surface (text-renderer.ts:289)\ninterview-t6-f44439-1\tevidence: ```\\nexport function registerRenderingContext(surface: SurfaceContext, context: RenderingContext): boolean {\\n    if (surface._renderingContexts.indexOf(context) !== -1) {\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n    surface._renderingContexts.push(context);\\n    return true;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOne caveat: this only proves coexistence, not order. Whether the scene draws first and the text (with `clear: false`) lands on top still hinges on the frame loop at `engine.ts:512` (`const ctxs = surface._renderingContexts;`) — I hadn't read that function yet.\\n\\n2. Not needed now, may matter later:\ninterview-t6-f44439-2\tpointer: `engine.ts:512` iterates `surface._renderingContexts` in the frame loop — read it before wiring the HUD to confirm contexts render in registration order (scene first, text after) (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44439-3\tpointer: `device-lost-recovery.ts:217` iterates all contexts on device loss — if the text renderer's per-layer GPU resources aren't in that recovery path, the HUD could break after a GPU device reset; check only if device loss comes up during playtest (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/device-lost-recovery.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44439-4\tpointer: `gpu-task-timer.ts:121` times per-context GPU work — useful only if I need to attribute frame-time cost between the 3D scene and the text pass (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/gpu-task-timer.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44439-5\tpointer: `surface.ts:241/256` clears `_renderingContexts` on surface teardown — only relevant if I add/stop the engine mid-session (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/surface.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44822-0\tgotcha: `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs?)` (mesh-factories.ts) is the dedicated in-place geometry-replacement seam — it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record — IMPLICATION: tile removal must use it, not a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `mesh._gpu` swap (which would leave cached bundles binding the freed buffers).\ninterview-t6-f44822-1\tgotcha: FreeCamera's world matrix is built from `position` + `target` via `mat4LookAtLH`; `_yaw`/`_pitch` are internal and only consumed by `attachFreeControl` — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera must set `cam.position` and `cam.target` each frame from the player's eye + (yaw, pitch); setting `_yaw`/`_pitch` alone changes nothing.\ninterview-t6-f44822-2\tgotcha: NavCrowd has no agent reposition/teleport API — the agent's simulated position can drift from the character controller's real position — IMPLICATION: drive the bot controller with the agent's velocity and re-target via `agentGoto` periodically/on drift; fall back to `computePath` waypoint-following if drift gets bad.\ninterview-t6-f44822-3\tgotcha: A TextRenderer is a separate rendering context on the same surface, and render order = registration order in `surface._renderingContexts` — IMPLICATION: register the 3D scene first and the text renderer second, and create it with `clear: false` so it overlays instead of wiping the 3D frame.\ninterview-t6-f44822-4\tgotcha: `PhysicsWorld._timestep` is `@internal` — IMPLICATION: read dt from the `onPhysicsAfterStep(world, (timestep) => ...)` callback parameter instead.\ninterview-t6-f44822-5\tgotcha: The controller's friction is plain public fields (`staticFriction`, `dynamicFriction`) on `PhysicsCharacterController` — the plan's open question (\"does the controller expose its shape for friction?\") resolves as: no shape access needed, tune the fields directly — IMPLICATION: Quake slide is tuned via controller fields + `setPhysicsShapeMaterial` on the tile shapes, both already planned.\ninterview-t6-f45976-0\tgotcha: the plan's terrain-rebuild seam was `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap, but the correct API is `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs?)` (mesh-factories.ts) — it reallocates buffers in place AND bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record; a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` swap would keep stale buffer bindings — IMPLICATION: destroyTile must call `resizeMeshGeometry`, and must read fresh geometry from `mesh._cpuIndices`/`_cpuPositions` each time (resize updates those copies), not from a cached copy I own.\ninterview-t6-f45976-1\tgotcha: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, ...)` is typed `node: Mesh`, not `SceneNode` — a bare TransformNode per tile won't compile against it — IMPLICATION: per-tile static boxes must be built with `createPhysicsBody(world, node, PhysicsMotionType.STATIC)` + `createPhysicsShape(world, {type: BOX, parameters: {extents}})` + `setPhysicsBodyShape(world, body, shape)` (the character controller proves TransformNode + physics body works; `createPhysicsBody` reads `node.position`/`rotationQuaternion` at creation, so the node must be positioned before the call).\ninterview-t6-f45976-2\tgotcha: the controller's friction is exposed as plain public fields (`staticFriction` default 0, `dynamicFriction` default 1) on `PhysicsCharacterController` — the plan's fallback assumed I'd tune friction on the terrain tile shapes — IMPLICATION: Quake slide is set on the controller fields directly (low `dynamicFriction`), and terrain-shape friction (`setPhysicsShapeMaterial`) is a secondary lever for bots/debris only.\ninterview-t6-f45976-3\tgotcha: `FreeCamera`'s world matrix is computed from `position` + `target` (`mat4LookAtLH`), not from `_yaw`/`_pitch` (those are internal state only `attachFreeControl` consumes) — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera must write `cam.position` and `cam.target` every frame from player eye + yaw/pitch; I keep yaw/pitch as game state but never expect the camera to expose a rotation setter.\ninterview-t6-f45976-4\tgotcha: NavCrowd has no agent reposition API (navigation.ts exports only `addAgent`/`getAgentPosition`/`getAgentVelocity`/`agentGoto`/`updateNavCrowd`) — the plan assumed `agentGoto`+`updateNavCrowd` per step would keep bots glued to their controllers — IMPLICATION: nav agents will drift from colliding controllers; I must re-target periodically (and fall back to `computePath` waypoint steering if drift causes stuck bots during playtest), and the drift check belongs in the bot update.\ninterview-t6-f45976-5\tgotcha: `PhysicsWorld._timestep` is `@internal` — IMPLICATION: game code must take the timestep from the `onPhysicsAfterStep(world, (timestep) => ...)` callback parameter, not from the world.\ninterview-t6-f45976-6\tgotcha: the debris impulse export is `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse(body, impulse, location)` — the working-set note's `applyPhysicsImpulse` is a wrong name — IMPLICATION: import `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (havok.ts) for debris kicks; note it takes the body, not the world.\ninterview-t6-f46994-0\tdecision: Per-tile static boxes will be built with `createPhysicsShape({ type: BOX, parameters: { extents } })` + `setPhysicsBodyShape` rather than `createPhysicsAggregate` — WHY: aggregate's auto-size path is typed for `Mesh` nodes and derives extents from the node's bounding box, while tiles are plain TransformNodes with explicit extents I compute from corner heights.\ninterview-t6-f46994-1\tdecision: Follow the engine's own ordering convention of assigning the shape to the body before calling `setPhysicsShapeMaterial` — WHY: the code comment in `createPhysicsAggregate` states material must be set after shape assignment to match HavokPlugin ordering.\ninterview-t6-f47214-0\tconstraint: Box physics shapes are built with `extents` (Vec3) — `createPhysicsShape(world, { type: BOX, parameters: { extents } })` or `createPhysicsAggregate` `options.extents`; there is no other box-parameter spelling.\ninterview-t6-f47214-1\tconstraint: `createPhysicsAggregate` supports only primitive shapes — MESH/CONVEX_HULL must be passed as a pre-built `options.shape` or it throws, so tile/debris colliders are primitives only.\ninterview-t6-f47214-2\tconstraint: Havok shape material (friction/restitution) must be set after the shape is assigned to the body (`setPhysicsShapeMaterial` following `setPhysicsBodyShape`), matching the BJS HavokPlugin ordering.\ninterview-t6-f47214-3\tconstraint: A physics body's initial transform is read from its anchored node at creation (`createPhysicsBody` reads `node.position`/`node.rotationQuaternion`) — per-tile TransformNodes must be positioned at the box center before body creation, and a STATIC body's transform never follows the node afterward.\ninterview-t6-f47519-0\tgotcha: `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) is the intended in-place geometry swap — it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record, and refreshes `_cpuPositions/_cpuNormals/_cpuIndices` — IMPLICATION: tile removal uses `resizeMeshGeometry`, not a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap as the plan's wording suggested (already written that way in fps-terrain.ts).\ninterview-t6-f47519-1\tgotcha: `PhysicsCharacterController` exposes friction as public fields (`staticFriction` default 0, `dynamicFriction` default 1) — there is no shape accessor needed — IMPLICATION: Quake slide is tuned via `cc.staticFriction`/`cc.dynamicFriction`; the plan's \"controller shape getter unverified\" risk is resolved in favor of the controller fields.\ninterview-t6-f47519-2\tgotcha: `FreeCamera`'s world matrix is built from `position` + `target` (`mat4LookAtLH`); `_yaw`/`_pitch` are internal state only `attachFreeControl` uses — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera is driven by setting `cam.position` and `cam.target` each frame from the player's eye + (yaw, pitch); do not write `_yaw`/`_pitch`.\ninterview-t6-f47519-3\tgotcha: the navigation API has no agent reposition/teleport — only `agentGoto(dest)` — IMPLICATION: NavCrowd agents will drift from character controllers when collisions divert them; mitigate by re-targeting on arrival/periodically, and fall back to `computePath` waypoint-following if drift degrades.\ninterview-t6-f47519-4\tgotcha: `PhysicsWorld._timestep` is `@internal` — IMPLICATION: read dt from the `onPhysicsAfterStep(world, (timestep) => ...)` callback parameter, not from the world.\ninterview-t6-f47519-5\tgotcha: static physics bodies take their transform from the node only at creation (no ongoing sync, unlike ANIMATED) — IMPLICATION: tile box positions are baked at creation; TransformNodes must be placed correctly up front and later node moves won't move the box.\ninterview-t6-f47519-6\tgotcha: the debris impulse function is `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (havok.ts), not `applyPhysicsImpulse` as the older note said — IMPLICATION: use `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` for debris knockback.\ninterview-t6-f47519-7\tgotcha: `registerTextRenderer` registers a separate rendering context on the surface and render order is registration order — IMPLICATION: register the 3D scene first, then the HUD `TextRenderer` with `clear: false`, so text draws on top of the frame instead of wiping it.\ninterview-t6-f48525-0\tdecision: Tile removal re-uploads via `resizeMeshGeometry` (not raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap) — WHY: it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record, and maintains the `_cpu*` copies.\ninterview-t6-f48525-1\tdecision: Grid density is 33×33 vertices (subdivisions = 32, one quad per tile) — WHY: destruction is tile removal, so the mesh only needs to align with the tile grid, not the 256×256 vertex-level density of the earlier in-place-deformation idea.\ninterview-t6-f48525-2\tdecision: Per-tile static box has its top at the tile's max corner height with a minimum half-height of 0.5 — WHY: flat tiles would produce degenerate zero-height boxes, and a max-height top guarantees characters never fall through the box edge.\ninterview-t6-f48525-3\tdecision: Tile shape material is friction 0.1 / restitution 0 — WHY: Quake-style ground slide wants low friction and no bounce on the surfaces both player and bots stand on.\ninterview-t6-f48525-4\tdecision: Tile shapes get membership mask `TERRAIN_LAYER = 1` with the collide mask left at default — WHY: bullet raycasts can filter to terrain-only via the query's membership, while character controllers still collide with the tiles.\ninterview-t6-f48525-5\tdecision: Terrain heights come from a seeded mulberry32 PRNG driving an 8×8 coarse value-noise grid with smoothstep bilinear interpolation, amplitude 5u — WHY: deterministic, allocation-free, low-frequency enough that flat-topped tile boxes stay close to the mesh surface.\ninterview-t6-f48525-6\tdecision: `destroyTerrainTile` reads `_cpuIndices`/`_cpuPositions`/`_cpuUvs` off the mesh each call rather than caching private copies — WHY: `resizeMeshGeometry` refreshes those fields, so the mesh is the single source of truth after every re-upload.\ninterview-t6-f48525-7\tdecision: Bullet→tile mapping uses `tileFromPoint(terrain, x, z)` on the raycast hit point — WHY: no body→tile map is needed; the tile containing the hit point is the tile to destroy.\ninterview-t6-f49376-0\tconstraint: All new scene files must have zero module-level side effects — no module-level `new Map()/Set()/WeakMap()`; caches must be lazy-init (GUIDANCE §4, re-read this step; governs fps-input.ts, fps-audio.ts, scene252.ts).\ninterview-t6-f49376-1\tconstraint: All code must pass ESLint + Prettier (4-space indent, 180 print width, es5 trailing commas); run `pnpm run lint:fix` after every code change, then verify with `pnpm run lint`.\ninterview-t6-f49376-2\tconstraint: Never read `PhysicsWorld._timestep` (@internal) from scene code — use the `timestep` parameter of the `onPhysicsAfterStep` callback for dt.\ninterview-t6-f49376-3\tconstraint: The FPS camera must be driven by setting `cam.position` and `cam.target` each frame — FreeCamera's world matrix is `lookAt(position, target)`; `_yaw`/`_pitch` are internal and only consumed by `attachFreeControl`.\ninterview-t6-f49376-4\tconstraint: Static per-tile physics bodies take their transform from the node at `createPhysicsBody` time — tile TransformNodes must be positioned at the box center before creation and never moved afterward.\ninterview-t6-f49376-5\tconstraint: Tile destruction must re-upload geometry via `resizeMeshGeometry` (in-place realloc that bumps every scene's renderable version so cached bundles re-record) — do not hand-assign `mesh._gpu` and do not destroy old GPU buffers synchronously.\ninterview-t6-f49376-6\tconstraint: The character controller's capsule shape is private (`_shape`) — controller collision-filter masks cannot be set; player/bot slide friction must use the controller's public `staticFriction`/`dynamicFriction` fields plus the tile shape material.\ninterview-t6-f49376-7\tconstraint: NavCrowd has no agent-reposition API — bot logic must tolerate agent/controller position drift (re-target via `agentGoto`); never assume the nav agent mirrors the controller.\ninterview-t6-f49376-8\tconstraint: scene252 is a lab demo, not a parity scene — it stays out of scene-config.json, bundle-size.spec.ts, golden references, and the gallery's parity machinery; no ceiling entry to add or raise.\ninterview-t6-f50931-0\tevidence: ```\\n45:    clearColor: GPUColorDict;\\n...\\n150:        clearColor: { r: 0.2, g: 0.2, b: 0.3, a: 1.0 },\\n```\\nplus, from the `SceneContext` interface dump in the same output:\\n```\\n    /** Surface this scene renders into. Set at scene-creation time and immutable\\n     *  afterwards — the default render task is sized and MSAA-matched to this surface,\\n     *  and `registerScene` attaches the scene to it. For the engine's primary surface\\n     *  (the common single-canvas case) this is the engine itself. The owning engine is\\n     *  reachable via `scene.surface.engine`. */\\n    readonly surface: SurfaceContext;\\n    clearColor: GPUColorDict;\\n    camera: Camera | null;\\n```\\nLoad-bearing: `clearColor` is a plain public mutable field on `SceneContext` (default `{r:0.2,g:0.2,b:0.3,a:1}`), so scene252 sets the sky with `scene.clearColor = {...}` — no extra API to hunt for.\ninterview-t6-f50931-1\tpointer: `fog: FogConfig | null` exists on `SceneContext` (scene-core.ts:57) — the engine has fog; a light distance fog could improve visibility on the 128×128u arena if the playtest looks flat.\ninterview-t6-f50931-2\tpointer: `imageProcessing: ImageProcessingConfig` on `SceneContext` (scene-core.ts:48) — tone-mapping/gamma knob; may matter if the standard material looks off under the hemispheric light.\ninterview-t6-f50931-3\tpointer: `environmentPrimaryColor?: [number, number, number]` + `envRotationY` (scene-core.ts:66-69) — background color from the environment path; alternate to `clearColor` if fog/depth-fade interactions look wrong.\ninterview-t6-f50931-4\tpointer: `createSceneContext(surface, options)` takes a `SurfaceContext` and `EngineContext extends SurfaceContext` (scene-core.ts:144) — confirms `createSceneContext(engine)` is the idiom the lab scenes use; matters if I ever need a second surface (I don't).\ninterview-t6-f51756-0\tgotcha: the plan's tile-removal path called for raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + `mesh._gpu` swap, but `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) is the real in-place seam — it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record, and refreshes the `_cpu*` copies; a raw `_gpu` swap would leave cached bundles binding the old buffers — IMPLICATION: fps-terrain.ts uses `resizeMeshGeometry` (already written that way); index surgery reads `mesh._cpuIndices` each time since the function updates it.\ninterview-t6-f51756-1\tgotcha: `PhysicsCharacterController` exposes friction as plain public fields (`staticFriction` default 0, `dynamicFriction` default 1) — no shape getter needed, settling the plan's open question — IMPLICATION: Quake slide is tuned via `cc.staticFriction`/`cc.dynamicFriction` in scene252, not via controller-shape mask access.\ninterview-t6-f51756-2\tgotcha: `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` integrates only the given displacement — it does NOT apply gravity (only `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` does) — IMPLICATION: player and bot stepping must add `vel.y -= g*dt` manually before each `moveWithCollisions` call.\ninterview-t6-f51756-3\tgotcha: FreeCamera's world matrix is computed from `position` + `target` (`mat4LookAtLH`), not from `_yaw`/`_pitch` — those internal fields are only used by `attachFreeControl` — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera is driven by setting `cam.position` (eye) and `cam.target` (eye + forward(yaw,pitch)) each frame; don't touch `_yaw`/`_pitch`.\ninterview-t6-f51756-4\tgotcha: the navigation API has no agent reposition/teleport — NavCrowd agents will drift from the character controller when collisions push a bot off its path — IMPLICATION: re-sync the agent (agentGoto to the bot's actual position) when agent/bot distance exceeds ~4u, plus periodic re-targeting.\ninterview-t6-f51756-5\tgotcha: the impulse export is `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse`, not `applyPhysicsImpulse` as the audit note had — IMPLICATION: debris knockback uses `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (also `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity` at havok.ts:1056 is confirmed).\ninterview-t6-f51756-6\tgotcha: `createPhysicsBody` copies the node's transform into the Havok body at creation time; STATIC bodies do not track the node afterwards — IMPLICATION: the per-tile TransformNode must be placed at the box center BEFORE `createPhysicsBody` (fps-terrain.ts does this); never move a tile node after creation.\ninterview-t6-f51756-7\tgotcha: multiple rendering contexts on one surface render in registration order, and `createTextRenderer` clears by default — IMPLICATION: register the 3D scene first, then `registerTextRenderer` with `clear: false`, or the HUD wipes the frame (or vice versa).\ninterview-t6-f53352-0\tdecision: Player and bot stepping will use `moveWithCollisions(displacement)` with gravity integrated manually in the scene loop — WHY: `integrate` couples stepping to a prior `checkSupport` surface-projection and hides the velocity integration, while the controller header documents `moveWithCollisions` as the primary idiom and it gives full control over Quake-style velocity (input accel, gravity, slide friction).\ninterview-t6-f53352-1\tdecision: `checkSupport(dt, direction)` will be called each physics step with the gravity direction `(0,-1,0)` to gate jumps — WHY: its doc says \"direction to probe, usually the gravity direction\" and `supportedState === SUPPORTED` is the jump gate.\ninterview-t6-f53352-2\tdecision: Thin-instance matrices for bots and debris will be built with the exported `mat4Compose`/`mat4Translation` helpers — WHY: both are confirmed exports of the `babylon-lite` barrel (index.ts:313/316), so scene code stays high-level without hand-rolled matrix math.\ninterview-t6-f53352-3\tdecision: Debris visuals will be a uniform-size `createBox` rendered as thin instances — WHY: `createBoxData(size)` only supports a single uniform size, which is sufficient for cube debris, and thin instances already cover the per-frame transform update.\ninterview-t6-f53926-0\tconstraint: `checkSupport(deltaTime, direction)` must be probed along the gravity direction (0, -1, 0) — the docs say \"usually the gravity direction\" — not the movement/velocity direction, or the SUPPORTED/SLIDING classification is wrong.\ninterview-t6-f53926-1\tconstraint: `integrate(dt, surfaceInfo, gravity)` applies gravity internally to the character — if the player/bot stepping uses `integrate`, gravity must not be added again by the scene code (double application).\ninterview-t6-f53926-2\tconstraint: `createBox`/`createBoxData` only produce uniform cubes (single `size` parameter) — non-uniform debris visuals need mesh `scaling` (physics box extents are separate via shape parameters).\ninterview-t6-f54756-0\tgotcha: `mat4Compose(tx, ty, tz, qx, qy, qz, qw, sx, sy, sz)` allocates and returns a fresh `F32(16)` — it does not write into a caller-supplied buffer — IMPLICATION: my draft `mat4Compose(botMatrices.subarray(...), ...)` won't compile; per-frame thin-instance matrices need `mat4ComposeInto` (if exported) or compose-into-temp + copy into the instance buffer.\ninterview-t6-f54756-1\tgotcha: nav `raycast(plugin, start, end)` returns `hit: true` when the ray is BLOCKED between start and end (0 < t < 1), not when it reaches the destination — IMPLICATION: bot line-of-sight logic is inverted from my draft: player is visible when `!los.hit`, not `los.hit`.\ninterview-t6-f54756-2\tgotcha: FreeCamera's world matrix is built from `position`→`target` via `mat4LookAtLH`; `_yaw`/`_pitch` are internal state of `attachFreeControl`, not the matrix source — IMPLICATION: FPS mouse-look must set `cam.position` and `cam.target` every frame from eye + (yaw, pitch); setting `_yaw`/`_pitch` alone won't turn the camera.\ninterview-t6-f54756-3\tgotcha: `createBox(engine, size)` takes a single uniform size (no per-axis extents) — IMPLICATION: debris cubes are fine, but any non-cube box mesh needs `createMeshFromData` with custom data; physics boxes (per-tile) are unaffected since `createPhysicsShape` takes `extents`.\ninterview-t6-f54756-4\tgotcha: STATIC physics bodies take their transform from the node only at `createPhysicsBody` time and do not track node changes afterward — IMPLICATION: per-tile TransformNodes must be positioned before body creation (they are, in `createFpsTerrain`); never rely on moving the node later.\ninterview-t6-f54756-5\tgotcha: `TextRenderer` is a separate rendering context on the same surface, rendered in registration order, and its default `clear: true` would wipe the 3D frame — IMPLICATION: call `registerScene(scene)` BEFORE `registerTextRenderer(tr)` and pass `clear: false`, or the HUD pass erases the scene.\ninterview-t6-f54756-6\tgotcha: `physicsRaycast` reports no hits until at least one physics step has run (broadphase) — IMPLICATION: don't issue raycasts during init before the world has stepped; shots in the game loop are safe.\ninterview-t6-f54756-7\tgotcha: the plan's \"uploadMeshToGPU rebuild\" seam is actually `resizeMeshGeometry` — raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap would leave cached draw bundles binding freed buffers; `resizeMeshGeometry` bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached bundles re-record — IMPLICATION: tile destruction must use `resizeMeshGeometry` (already done in `fps-terrain.ts`); do not regress to the raw upload path.\ninterview-t6-f55944-0\tdecision: Bot line-of-sight is tested with the navmesh `raycast(nav, start, end)` and \"visible\" means `hit === false` — WHY: the API contract says `hit` is true only when `0 < t < 1` (i.e. blocked), so clear sight is the negation.\ninterview-t6-f55944-1\tdecision: The HUD will use `createTextRenderer(engine, { layers, clear: false })` registered with `registerTextRenderer` after `registerScene`, so the text context draws on top of the 3D frame without wiping it — WHY: `TextRendererOptions.clear` is documented exactly for HUD overlays, and rendering-context registration order sets draw order.\ninterview-t6-f55944-2\tdecision: Per-frame thin-instance matrices (bots + debris) will be written directly into pre-allocated `Float32Array` buffers instead of going through `mat4Compose` — WHY: `mat4Compose` allocates a fresh 16-float array on every call, which is wasteful per-frame GC churn for ~36 instances.\ninterview-t6-f55944-3\tdecision: Debris will be uniform cubes via `createBox(engine, size)` — WHY: the factory only takes a single uniform size, which is all a debris chunk needs.\ninterview-t6-f56342-0\tconstraint: The HUD's TextRenderer must be created with `clear: false` (TextRendererOptions) — the default `clear: true` would wipe the 3D scene color before the text pass draws.\ninterview-t6-f56342-1\tconstraint: `mat4Compose` (math/mat4-compose.ts) takes scalar args and returns a freshly allocated Mat4 — no exported in-place variant — so per-frame thin-instance matrices must be copied into the persistent Float32Array backing `setThinInstances`.\ninterview-t6-f56342-2\tconstraint: Navmesh `raycast(plugin, start, end)` returns `hit: true` only when the ray is blocked (0 < t < 1) and ignores the end point's Y — so line-of-sight logic must treat `!hit` as \"clear\" and endpoints must be given surface-level Y.\ninterview-t6-f56700-1\tevidence: ```ts\\n        const ctxs = surface._renderingContexts;\\n        for (let j = 0; j < ctxs.length; j++) {\\n            const s = ctxs[j]!;\\n            s._update();\\n            drawCalls += s._drawCallsPre;\\n            drawCalls += s._record();\\n        }\\n```\\n\\nAnd in `registerScene` (same output), the final `registerRenderingContext(surface, ctx)` confirms the scene itself is just another context on the surface — so `registerScene(scene)` before `registerTextRenderer(tr)` fixes the draw order.\\n\\n2.\ninterview-t6-f56700-2\tpointer: `captureScreenshot` lazily installs `_capturePreFrame`/`_captureService` hooks that mark the swapchain COPY_SRC and copy it into the frame encoder — an in-engine screenshot path if I ever need deterministic frame captures without Playwright (engine.ts, `_capturePreFrame`/`_captureService` hooks)\ninterview-t6-f56700-3\tpointer: `setGpuTimingEnabled` installs GPU frame-timer hooks exposing `gpuFrameTimeMs` — a free FPS signal for the playtest without adding code (engine.ts, `_gpuTimerBegin`/`_gpuTimerResolve`)\ninterview-t6-f56700-4\tpointer: `engine.drawCallCount` is recomputed each frame from all contexts — the `canvas.dataset.drawCalls` readout I planned will include the text pass, not just the 3D scene (engine.ts, end of tick)\ninterview-t6-f56700-5\tpointer: `configureSwapchainOverlayScene` runs when a scene is registered on a surface that already has contexts — I'm registering the scene first so it never triggers, but reordering registration would change how the scene composites (scene-core.ts, `registerScene`)\ninterview-t6-f57025-6\topen: unverified at runtime — nav agent re-sync (`agentGoto` to bot position when >4u apart) may cause bot/agent chase loops or jitter; playtest will tell.\ninterview-t6-f57025-7\topen: unverified at runtime — TextRenderer compositing over the 3D scene (registration order scene→text, `clear: false`) is reasoned but not yet seen on screen.\ninterview-t6-f57025-8\topen: unverified — init cost of 1024 static tile bodies (plan fallback: 8×8u tiles → 256 bodies) and navmesh build cost/quality at cs=0.5 over the 128×128u mesh.\ninterview-t6-f60621-0\tdecision: Import `computeNormals` from `babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js` and `createFlatGroundData` from `babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js` via subpath — WHY: neither is exported from the `babylon-lite` barrel, and subpath imports are the established lab idiom (scene114).\ninterview-t6-f60621-1\tdecision: Drop the unused type imports (`EngineContext`, `NavigationPlugin`, `PhysicsWorld`, `SceneContext`) from scene252.ts — WHY: tsc flagged them as declared-but-never-used (TS6196) since types are inferred from the factory calls.\ninterview-t6-f60621-2\tnote: the lint run surfaced a new, still-unresolved mismatch — `computeNormals` is typed against `number[]` (not `Float32Array`) for its positions arg and return, so my two `computeNormals(...)` call sites in fps-terrain.ts (lines 124-125, 197-198) fail. I have not decided on the fix yet.\ninterview-t6-f60839-0\tconstraint: `computeNormals` (packages/babylon-lite/src/mesh/compute-normals.ts) takes and returns plain `number[]`, not typed arrays — its output must be wrapped in `new Float32Array(...)` before passing to `createMeshFromData`/`resizeMeshGeometry`, and typed arrays must be converted for its input.\ninterview-t6-f60839-1\tconstraint: `computeNormals` and `createFlatGroundData` are not exported from the `babylon-lite` barrel — lab scene code must import them via subpath (`babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js`, `babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js`), per the lab's directory alias.\ninterview-t6-f61776-0\tgotcha: `computeNormals` (mesh/compute-normals.ts) takes and returns plain `number[]`, not typed arrays — IMPLICATION: wrap calls with `Array.from(...)` / `new Float32Array(...)`; per-shot destruction allocates ~390k-element temp arrays (fine for a demo, would matter if shooting got rapid-fire).\ninterview-t6-f61776-1\tgotcha: `createFlatGroundData` and `computeNormals` are NOT in the `babylon-lite` barrel — IMPLICATION: lab scenes must import them via subpath (`babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js`, `babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js`), which the vite alias + lab tsconfig support (scene114 pattern).\ninterview-t6-f61776-2\tgotcha: the plan's terrain-reupload seam (`uploadMeshToGPU` + `_gpu` swap) is the wrong tool — `resizeMeshGeometry` (mesh-factories.ts) is the engine's in-place geometry replacement: it reallocates buffers, bumps every scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record, and refreshes `_cpuPositions/_cpuIndices` — IMPLICATION: use `resizeMeshGeometry` for tile removal; a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` swap would leave cached bundles binding freed buffers.\ninterview-t6-f61776-3\tgotcha: character-controller friction is exposed as public fields on the controller (`staticFriction`, `dynamicFriction`), not via a shape getter — IMPLICATION: set `cc.staticFriction = 0; cc.dynamicFriction = 0.1` directly for Quake slide; no terrain-shape friction masking needed for the player.\ninterview-t6-f61776-4\tgotcha: NavCrowd has no agent reposition API — agents drift from the character controller as collisions detour the bot — IMPLICATION: re-target with `agentGoto(crowd, i, botPos)` when agent/bot separation exceeds ~4u (implemented); don't assume the agent tracks the bot.\ninterview-t6-f61776-5\tgotcha: `FreeCamera`'s world matrix is built from `position` + `target` (lookAt), not from `_yaw`/`_pitch` (those only feed `attachFreeControl`) — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera must set `cam.position` and `cam.target` every frame from the player eye + (yaw, pitch); `attachFreeControl` must not be used.\ninterview-t6-f61776-6\tgotcha: the audit note's `applyPhysicsImpulse` is stale — the real export is `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (havok.ts) — IMPLICATION: any future debris impulse code must use `applyPhysicsBodyImpulse` (I used `setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity`, which is correct at :1056).\ninterview-t6-f61776-7\tgotcha: HUD overlay works by registering a `TextRenderer` (with `clear: false`) as a second rendering context on the same surface — contexts render in registration order (scene first, text second) — IMPLICATION: call `registerScene(scene)` before `registerTextRenderer(tr)`; `clear: false` is the documented flag for HUD overlays (text-renderer.ts:86).\ninterview-t6-f61776-8\tgotcha: `pnpm dev:lab` failed at startup — `tsx` gets `EPERM` creating its IPC pipe under `/var/folders/.../T/tsx-501/` (sandbox denies that temp path), and `dev:lab` chains `tsx scripts/build-bundle-scenes.ts && tsx scripts/build-bundle-demos.ts && pnpm --filter @babylon-lite/lab dev` — IMPLICATION: skip the bundle-build steps and start vite directly (`cd lab && LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite` or `pnpm --filter @babylon-lite/lab dev`); the bundle scenes are only needed for the Bundle tab, not for scene252.\ninterview-t6-f62790-0\tdecision: The Playwright smoke script lives at the repo root (`fps-smoke.cjs`) instead of /tmp — WHY: the sandbox refuses writes to /tmp, so the script must sit in a writable path, and I flagged it for deletion before commit per GUIDANCE §0b (clean up temp files).\ninterview-t6-f62790-1\tdecision: Browser-based smoke verification needs a redirected temp dir before it can run — WHY: Playwright's default artifact temp (`/var/folders/.../T/playwright-artifacts-*`) is also sandbox-blocked (EPERM mkdtemp), so the next attempt must point Playwright's temp output at a repo-local directory (e.g. `TMPDIR`/`PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` within the repo) or verify the scene by non-browser means.\ninterview-t6-f63754-0\tgotcha: `computeNormals` takes/returns plain `number[]`, not typed arrays (mesh/compute-normals.ts) — IMPLICATION: wrap with `Array.from(...)` and convert the result back to `Float32Array` at both call sites in fps-terrain.ts.\ninterview-t6-f63754-1\tgotcha: `createFlatGroundData` and `computeNormals` are NOT in the `babylon-lite` barrel — IMPLICATION: lab scenes import them via subpath (`babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js`, `babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js`), the same idiom scene114/scene17 use.\ninterview-t6-f63754-2\tgotcha: `pnpm dev:lab` fails in this sandbox — the script's `tsx scripts/build-bundle-scenes.ts` step EPERMs on an IPC pipe in the system temp dir — IMPLICATION: start the lab with `LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite` from `lab/` directly (skips the bundle build, which a dev playtest doesn't need).\ninterview-t6-f63754-3\tgotcha: 