ually — hold on. Let me reconsider. The loader.js spinner: let me read it. Maybe the spinner only hides when `canvas.dataset.ready` is set. If the scene throws before setting ready, the spinner stays. But then there should be an error in the console… unless the error is an unhandled rejection that doesn't get logged? No, unhandled rejections do get logged.\n\nWait, actually there's another possibility: a **module-level import failure**. If an import from \"babylon-lite\" doesn't exist (e.g., `createNavigationPluginAsync` isn't exported from the barrel, or `findRandomPointAroundCircle` isn't exported), Vite's dev server would return a 500 for the module, and the browser would log an error like \"Failed to resolve import\". The user says \"no errors\" — but they might have only looked at the console at first glance. Hmm, but they explicitly said the console only shows the engine banner.\n\nActually wait — the engine banner \"Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine\" — where does that come from? Probably loader.js or the engine itself. If it's coming from the engine, then `createEngine` succeeded.\n\nLet me think about what could hang without an error:\n\n**`createNavigationPluginAsync`** — loads recast-navigation.wasm. If that wasm load hangs… the asset is 200. Hmm.\n\n**`createNavMesh`** — this is synchronous. But wait, could it throw? If it throws, main()'s catch logs the error. No error is shown.\n\nHmm, what about **`HavokPhysics({ locateFile: ... })`**? That's a Promise. It should work like in scene 40.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the loader.js spinner. The loader probably shows \"Initializing Babylon Lite\" and hides it when… let me read loader.js. Maybe it hides when the canvas gets a `ready` dataset, or maybe it hides after a timeout. If the loader waits for `canvas.dataset.ready`, and the scene's `main()` resolves but `startEngine` never fires the first frame… no, that's not it.\n\nLet me think about `startEngine` — it resolves after the first frame. If the render loop never produces a frame (e.g., a WebGPU pipeline compilation hang?), startEngine would never resolve. But then the canvas should still display the scene's clear color… the user's screenshot shows a dark background with the spinner — the canvas background is dark (clearColor 0.04, 0.06, 0.09 is almost black). The page background is also dark. So we can't tell if the engine is rendering.\n\nHmm, but actually, the screenshot shows the loader overlay. Let me read loader.js to understand when it goes away.\n\nAnother possibility: **`createNavMesh` is blocking the main thread for a long time** — building a navmesh over a dense 33×33 mesh should be fast. Not an infinite hang.\n\nWhat about **`createFpsTerrain`** — creating 1024 static physics bodies. Could that take several seconds? It's synchronous — during that time the spinner stays, but eventually it should complete. The user says \"doesn't reach the game\" — maybe they waited a long time. Let me measure: how long does 1024 × createPhysicsBody take? In scene 40, aggregates are few. 1024 bodies might take 1–5 seconds in wasm. That's a stall, but not infinite.\n\nBut an infinite hang with no errors — let me look at loader.js to understand the spinner logic. Also check whether `createNavigationPluginAsync` is actually exported from the \"babylon-lite\" barrel — if the import failed, Vite would show an error in the console (the user might have missed it).\n\nActually, hold on — the most suspicious thing: the user said \"the console only shows 'Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine'\". If module imports had failed, Vite would show a big red error overlay and console errors. The user isn't seeing that. So all imports resolved.\n\nSo the hang is one of the awaits. Which one? Let me add instrumentation: I can't run a browser. But I can reason.\n\nActually — wait. Let me look at `createEngine` in the scene: `const engine = await createEngine(canvas);`. The engine banner is showing. So createEngine resolved.\n\nNext await: `HavokPhysics(...)`. Scene 40 does exactly this. Works.\n\nNext: `createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })`. Scenes 170/171 do this. Works.\n\nNext: `loadFont(\"/fonts/Inter.ttf\")`. Scenes 180/181 do this. Works.\n\nNext: `registerScene(scene)`, `startEngine(engine)`. Standard.\n\nHmm — so which of these could hang only in scene 252?\n\n**`createNavMesh(nav, [terrain.mesh], {...})`** — this runs before loadFont, and it's synchronous. If it threw… an error would be logged. If it takes forever… hmm, navmesh construction is done in wasm; the mesh is 33×33 vertices, 32×32 quads = 2048 triangles. Fast.\n\nWait — actually, maybe the problem is that `createNavMesh` **doesn't exist as an export** under that name, or has a different signature, and it throws synchronously — but then the error would be logged. No error.\n\nLet me step back: **the loader.js spinner** — when does it hide? If the loader hides the spinner on `canvas.dataset.ready`, and the scene never sets it… because `main()` never completes. The scene sets `canvas.dataset.ready = \"true\"` after `startEngine`.\n\nSo what's blocking `main()`? Let me actually check whether the module is even being executed in the first place. The engine banner — where does it come from? Let me check loader.js and see if the banner is coming from the engine module import (a side effect at import time). If the banner appears at import time, then the module graph loaded fine and main() started.\n\nKey insight: the user says other gallery scenes render fine in the same browser. So WebGPU works, wasm loading works, fonts work.\n\nSo what's unique to scene 252?\n1. 1024 static physics bodies (synchronous, possibly slow but finite).\n2. Navmesh construction (synchronous, fast).\n3. Thin instances (setThinInstances before registerScene — hmm!).\n4. Text renderer with clear: false.\n5. FreeCamera.\n\nWait — `setThinInstances(botMesh, botMatrices, BOT_COUNT)` is called **before** `registerScene(scene)`. And `addToScene(scene, botMesh)` — then setThinInstances. Then `registerScene`. If `setThinInstances` before registration has issues… that's a runtime issue, not a hang.\n\nHmm, let me think about `createTextRenderer(engine, { layers: [...], clear: false })` — created before registerScene. Registration happens after registerScene. Per my earlier notes, registration order determines draw order; no problem.\n\nOK, another thought: **`await HavokPhysics(...)`** — in scene 40, is it `await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: ... })`? Let me check scene 40's exact pattern. My notes say \"scene 40 imports @babylonjs/havok with locateFile: () => '/HavokPhysics.wasm'\". And `createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, gravity)` — my notes (interview-t5-f25985-1) say \"the exported createHavokWorld is createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, gravity?) — non-async, requires a pre-loaded Havok module handle\". OK, matches.\n\nHmm, wait, actually — let me double-check: in scene 40, does the first argument to `HavokPhysics()` include other options like `async: true`? The @babylonjs/havok default export is a factory function that loads the wasm. The `locateFile` pattern is standard emscripten. No problem.\n\nSo let me read loader.js to understand the spinner, and check if there's anything about dataset.ready. Also let me quickly check fps-terrain.ts's createFpsTerrain for anything that could hang (like a synchronous infinite loop — e.g., an unbounded while loop, or a huge allocation).\n\nActually — **a synchronous infinite loop** is a classic: no console error, spinner spins forever (main thread blocked → spinner animation freezes? No wait — if the main thread is blocked, the spinner wouldn't animate either!).\n\nImportant observation: in the user's screenshot, the spinner appears to be animating (a partial arc is shown). If the main thread were blocked in a synchronous loop, the spinner wouldn't move and the screenshot would look frozen. The user says \"it stays on the spinner\" — if the spinner is still animating, the main thread is free → we're waiting on an await (I/O), not a synchronous loop.\n\nBut we can't be sure from a single screenshot that it's animating. The spinner arc in the screenshot is partial — it could be either.\n\nSo: awaiting something that never resolves. Candidates: HavokPhysics, createNavigationPluginAsync, loadFont, registerScene, startEngine.\n\nWait — actually, one more: **`createEngine(canvas)`** — resolves. The banner shows.\n\nHmm, let me look at the banner more carefully: \"Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine\". Let me grep for that string in the codebase to see where it's logged. That'll tell me how far the module got.\n\nAlso — one more possibility: **the loader.js spinner is the only thing on the page, and the canvas never renders because `startEngine`'s first frame hangs in WebGPU pipeline compilation**… but then startEngine wouldn't resolve, dataset.ready wouldn't be set, the spinner would stay, and there'd be no console error. That's plausible! If some material/mesh combination (thin instances + text renderer + standard material) causes a pipeline hang… unlikely but possible.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: is the loader spinner hidden by a `dataset.ready` observer? Let me read loader.js now.\n\nLet me also grep for the banner string.\n\nThen, the most productive next step: I can't run a browser, but I can add lightweight instrumentation to the scene (console.warn at each stage — wait, ESLint forbids console.log but allows console.warn). Per GUIDANCE, console.warn is allowed. But I should be careful about adding debug code that I have to remove later.\n\nBetter: the scene already sets `canvas.dataset.initMs` and `dataset.ready` at the end. I could add dataset markers at each stage — but that's debug code. Alternatively, use console.warn at each stage — GUIDANCE allows console.warn. Hmm, but clean code before commit.\n\nActually, the simplest and most robust diagnostic: add a `canvas.dataset.stage = \"...\"` update at each stage in main(). That's debug code — I'll remove it once it's diagnosed. But the user has to reload and tell me the value of `canvas.dataset.stage` (or I can't read the browser). Hmm, the user would have to inspect.\n\nAlternatively — maybe I can find the cause just by reading the code. Let me think harder.\n\n**`createNavMesh(nav, [terrain.mesh], {...})`** — let me check the signature in navigation.ts: `createNavMesh(plugin: NavigationPlugin, meshes: Mesh[], params: NavMeshParameters): void`. It takes Mesh[] — terrain.mesh is a Mesh. OK.\n\n**`findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, {x: s.x, y: 0, z: s.z}, 8)`** — takes a position; y: 0. OK.\n\n**`addAgent(crowd, p, {...})`** — OK.\n\n**`setThinInstances(botMesh, botMatrices, BOT_COUNT)`** — signature? From my notes: `setThinInstances` bumps `_version`. Let me check its signature — probably `setThinInstances(mesh, matrices, count)`. OK.\n\n**`createDefaultTextData(font, 28, \"HP 100...\", [1,1,1,1], { maxWidth: 1200, align: \"left\" })`** — let me check the signature. From scene 180: `createDefaultTextData(font, size, text, color, options?)`. OK.\n\n**`createTextLayer(hudData, { positionPx: {x,y}, scale, rotationRad, opacity })`** — check the signature.\n\n**`createTextRenderer(engine, { layers: [...], clear: false })`** — per my notes, scene 180 passes the engine directly. OK.\n\n**`createFpsInput(canvas)`** — my own code. Let me check fps-input.ts — could `createFpsInput` be registering listeners at module level? No, it's a factory.\n\n**`createFpsAudio()`** — my own code, lazy initialization.\n\nHmm, everything looks fine. So let me check loader.js — maybe the spinner hides on a different condition and the scene is actually loading but the spinner is stuck for a different reason. For example, loader.js hides the spinner on the first `requestAnimationFrame` after the canvas is ready, or it waits for `data-ready`.\n\nLet me read loader.js and grep for the banner.\n","seq":102149,"t":5658176,"text":"\n\nAll the assets 