\n\nWhat about `createNavigationPluginAsync`? Same reasoning — if it rejects, the error would be displayed.\n\nSo... what about `registerScene`? Looking at the bundled scene46 registerScene: it awaits `Promise.all(n.ye.we.map(t=>t.Ye?.())...)` — renderable pipeline construction. In dev (source), same code. If some pipeline construction promise never resolves... for example the thin-instance module's `createThinInstanceFragment`... that's just a dynamic import, resolves immediately.\n\nAnd `startEngine` — awaits the first frame via rAF. If rAF never fires... rAF fires on any page. Unless the page is backgrounded. The user is looking at the page, so rAF fires.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Actually — let me look at the startEngine promise in scene46's code more carefully:\n\n```js\nt.ie = o => { ...; n && (n=!1, e()); t.se = requestAnimationFrame(t.ie) }\n```\n\nThe frame callback runs the full tick: for each surface, `_update()`, `_record()`, submit. If any of those throw inside the rAF callback, the exception propagates to window's error handler → the loader should display an error box. The user says no error box. So the tick isn't throwing.\n\nWait... unless the tick throws asynchronously (a rejected promise inside the tick that isn't awaited) → `unhandledrejection` → the loader displays the error. The user says no error box.\n\nOK, so let me think about what could silently hang in scene252:\n\n1. **`createFpsTerrain(engine, world)`** — creates 1024 static bodies synchronously. Each `createPhysicsBody` + `createPhysicsShape` + `setPhysicsBodyShape` is a wasm call. 1024 × 3 wasm calls — could take several seconds, but not infinite. While it's running, the main thread is blocked → the spinner animation freezes. The user says \"stuck on the spinner\" — the screenshot shows a spinner arc, which could be frozen. If it's taking forever... no, wasm calls are fast. Even 3000 wasm calls should be under a second.\n\n2. **`createNavMesh`** — recast navmesh generation from a 33×33 grid (2048 triangles). Fast.\n\nHmm, wait a moment. Let me reconsider #1: what if `createFpsTerrain` is stuck in an infinite loop? For example, my value-noise implementation has an unbounded loop, or the index surgery... no, destruction isn't called at init.\n\nActually — wait. I should look at fps-terrain.ts. There might be a bug like an infinite loop in the noise grid generation, or a `while` that never terminates. Let me read it.\n\nAlso `fps-input.ts` and `fps-audio.ts` — createFpsInput(canvas) adds event listeners; no problem.\n\nBut actually, hmm, let me reconsider the hang more carefully. Key insight: **the spinner in the screenshot is animating** (the user says \"stuck on the spinner\" — and the arc is at about the 7 o'clock position; from a single screenshot you can't tell if it's animating, but the user described it as \"spinning and never reaching the game\").\n\nIf the main thread is blocked (synchronous infinite loop), the spinner freezes and the page becomes unresponsive. If an await never resolves, the spinner keeps animating.\n\nGiven that the user described it as spinning forever, I lean toward an await that never resolves.\n\nCandidates: `HavokPhysics`, `createNavigationPluginAsync`, `loadFont`, `registerScene`, `startEngine`.\n\nWait — actually, hold on. Let me re-read the code. There's a subtle issue:\n\n```ts\nconst nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n```\n\nHow does scene170 call this? Let me check scene170/171 to confirm the exact idiom. My notes say \"the bot uses createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => '/recast-navigation.wasm' }) — scenes 170/171 confirm it as the established lab idiom\". OK.\n\nAnd `loadFont(\"/fonts/Inter.ttf\")` — how does scene180 call it? Probably `loadFont(\"/fonts/Inter.ttf\")`. OK.\n\nHmm, so what about `registerScene` — could it hang if a material's pipeline builder is waiting for something? In scene252, materials are: standard material for terrain, standard for botMesh (thin instances), standard for debrisMesh (thin instances). Text layers for the text renderer.\n\nWait — the **text renderer**! `createTextRenderer(engine, { layers: [...], clear: false })` — I create this before `registerScene`. And `registerTextRenderer(tr)` is after registerScene.\n\nHmm, but how does registerScene interact with the text renderer? It doesn't — the text renderer is a separate rendering context.\n\nOK, another thought — **`createTextRenderer`** might need the engine's canvas... let me look at the text-renderer code from turn 6:\n\n```ts\nexport function createTextRenderer(surface: SurfaceContext, opts: TextRendererOptions): TextRenderer {\n    const canvas = surface.canvas;\n    ...\n```\n\nPassing `engine` — EngineContext extends SurfaceContext and has `.canvas`. OK.\n\nHmm, let me think about `startEngine` — actually wait. Let me re-read the engine's start: in scene46's bundle, startEngine is:\n\n```js\nawait function(t){return new Promise(e=>{let n=!0,r=0;t.ie=o=>{...frame tick...;n&&(n=!1,e()),t.se=requestAnimationFrame(t.ie)},t.se=requestAnimationFrame(t.ie)})}(c)\n```\n\nSo the first rAF tick runs the frame. The frame tick runs `surface._update()` and `surface._record()` for each context. If the text renderer's `_record()` (GPU task) is waiting on something async that never resolves... for example, text glyph extraction is async (font parsing), and the record promise hangs...\n\nActually, this is getting too speculative. Let me take a more empirical approach: I can't run a browser, but I can add lightweight, non-invasive instrumentation. The cleanest thing: the scene already writes `canvas.dataset.initMs` and `dataset.ready` at the end. I can add a `console.warn` per stage — GUIDANCE allows console.warn (only console.log is forbidden). Then the user reloads and tells me which stage was reached. That's the fastest path to diagnosis.\n\nBut wait — before adding debug code, let me look at fps-terrain.ts for synchronous infinite-loop possibilities. That's a single file I wrote, and it runs before any of the later awaits (nav, font). If `createFpsTerrain` is looping forever, the spinner freezes (main thread blocked) — the user's screenshot might just be a frozen spinner.\n\nActually, let me re-read the user's words: \"stuck on the 'Initializing Babylon Lite' spinner and never reaches the game\". And \"the console only shows 'Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine', no errors\".\n\nHmm, one more thing to check: `createFpsTerrain` is called with `(engine, world)`. Let me look at fps-terrain.ts's signature and implementation. I read it earlier (turn 6), but let me re-read it now — specifically looking for an infinite loop or something that could hang.\n\nAlso, let me check how scene170 creates the nav plugin (async signature), and scene180's loadFont usage — to rule out signature mismatches. A signature mismatch would throw (error box), not hang. So those are less likely.\n\nLet me read fps-terrain.ts in full.\n","seq":104144,"t":5792144,"text":"\n\nThe loader hides the spinner when `canvas.dataset.ready` gets set, and it should display a red error box on any `error`/`unhandledrejection` — the fact that neither is happening means `main()` is stuck waiting on something (or the main thread is stuck in a synchronous loop) *without* throwing an exception. The engine banner proves that `createEngine` has completed. Next suspects: `createFpsTerrain` (synchronous — 1024 bodies, noise grid), or one of the awaits (Havok, nav plugin, font, registerScene, startEngine). Let me take a look at `fps-terrain.ts`.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":90112,"draft_n":6881,"draft_n_accepted":1936,"predicted_ms":96940.0,"predicted_n":2919,"prompt_ms":35730.0,"prompt_n":22034},"to_request":102162,"usage":{"cached_tokens":90112,"completion_tokens":2919,"prompt_tokens":112146}}
{"arguments":"{\"path\": \"~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/lab/lite/src/lite/fps-terrain.ts\"}","id":"call_cbedf2c777354300b7a45ce5","kind":"tool_call","name":"read","outcome":"ran","request":102162,"seq":104145,"shown":"// FPS terrain — destructible ground for the Quake-style demo (scene252).\n//\n// A dense grid over a 128×128u arena, displaced by seeded low-frequency value noise\n// (amplitude ~5u), with one static box collider per 4×4u tile. Destroying a tile\n// removes its 6 indices from the mesh (index surgery + normal recompute + in-place\n// GPU re-upload via resizeMeshGeometry), removes the tile's physics body, and adds a\n// navmesh obstacle so bots route around the hole.\n\nimport {\n    addBoxObstacle,\n    createMeshFromData,\n    createPhysicsBody,\n    createPhysicsShape,\n    createTransformNode,\n    removePhysicsBody,\n    resizeMeshGeometry,\n    setPhysicsBodyShape,\n    setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask,\n    setPhysicsShapeMaterial,\n    PhysicsMotionType,\n    PhysicsShapeType,\n} from \"babylon-lite\";\nimport { computeNormals } from \"babylon-lite/mesh/compute-normals.js\";\nimport { createFlatGroundData } from \"babylon-lite/mesh/create-ground.js\";\nimport type { EngineContext, Mesh, NavigationPlugin, ObstacleHandle, PhysicsBody, PhysicsWorld } from \"babylon-lite\";\n\n/** Arena side length in metres. */\nexport const ARENA_SIZE = 128;\n/** Tile footprint in metres (square). */\nexport const TILE_SIZE = 4;\n/** Tiles per axis. */\nexport const TILE_COUNT = ARENA_SIZE / TILE_SIZE;\n/** Noise amplitude in metres — gentle enough to keep flat-topped tile boxes close to the mesh. */\nexport const NOISE_AMPLITUDE = 5;\n/** Collision layer bit for terrain tile boxes (used by bullet raycasts). */\nexport const TERRAIN_LAYER = 1;\n\n/** One destructible tile of the arena. */\nexport interface FpsTile {\n    /** Tile column (0..TILE_COUNT-1), X axis. */\n    tx: number;\n    /** Tile row (0..TILE_COUNT-1), Z axis. */\n    tz: number;\n    /** Whether the tile still exists. */\n    alive: boolean;\n    /** Static box collider, or null once destroyed. */\n    body: PhysicsBody | null;\n    /** Navmesh obstacle marking the hole, or null. */\n    obstacle: ObstacleHandle | null;\n}\n\n/** The terrain mesh plus its tile grid. */\nexport interface FpsTerrain {\n    /** The single dense grid mesh (re-uploaded in place on destruction). */\n    mesh: Mesh;\n    /** Tiles indexed tz * TILE_COUNT + tx. */\n    tiles: FpsTile[];\n    /** Number of tiles destroyed so far. */\n    destroyedCount: number;\n    /** @internal Coarse noise grid for heightAt. */\n    _noiseGrid: Float32Array;\n}\n\n// ─── Seeded value noise ──────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst NOISE_CELLS = 8; // coarse cells per axis — low frequency, long undulations\n\n/** Mulberry32 — small seeded PRNG, allocation-free per call. */\nfunction makeRng(seed: number): () => number {\n    let a = seed >>> 0;\n    return () => {\n        a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0;\n        let t = a;\n        t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1);\n        t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61);\n        return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;\n    };\n}\n\nfunction smoothstep(t: number): number {\n    return t * t * (3 - 2 * t);\n}\n\n/** Bilinear value noise over the coarse grid, scaled to ±NOISE_AMPLITUDE. */\nfunction noiseHeight(x: number, z: number, grid: Float32Array): number {\n    const u = ((x + ARENA_SIZE / 2) / ARENA_SIZE) * NOISE_CELLS;\n    const v = ((z + ARENA_SIZE / 2) / ARENA_SIZE) * NOISE_CELLS;\n    const u0 = Math.min(Math.floor(u), NOISE_CELLS - 1);\n    const v0 = Math.min(Math.floor(v), NOISE_CELLS - 1);\n    const su = smoothstep(u - u0);\n    const sv = smoothstep(v - v0);\n    const n = NOISE_CELLS + 1;\n    const a = grid[v0 * n + u0]! * (1 - su) + grid[v0 * n + u0 + 1]! * su;\n    const b = grid[(v0 + 1) * n + u0]! * (1 - su) + grid[(v0 + 1) * n + u0 + 1]! * su;\n    return (a * (1 - sv) + b * sv) * NOISE_AMPLITUDE;\n}\n\n/** Ground height at a world (x, z) point — for spawning and bot logic. */\nexport function terrainHeightAt(terrain: FpsTerrain, x: number, z: number): number {\n    return noiseHeight(x, z, terrain._noiseGrid);\n}\n\n// ─── Construction ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * Build the FPS terrain: one dense grid mesh plus a static box collider per tile.\n * The caller adds `terrain.mesh` to the scene.\n */\nexport function createFpsTerrain(engine: EngineContext, world: PhysicsWorld, seed = 1337): FpsTerrain {\n    const rng = makeRng(seed);\n    const grid = new Float32Array((NOISE_CELLS + 1) * (NOISE_CELLS + 1));\n    for (let i = 0; i < grid.length; i++) {\n        grid[i] = rng() * 2 - 1;\n    }\n\n    const ground = createFlatGroundData({ width: ARENA_SIZE, height: ARENA_SIZE, subdivisions: TILE_COUNT });\n    const cols = TILE_COUNT + 1;\n    for (let row = 0; row < cols; row++) {\n        for (let col = 0; col < cols; col++) {\n            const i = (row * cols + col) * 3;\n            ground.positions[i + 1] = noiseHeight(ground.positions[i]!, ground.positions[i + 2]!, grid);\n        }\n    }\n    const normals = new Float32Array(computeNormals(Array.from(ground.positions), Array.from(ground.indices)));\n    const mesh = createMeshFromData(engine, \"fpsTerrain\", ground.positions, normals, ground.indices, ground.uvs);\n\n    const tiles: FpsTile[] = [];\n    for (let tz = 0; tz < TILE_COUNT; tz++) {\n        for (let tx = 0; tx < TILE_COUNT; tx++) {\n            // Corner heights of this tile (4 grid vertices).\n            let yTop = -Infinity;\n            let yBottom = Infinity;\n            for (let dr = 0; dr <= 1; dr++) {\n                for (let dc = 0; dc <= 1; dc++) {\n                    const y = ground.positions[((tz + dr) * cols + (tx + dc)) * 3 + 1]!;\n                    if (y > yTop) {\n                        yTop = y;\n                    }\n                    if (y < yBottom) {\n                        yBottom = y;\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            const cx = -ARENA_SIZE / 2 + tx * TILE_SIZE + TILE_SIZE / 2;\n            const cz = -ARENA_SIZE / 2 + tz * TILE_SIZE + TILE_SIZE / 2;\n            // Box top sits at the tile's highest corner so characters never fall through;\n            // a minimum half-height keeps the box non-degenerate on flat tiles.\n            const halfH = Math.max((yTop - yBottom) / 2, 0.5);\n            const node = createTransformNode(\"fpsTile\", cx, yTop - halfH, cz);\n            const body = createPhysicsBody(world, node, PhysicsMotionType.STATIC);\n            const shape = createPhysicsShape(world, {\n                type: PhysicsShapeType.BOX,\n                parameters: { extents: { x: TILE_SIZE / 2, y: halfH, z: TILE_SIZE / 2 } },\n            });\n            setPhysicsBodyShape(world, body, shape);\n            // Low friction for Quake-style ground slide; no bounce.\n            setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, 0.1, 0);\n            setPhysicsShapeFilterMembershipMask(world, shape, TERRAIN_LAYER);\n            tiles.push({ tx, tz, alive: true, body, obstacle: null });\n        }\n    }\n\n    return { mesh, tiles, destroyedCount: 0, _noiseGrid: grid };\n}\n\n/** Look up the tile containing a world (x, z) point, or null outside the arena. */\nexport function tileFromPoint(terrain: FpsTerrain, x: number, z: number): FpsTile | null {\n    const tx = Math.floor((x + ARENA_SIZE / 2) / TILE_SIZE);\n    const tz = Math.floor((z + ARENA_SIZE / 2) / TILE_SIZE);\n    if (tx < 0 || tx >= TILE_COUNT || tz < 0 || tz >= TILE_COUNT) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    return terrain.tiles[tz * TILE_COUNT + tx]!;\n}\n\n/**\n * Destroy one tile: drop its 6 indices from the grid mesh, recompute normals, re-upload\n * in place, remove its static body, and mark the hole on the navmesh.\n */\nexport function destroyTerrainTile(terrain: FpsTerrain, engine: EngineContext, world: PhysicsWorld, nav: NavigationPlugin | null, tile: FpsTile): void {\n    if (!tile.alive) {\n        return;\n    }\n    tile.alive = false;\n    terrain.destroyedCount++;\n\n    const mesh = terrain.mesh;\n    const m = mesh as unknown as { _cpuIndices?: Uint32Array; _cpuPositions?: Float32Array; _cpuUvs?: Float32Array };\n    const old = m._cpuIndices;\n    if (!old) {\n        return;\n    }\n    const start = (tile.tz * TILE_COUNT + tile.tx) * 6;\n    const next = new Uint32Array(old.length - 6);\n    next.set(old.subarray(0, start), 0);\n    next.set(old.subarray(start + 6), start);\n    const normals = new Float32Array(computeNormals(Array.from(m._cpuPositions!), Array.from(next)));\n    resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, m._cpuPositions!, normals, next, m._cpuUvs);\n\n    if (tile.body) {\n        removePhysicsBody(world, tile.body);\n        tile.body = null;\n    }\n    if (nav) {\n        tile.obstacle = addBoxObstacle(nav, { x: -ARENA_SIZE / 2 + tile.tx * TILE_SIZE + TILE_SIZE / 2, y: 0, z: -ARENA_SIZE / 2 + tile.tz * TILE_SIZE + TILE_SIZE / 2 }, { x: TILE_SIZE / 2, y: 2, z: TILE_SIZE / 2 }, 0);\n    }\n}","t":5792167,"turn":9}
