{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"`createNavigationPluginAsync` awaits `core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }))` — emscripten initialization. If the wasm fetch fails, it rejects → error box. Asset is 200. So it should resolve.\n\nEverything I've checked so far either resolves or throws. So the hang must be in one of the following:\n1. `registerScene(scene)` — the await for material pipeline processing\n2. `startEngine(engine)` — the await for the first frame\n\nOr, something in the synchronous section is actually blocking the main thread (and the user just perceives it as \"stuck on the spinner\").\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider #2 more carefully. `startEngine` starts the rAF loop and resolves after the first frame. The first frame runs all contexts' `_update()` and `_record()`.\n\n**The text renderer!** `registerTextRenderer(tr)` — registered after `registerScene`. On the first frame, the text renderer's `_record()` runs. What does it do? If it's waiting on glyph extraction (a Promise) that never resolves...\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. TextRenderer's `_record()` — in the frame loop (engine.ts:512):\n\n```ts\nconst ctxs = surface._renderingContexts;\nfor (let j = 0; j < ctxs.length; j++) {\n    const s = ctxs[j]!;\n    s._update();\n    drawCalls += s._drawCallsPre;\n    drawCalls += s._record();\n}\n```\n\nIf `s._record()` throws, the error propagates through the rAF callback → window error → loader's error box. The user says there's no error box.\n\nHmm, so what if `startEngine`'s Promise resolves on the first frame, but the frame itself hangs on an internal await? No — the frame loop is synchronous (record + submit).\n\nOkay, another thought — what if `registerScene` is the problem? Let me look at registerScene's source in dev (scene-core.ts). From the bundled scene46:\n\n```js\nawait (async t => {\n  const e = t;\n  for (; e.Et.length > 0;) {\n    const t = [...e.Et];\n    e.Et = [];\n    await Promise.all(t.map(async t => t()));\n  }\n  e.P.length = 0;\n  e._t++;\n  e.Bt = true;\n})(t)\n```\n\nWait — `for (; e.Et.length > 0;)` — this loop keeps draining `Et` (the pending material processing Promise queue) until it's empty. Each item in `Et` is an async function that builds material renderables. In scene252:\n\n- terrainMat (standard, terrain mesh)\n- botMat (standard, botMesh with thin instances)\n- debrisMat (standard, debrisMesh with thin instances)\n\nThe standard material builder (`jt` in the bundle): for thin instances, it does `import(\"./scene46-thin-instance-gpu-...js\")` etc. In dev, these are normal module imports.\n\nThen `await Promise.all(n.ye.we.map(t => t.Ye?.())...)` — the renderable pipeline's `Ye` (async pipeline creation). For thin instance renderables, the pipeline build might await something.\n\nHmm, I'm going in circles with speculation. Two of my strongest candidates remain:\n\n**A. `registerScene` hangs** — some material/renderable pipeline Promise never resolves.\n**B. `startEngine` hangs** — the first frame never completes.\n**C. Synchronous blocking** — 1024 bodies + navmesh build taking a very long time (several seconds to several minutes?). The user said \"stuck\" — maybe they only waited about 30 seconds. Navmesh generation from a 33×33 grid should be fast (under 1 second). 1024 static bodies — each involves HP_Body_Create + HP_Shape_CreateBox + HP_Body_SetShape + material setting... about 4–6 wasm calls each, 5000–6000 calls total. Each wasm call is a JS→wasm crossing, on the order of microseconds. Total: probably 100ms–1 second. Not a multi-minute hang.\n\nSo C is unlikely. It's A or B.\n\n**How to diagnose without a browser:** Add `console.warn` stage markers in main() — GUIDANCE allows console.warn. Then the user reloads and reports the last stage reached. That's the definitive answer.\n\nActually wait — there's a smarter approach. The scene already sets `canvas.dataset.initMs`/`dataset.ready` at the end. I could have the user run a one-liner in the DevTools console: `document.getElementById('renderCanvas').dataset` — but that only tells me the final state (nothing if main() didn't complete).\n\nStage markers are better. Let me add console.warn with a \"scene252:\" prefix at each stage:\n1. After createEngine\n2. After createHavokWorld\n3. After createFpsTerrain\n4. After createNavigationPluginAsync\n5. After createNavMesh\n6. After bot creation\n7. After loadFont\n8. After registerScene\n9. After startEngine\n\nThat's a lot of debug code, but... it's the right tool here. I'll tell the user to reload, tell me the last warning, and then I'll remove them.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, let me think about whether I can narrow it down more before asking the user for another round trip.\n\nActually — hold on. Let me look at `createNavMesh` more carefully:\n\n```ts\nexport function createNavMesh(plugin: NavigationPlugin, meshes: Mesh[], params: NavMeshParameters): void {\n    const { positions, indices } = _mergeMeshes(meshes, params.doNotReverseIndices === true);\n    _createNavMeshFromMerged(plugin, positions, indices, params);\n}\n```\n\n`_mergeMeshes` reads the mesh's CPU positions... the terrain mesh was created with `createMeshFromData`. Does it hold `_cpuPositions`? My notes said it does. And `_createNavMeshFromMerged` — builds the navmesh via recast wasm. **Solo navmesh** (no obstacles) — but wait, I'm using `addBoxObstacle` at runtime for destroyed tiles!\n\nLooking at the docs comment: \"Solo navmesh by default. If `maxObstacles > 0`, builds a tile-cache navmesh so obstacles can be added/removed dynamically.\"\n\nI didn't pass `maxObstacles` to `createNavMesh`! So I'm building a **solo** navmesh, and then `addBoxObstacle` is... what happens? If a solo navmesh doesn't support obstacles, `addBoxObstacle` might throw (→ error box, but only on the first shot, not at init time) — that's a runtime bug for destruction, not an init hang. It's not the cause of the spinner. But it's a real bug I should fix (add `maxObstacles` to params).\n\nOkay, that's a side issue (affects tile destruction, not init).\n\nSo let me think about **what's unique to scene252 compared to scenes that work** — scene40 (physics), scene170 (nav), scene180 (text), scene181 (in-scene text), scene113-115 (picking):\n\n- scene40: Havok world + physics bodies ✓ works\n- scene170: nav plugin + navmesh + crowd ✓ works\n- scene180: text renderer (standalone) ✓ works\n- scene181: in-scene text renderable ✓ works\n\nscene252 combines all of them. What combination could hang?\n\n**Text renderer + 3D scene on the same surface.** The text renderer is registered as a second rendering context. On the first frame:\n1. Scene context: `_update()` + `_record()` — builds the scene render pass.\n2. Text context: `_update()` + `_record()` — builds the text pass.\n\nIf the text renderer's `_record()` awaits something... hmm, from the engine's frame loop I saw earlier, `_record()` returns a number (draw calls) — it's synchronous.\n\nWait, actually — let me look at that frame loop code from the bundled scene46 one more time:\n\n```js\nt.ie = o => {\n  const s = n ? 0 : r > 0 ? o - r : 16.667;\n  r = o;\n  // for each surface:\n  //   e.Je?.(e)  (_update)\n  //   m(e)      (resize)\n  //   for each context: e.ge() (update), s += e.xe (drawCallsPre), s += e.be() (record)\n  ...\n  t.B.queue.submit(t.ue)\n  n && (n = !1, e())  // resolve on first frame\n  t.se = requestAnimationFrame(t.ie)\n}\n```\n\nAll synchronous. If it throws → window error → error box. No error box. So if startEngine starts, the first frame either completes (and the Promise resolves, and dataset.ready gets set, and the spinner disappears) or throws (error box).\n\nWait. That means if `startEngine` was reached, the spinner should disappear or an error box should appear. Neither is happening. So **main() never reached startEngine** — it's stuck at an earlier await:\n\n- `HavokPhysics(...)` — no (asset 200, same idiom as scene40)\n- `createNavigationPluginAsync(...)` — no (asset 200, same idiom as scene170)\n- `loadFont(...)` — no (asset 200, throws on failure)\n- `registerScene(scene)` — **possible**\n\nSo the hang is almost certainly in `registerScene` — specifically in the material pipeline processing loop:\n\n```js\nfor (; e.Et.length > 0;) {\n  const t = [...e.Et];\n  e.Et = [];\n  await Promise.all(t.map(async t => t()));\n}\n```\n\nWhat's in `Et` for scene252? The material processing Promises: for each mesh with a material, `n.Et.push(async () => { const t = await r(n, e); ... })` where `r` is the material's builder (`zt: jt` — the standard material builder).\n\nThe standard builder (`jt`) dynamically imports modules: thin-instance-gpu, thin-instance-fragment, etc. In dev mode (source), these are just module imports — they resolve.\n\nThen `buildStandardMeshRenderables` — creates renderables, and each renderable's pipeline build (`Ye`) is awaited: `await Promise.all(n.ye.we.map(t => t.Ye?.())...)`.\n\nHmm, wait — is the pipeline build awaited inside registerScene? From the bundle:\n\n```js\nawait Promise.all(n.ye.we.map(t => t.Ye?.()).filter(t => void 0 !== t))\n```\n\nYes — the renderable's `Ye` (pipeline creation) is awaited in registerScene. Pipeline creation calls `device.createRenderPipeline` — it's a Promise. If pipeline creation **fails** (e.g., a WGSL compilation error), the Promise rejects → `Promise.all` rejects → registerScene rejects → error box. The user says there's no error box...\n\nHmm, but wait — what if the pipeline creation Promise neither resolves nor rejects? WebGPU's `createRenderPipeline` always settles.\n\nOkay, let me look at the actual dev source of registerScene rather than the minified bundle. Let me read registerScene in scene-core.ts.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing. Let me re-read the user's exact words: \"Stays on the 'Initializing Babylon Lite' spinner and never reaches the game — no terrain, no HUD. The console only shows 'Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine', no errors.\"\n\nHmm, \"the console only shows X, no errors\" — did they check the console thoroughly? If a Promise rejects, `main().catch` runs `console.error(err)` and sets `canvas.dataset.error`. The loader also displays an error box via `unhandledrejection`... but wait — `main().catch(...)` handles the rejection, so `unhandledrejection` doesn't fire. But `console.error(err)` should show up in the console. The user says the console only shows the banner. So there's no rejection.\n\nOkay, one more possibility I haven't considered: **`createEngine` resolved, but the hang is before main() even starts** — i.e., in the module import graph. If one of the `babylon-lite` imports fails... no, that would show a Vite error overlay + console error.\n\nIf the hang is in **`HavokPhysics`**? Let me double-check: `import HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\"` — this is a direct package import (not the lab alias). In scene40.ts, how is it imported? Let me check scene40's import and the exact call. My notes: \"scene40 imports @babylonjs/havok with locateFile: () => '/HavokPhysics.wasm'\". Same.\n\nHmm, okay, wait, actually — let me look at this from a completely different angle. Let me check the **vite config aliases** for `@babylonjs/havok` and `@recast-navigation/*`. If vite resolves `@babylonjs/havok` differently in dev... scene40 works in the same lab, so no.\n\nAnd `@recast-navigation/core`, `@recast-navigation/generators`, `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` — scene170 works, so no.\n\nSo all the pieces work individually. The hang is in the combination. The most likely: **registerScene with this specific material/mesh combination** (thin instances + text on the same surface), or something about my scene setup that breaks the first frame in a way that doesn't throw...\n\nHmm, wait, actually — one more: what if `startEngine` was reached, and the first frame ran, but **the engine's rAF loop stopped** before resolving? Looking at the code: `n && (n = !1, e())` — the resolve happens at the end of the first frame callback, right before scheduling the next rAF. If the frame callback throws before that line, the Promise never resolves, and the error goes to window.onerror → error box. The user says no error box...\n\nBut — what if the error occurs inside a nested Promise (e.g., inside `queue.submit`'s Promise chain)? No, submit is fire-and-forget.\n\nOkay — actually, you know what? Let me stop speculating and go empirical. Stage markers via console.warn. It's the fastest, most definitive diagnostic, and it's allowed by GUIDANCE (console.warn is on the allowlist). Let me add them, have the user reload, and tell me the last marker that appeared.\n\nWait, actually, one more idea before that — let me check whether the scene252.html page itself is correct. Let me look at it. If the HTML references the wrong module path, the module script never runs... but then the engine banner wouldn't show (the banner comes from createEngine inside the module). The banner did show, so the module ran and createEngine resolved.\n\nHmm, unless the banner comes from a different module loaded by the page... let me check scene252.html.\n\nAlso — let me quickly verify how the engine's startEngine works in the dev source (not the bundle), to confirm the first-frame resolve logic. And also registerScene. Two quick reads, then I'll decide.\n\nActually, let me be efficient: read scene252.html + registerScene source + startEngine source, all in one bash call.\n","seq":107341,"t":5977964,"text":"\n\n`createNavigationPluginAsync` and `loadFont` both resolve or throw, and the assets are fine. Also, I found an actual bug along the way: `createNavMesh` only builds a **tile-cache** navmesh when `maxObstacles > 0`, but I never passed that at all — meaning my `addBoxObstacle` calls for destroyed tiles will fail at runtime. I'll fix that too. But first, let me trace `registerScene`/`startEngine` in the dev source and check my HTML page.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":119808,"draft_n":8701,"draft_n_accepted":2492,"predicted_ms":111280.0,"predicted_n":3735,"prompt_ms":2570.0,"prompt_n":1004},"to_request":105332,"usage":{"cached_tokens":119808,"completion_tokens":3735,"prompt_tokens":120812}}
