{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"In the step I just completed, I was investigating why scene252 is stuck on the initialization spinner. Main findings from this step:\n\n1. `addBoxObstacle` throws `Error(\"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with maxObstacles > 0 to enable obstacles.\")` when the navmesh was built without `maxObstacles > 0`. My scene252 calls `createNavMesh(nav, [terrain.mesh], {...})` without `maxObstacles`, and then calls `addBoxObstacle` inside `destroyTerrainTile`. This will throw on the first shot.\n\nWait — but is this the cause of the spinner hang? No, it's not. The hang is at initialization time, before any shots are fired. This is a runtime bug that will occur on the first shot, not an initialization hang.\n\n2. The loader.js overlay only disappears when `canvas.dataset.loaded === 'true'` or `canvas.dataset.ready === 'true'`. My scene sets `canvas.dataset.ready = \"true\"` after `startEngine` resolves. Also, the loader displays an error box on `window.error` and `unhandledrejection`. The user is not seeing an error box.\n\n3. `startEngine` resolves after the first rAF frame. If `renderFrame` throws, the promise doesn't resolve, and the error goes to window.onerror → the loader's error box should be displayed. The user says there's no error box.\n\n4. `registerScene` → `buildScene` runs deferred builders; `_preload` is a simple dynamic import and can't hang.\n\nHmm, but wait — the question is specifically: \"In the step you just completed, was there anything that didn't behave as expected — APIs that work differently, errors and their actual causes, limitations or unsupported features you discovered? List each item in the form GOTCHA: <what is true> — IMPLICATION: <what needs to be done differently>. Only discoveries from this step.\"\n\nThe step I just completed was the grep for `maxObstacles`/`addBoxObstacle`. What I discovered there:\n\n- `addBoxObstacle` requires a tile-cache navmesh built with `maxObstacles > 0`; otherwise it throws \"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with `maxObstacles > 0` to enable obstacles.\" My scene builds a solo navmesh (no maxObstacles) and calls addBoxObstacle in destroyTerrainTile → the first terrain shot will throw. IMPLICATION: add `maxObstacles` (and per the documentation example `tileSize: 32`, `tileSize` and `expectedLayersPerTile` are also required) to createNavMesh's parameters.\n\nLooking at line 21: `createNavMesh(nav, [...], { ..., maxObstacles: 32, tileSize: 32 });` — the documentation example shows both maxObstacles and tileSize. And line 255: \"Tile cache config: requires tileSize, expectedLayersPerTile, maxObstacles\" — so the tile cache requires tileSize, expectedLayersPerTile, and maxObstacles. Let me check what NavMeshParameters includes — `maxObstacles?: number` is at line 67. I haven't fully verified tileSize/expectedLayersPerTile, but the comment says they're required.\n\nAlso from this step: `addBoxObstacle` calls `_waitForFullTileCacheUpdate(plugin)` — a synchronous wait? It says \"the tile cache is fully updated before [returning]\". That's fine.\n\nAnything else from this step? The step before this (registerScene/startEngine investigation) was a separate step. The question says \"only discoveries from this step\" — meaning the most recent step, the maxObstacles/addBoxObstacle grep.\n\nSo the main discovery is:\n\nGOTCHA: `addBoxObstacle` (navigation.ts:647) throws \"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with `maxObstacles > 0` to enable obstacles.\" unless the navmesh was built with `maxObstacles > 0` — my scene252 builds a solo navmesh and calls addBoxObstacle when destroying a tile — IMPLICATION: tile destruction will crash on the first shot; I need to pass `maxObstacles` (plus `tileSize`/`expectedLayersPerTile` per the tile-cache config comment at navigation.ts:255) to `createNavMesh` in scene252.ts.\n\nShould I also mention that this is not the cause of the spinner hang? That's not a discovery about behavior, but contextual information. The instructions say to list the discoveries. I'll stick to the discovered facts.\n\nShould I also record this with update_record? The format of the instructions is just to list the GOTCHA lines. I'll follow the requested format. I could also call update_record to persist it — that seems useful. But the question asks to \"list each item in the form GOTCHA: ...\". I'll answer in that format, and it might be okay to also record it. I'll do both — record and then answer. Actually, the answer should be concise; I'll use update_record for persistence and list the gotchas in the reply.\n","seq":110057,"t":6162839,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":131072,"draft_n":2485,"draft_n_accepted":925,"predicted_ms":27280.0,"predicted_n":1280,"prompt_ms":1150.0,"prompt_n":205},"to_request":109646,"usage":{"cached_tokens":131072,"completion_tokens":1280,"prompt_tokens":131277}}
