{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to report the gotchas (pitfalls) discovered in the step I just completed. Let me review what I found in this step:\n\n1. **`createNavMesh`'s `addBoxObstacle` throws an exception on a solo navmesh** — I built the navmesh without `maxObstacles`, so `addBoxObstacle` throws the error \"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with `maxObstacles > 0` to enable obstacles.\" (navigation.ts:697). Implication: I added `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` to `createNavMesh` in scene252.ts, so that `destroyTerrainTile`'s `addBoxObstacle` works.\n\n2. **NavMeshParameters parameter names** — I had assumed `cellSize`/`agentHeight`/`agentRadius`/`agentClimb` from my notes, but the actual interface uses `cs`, `ch`, `walkableSlopeAngle`, `walkableHeight`, `walkableClimb`, `walkableRadius`. The scene code was already using the correct names (cs/ch etc.), so this is partially a correction to my notes. The implication is that my note-based assumptions about the parameter names were wrong, and the actual names are in NavMeshParameters in navigation.ts.\n\n3. **The spinner hang** — the loader's error box only shows window errors / unhandled rejections. `main().catch` handles rejections, so if an await rejects, it will show `console.error` and `dataset.error`, but the user isn't seeing any errors. Therefore the hang is an await that never settles, or a blocked main thread. I instrumented with console.warn stage markers. This is more of a diagnostic state than a gotcha... actually, the insight \"no error box + no console error = an await that never settles, not a failure\" is a genuinely useful finding. But is this \"discovered in this step\"? This was reasoned from loader.js's behavior (from a previous step) — in this step I confirmed via the source that `startEngine` resolves after the first rAF frame, `registerScene` awaits buildScene + preloads, and `buildScene`/`_preload` are simple dynamic imports that can't hang. The conclusion \"hang is inside an await before startEngine, or a synchronous block\" is from this step.\n\n4. **`createNavigationPluginAsync` internally lazily loads recast via dynamic import, and caches `_initPromise`** — a second call reuses it. If the first init rejects, does `_initPromise`... let me think — I didn't see the whole `_ensureRecast` code. I saw the tail: `if (locateFile) { ... await core.init(...) } else { await core.init() }` followed by `_coreModule = core; ...` and `await _initPromise`. If init fails, the promise rejects, and `_initPromise` presumably stays rejected — but the scene only calls it once, so it's not really a gotcha for my scene.\n\n5. **`loadFont` throws on fetch failure** — confirmed. It can't hang silently.\n\n6. **The long-running vite server serves stale transforms** — this is already known and recorded (r91869), not new in this step.\n\n7. **In this step, I didn't actually find the cause of the hang** — I instrumented. So the honest answer is: the main \"discovery\" is the `addBoxObstacle` tile-cache requirement (an error and its actual cause), and the NavMeshParameters naming.\n\nWait — one more: I tried to edit scene252.ts and it failed with \"0 occurrences found\" — that's because the actual file content differed from what I had assumed (the file had a camera setup section, different parameter names, etc.). This is a process note, not an API gotcha.\n\nAlso: `startEngine` is `export function startEngine(engine): Promise<void>` (not `export async function`) — I found this after previously assuming it was async in engine.ts. Trivial.\n\nAlso, in registerScene: `if (surface._renderingContexts.length > 0) { configureSwapchainOverlayScene }` — at the point of calling registerScene, the surface has no contexts yet (the text renderer is registered later), so the overlay path isn't triggered. This confirms my registration order is safe. Not really a gotcha.\n\nThe most important discoveries in this step:\n\n1. GOTCHA: `addBoxObstacle` throws on a solo navmesh (\"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with `maxObstacles > 0`...\") — since scene252's `createNavMesh` call didn't pass `maxObstacles`, the first tile destruction will throw an exception. Implication: fixed by adding `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` to the `createNavMesh` parameters in scene252.ts.\n\n2. GOTCHA: `NavMeshParameters` uses short names `cs`/`ch`/`walkableSlopeAngle`/`walkableHeight`/`walkableClimb`/`walkableRadius` (navigation.ts:43-54), not `cellSize`/`agentHeight`/`agentRadius`/`agentClimb` as I had recorded in my notes. Implication: trust the interface, not the notes. (The scene code was already correct.)\n\n3. The spinner hang itself — what I learned in this step: `startEngine` resolves after the first rAF frame (engine.ts:435), `registerScene` awaits `buildScene` + frame-graph `_preload` (both simple dynamic imports that settle or throw), and `loadFont`/`createNavigationPluginAsync` throw on failure. Since there's no error box and no console error, the stall is an await that never settles, or a blocked main thread — so I instrumented main() with `console.warn` stage markers (engine/havok/terrain/nav-plugin/navmesh/bots/font/pre-register/registered/started) and restarted the dev server (bg_ec39a3f1). Implication: user reloads and reports the last marker shown.\n\nShould I report #3 as a gotcha? It's more of a diagnostic state. The prompt says \"APIs that behaved differently than expected, errors and their actual causes, limitations or unsupported features you discovered.\" The addBoxObstacle thing is exactly \"an error and its actual cause.\" The NavMeshParameters naming is \"an API that behaves differently than expected\" (differently than my notes). The hang analysis is ongoing work, not a confirmed discovery.\n\nI'll list the two confirmed gotchas and briefly note the state of the instrumentation. Actually, the instruction says \"list each on a separate line as GOTCHA: ... Only discoveries from this step.\" I'll keep it concise — two gotchas, plus maybe the startEngine signature finding. Let me include:\n\n- addBoxObstacle / tile cache (the big one)\n- NavMeshParameters naming\n- (maybe) startEngine is a non-async function returning a Promise — trivial, skip? It's a small API-behavior discovery that corrected my assumption. I'll include it briefly.\n\nKeep it concise.\n","seq":114209,"t":6543677,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: `addBoxObstacle` throws `\"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with maxObstacles > 0 to enable obstacles.\"` (navigation.ts:697) — my `createNavMesh` call had no `maxObstacles`, so the navmesh was solo and every `destroyTerrainTile` → `addBoxObstacle` would throw on the first shot — IMPLICATION: fixed by adding `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` to the `createNavMesh` params in scene252.ts (tile-cache build; `tileSize` defaults to 32 otherwise).\nGOTCHA: `NavMeshParameters` uses short names `cs`/`ch`/`walkableSlopeAngle`/`walkableHeight`/`walkableClimb`/`walkableRadius` (navigation.ts:43-54), not the `cellSize`/`agentHeight`/`agentRadius`/`agentClimb` spellings my earlier notes assumed — IMPLICATION: trust the interface, not the notes; the scene code already used the correct short names, so no code change was needed beyond the two new fields.\nGOTCHA: the spinner stall is not a failure — `startEngine` (engine.ts:435, non-async `export function` returning a Promise) resolves after the first rAF frame, `registerScene` awaits only `buildScene` + frame-graph `_preload` (plain dynamic imports that settle or throw), and `loadFont`/`createNavigationPluginAsync` throw on failure — so with no error box and no console error, `main()` is stuck on an await that never settles or a blocked main thread — IMPLICATION: I instrumented `main()` with `console.warn` stage markers (engine → havok → terrain → nav-plugin → navmesh → bots → font → pre-register → registered → started), restarted the dev server (bg_ec39a3f1, verified serving the new transform), and need you to reload scene252.html and tell