{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The hang is at `createNavigationPluginAsync` — after \"terrain +90ms\", nothing comes out. That means `await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` never resolves.\n\nLet me look at the implementation again:\n\n```ts\nexport async function createNavigationPluginAsync(options?: { locateFile?: (url: string) => string }): Promise<NavigationPlugin> {\n    const { core, gens } = await _ensureRecast(options?.locateFile);\n    return {\n        _recast: core,\n        _generators: gens,\n    };\n}\n```\n\nAnd `_ensureRecast`:\n\n```ts\nasync function _ensureRecast(locateFile?: (url: string) => string): Promise<{ core: ...; gens: ... }> {\n    if (!_initPromise) {\n        _initPromise = (async () => {\n            const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n            const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n            if (locateFile) {\n                const wasmFactory = (await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")).default;\n                await core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }) as typeof wasmFactory);\n            } else {\n                await core.init();\n            }\n            _coreModule = core;\n            _generatorsModule = gens;\n        })();\n    }\n    await _initPromise;\n    return { core: _coreModule, gens: _generatorsModule };\n}\n```\n\nSo the candidates for the hang are:\n1. `import(\"@recast-navigation/core\")` — dynamic import of the wasm package.\n2. `import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\")`\n3. `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")`\n4. `core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }))` — emscripten initialization: fetches `/recast-navigation.wasm` (200 via curl), instantiates, and runs.\n\nInteresting point: scene170 works in the same browser — meaning all of these work there. What's different in scene252?\n\nWait... hold on. Let me think. What's different between scene170 and scene252 at this point?\n\nscene170:\n```ts\nconst nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n```\n\nscene252:\n```ts\nconst nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n```\n\nIdentical. Hmm.\n\nBut wait — is `_initPromise` a module-level singleton? Yes: `let _initPromise` is at module level in navigation.ts. This is a module-level mutable variable — but since it's per-page (each page load gets a fresh module), there's no cross-scene issue.\n\nSo why does it hang in scene252 but not in scene170?\n\nHmm, wait. Let me think about emscripten's `core.init(...)`. The recast-navigation wasm factory with `locateFile` — it fetches the wasm. If the fetch succeeds (200), it instantiates. `core.init` should either resolve or reject.\n\nCould it be... the fetch is hanging? Via curl, `/recast-navigation.wasm` returns 200. But wait — could the fetch be hanging due to some weird behavior of vite's dev server for wasm files? curl got a 200 immediately. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider. Is the hang really at createNavigationPluginAsync? The markers show \"terrain +90ms\" and then nothing. Between \"terrain\" and \"nav-plugin\" there's:\n\n```ts\nconst terrain = createFpsTerrain(engine, world);\nmark(\"terrain\");\nconst terrainMat = createStandardMaterial();\nterrainMat.diffuseColor = [0.42, 0.48, 0.4];\nterrainMat.specularColor = [0.05, 0.05, 0.05];\nterrain.mesh.material = terrainMat;\naddToScene(scene, terrain.mesh);\n\n// Player — character controller ...\nconst player = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, {...}, {...});\nplayer.staticFriction = 0;\nplayer.dynamicFriction = 0.1;\n\n// Navigation\nconst nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\nmark(\"nav-plugin\");\n```\n\nSo between \"terrain\" and \"nav-plugin\" there's: material creation, addToScene, player controller creation, and the nav plugin await. All synchronous except the nav await.\n\nHmm wait — could `createPhysicsCharacterController` be hanging? That's synchronous — it creates a Havok body. If it throws, we'd see an error. If it's just slow... 90ms for terrain (1024 bodies) is fast. A single controller should be millisecond-scale.\n\nSo the hang is `createNavigationPluginAsync`. But scene170 works with the exact same call...\n\nWait. Could it be — the user opened scene252 from the gallery, or... no, they opened it directly.\n\nHmm, let me think about `core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }))`.\n\nActually — wait. I should look at how the `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` module is resolved in the dev environment. The dynamic import `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")` — in vite's dev, this resolves via node_modules. The wasm factory (emscripten-generated) will, when called with `{ locateFile }`, fetch `locateFile(\"recast-navigation.wasm\")` = `/recast-navigation.wasm`.\n\nEmscripten's fetch: it uses `fetch()` (or `readAsync`). If the response is OK, it instantiates the wasm. That should work — scene170 proves it.\n\nCould it be... **the issue is that `core.init` is being called with the wrong binding**? `wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile })` — hmm, the factory's signature is `wasmFactory(options)`. Bind null as this, first arg is options. Then `core.init(wasmFactory)` — core.init probably calls `wasmFactory()`... wait, but the bound function already has options. `core.init` probably expects a factory that it calls with no arguments. OK.\n\nHmm, wait — but scene170 works with the exact same call! So the plugin loading mechanism itself is fine.\n\nSo what's different?? Let me think...\n\nOh, wait. I think I've got it. **Module-level state + vite's module graph.** No wait...\n\nHmm, let me think about it from another angle. What if the hang isn't inside `createNavigationPluginAsync` at all, but the marker just didn't get printed because... no, the marker prints synchronously right after the await.\n\nActually — hold on. What if `await createNavigationPluginAsync(...)` is hanging because `_initPromise` is stuck? `_initPromise` is created on the first call. If the first call's async IIFE threw synchronously... no, it's wrapped in an async IIFE, so the rejection propagates.\n\nWait, actually — let me look at the actual current source of `_ensureRecast` again. I saw it before compaction, but let me re-read the exact code. In particular, I want to check whether there's a `while` loop or some condition that could spin forever.\n\nAlso — one important thing: **the emscripten module might be waiting for something on `globalThis` or `window`**. For example, emscripten's wasm loading in the browser uses `fetch`. No problem there.\n\nHmm, but here's a thought: **what if the wasm instantiation is failing silently?** Emscripten's `instantiateAsync` — if instantiation fails, it should reject.\n\nOK, another angle: let me test the plugin loading in isolation. I can't run a browser in this sandbox... but wait — can I run the recast navigation plugin in Node? Node 18+ has WebAssembly. `@recast-navigation/core` — if it's pure JS + wasm (not WASI), it might work in Node with a `locateFile` pointing to a file path... emscripten's Node environment uses `fs.readFile` or `fetch` for wasm loading. Actually, emscripten modules support Node via `fs`. Let me try:\n\n```js\nconst core = require(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\nconst wasmFactory = require(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\").default;\ncore.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile: () => \"~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm\" }))\n  .then(() => console.log(\"OK\"))\n  .catch(e => console.log(\"ERR\", e));\n```\n\nIf this works in Node, the plugin itself is fine, and the problem is browser-specific (e.g., a fetch hanging on the dev server?). If it hangs in Node too, I can debug it directly.\n\nWait, actually — before that, let me reconsider: is it possible that the fetch for `/recast-navigation.wasm` is hanging in the browser even though curl gets a 200? curl was run from the sandbox... the user's browser hits the same server. Vite serves `/recast-navigation.wasm` from `lab/public/`. It worked in scene170 (the user said other gallery scenes render fine — including nav scenes? They said \"other gallery scenes render fine in the same browser\" — that's a general statement).\n\nHmm, wait, actually — let me reconsider the whole thing. What if the hang is actually at `createPhysicsCharacterController` (synchronous, blocking the main thread)? The spinner should freeze (not animate). The user said \"still spinner\" — I don't know if it's animating.\n\nBut wait — if `createPhysicsCharacterController` is blocking, the marker after it... no, there's no marker between \"terrain\" and \"nav-plugin\" except nav-plugin itself. `createPhysicsCharacterController` is synchronous — if it blocks forever, we'd see exactly this symptom: \"terrain\" is printed, then nothing, and the spinner freezes.\n\nHmm! That's a realistic possibility. `createPhysicsCharacterController` — creates a Havok capsule body. Why would it hang? It's a wasm call...\n\nActually, let me look at character-controller.ts to see what the constructor does. From my notes: \"the character controller proves that a TransformNode + physics body works\". It creates a node, a body, a capsule shape, and sets it up. All synchronous wasm calls.\n\nHmm, but here's a subtle point — could `createPhysicsCharacterController` register something with the scene's frame loop? No...\n\nOK, let me be systematic. I'll add finer-grained markers between \"terrain\" and \"nav-plugin\":\n\n```ts\nmark(\"terrain\");\n// ... material, addToScene\nmark(\"scene-added\");\n// ... player controller\nmark(\"player\");\n// ... nav\nconst nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync(...);\nmark(\"nav-plugin\");\n```\n\nAnd also inside the nav plugin load — but I can't modify the engine (no engine changes allowed!). Hmm, right — GUIDANCE says no engine changes... well, that was my decision for this project (\"zero changes to packages/babylon-lite\"). I shouldn't modify the engine to add debug.\n\nBut I can test the plugin load in Node to see if it hangs there. Let me try that.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider the difference between scene170 and scene252 one more time:\n\nscene170's nav plugin load happens right after `createEngine` + `createSceneContext` + camera + light + mesh. No physics world!\n\nscene252's nav plugin load happens after creating the **Havok physics world** (with 1024 static bodies + player controller).\n\nSo the difference is: **the Havok world exists first**. Could creating the recast wasm after Havok wasm cause problems? Both are emscripten modules... could there be a conflict on a shared global? Emscripten modules use `globalThis` for some things (e.g., `globalThis.__wasm_call_...` for imported functions, or memory-related stuff). Two emscripten modules coexisting is common (Havok + recast + draco + meshopt all coexist in this repo).\n\nHmm wait — actually, in scene40 (physics) only Havok is loaded. In scene170 (nav) only recast is loaded. **No lab scene loads both Havok and recast!** Scene252 is the first to do so!\n\nSo the bug might be in the interaction between the two emscripten modules. What could hang?\n\nEmscripten's wasm instantiation: `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` or `instantiate`. If Havok's module has polluted something... unlikely.\n\nBut here's a classic one: **emscripten's `fetch` for wasm uses `wasmBinaryFile`** — if `locateFile` returns `/recast-navigation.wasm`, emscripten fetches it. Fine.\n\nAnother classic: **emscripten modules check `ENVIRONMENT_IS_NODE` vs browser** — irrelevant here.\n\nHmm, what about **memory growth / `--shared-memory`**? If recast's wasm requires shared memory and the browser... no, scene170 works.\n\nOK, yet another angle — **`core.init`**. Let me actually read `@recast-navigation/core`'s init function. It's in node_modules. Let me see what `core.init` does — maybe it does something that could hang when called a second time, or when called with a bound factory.\n\nWait — actually, hold on. Let me re-read `_ensureRecast`:\n\n```ts\nconst wasmFactory = (await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")).default;\nawait core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }) as typeof wasmFactory);\n```\n\nHmm, `wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile })` — the emscripten factory is called as `wasmFactory(options)`. Bind with null this and pre-fill options. Then `core.init(boundFactory)`.\n\nWhat does `core.init` do with the factory? Probably: `const module = await factory(); ... initialize core with module`. Let me look at the actual source.\n\nActually — the key insight I keep coming back to: **scene170 works with the identical call**. So `core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }))` works in the browser. The only difference in scene252 is that the Havok world was created first.\n\nSo let me test: in Node, load Havok first, then recast, and see if recast hangs. That would reproduce the interaction.\n\nActually wait, Node and browser environments are different for emscripten modules (different loading paths: fs vs fetch). The interaction bug might be browser-specific (e.g., something on `globalThis`). Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the recast-navigation package's init code first. Let me find it in node_modules.\n\nActually — wait. Let me step back and think about what else is different.\n\nscene252 loads (in order):\n1. `@babylonjs/havok` — imported at the top of the module (static import!)\n2. `babylon-lite` — static import\n3. Then at runtime: `HavokPhysics({locateFile})` → Havok wasm\n4. Then: `createNavigationPluginAsync` → dynamic import of `@recast-navigation/core`, `@recast-navigation/generators`, `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` → recast wasm\n\nscene170 loads:\n1. `babylon-lite` — static import\n2. Then: `createNavigationPluginAsync` → recast wasm\n\nSo scene252 statically imports `@babylonjs/havok` at the top. scene170 doesn't import Havok at all.\n\nThe static import of `@babylonjs/havok` just loads the JS factory — the wasm isn't instantiated until `HavokPhysics()` is called. That happens before the nav plugin.\n\nSo the order in scene252 is: Havok wasm instantiated → recast wasm instantiated.\n\nCould instantiating recast after Havok hang? Let me think about what emscripten does at instantiation:\n- `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(fetch(url))` or fallback\n- Set up the module's memory, tables, and globals\n- Run the startup code\n\nOne classic issue: **emscripten modules that use `--imported-memory` or shared globals**...\n\nAnother classic: **`globalThis.__heap_base` or symbol pollution** — if Havok's emscripten build exports a symbol like `__wasm_call_ctors` to globalThis (some builds do `globalThis.__wasm_call_ctors = ...` for the async startup path), and recast's build uses the same global name... but that wouldn't cause a hang, just a wrong call.\n\nHmm, actually — you know what, let me look at the actual emscripten-generated code for both wasm modules and see if there's any shared global usage. The wasm module JS is in node_modules/@babylonjs/havok and node_modules/@recast-navigation/wasm.\n\nActually, a more productive approach: **reproduce in Node**. Let me write a small Node script:\n\n```js\n// Load Havok first, then recast — does recast hang?\nconst HavokPhysics = require(\"@babylonjs/havok\").default;\n(async () => {\n  const hk = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"~/.../lab/public/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\n  console.log(\"havok OK\");\n  const core = require(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n  const wasmFactory = require(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\").default;\n  await core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile: () => \"~/.../lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm\" }));\n  console.log(\"recast OK\");\n})().catch(e => console.log(\"ERR\", e));\n```\n\nNode has fetch (Node 18+), and emscripten's Node path uses fs — `locateFile` returning an absolute path works in Node (emscripten's Node loader reads files). If recast hangs in Node after Havok, I've reproduced it and can debug offline. If it works in Node, the problem is browser-specific.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: in Node, `ENVIRONMENT_IS_NODE` is true, so emscripten uses `fs.readFile` — a completely different code path from the browser's fetch. A browser-specific hang wouldn't reproduce.\n\nHmm. OK, let me think about browser-specific things:\n\nIn the browser, emscripten's wasm loading (modern builds) is:\n```js\nasync function instantiateArrayBuffer() {\n  const { instance, module } = await WebAssembly.instantiate(await inputBuffer, imports);\n  ...\n}\n// or instantiateStreaming\n```\n\nAnd before that, it fetches the wasm binary:\n```js\nasync function getBinaryPromise() {\n  if (!wasmBinary && dataURIPrefix...) ...\n  return fetch(wasmBinaryFile, { credentials: 'same-origin' }).then(...);\n}\n```\n\nCould `fetch(\"/recast-navigation.wasm\")` hang in the browser but not in curl? That would be bizarre. Vite serves it fine.\n\nWait wait wait. Hold on. Let me reconsider. What if the hang isn't at the wasm load, but at the **dynamic import** itself?\n\n```ts\nconst core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n```\n\nIn vite's dev, `import(\"@recast-navigation/core\")` — this is a bare specifier, resolved by vite's node resolution. scene170 does the same thing. It works.\n\nBut — scene252 has a static import of `@babylonjs/havok` at the top of the module. Could that break vite's resolution of `@recast-navigation/*`? No — different packages.\n\nHmm, OK, yet another angle. What if the problem is **the Havok wasm's `locateFile` callback**? `HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" })` — that's fine, it loaded (the \"havok\" marker printed).\n\nLet me look at the recast init code. Actually, let me read the source of `@recast-navigation/core`'s init function. It's in node_modules/@recast-navigation/core. Let me find it.\n\nActually — wait. I just remembered something from the working set: \"scene170/171 confirm this as the established lab idiom, and lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm already exists, served like HavokPhysics.wasm.\" So the pattern is verified. And the user said other gallery scenes render fine — presumably including scene170/171.\n\nSo in scene170: recast loads fine. In scene252: recast hangs after Havok.\n\nThe difference: Havok loaded first. Let me seriously consider the emscripten interaction.\n\nActually — you know what, let me look at the emscripten glue code for both. Specifically, search for uses of `globalThis` in both packages' JS files.\n\nLet me also reconsider: could `core.init` be hanging waiting for something like `requestAnimationFrame` or a Promise that only resolves after the engine starts? No — core.init is just wasm instantiation.\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual code. I'll search node_modules.\n\nActually, actually, actually. Wait. Let me re-read `_ensureRecast` very carefully:\n\n```ts\nlet _initPromise: Promise<...> | null = null;  // (assumed)\n\nasync function _ensureRecast(locateFile?) {\n    if (!_initPromise) {\n        _initPromise = (async () => {\n            const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n            const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n            if (locateFile) {\n                const wasmFactory = (await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")).default;\n                await core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }) as typeof wasmFactory);\n            } else {\n                await core.init();\n            }\n            _coreModule = core;\n            _generatorsModule = gens;\n        })();\n    }\n    await _initPromise;\n    return { core: _coreModule, gens: _generatorsModule };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, what if `_initPromise` was already set by something else earlier? What else calls `_ensureRecast`? Only `createNavigationPluginAsync`. In scene252, only my call.\n\nOK, so the hang is inside the IIFE: one of the three dynamic imports, or `core.init`.\n\nLet me add finer markers... but I can't modify the engine (no engine changes). Hmm — but wait, is adding a temporary debug marker to the engine a \"change\"? My project decision was zero engine changes in the final code. For debugging, I could temporarily add a console.warn to `_ensureRecast` and remove it later. That's reasonable — it's a temporary diagnostic, removed before commit. GUIDANCE says to clean up debug code before commit.\n\nActually — a better idea: I can test the plugin load in isolation from a separate page! Create a small test HTML that just does `createNavigationPluginAsync` (with a marker) — if it loads fine on its own, the problem is the interaction with Havok. And a second test page that loads Havok first, then recast — to reproduce the interaction.\n\nHmm, but that's two round trips with the user. Alternatively, add temporary markers inside `_ensureRecast` (engine file, temporary, removed later) — one round trip, and I get precise stage info: after core import, after gens import, after wasm import, after core.init.\n\nActually — wait. Before I go modifying the engine, let me look at the recast package's init code. The answer might be obvious. Let me read node_modules/@recast-navigation/core and the wasm glue.\n\nLet me do that now.\n","seq":116762,"t":6722067,"text":"\n\nPinpointed the hang location: `createNavigationPluginAsync` — the nav plugin's wasm load never resolves. Notably, **no lab scene has ever loaded both Havok and recast** (scene40 = Havok only, scene170/171 = recast only), so scene252 is the first to instantiate two emscripten modules in one page. Let me read the recast plugin's init code and the wasm glue for both packages to check for interactions.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":110592,"draft_n":12362,"draft_n_accepted":3751,"predicted_ms":107590.0,"predicted_n":5517,"prompt_ms":3520.0,"prompt_n":1920},"to_request":115020,"usage":{"cached_tokens":110592,"completion_