{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The served wasm is valid, correct content-type (`application/wasm`), and identical to the real file. So the fetch/instantiate path should work — `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` would succeed (correct MIME).\n\nSo the wasm file is fine. The hang is somewhere else.\n\nNow let me reconsider. The strongest clue is: scene170 (recast alone) works, scene252 (Havok then recast) hangs on recast.\n\nLet me now actually TEST in Node whether loading recast after Havok hangs. This will tell me if it's a pure-JS issue or browser-specific.\n\nLet me write a Node script. I need to:\n1. Load Havok esm wrapper: `import HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\"` — but this is an ESM that expects a browser (uses `fetch`, `document`). In Node, `ENVIRONMENT_IS_WEB=true` but `document` is undefined, so `scriptDirectory` handling... Havok's `findWasmBinary` uses `locateFile` if provided. We pass `locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\"`. But `readAsync` uses `fetch(url)` — in Node 18+, `fetch` exists globally. But the URL `/HavokPhysics.wasm` is relative and won't resolve in Node. So loading Havok in Node with a `locateFile` returning a relative path won't work (fetch of `/HavokPhysics.wasm` fails).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. The Node test would need to point locateFile at absolute file paths, and `fetch` of a `file://` URL... Node's fetch doesn't support file://. \n\nSo a Node test is tricky because both emscripten modules use `fetch` for the wasm binary.\n\nLet me instead take a different empirical approach: **create a minimal test HTML page** that loads recast AFTER Havok (and one that loads recast alone), serve it, and... but I can't run a browser.\n\nOK so I can't run a browser, and Node is awkward for wasm+fetch. Let me step back and think harder about the ACTUAL difference, using the code I've now read.\n\nLet me reconsider the recast `run()` function vs Havok's:\n\nRecast:\n```js\nfunction run(){\n  if(runDependencies>0){dependenciesFulfilled=run;return}\n  preRun();\n  if(runDependencies>0){dependenciesFulfilled=run;return}\n  function doRun(){\n    Module[\"calledRun\"]=true;\n    if(ABORT)return;\n    initRuntime();\n    readyPromiseResolve?.(Module);\n    Module[\"onRuntimeInitialized\"]?.();\n    postRun()\n  }\n  if(Module[\"setStatus\"]){...}\n  else{doRun()}\n}\npreInit();\nrun();\n```\n\nWait — in recast, `run()` is called synchronously at the top level (after `preInit()`), but `createWasm()` is awaited BEFORE `run()` is defined/called. So by the time `run()` runs, `runDependencies` is 0 (createWasm already completed and called `removeRunDependency`). So `run()` → `preRun()` → `doRun()` → `initRuntime()` → `runtimeInitialized=true` → `readyPromiseResolve?.(Module)`.\n\nSo the recast module fully initializes synchronously (within the async factory) after the wasm is instantiated. That's fine.\n\nNow, `core.init`:\n```js\nconst init = async impl => {\n  if (Raw.Module !== undefined) return;\n  if (impl) {\n    Raw.Module = await impl();\n  } else { ... }\n  for (const instance of instances) { Raw[instance] = new Raw.Module[instance](); }\n  for (const clazz of classes) { Raw[clazz] = Raw.Module[clazz]; }\n  ...\n};\n```\n\nSo after `Raw.Module = await impl()`, it creates instances: `new Raw.Module.Recast()`, `new Raw.Module.Detour()`, etc. This requires `Raw.Module.Recast` to be a constructor. In the recast glue, `Module[\"Recast\"]` should be set. Let me verify the recast glue sets `Module[\"Recast\"]` and `Module[\"Detour\"]`.\n\nActually, this is standard emscripten embind — the constructors are registered. This works in scene170, so it's fine.\n\nOK so the recast module itself works (scene170 proves it). The ONLY difference in scene252 is that Havok was loaded first.\n\nLet me now seriously consider: **what does loading Havok do that breaks recast's subsequent load?**\n\nHavok is also an emscripten module. When both are loaded, both have their own `Module`, `wasmExports`, `HEAP8`, etc. — all module-scoped (via the IIFE closure). So no JS variable collision.\n\nBUT — here's a subtle one: **`WebAssembly` streaming compilation**. Actually no.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Both modules call `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`. That's fine, it's stateless.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the TIMING. What if the issue is that in scene252, by the time recast loads, the page has already:\n- Created the engine (WebGPU device)\n- Loaded Havok wasm\n- Created 1024 static physics bodies (synchronous, in `createFpsTerrain`)\n\nAnd `createFpsTerrain` with 1024 bodies might be doing something that blocks or leaves the event loop in a bad state? No, it's synchronous and completes (terrain marker printed).\n\nActually — WAIT. Let me reconsider. What if the issue is that `createFpsTerrain` is SO heavy (1024 bodies) that it takes a long time, and the \"terrain\" marker is printed, but then... no, the marker is after.\n\nLet me reconsider the possibility that it's NOT a collision but that **recast navmesh build is actually the slow part** and the marker is misleading. Let me re-examine: the user says the last marker is \"terrain +90ms\" and \"no nav-plugin line.\" \n\nLine 194: `const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync(...)` \nLine 195: `mark(\"nav-plugin\")`\n\nIf \"nav-plugin\" doesn't print, the await on 194 didn't resolve. That's the plugin init (wasm load), NOT the navmesh build (which is line 196, synchronous, after the marker).\n\nSo it's definitively the wasm load/plugin init that hangs.\n\nOK let me now try the Node test but work around the fetch issue. I can:\n- Pre-fetch the wasm binary in Node (read from disk)\n- Pass it via `Module.wasmBinary` to bypass fetch\n\nFor Havok: `HavokPhysics({ locateFile: ..., wasmBinary: <bytes> })` — the esm wrapper accepts `moduleArg` which becomes `Module`. And `wasmBinary` is read: `var wasmBinary=Module[\"wasmBinary\"]`. So if I pass `wasmBinary`, it uses that instead of fetching. \n\nFor recast: `wasmBinary` is also read: `var wasmBinary;` and `getWasmBinary` uses it: `if(!wasmBinary){...fetch...}` → so if `wasmBinary` is set, it skips fetch. And `instantiateAsync`: `if(!binary&&...)` → if `binary` (wasmBinary) is set, it skips streaming and goes to `instantiateArrayBuffer(binaryFile, imports)` → `getWasmBinary(binaryFile)` → `if(file==wasmBinaryFile&&wasmBinary){return new Uint8Array(wasmBinary)}`. \n\nSo I can pass `wasmBinary` to both and test the load sequence in Node. This tests the JS glue + wasm instantiation (via `WebAssembly.instantiate`, no streaming). If it hangs in Node, it's a JS/wasm issue. If it works, it's browser-specific (streaming or resource).\n\nLet me write this Node test. I'll use the actual packages from node_modules.\n\nActually, the recast factory is `async function(moduleArg)` and `moduleArg` becomes `Module`. So I call `Recast({ wasmBinary: bytes })`. And Havok: `HavokPhysics({ wasmBinary: bytes })`.\n\nBut wait — Havok's esm wrapper is `export default HavokPhysics` where `HavokPhysics = (() => { return (function(moduleArg={}){...}) })()`. So `HavokPhysics` is a function (not async — it returns `moduleRtn` which is `readyPromise`). So `await HavokPhysics({wasmBinary})` works.\n\nLet me write the Node test. I'll use `node` with ESM (the packages are ESM). I need to import from the pnpm paths. Let me use the workspace's node_modules resolution — I'll create the test file inside the repo so `import \"@babylonjs/havok\"` resolves.\n\nLet me write it.\n","seq":124653,"t":7220960,"text":"\n\nThe served wasm is valid (correct MIME, identical bytes), so the fetch/instantiate path is fine. The strongest clue is \"recast alone works (scene170), recast-after-Havok hangs (scene252).\" Let me test the load sequence in Node by pre-seeding the wasm binaries (bypassing fetch) to see if it's a pure JS/wasm collision or browser-specific.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":96256,"draft_n":5481,"draft_n_accepted":1683,"predicted_ms":71500.0,"predicted_n":2466