{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user wants me to record any discoveries from the step I just completed. Let me look at what happened in the step I just completed.\n\nThe step I just completed was the Node test that failed with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND: Cannot find package '@babylonjs/havok'`.\n\nLet me think about what I discovered:\n\n1. The Node test failed because `@babylonjs/havok` is not a top-level dependency resolvable from `.smoke-tmp/` — it's in `node_modules/.pnpm` (pnpm's isolated store), not hoisted to the root `node_modules`. So bare-specifier imports from a scratch script don't resolve.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. The error is `Cannot find package '@babylonjs/havok' imported from .../.smoke-tmp/load-order.mjs`. This means `@babylonjs/havok` is not resolvable from that location. In pnpm, packages are only linked into the `node_modules` of the workspace package that declares them as a dependency. The root might not have `@babylonjs/havok` in its `node_modules` — it's a dependency of `packages/babylon-lite` (or the lab). So from `.smoke-tmp/` (which is at repo root), the bare import doesn't resolve.\n\nSo the discovery: `@babylonjs/havok` is not resolvable from the repo root via bare specifier — it lives in pnpm's isolated store and is only linked into the node_modules of the package that depends on it. IMPLICATION: to test in Node, I need to import via the actual pnpm path (absolute path to the .pnpm store file) or place the test script inside the package that depends on it, rather than relying on bare-specifier resolution from the repo root.\n\nThat's a real discovery from this step. Let me record it.\n\nActually, let me double check — is this the only discovery? The step was: I wrote a Node test and ran it, and it failed with module-not-found. \n\nThe key discovery: bare-specifier imports (`@babylonjs/havok`, and presumably `@recast-navigation/*`) don't resolve from a scratch script at the repo root, because pnpm isolates them in `.pnpm` and only links them into the depending package's `node_modules`. So I need to use absolute paths into the `.pnpm` store (which I already know from earlier: `node_modules/.pnpm/@babylonjs+havok@1.3.12/node_modules/@babylonjs/havok/lib/esm/HavokPhysics_es.js` and `node_modules/.pnpm/@recast-navigation+wasm@0.43.0/...`).\n\nThat's the discovery. Let me also consider: is `@recast-navigation/core` resolvable? It's a dependency of `packages/babylon-lite` (which imports it in navigation.ts). So it'd have the same issue. \n\nSo the implication: to run the Node load-order test, import via absolute `.pnpm` paths, not bare specifiers. Or, alternatively, the whole Node-test approach may be less valuable than I thought — but the discovery itself is about module resolution.\n\nLet me record this as a GOTCHA. I should keep it to what I actually discovered this step.\n\nLet me be concise and only list genuine discoveries from this step.\n","seq":125234,"t":7257953,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: Bare-specifier imports (`@babylonjs/havok`, and by extension `@recast-navigation/*`) do not resolve from a scratch script at the repo root — pnpm isolates them in `node_modules/.pnpm` and only links them into the `node_modules` of the workspace package that declares them as a dependency (e.g. `packages/babylon-lite`), so a `load-order.mjs` sitting in `.smoke-tmp/` throws `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`. — IMPLICATION: to run the Node load-order test I must import via absolute `.pnpm` store paths (e.g. `node_modules/.pnpm/@babylonjs+havok@1.3.12/node_modules/@babylonjs/havok/lib/esm/HavokPhysics_es.js` and the matching `@recast-navigation/*` store paths), or place the script inside the depending package — not rely on bare-specifier resolution from the repo root.","timings":{"cache_n":99584,"draft_n":1925,"draft_n_accepted":660,"predicted_ms":27870.0,"predicted_n":935,"prompt_ms":450.0,"prompt_n":111},"to_request":124767,"usage":{"cached_tokens":99584,"completion_tokens":935,"prompt_tokens":99