{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The Node test failed because it couldn't resolve `@babylonjs/havok` from the `.smoke-tmp` subdirectory (pnpm's strict node_modules — packages are only linked at the repo root's node_modules, and `.smoke-tmp` is inside the repo so it should inherit... actually pnpm creates a `node_modules` at the repo root, and Node walks up the directory tree, so `.smoke-tmp/load-order.mjs` should find the root `node_modules`. But pnpm's root node_modules only contains the *direct* dependencies of the root package.json. `@babylonjs/havok` is a dependency of the `babylon-lite` package, not the root. So it's in `node_modules/.pnpm` and linked under `packages/babylon-lite/node_modules`, not the root `node_modules`. That's why it's not found.\n\nLet me reconsider what constraints were surfaced in this step. The step was: I read the recast and Havok emscripten glue code, confirmed the served wasm is valid, and tried a Node load-order test which failed on module resolution.\n\nConstraints surfaced:\n1. The sandbox blocks writes to /tmp (I hit this earlier with curl -o /tmp). That's a real environment limit. Actually I did hit it: \"bash: line 3: /tmp/served_recast.wasm: No such file or directory\" — the redirect to /tmp failed silently because /tmp is write-blocked. That's a constraint.\n2. pnpm strict node_modules: root-level scripts can't import packages that are only dependencies of workspace sub-packages (like `@babylonjs/havok`), must import from within the package that depends on them or use the `.pnpm` path.\n\nLet me focus on what's a genuine constraint for the rest of the project. The user is asking specifically about constraints that the rest of the project must honor.\n\n- The /tmp write block: yes, this is an environment limit that must be honored — scratch files must go in-repo (like `.smoke-tmp`). This is already a known gotcha from earlier sessions (interview-t6-f69808-1, etc.), but it was re-surfaced in this step. Actually, the instruction says \"Only constraints surfaced in this step.\" It was surfaced in this step (the curl to /tmp failed).\n\n- The pnpm resolution issue: this is more of a testing-harness gotcha than a project constraint. But it does matter: Node scripts placed at the repo root can't import workspace-sub-package deps. That's a real limit for any scratch test I write.\n\nLet me be careful. The question is about constraints the rest of the project must honor. Let me list the genuine ones from this step:\n\n1. /tmp (and system temp) is write-blocked in this sandbox — scratch files must live in-repo (e.g. `.smoke-tmp/`) and be deleted before commit.\n2. Root-level Node scripts can't resolve workspace sub-package dependencies (pnpm strict isolation) — `@babylonjs/havok` etc. only resolve from within `packages/babylon-lite`, so any scratch load-order test must import via the `.pnpm` path or be placed inside the package.\n\nBoth are environment limits surfaced this step. Let me report them.\n\nActually, let me reconsider #2 — is it a \"constraint the rest of the project must honor\"? It's a constraint on how I write scratch/test scripts in this sandbox. It's a legitimate environment limit I must honor going forward. I'll include it.\n\nLet me keep it concise.\n","seq":126186,"t":7302911,"text":"\n\nCONSTRAINT: The sandbox blocks writes to /tmp and the system temp dir — scratch files (including curl output and Node test scripts) must live in-repo (e.g. `.smoke-tmp/`) and be deleted before commit.\nCONSTRAINT: This is a pnpm-strict workspace — root-level Node scripts cannot resolve dependencies that only exist under a workspace sub-package (e.g. `@babylonjs/havok` is a dep of `packages/babylon-lite`, not the repo root), so any scratch load-order test must import via the `node_modules/.pnpm/...` path or be placed inside the depending package.","timings":{"cache_n":99584,"draft_n":2016,"draft_n_accepted":623,"predicted_ms":26520.0,"predicted_n":911,"prompt_ms":450.0,"prom