{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"In Node, loading Havok then recast works fine (64ms). So there's NO pure-JS collision between the two emscripten modules. The hang is browser-specific.\n\nSo the difference must be in the browser environment. What's different in the browser between scene170 (recast alone) and scene252 (Havok then recast)?\n\nKey differences in the browser:\n1. scene252 uses `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` (via `fetch`), while my Node test used `WebAssembly.instantiate` (via `wasmBinary`).\n2. scene252 has a WebGPU engine created.\n3. scene252 created 1024 physics bodies.\n\nThe most likely browser-specific cause: **`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`**.\n\nWait — but scene170 ALSO uses `instantiateStreaming` (same recast glue, same path) and it works. So streaming itself isn't the problem.\n\nHmm. Unless... the difference is that in scene252, the streaming fetch happens at a different time / under different conditions.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually, let me reconsider whether the issue is that **Vite's dev server is transforming the recast wasm import differently when it's loaded as a dynamic import in a module that also has other dynamic imports**.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing. Let me reconsider: maybe the hang is NOT in the wasm load at all, but in one of the three dynamic `import()` calls. In Vite dev mode, dynamic imports of bare module specifiers like `@recast-navigation/core` are resolved and served. But what if there's a Vite pre-bundling issue where one of these imports hangs?\n\nActually, the cleanest way to get signal here is to add FINE-GRAINED markers INSIDE the `_ensureRecast` function... but that's in `packages/babylon-lite/src`, which I shouldn't modify (engine changes are off the table, and it's the user-facing reference).\n\nAlternatively, I can add markers in scene252.ts between the dynamic imports — but the imports are inside `_ensureRecast`, which is opaque.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Actually, I CAN test which specific import hangs by creating a standalone test page (not modifying the engine) that does the same imports in the same order as scene252, with console markers. But I can't run a browser...\n\nWait — I keep saying I can't run a browser. Let me reconsider whether that's truly the case. The sandbox blocks Chrome due to crashpad. But maybe I can use a headless approach via the `playwright`/`puppeteer`... no, same issue.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the problem completely differently. Let me reconsider: **what if the issue is that scene252's recast load is fine, but it's SLOW (like 10-30 seconds) because of something, and the user just didn't wait long enough?** The user said \"even after waiting.\" But how long?\n\nActually, let me reconsider the navmesh build. Wait, no — the marker is on the plugin init, not the navmesh build.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider a COMPLETELY different hypothesis: **What if `createNavigationPluginAsync` is fine, but the hang is actually in `createFpsTerrain` (the 1024 bodies) and the \"terrain\" marker is being printed but then a LATER synchronous call before line 194 is hanging?**\n\nLet me re-read lines 180-194:\n```\n180: const terrain = createFpsTerrain(engine, world);\n181: mark(\"terrain\");\n182: const terrainMat = createStandardMaterial();\n183: terrainMat.diffuseColor = [0.42, 0.48, 0.4];\n184: terrainMat.specularColor = [0.05, 0.05, 0.05];\n185: terrain.mesh.material = terrainMat;\n186: addToScene(scene, terrain.mesh);\n187: (blank)\n188: // Player\n189: const player = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, {...}, {...});\n190: player.staticFriction = 0;\n191: player.dynamicFriction = 0.1;\n192: (blank)\n193: // Navigation\n194: const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({...});\n195: mark(\"nav-plugin\");\n```\n\nSo between \"terrain\" (181) and the recast await (194), there's: material creation, addToScene, and `createPhysicsCharacterController`. These are all synchronous. If any of them hangs (infinite loop), the marker at 195 wouldn't print AND the await at 194 wouldn't even be reached.\n\nBut these are the same APIs scene40 uses (physics character controller). They shouldn't hang.\n\nActually, wait — `createPhysicsCharacterController` creates a capsule shape and a body. That's fast.\n\nSo the hang is most likely at the `await createNavigationPluginAsync` (line 194).\n\nOK, let me reconsider the browser-specific streaming hypothesis more carefully, because I think this is the answer.\n\nHere's a subtle but real issue: **`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` + Vite's dev server + the wasm being served.**\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. In scene170, recast loads fine via streaming. In scene252, it doesn't. Same server, same file, same glue. The ONLY difference is Havok was loaded first.\n\nWhat could Havok's load do to break recast's streaming load?\n\nOH WAIT. I think I might have it. Let me reconsider `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`. There's a known behavior: **`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` caches the compiled module based on the URL** in some browsers? No, that's not standard.\n\nActually, let me reconsider a different angle: **What if the problem is that BOTH modules, when using `instantiateStreaming`, and the browser has a limit on concurrent wasm compilations or the compilation is single-threaded and the first (Havok) compilation left the compiler in a bad state?** That's a stretch.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's MOST actionable. \n\nActually — I just realized I should reconsider whether the issue is that **`createNavigationPluginAsync`'s dynamic imports in Vite dev mode are the problem**, specifically the `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` subpath import. Let me check: does Vite correctly resolve `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm`? The package's `exports` has `\"./wasm\"` mapping. In Vite dev, dynamic import of a subpath that's an `exports` field should work. But what if Vite's optimizer (pre-bundling) has a stale cache?\n\nActually, the most productive next step: let me check the **Vite dev server logs** (the background task output) for any errors or warnings when scene252 was loaded. And let me check if there are any Vite transform errors for the recast modules.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe I should just check the browser console more thoroughly. The user said \"no errors.\" But maybe there are warnings, or the loader.js error box only catches certain things.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the loader.js behavior. Earlier I noted: \"loader.js only shows its error box on window error/unhandledrejection, and main().catch handles rejections (console.error + dataset.error) — so 'no errors, spinner forever' means main() is stuck on an await that never settles (or a blocked main thread).\"\n\nSo it's either:\n(a) an await that never settles, or\n(b) a blocked main thread.\n\nGiven my Node test proves the JS is fine, and the wasm file is valid, the most likely browser-specific cause is (a) the streaming instantiate never settling, OR (b) something blocking the main thread.\n\nLet me reconsider (b): what could block the main thread after \"terrain\"? If `createFpsTerrain` created 1024 bodies and that's fine (marker printed after), then... actually the marker IS printed after createFpsTerrain. So the main thread isn't blocked there.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — what if the hang is actually a **`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` that never resolves because the fetch response stream is being consumed by something else**? \n\nActually, you know what, let me just look at the Vite dev server output to see if there's any clue, and also grep the scene252 markers once more to be 100% sure about the ordering. Let me also check: is it possible the user's \"scene170 works\" is based on a DIFFERENT (cached) build?\n\nLet me look at the dev server output.\n","seq":126219,"t":7307716,"text":"\n\nNode loads Havok→recast cleanly in 64ms, so there's no pure-JS collision — the hang is browser-specific. 