{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I have the exact code. Let me make the fix: parallelize the Havok and nav-plugin loads. \n\nCurrently:\n```js\n    // Physics world (Havok V2 wasm served from the lab root).\n    const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\n    const world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -GRAVITY, z: 0 });\n    mark(\"havok\");\n\n    // Destructible terrain: one dense grid mesh + a static box per tile.\n    const terrain = createFpsTerrain(engine, world);\n    mark(\"terrain\");\n    ...\n    // Player — character controller with Quake-style slide (low dynamic friction).\n    const player = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, {...}, {...});\n    player.staticFriction = 0;\n    player.dynamicFriction = 0.1;\n\n    // Navigation — navmesh from the terrain mesh; bots are NavCrowd agents driving controllers.\n    const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n    mark(\"nav-plugin\");\n```\n\nI want to:\n1. Start the nav plugin load in parallel with the Havok load (both are independent wasm loads).\n2. Keep the `world` creation and terrain after Havok resolves.\n3. Await the nav plugin later (it's already loading in the background).\n\nLet me restructure:\n```js\n    // Physics world (Havok V2 wasm) and navigation plugin (Recast wasm) are\n    // independent loads — start them concurrently so the two wasm modules\n    // compile in parallel rather than serially.\n    const navPromise = createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n    const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\n    const world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -GRAVITY, z: 0 });\n    mark(\"havok\");\n\n    // ... terrain, player ...\n\n    const nav = await navPromise;\n    mark(\"nav-plugin\");\n```\n\nThis starts the recast load immediately (concurrent with Havok), and awaits it after the player setup. This is the cleanest fix.\n\nBut wait — I need to make sure this actually addresses the root cause. If the root cause is \"second streaming compile hangs after first completes,\" then running them concurrently means recast's compile starts while Havok's is still going. This might avoid the hang. But it's not guaranteed.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider whether I should ALSO force the ArrayBuffer path as a belt-and-suspenders. Actually, let me not over-engineer. Let me do the parallel load first, and if it doesn't work, I'll force the ArrayBuffer path.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the root cause ONE more time, because I want to be confident.\n\nLet me reconsider: is it possible the issue is that **`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` is simply not supported or buggy in the user's browser, and scene170 \"works\" because... no, scene170 uses the same streaming path.**\n\nUnless scene170's recast load ALSO uses streaming and works, which means streaming IS supported. So streaming works in scene170. The difference is Havok-first.\n\nOK so the hypothesis \"second streaming compile after Havok hangs\" is the best explanation. Parallel loading is the natural fix.\n\nBut actually — let me reconsider whether parallel loading truly avoids the issue. If the browser's wasm compiler is single-threaded and the issue is that compiling recast AFTER Havok (serially) triggers a bug, then compiling them concurrently might still serialize internally in the browser and hit the same bug. \n\nThe MOST robust fix is to force recast to use `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) instead of `instantiateStreaming`. This definitively avoids the streaming path. And since Node (which uses `instantiate`) works, this is very likely to fix it.\n\nHow to force the ArrayBuffer path from scene code? The recast factory chooses streaming if `typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming==\"function\"`. I can't remove that global. BUT — I can monkey-patch it to reject, which forces 