prit).\n- Cons: Global mutation, hacky, affects all wasm loads.\n\nI think the BEST approach is to do BOTH: parallelize AND force ArrayBuffer. But that's a lot of change. Let me start with the parallel approach (cleaner) and if it doesn't work, add the monkey-patch.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — I can give the user a definitive diagnostic AND a fix in one shot. Let me do the parallel load (clean fix) and ALSO tell the user: if it still hangs, I'll force the ArrayBuffer path.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider the whole thing from a completely different angle, because I've been assuming \"second wasm compile\" but let me double-check by considering: **what if the hang is actually in the DYNAMIC IMPORT of `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` (the emscripten glue), not the wasm load?**\n\nIn Vite dev mode, dynamic imports of bare specifiers get transformed. The `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` subpath — does Vite correctly handle the `exports` field mapping `\"./wasm\"` → `./dist/recast-navigation.wasm.js`? \n\nIf Vite's optimizer (esbuild pre-bundling) has a stale or broken cache for this subpath, the dynamic import might hang or fail. But it would fail (reject), not hang. And scene170 uses the same import successfully.\n\nSo it's not the import.\n\nOK, I'm confident: the hang is the recast wasm load via streaming, triggered by Havok loading first. Let me implement the parallel-load fix.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — I realize I should just TEST whether parallel loading works by... I can't (no browser). So I'll implement it and ask the user to test.\n\nBut let me ALSO prepare the monkey-patch as a fallback in case parallel doesn't work. Actually, let me just implement the parallel load now (it's the cleanest and most likely fix), restart the dev server, and ask the user to reload.\n\nHold on — let me reconsider whether I should do the monkey-patch instead, because it's more likely to actually work. The reasoning:\n- Node uses `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) and works.\n- The browser uses `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` and hangs.\n- Therefore, forcing the browser to use `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) is very likely to fix it.\n\nThe monkey-patch is the higher-confidence fix. Let me do the monkey-patch. But I'll make it clean and well-documented.\n\nActually, the cleanest way to force the ArrayBuffer path WITHOUT monkey-patching a global: I realize I can't easily, because the factory reads `wasmBinary` from moduleArg which I don't control.\n\nSo monkey-patching `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to reject is the way. Let me make it targeted: I'll restore it after the recast load completes, so it only affects the recast load.\n\nActually, simplest and cleanest: patch it right before the recast load, restore after. But the recast load is inside `createNavigationPluginAsync` which I await. So:\n\n```js\n// Recast's emscripten glue prefers WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming, which\n// hangs in some browsers when it's the second wasm module compiled after\n// Havok. Force the ArrayBuffer path (WebAssembly.instantiate) for the load.\nconst _streaming = WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\nWebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (): Promise<never> => Promise.reject(new Error(\"force arraybuffer\"));\nlet nav;\ntry {\n    nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n} finally {\n    WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = _streaming;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but this is still hacky. And it only works if the fallback path (`instantiateArrayBuffer`) actually works in the browser. Since Node's `instantiate` works, and the fallback uses `fetch` + `WebAssembly.instantiate`, it should work.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The recast `instantiateAsync` catches the streaming rejection and calls `instantiateArrayBuffer(binaryFile, imports)`. `instantiateArrayBuffer` → `getWasmBinary(binaryFile)` → since `wasmBinary` is undefined, it does `readAsync(binaryFile)` → `fetch(url)` → `response.arrayBuffer(