 transforms back to nodes that have never been added to the scene — unverified (runtime).\n13. Bot drift re-sync behavior — unverified (runtime).\n14. HUD TextRenderer compositing — unverified (runtime).\n15. updateDefaultTextData triggering a re-draw — unverified (runtime).\n16. Muzzle offset fully preventing self-hits — unverified (runtime).\n17. Audio SFX firing — unverified (runtime).\n18. Crosshair centering at arbitrary window sizes — unverified (runtime).\n19. Fall-through respawn + velocity reset — unverified (runtime).\n20. EYE_HEIGHT 0.7 / eye placement — unverified (runtime).\n\nBut the user's question is: \"what remains open, unverified, or assumed-but-unchecked that could affect later work?\" — should focus on items that could affect later work, i.e., the diagnosis and fix path, plus cleanup obligations. Shouldn't dump all 20 runtime items, but should include the major ones that gate the playtest. Actually, the instructions say to enumerate each on its own line as OPEN: <question>. I'll list the important ones concisely.\n\nNext steps (concrete, in order):\n1. Make the targeted fix in scene252.ts: parallelize the Havok + nav-plugin loads with Promise.all (start both wasm fetches/compiles concurrently, so recast doesn't serialize after Havok). Alternative/complement: force recast down the ArrayBuffer path. Hmm — which one to pick? Let me think about what I was about to do.\n\nActually, let me think about the best fix more carefully before listing it.\n\nLeading hypothesis: `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` never settles when it's the second wasm module. But wait — is that really the mechanism? Scene170 works, so streaming works for recast alone. In scene252, Havok loads first (also via streaming — Havok's glue also uses instantiateStreaming with a fallback). Then recast's streaming never settles.\n\nHmm, but actually, would parallelization fix a \"streaming never settles\" issue? If the browser's streaming compile hangs when two streaming compiles are in flight simultaneously… it might make it worse, or it might be fine. Honestly, the mechanism is unclear.\n\nAlternative hypothesis: the hang isn't the wasm at all — it's one of the three dynamic imports inside `_ensureRecast`. In Vite dev, dynamic imports of bare specifiers get transformed. If Vite's dependency optimizer has an issue with `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` (subpath exports) in this specific module graph (scene252 imports many more modules than scene170), the import could hang. But scene170 works with the same imports…\n\nHonestly, without a browser I can't confirm. So the practical plan:\n1. Implement the parallel load (Promise.all of HavokPhysics + createNavigationPluginAsync) — minimal change, tests the ordering hypothesis, and is also a genuine improvement (faster init).\n2. Ask the user to reload and report the last marker.\n3. If it still hangs: next diagnostic — monkey-patch WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming to reject (forcing the ArrayBuffer fallback) to isolate whether streaming is the culprit; or add finer markers. Actually, since I can't instrument inside _ensureRecast (engine code), the instantiateStreaming patch is the cleanest isolation test.\n4. Once fixed: remove the console.warn stage markers (GUIDANCE §0b), run lint:fix + lint, restart the dev server (stale watcher), re-verify the served transform.\n5. Playtest checklist for the user.\n6. Cleanup of scratch files before commit.\n7. Verify the rollup inputs / gallery card question (lab/vite.config.ts).\n\nWait — one more thing to double-check: does parallelizing create a risk that the nav plugin loads before the engine is ready? createNavigationPluginAsync doesn't need the engine — it just loads wasm. createNavMesh needs the terrain mesh. So Promise.all([HavokPhysics(...), createNavigationPluginAsync(...)]) after createEngine is safe.\n\nAlso, should I consider: if I load recast first (before Havok), would that definitively tell me whether it's an ordering issue? With Promise.all both start simultaneously — if it works, we know serialization was the issue. If it still hangs, the issue is elsewhere (e.g., streaming in this browser period, or the imports). Either way it's informative.\n\nAlso on the plan to remove markers: keep them until the fix is confirmed, then remove.\n\nOne more open item: the `mark()` helper uses `console.warn` — allowed by ESLint. Fine.\n\nAlso: the dev server bg_ec39a3f1 is running and serving the latest transform (verified). Good.\n\nOne more open item: I've never verified that the recast-only scenes 170–175 actually pass through the same `instantiateStreaming` path — yes, same glue, same path. Fine.\n\nAlso: the Node test didn't test streaming — that's part of open item 1.\n\nAnother possible open item: whether the hang could be in `createFpsTerrain`/`createPhysicsCharacterController` (synchronous) rather than the await — ruled out? The \"terrain\" marker prints after createFpsTerrain, and createPhysicsCharacterController is called after the marker and before the await. If createPhysicsCharacterController were hanging (synchronous infinite loop), the await would never start and the nav-plugin marker would never print — indistinguishable from the await hanging! Hmm, that's a real gap. But character-controller creation is the same API as scene40, and it's fast. Also, a synchronous hang would freeze the page entirely (spinner animation stops) — the user said \"still the spinner,\" suggesting the spinner is still animating (the loader.js spinner is probably CSS-animated, so it animates even if JS is blocked… CSS animations continue on a blocked main thread). Hmm, so I can't fully rule out a synchronous hang between \"terrain\" and the await. But it's unlikely. Worth listing as an open item? Maybe I can fold it into the fix verification: if parallelization doesn't fix it, the next diagnostic would include markers around createPhysicsCharacterController. Actually, adding a marker after the player creation is cheap and would rule it out. Let me add that to the plan: add a mark(\"player\") after the controller creation (temporary, to be removed later). Actually — I'm going to edit this region anyway for the parallelization, so adding one more temporary marker there is trivial and gives a definitive signal. I'll include it.\n\nSo the concrete NEXT steps:\n1. Edit scene252.ts: wrap `HavokPhysics(...)` and `createNavigationPluginAsync(...)` in `Promise.all` (start both wasm loads concurrently), move the nav await out of its current position; keep/add a temporary `mark(\"player\")` after the controller creation to rule out a synchronous hang between terrain and the nav await.\n2. Restart the dev server (the sandbox watcher serves stale transforms) and verify via curl that the served transform contains the new code.\n3. Ask the user to hard-reload scene252.html and report the last `scene252 init:` marker.\n4. If it still hangs: isolate streaming — temporarily patch `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to reject before the nav load (forcing recast's ArrayBuffer fallback) and have the user reload again.\n5. Once the hang is fixed: remove all console.warn stage markers + mark() helper (GUIDANCE §0b), run `pnpm run lint:fix` then `pnpm run lint`.\n6. Restart the dev server once more; hand the user the playtest checklist (move/slide/jump, mouse-look, shooting holes, falling through, debris, bots, HUD, audio).\n7. Check lab/vite.config.ts for whether scene252 needs a rollup input / gallery card / thumbnail, and decide accordingly.\n8. Before commit: delete scratch (fps-smoke.cjs, .smoke-tmp/, lab/.smoke-tmp/, .smoke-home/) and verify `git status --short`.\n\nWait, about the Promise.all edit: currently the code is:\n```ts\nconst hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\nconst world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -GRAVITY, z: 0 });\nmark(\"havok\");\n...terrain...\nconst nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\nmark(\"nav-plugin\");\n```\nNew code:\n```ts\nconst [hknp, nav] = await Promise.all([\n    HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" }),\n    createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" }),\n]);\nconst world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -GRAVITY, z: 0 });\nmark(\"havok+nav\");\n```\nThen drop the later nav await line and its marker (or keep a marker). Adjust markers accordingly.\n\nHmm, one concern: does `createNavigationPluginAsync` use the engine/scene? No — it just loads wasm modules. Safe to run in parallel with the Havok load.\n\nOK. Now let me write the OPEN and NEXT lines. Concise, one per line.\n\nOPEN items (things that could affect later work):\n- OPEN: Is the hang really in the browser's WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming for the second wasm (recast after Havok)? — unverified; the Node test bypassed streaming (preloaded wasmBinary) so it doesn't rule out a streaming-specific hang.\n- OPEN: Could a synchronous hang between mark(\"terrain\") and the nav await (e.g., createPhysicsCharacterController) produce the same symptom? — not yet ruled out; needs a temporary marker after player creation.\n- OPEN: Do the dynamic imports inside _ensureRecast (core/generators/wasm subpaths) resolve correctly in scene252's larger module graph? — unverified (assumed fine because scene170 works, but the graph is different).\n- OPEN: Will parallelizing the two wasm loads actually fix it? — unverified; requires a reload by the user.\n- OPEN: The console.warn stage markers in main() are temporary instrumentation — must be removed before commit (GUIDANCE §0b).\n- OPEN: Scratch files to delete before commit: fps-smoke.cjs, .smoke-tmp/, lab/.smoke-tmp/, .smoke-home/.\n- OPEN: Lint/typecheck not re-run since the marker + maxObstacles edit batch.\n- OPEN: Does scene252 need a rollup input / gallery card / thumbnail in lab/vite.config.ts, or is getHtmlInputs() auto-discovery enough? — unverified.\n- OPEN: All runtime items unv