{"kind":"delta","request":133066,"seq":134842,"t":7787527,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"content\": \"scene252 init-hang root cause: the Recast navmesh wasm (2nd wasm module) compiled via WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming after the 2MB Havok wasm stalls in the browser. Evidence: (1) Node loads Havok→recast fine in 64ms via WebAssembly.instantiate (ArrayBuffer) → no JS/wasm collision; (2) served /recast-navigation.wasm is valid (application/wasm MIME, identical bytes to disk); (3) scene252 is the ONLY scene combining both wasm modules — every recast scene (170–175) is recast-only, so no working \\\"both\\\" reference; (4) terrain init <90ms, so not the 1024 bodies. Fix applied: load Havok + nav plugin in parallel (navPromise kicked off before the Havok await, awaited after player setup) so the two wasm modules compile concurrently instead of serially.\", \"field\": \"evidence\"}","id":"call_569560d74d5b4c48ab8dee02","index":0,"name":"update_record"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The served transform has my change. The server is up and serving the new code.\n\nNow, I've made the parallel-load fix. But I'm not 100% confident it will work (it depends on whether the browser serializes wasm compilation internally). Let me prepare a fallback: if the user reports it still hangs, I'll force the ArrayBuffer path.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should ALSO add the ArrayBuffer-forcing now, to maximize the chance of a working fix in one round-trip. The tension is:\n- Parallel load: clean, likely works, but not guaranteed.\n- ArrayBuffer-forcing: higher confidence, but ugly (global monkey-patch).\n\nGiven the user has already been through multiple rounds of \"reload and it still hangs,\" I want to maximize the chance this round works. Let me think about which is more likely to actually fix it.\n\nThe root cause hypothesis: \"second streaming compile after Havok hangs.\" \n\nIf that's the exact mechanism, then:\n- Parallel load: recast's streaming compile starts while Havok's is in progress. If the bug is triggered by \"a streaming compile starting after another has COMPLETED,\" parallel avoids it. If triggered by \"a streaming compile starting after another has STARTED,\" parallel doesn't avoid it.\n- ArrayBuffer-forcing: recast uses `WebAssembly.instantiate` (no streaming). This definitively avoids the streaming path. Highest confidence.\n\nI think ArrayBuffer-forcing is the higher-confidence fix. But it's ugly.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — is there a way to force the ArrayBuffer path that's cleaner than monkey-patching `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`?\n\nActually — YES. I can fetch the wasm bytes myself and... no, I can't pass them to the factory.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. What if I monkey-patch it in a way that's clean and self-contained, restored immediately after? Like:\n\n```js\n// Recast's emscripten glue prefers WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming, which\n// stalls