{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me reflect on what I actually did in this step.\n\n1. I diagnosed the root cause: the Recast navmesh wasm (2nd wasm module) compiled via `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` after the Havok wasm stalls in the browser. I confirmed this via Node test (Havok→recast works in 64ms via ArrayBuffer), verifying the served wasm is valid, and noting scene252 is the only scene combining both.\n\n2. I made a code change: I changed scene252.ts to load the two wasm modules in parallel (navPromise kicked off before the Havok await, awaited after player setup).\n\n3. I restarted the dev server and verified the served transform has the change.\n\n4. I ran the lab typecheck (passed).\n\n5. I tried to record via update_record but it was dropped (groundedness gate kept nothing).\n\nNow, the question asks: did I make, confirm, or change any DECISION about how this project is built — an API choice, a convention, a structure, a tooling choice, a workaround?\n\nLet me think about what decisions were made in THIS step (not earlier ones):\n\n- DECISION: Load the two wasm modules (Havok + Recast nav plugin) in parallel in scene252 — WHY: a second streaming wasm compile after Havok stalls in the browser, so compiling them concurrently avoids the stall. This is a workaround / structural choice about the init sequence.\n\nThat's the main decision. Let me consider if there are others:\n\n- I confirmed the diagnosis (root cause is the streaming compile of the 2nd wasm module). But that's more of a finding than a \"decision about how the project is built.\"\n\n- I chose to keep the diagnostic `console.warn` markers for now (to confirm the fix on the next reload) rather than removing them. That's a minor decision. Actually, I did keep them — but did I explicitly decide that? I mentioned it in my reasoning. It's a decision about whether to remove debug code now or later.\n\n- I decided to restart the dev server after editing (the stale-transform gotcha). But that's a known convention from earlier, not a new decision this step.\n\n- I decided NOT to force the ArrayBuffer path (monkey-patch WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming) and instead go with the cleaner parallel-load approach. That's a decision — I weighed the two approaches and chose parallel-load over the monkey-patch. This is a workaround choice.\n\nLet me list the decisions from this step:\n\n1. DECISION: Load Havok and the Recast nav plugin in parallel (navPromise started before the Havok await, awaited after player setup) in scene252 — WHY: the 2nd wasm module's streaming compile stalls after Havok in the browser, so concurrent compilation avoids the serial stall.\n\n2. DECISION: Use the parallel-load workaround rather than monkey-patching WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming to force the ArrayBuffer path — WHY: parallel-load is high-level/clean (fits the \"scene code reads like an API demo\" convention) while the monkey-patch is a low-level global mutation; parallel-load is the first thing to try, with the ArrayBuffer-forcing as the documented fallback if it still hangs.\n\n3. DECISION: Keep the `console.warn` init-stage markers in scene252 for now (remove once the fix is confirmed) — WHY: they're needed to confirm on the next reload that all stages (up to \"started\") now print.\n\nLet me reconsider #3 — did I actually make that decision explicitly? In my thinking I said \"Let me keep the markers for now (they're useful for the next reload), and remove them once the fix is confirmed.\" Yes, that's a decision.\n\nActually, let me be careful. The instruction says \"Only decisions from this step, not earlier ones.\" So I should only list decisions made in this most recent step.\n\nThe main decisions from this step:\n1. The parallel-load fix (structural/init-sequence choice + workaround).\n2. Choosing parallel-load over the ArrayBuffer monkey-patch (workaround choice).\n3. Keeping the diagnostic markers for now (to remove later).\n\nLet me also reconsider: the diagnosis itself — \"root cause is the 2nd wasm streaming compile after Havok\" — is that a decision? It's more of a confirmed finding. But it does inform the build approach. I think it's borderline. I'll focus on the actual build decisions.\n\nLet me write these out concisely.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether #2 is distinct from #1 or just the reasoning behind it. They're related but #2 is specifically the choice between two alternative workarounds. I think it's worth listing separately because it's a genuine decision point (I had two options and picked one).\n\nLet me finalize.\n","seq":136136,"t":7867668,"text":"\n\nDECISION: Load the Havok physics wasm and the Recast nav-plugin wasm in parallel in scene252 (start `navPromise` before the `HavokPhysics` await, `await` it after player setup) — WHY: the second wasm module's `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` compile stalls after the 2MB Havok compile in the browser, so compiling them concurrently avoids the serial stall.\n\nDECISION: Prefer the parallel-load workaround over monkey-patching `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to force the ArrayBuffer path — WHY: parallel-load is high-level and fits the \"scene code reads like an API demo\" convention, whereas the monkey-patch is a low-level global mutation; parallel-load is the first attempt, with ArrayBuffer-forcing kept as the documented fallback if it still hangs.\n\nDECISION: Keep the `console.warn` init-stage markers in scene252 for now and remove them only after the fix is confirmed — WHY: the next reload needs them to verify all stages (up to \"started\") now print, and GUIDANCE §0b requires deleting debug code before commit.","timings":{"cache_n":121344,"draft_n":2324,"draft_n_accepted":918,"predicted_ms":20710.0,"predicted_n":1250,"prompt_ms":1080.0,"prompt_n":203},"to_request":135801,"usage":{"cached_tokens":121344,"completion_tokens":1250,"prompt_tokens":121547}}
