{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking me to list any constraints that surfaced in this step. Let me think about what constraints were surfaced in the step I just completed.\n\nIn this step, I:\n1. Diagnosed the root cause of the init hang (recast wasm as 2nd module after Havok stalls via streaming).\n2. Made a fix (parallel loading).\n3. Restarted the dev server.\n4. Verified typecheck and served transform.\n\nDid I learn of or commit to any constraint the rest of the project must honor?\n\nLet me think carefully. The key things surfaced:\n- The dev server's file watcher doesn't pick up edits (stale transform) — but that was already recorded in prior steps (r91869, etc.), not NEW in this step.\n- The root cause: recast wasm as second module after Havok stalls via streaming in the browser. This is more of a diagnostic finding than a \"constraint.\"\n- The fix: parallel loading.\n\nHmm, is there a constraint here? Let me think about what's a genuine constraint vs. just a finding.\n\nActually, I think the meaningful constraint that surfaced is: **scene252 must load its two wasm modules (Havok + Recast) in parallel, not serially** — because a second streaming wasm compile after Havok stalls in the browser. This is a compatibility rule that the scene must honor going forward (i.e., don't regress to serial loading).\n\nBut wait — is that really a \"constraint the rest of the project must honor\"? It's specific to scene252's init sequence. It's more of a fix/decision than a project-wide constraint.\n\nLet me reconsider. The instruction says: \"did you learn of or commit to any constraint the rest of this project must honor — a requirement, an environment limit, a compatibility rule, something that must never be done?\"\n\nThe environment limit that surfaced: **In this sandbox, a second wasm module loaded via WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming after a large (Havok) wasm module stalls/hangs in the browser** — this is an environment/compatibility limit. But actually, that's a browser behavior, not a sandbox limit.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. Did I actually COMMIT to anything? I made the parallel-load fix. I haven't confirmed it works yet (the user needs to reload). So I've committed to the approach of parallel loading as the fix.\n\nIs there a constraint? Let me think about whether the parallel-load fix implies a constraint: \"scene252's two wasm loads must remain concurrent (don't serialize them), because serial streaming compilation of the second module after Havok stalls.\" That's a compatibility rule for this scene.\n\nActually, I think the honest answer is that the main thing I surfaced is a diagnostic finding + a fix, not really a hard \"constraint the rest of the project must honor.\" \n\nBut let me reconsider — the instruction is specifically asking me to surface constraints. Let me think about whether there's one.\n\nThe strongest candidate: the browser stalls on a second streaming wasm compile after Havok. This means: **any scene that loads multiple wasm modules should load them in parallel (or force the ArrayBuffer path), not serially.** That's a compatibility rule.\n\nBut actually, I'm not 100% sure the parallel fix works yet. So I shouldn't over-commit to it as a constraint.\n\nLet me reconsider whether I should say NONE. The step was mostly diagnosis + a fix attempt. The \"constraint\" aspect is weak.\n\nActually, I think there IS a legitimate constraint that surfaced: the dev-server stale-transform issue means I must restart the dev server after editing scene files before asking the user to test. But that was already recorded in prior steps (r91869, interview-t6-f92969-1, etc.), so it's not NEW in this step.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's genuinely NEW in this step:\n1. Root cause: recast (2nd wasm) streaming after Havok stalls in browser.\n2. Fix: parallel loading.\n3. Node test proves no JS collision.\n\nIs #1 a constraint? It's a finding about the environment/browser. It implies: \"don't load the recast wasm serially after Havok via streaming.\" That's a compatibility rule for scene252.\n\nI think the most honest answer is that there's a compatibility rule that surfaced: scene252 must load its two wasm modules concurrently (or via the ArrayBuffer path), because a second streaming wasm compile after Havok stalls in the browser. \n\nBut I'm hesitant because I haven't confirmed the fix works. However, the FINDING (that serial streaming of the 2nd module after Havok stalls) is a real constraint regardless of whether parallel fixes it — it tells us what NOT to do (don't serialize).\n\nLet me go with surfacing that as a constraint. Actually, let me reconsider the exact wording. The constraint is really: \"In this environment, loading a second wasm module via WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming after the Havok wasm stalls; scene252 must load its wasm modules in parallel (or force the ArrayBuffer path) rather than serially.\"\n\nHmm, but actually I want to be careful not to overstate. Let me reconsider whether I \"committed to\" anything. I committed to the parallel-load fix. I have NOT committed to the ArrayBuffer-forcing (that's a fallback I mentioned).\n\nI think the cleanest honest answer: there's a compatibility rule that surfaced — scene252's two wasm loads must stay concurrent (not serialized), because a second streaming wasm compile after Havok stalls in the browser. This is a real constraint the scene must honor.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether this is really a \"constraint the rest of the project must honor\" or just a scene-specific fix. The phrase \"the rest of this project\" suggests project-wide. This finding is scene252-specific (it's the only scene with two wasm modules). So it's not really project-wide.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, and that the main output of this step was a diagnosis + fix (not a hard constraint), I'm now leaning toward saying there's a scene-specific compatibility rule but it's borderline.\n\nLet me make a decision: I'll surface the one genuine compatibility rule that emerged — that scene252 must not load its two wasm modules serially (2nd streaming compile after Havok stalls in the browser). This is a real \"must never do\" (don't serialize) that the scene must honor. I'll phrase it as a constraint.\n\nActually, I realize I should be careful. The user is doing a \"pause\" check to make sure I'm tracking constraints. The honest, useful answer is to surface the browser compatibility limit I discovered. Let me phrase it precisely.\n\nCONSTRAINT: scene252 must load its two wasm modules (Havok + Recast) concurrently — a second wasm module compiled via WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming after the ~2MB Havok wasm stalls/hangs in the browser (verified: Node's ArrayBuffer path handles the same sequence fine, so it's the serial streaming compile that's the problem).\n\nThat's the constraint. Let me also consider: is there a \"must never do\" here? \"Never serialize the recast load after Havok.\" Yes.\n\nI'll surface that one constraint. I don't think there are others that are genuinely NEW in this step (the dev-server restart, sandbox limits, etc. were all recorded before).\n\nLet me write it concisely.\n","seq":136679,"t":7902593,"text":"\n\nCONSTRAINT: scene252 must load its two wasm modules (Havok + Recast) concurrently, not serially — a second wasm module compiled via `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` after the ~2MB Havok wasm stalls/hangs in the browser (Node's `WebAssembly.instantiate`/ArrayBuffer path handles the same Havok→recast sequence fine in 64ms, so the serial streaming compile is the culprit; the fix is the parallel `navPromise` load, and if that proves insufficient the fallback is forcing the ArrayBuffer path by making `instantiateStreaming` reject for the recast load).","timings":{"cache_n":121344,"draft_n":3738,"draft_n_accepted":1191,"predicted_ms":33360.0,"predicted_n":1725,"prompt_ms":1100.0,"prompt_n":180},"to_request":136142,"usage":{"cached_tokens":