{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The transform looks complete and correct. The module script ends properly with `main().catch(...)`. So the served transform is fine.\n\nSo why does the user see no banner and no markers? The transform is correct. The module should execute.\n\nWait — let me reconsider. The user says \"no 'Babylon Lite v0.1.0' banner and no scene252 init markers at all, not even 'engine'.\" And \"no errors.\"\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider what could cause the module to not execute at all, without errors:\n\n1. **The module script never loaded** — but that would typically show a network error or the loader's error box.\n2. **The module loaded but `main()` threw synchronously before the first marker** — but the catch would set dataset.error + console.error.\n3. **A browser caching issue** — the user reloaded 3 times, but maybe a stale service worker or disk cache served an old/broken version.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider the most important clue: **the user says \"no errors.\"** But if the module failed to load or execute, there would usually be an error. Unless...\n\nActually — let me reconsider. What if the issue is that **`createNavigationPluginAsync` is now called at the top of `main()`, and it triggers a dynamic import that Vite resolves, but this causes the whole module graph to fail in the browser?** No, that doesn't make sense — the same import worked before.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider. Let me re-read the user's report: \"the console shows only the two '[vite] connecting / connected' lines — no 'Babylon Lite v0.1.0' banner and no scene252 init markers at all, not even 'engine'.\"\n\nSo the Vite HMR client connected (the page loaded, the HMR websocket connected). But the module script didn't run.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — maybe the issue is a **module load failure** that IS showing as an error but the user didn't notice, OR the loader's error box. But the user explicitly says \"no errors.\"\n\nActually — let me reconsider a subtle possibility. What if calling `createNavigationPluginAsync` **before** `await HavokPhysics` causes a problem where the recast wasm load and the Havok wasm load now START at the same time, and this causes a browser-level issue (e.g., two concurrent `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` calls) that throws an unhandled error or blocks?\n\nBut that still doesn't explain why `mark(\"engine\")` (which is BEFORE my change) doesn't print.\n\nWait — unless the module failed to load entirely. Let me reconsider: is it possible that my edit introduced a syntax error that Vite's transform handles but the browser rejects? No — the transform looks clean and complete.\n\nLet me reconsider from a different angle. **Maybe the user's browser cached the OLD module (before my markers were added) and is now serving a version that hangs differently.** But the user said \"reloaded three times\" — a hard reload would bypass cache.\n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider the timeline:\n- Before this session: user saw spinner, banner + 3 markers (engine/havok/terrain).\n- I added more markers (nav-plugin, navmesh, bots, font, pre-register, registered, started) and the maxObstacles fix.\n- User reloaded: saw engine/havok/terrain, stuck after terrain.\n- I made the parallel-load change (navPromise before HavokPhysics).\n- User reloaded 3x: now sees NOTHING — no banner, no markers.\n\nSo my parallel-load change caused the module to not execute at all. But the transform is correct...\n\nOH WAIT. I think I might have it. Let me reconsider the **order of operations** with the parallel load. \n\nWhen I call `createNavigationPluginAsync(...)` at the top of `main()`, it starts loading the recast wasm via `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`. This is now happening **concurrently** with... no wait, it's BEFORE `await HavokPhysics`. So the recast load starts first, then Havok starts.\n\nBut here's the thing: `createNavigationPluginAsync` is an async function. When called, it