HTML page references a different module path, or there's a caching layer.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the possibility that **the browser is caching the old module script**. The module script in the HTML is `<script type=\"module\" src=\"/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\">`. Vite serves this with a transform. If the browser cached the OLD transform (from before I added markers), and the user did a soft reload (not hard), they'd get the old version. But the user said \"reloaded three times\" — if it was a hard reload (Cmd+Shift+R), cache would be bypassed.\n\nBut wait — the user's report BEFORE this change (in the previous session) showed the markers (engine/havok/terrain). So the browser WAS getting my markers. Now after my parallel-load change, they get NOTHING. \n\nHmm, this is really confusing. Let me think about what's DIFFERENT now:\n1. I changed the module (parallel load).\n2. I restarted the dev server (bg_08df777b).\n\nWait — could the dev server restart have caused the issue? Let me check: maybe the new server (bg_08df777b) is serving a BROKEN version because of some state issue. But I curled it and got the correct transform.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. Let me check: is it possible that the module script is now failing to LOAD because of a dependency that Vite can't resolve? The transform I curled is the entry module. But it imports other modules (fps-terrain.js, fps-audio.js, fps-input.js, @babylonjs/havok, babylon-lite). If one of THOSE imports fails to transform, the whole module graph fails.\n\nBut those didn't change. Unless... the restart caused Vite to re-transform them and something broke.\n\nActually — let me step back and think about the SIMPLEST explanation: **the user's browser cached the old module (pre-parallel-load) and is now hanging at a different point, OR the parallel-load change itself is the problem.**\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider. Let me re-read the user's exact words: \"Worse now: still the spinner, and the console shows only the two '[vite] connecting / connected' lines — no 'Babylon Lite v0.1.0' banner and no scene252 init markers at all, not even 'engine'.\"\n\n\"not even 'engine'\" — this is the key. The FIRST marker (`mark(\"engine\")`) is not printing. And the banner (inside `createEngine`) is not printing.\n\nFor `createEngine` to not print its banner, it must not have been called or must have not completed.\n\nBut `createEngine` is the FIRST await in `main()`. For it to not run, `main()` must not have started, OR the module must not have executed.\n\nThe most likely explanation: **the module script failed to load or execute, and the error is not being surfaced** (either the user missed it, or it's a type of error the loader's error box doesn't catch).\n\nActually — WAIT. I bet I know what happened. Let me reconsider the **Vite dev server** behavior. When I restarted the server (bg_08df777b), the server re-reads all files. But there's a subtlety: **the browser might have cached the HMR connection and the old module graph.** When the server restarts, the old HMR connection dies, and the browser reconnects (the \"[vite] connecting / connected\" lines). But the module scripts might be served from the browser's cache if they have long cache headers.\n\nHmm, but Vite dev modules typically have `Cache-Control: no-cache` or similar.\n\nActually — let me reconsider a COMPLETELY different possibility. What if **my parallel-load change introduced a subtle issue where `createNavigationPluginAsync` is called, and it now runs its body synchronously up to the first await, and that body includes a top-level side effect that fails?**\n\nNo — `createNavigationPluginAsync` worked before (in the serial version, it was called later but still called). Moving it earlier shouldn't break it.\n\nOK let me take a totally different approach. Let me reconsider: **maybe the problem is NOT my code change at all.** Maybe the user's browser hit a transient issue (e.g., the dev server was briefly unavailable during the restart, and the module failed to load, and the browser cached the failure).\n\nActually — the cleanest way to resolve this: **I should make the diagnostic more robust so that even if something fails, the user can see WHERE it failed.** But the user says no errors...\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider the possibility that **the module is not executing because of a JavaScript error that occurs during module evaluation (top-level), before `main()` is even called.**\n\nWhen does top-level code run? The module's top-level code is:\n1. Imports (resolved).\n2. Constant declarations (PHYSICS_FPS, etc.).\n3. `interface Bot {...}` (type-only, erased).\n4. `function rayVsCapsule(...)` (declaration, hoisted).\n5. `async function main() {...}` (declaration, hoisted).\n6. `main().catch(...)` (the call).\n\nNone of this should throw. `main().catch(...)` is called, and it starts executing `main()`. The first line is `const __initStart = performance.now();`, then `const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;`, then `const mark = ...`, then `const engine = await createEngine(canvas);`.\n\n`createEngine(canvas)` — this is where the banner is logged. If `createEngine` throws synchronously (before the banner), the promise rejects, and `main().catch` catches it → `console.error(err)` + `dataset.error`. The user would see a console error.\n\nBut the user says NO errors. So `createEngine` is either not being called, or it's hanging (not rejecting).\n\nHmm wait — could `createEngine` be HANGING now? Before, it completed (banner printed). Now it doesn't print the banner. \n\n`createEngine` does:\n```\nconst adapter = await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter();\n... \nconst device = await adapter.requestDevice({requiredFeatures, requiredLimits});\nconsole.log(\"Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine\");  // ← banner\n...\n```\n\nSo the banner is logged AFTER `requestDevice` resolves. If `requestDevice` is now hanging, the banner wouldn't print.\n\nBut why would `requestDevice` hang now? It worked before. My change is AFTER `createEngine`...\n\nUNLESS — the module is not even reaching `createEngine` because `main()` isn't being called. \n\nOK, I'm going in circles. Let me take a step back and think about what's MOST LIKELY and what I can actually DO.\n\nGiven:\n- The served transform is correct (verified by curl).\n- The user sees no banner, no markers, no errors.\n- This got WORSE after my parallel-load change.\n\nThe most pragmatic hypothesis: **my parallel-load change is somehow causing the module to not execute, OR the browser is caching a broken state.** \n\nActually — let me reconsider ONE more time. What if the issue is that **`createNavigationPluginAsync` now starts the recast wasm load at the very beginning of `main()`, and this concurrent wasm load (recast + Havok) causes the browser to choke such that even `createEngine`'s WebGPU `requestDevice` hangs?**\n\nThat's possible but unlikely — `createEngine` runs BEFORE `createNavigationPluginAsync` in the code, so by the time the recast load starts, `createEngine` has already completed (banner printed).\n\nWait, no. Let me re-read the order AGAIN:\n```\nconst engine = await createEngine(canvas);   // ← completes, banner prints\nmark(\"engine\");                                // ← prints\n...\nconst navPromise = createNavigationPluginAsync({...});  // ← recast load starts\nconst hknp = await HavokPhysics({...});       // ← Havok load\n```\n\nSo `createEngine` and `mark(\"engine\")` happen BEFORE `createNavigationPluginAsync`. So my change CANNOT affect them. If `mark(\"engine\")` doesn't print, my change is not the cause.\n\nTherefore, the issue must be:\n1. The module isn't executing at all (load failure / cache), OR\n2. `createEngine` is hanging (WebGPU issue).\n\nGiven that the user said \"Other gallery scenes render fine in the same browser\" (from the original report), WebGPU works. So `createEngine` should work.\n\nSo the most likely explanation is **#1: