G server. There might be TWO servers on port 5182 (the old one didn't fully die, or a zombie). Let me check — earlier I saw only one process (PID 28724) on 5182. So there's one server.\n\nOK. Let me take the most pragmatic approach:\n\n1. The server is verified fine (all modules 200, correct transforms, correct imports).\n2. The problem is browser-side (cache or transient failure).\n3. My code change is sound (it's after createEngine, can't affect the \"engine\" marker).\n\nThe most productive next step: **have the user do a hard reload (Cmd+Shift+R) and report what they see, specifically checking the Network tab for the scene252.ts module status (200 vs error) and whether there's any error in the console (even a red one they might have missed).**\n\nBut actually — before asking the user, let me reconsider whether I should just REVERT my parallel-load change, since it's \"worse now.\" If the parallel-load change is somehow the issue (even though I can't see how), reverting it would restore the previous state (which at least got to \"terrain\").\n\nActually — that's a good idea. The previous state (serial load) at least got to \"terrain\" and printed markers. The new state (parallel load) prints nothing. If I revert to serial, the user should see the markers again (up to \"terrain\"), which gives us more diagnostic info.\n\nBut wait — if the issue is browser cache, reverting won't help (the cache is still stale).\n\nHmm. Let me think about this differently. \n\nActually — I just realized something. Let me reconsider the possibility that **the browser is caching the OLD module (pre-parallel-load) which has the markers, and the user is seeing a STALE version that hangs at \"terrain\" — but the user misreported and actually sees \"terrain\" as the last marker, not \"nothing\".**\n\nNo — the user explicitly said \"no scene252 init markers at all, not even 'engine'.\" So they see nothing.\n\nOK here's another thought. What if the issue is that **the browser cached the entry module from a point when it was in a BROKEN state** (e.g., during one of my edits, the file was temporarily incomplete/invalid, and the browser cached that broken version). On reload, the browser serves the broken cached version, which fails to parse → no execution → no banner, no markers, and the parse error might not show in the console (or the user missed it).\n\nThis is plausible! If during one of my edits, the file was saved in a broken state (e.g., a syntax error), and the browser cached that broken transform, then a reload would serve the broken version.\n\nBut a hard reload would bypass the cache and fetch the current (correct) version.\n\nSo the fix is: **hard reload** (Cmd+Shift+R).\n\nActually — let me reconsider whether Vite dev modules are cacheable. Vite serves dev modules with `Cache-Control: no-cache` (I believe), which means the browser must revalidate with the server on each load. So even a soft reload should get the current version. Unless the browser is aggressively caching despite the header.\n\nHmm. Actually, `no-cache` means \"revalidate with the server\" — the browser sends a conditional request (If-Modified-Since / If-None-Match) and the server responds 304 (not modified) or 200 (modified). So the browser should always get the current version.\n\nBut the `/@fs/...` modules (like HavokPhysics_es.js) might be served with different cache headers. If those are cached and stale, the module graph could fail.\n\nOK, I think the most productive thing is:\n1. Verify the `HavokPhysics_es.js` module transforms correctly (it's the most likely transitive failure point, since it's an emscripten glue file served via /@fs/).\n2. Ask the user to hard-reload and check the Network tab.\n\nLet me check the HavokPhysics_es.js module first.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the whole situation from the user's perspective. They're frustrated (\"Worse now\"). I've been going back and forth. Let me be decisive.\n\nThe server is verified fine. The most likely explanation is a browser cache / stale module issue. The most productive next step is:\n\n1. **Revert my parallel-load change** — because it's \"worse now\" and the previous state (serial) at least printed markers. This gives us a known-good baseline to diagnose from. If the parallel-load change is genuinely the issue (even if I can't see how), reverting restores the markers.\n\nWait, but I established the parallel-load change is after createEngine and can't affect the \"engine\" marker. So reverting won't restore the \"engine\" marker if the issue is cache.\n\nHmm. Actually — let me reconsider ONE more time whether the parallel-load change could cause a module-load failure.\n\n`createNavigationPluginAsync` is imported from `babylon-lite` at the top of the module. It's ALWAYS imported (regardless of when it's called). So the module graph is the same. Moving the call doesn't change the imports.\n\nSo the parallel-load change CANNOT cause a module-load failure.\n\nTherefore, the \"worse now\" is NOT from my code change. It's from the **dev server restart** or a **browser cache issue**.\n\nWait — the dev server restart! When I restarted the server (bg_08df777b), the browser's open page lost its HMR connection. On reload, the browser reconnects. But maybe the browser cached a broken state during the restart window.\n\nActually — here's a very plausible scenario:\n1. I stopped the old server (bg_ec39a3f1) and started the new one (bg_08df777b).\n2. During the restart window, the user's browser (which had the page open) tried to reload but the server was down → the module script failed to load → the browser cached the failure.\n3. Now the server is up, but the browser is serving the cached failure.\n\nA hard reload would fix this.\n\nOK, I'm confident the issue is browser-side (cache / transient failure during restart). The server is fine.\n\nThe most productive next step: **ask the user to hard-reload (Cmd+Shift+R) and report what they see, including any console errors (even subtle ones) and the Network tab status of scene252.ts.**\n\nBut actually — let me reconsider whether I should ALSO revert the parallel-load change, just to be safe. If the parallel-load change is fine (which I believe), keeping it is fine. But if it's \"worse now,\" reverting gives a cleaner baseline.\n\nHmm. Actually — let me reconsider the parallel-load change's validity. My hypothesis was that the second wasm (recast) hangs when compiled AFTER Havok via streaming. I changed it to load in parallel. But if the real issue is a browser cache / restart issue, the parallel-load change is irrelevant to the current symptom.\n\nBut the parallel-load change is STILL a valid improvement (it loads the two wasm modules concurrently, which is faster and avoids the serial stall). So I should keep it.\n\nWait — but if the parallel-load change is what caused the \"worse now\" (which I doubt, but the user perceives it as a regression), keeping it might make the user distrust the fix.\n\nLet me reconsider: the user's \"worse now\" is almost certainly from the **dev server restart** (the browser cached a failure during the restart window), NOT from my code change. My code change is sound.\n\nSo the right move is:\n1. Keep the parallel-load change (it's a valid improvement).\n2. Ask the user to hard-reload (Cmd+Shift+R) to bypass the browser cache.\n3. If it still fails, ask them to check the Network tab and console for errors.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider whether I should verify the `HavokPhysics_es.js` module transforms correctly, since it's the most likely transitive failure point. If that module has a bare import that doesn't resolve (e.g., it imports a sibling file with a bare specifier), the module graph fails.\n\nLet me curl the HavokPhysics_es.js module and check its imports.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nActually — I realize I should be more efficient. Let me:\n1. Check the HavokPhysics_es.js module (the most likely transitive failure).\n2. Check the babylon-lite index.ts module's imports (to make sure they resolve).\n\nIf both are fine, then the server is definitely fine and the issue is browser-side, and I should ask the user to hard-reload.\n\nLet me check the HavokPhysics_es.js module.\n","seq":140673,"t":8462639,"text":"\n\nServer side is fully verified: every module returns 200, the entry transform is complete, and bare imports are rewritten to resolvable `/@fs/...` paths. My parallel-load edit sits *after* `createEngine`, so it genuinely cannot stop the \"engine\" marker — which means the module graph isn't executing at all in the browser, almost certainly a stale/failed module load cached during the dev-server restart window. Let me confirm the one transitive dependency most likely to break (the emscripten glue) resolves cleanly.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":98304,"draft_n":9198,"draft_n_accepted":3002,"predicted_ms":76570.0,"predicted_n":4316,"prompt_ms":4120.0,"prompt_n":2179},"to_request":139446,"usage":{"cached_tokens":98304,"completion_tokens":4316,"prompt_tokens":100483}}
