 {\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart)}ms`);\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n    ...\n}\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Add a visible overlay div that shows the init stages.\n2. Modify `mark()` to also write to the overlay.\n3. Modify the `catch` to also write the error to the overlay.\n\nLet me design the overlay:\n- A fixed-position div, top-left or center, with a high z-index (above the spinner).\n- It shows a list of stages: \"engine +22ms\", \"havok +64ms\", etc.\n- On error, it shows the error in red.\n\nLet me write this. I'll create the overlay at the start of `main()` (before the first await), so it's visible immediately.\n\nActually — even better: I should create the overlay element at the very top of the module (or at the start of main()), so it's visible even if `main()` fails to start. But the module-level code runs before `main()`, so I could create the overlay at module level. But that might be too early (before the DOM is ready? No — the module script is at the end of the body, so the DOM is ready).\n\nWait — the HTML has `<script type=\"module\" src=\"/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\"></script>` at the end of the body (after the canvas). So the DOM is ready when the module runs. I can create the overlay at module level or at the start of main().\n\nLet me create it at the start of main() (after getting the canvas), and have `mark()` append to it.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The user wants to see WHERE it stops. So the overlay should show:\n- Each stage as it's reached (so the last visible stage = where it stopped).\n- Any error.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    // Visible init-progress overlay (the user's browser only captures screenshots + console,\n    // and the console is empty, so progress must be on-page).\n    const overlay = document.createElement(\"div\");\n    overlay.id = \"fps252-init-overlay\";\n    overlay.style.cssText = \"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:9999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.85);color:#7df07d;font:14px/1.5 monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:60vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\";\n    overlay.textContent = \"scene252 init: starting…\";\n    document.body.appendChild(overlay);\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart);\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`);\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nscene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`;\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n    ...\n}\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init-overlay\");\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.style.color = \"#ff6b6b\";\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nERROR: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`;\n    }\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nWait — but the user said \"the console is completely empty, no banner and no init markers (maybe the build strips them?)\". This is important. If the build strips console.warn, then the markers won't show in the console. But the overlay (DOM text) will show regardless.\n\nBut — the fact that the console is EMPTY (no banner, no markers) is itself a clue. The banner is `console.log` inside `createEngine`. If the console is empty, then either:\n1. The build strips console.log/warn (so the banner and markers don't show).\n2. The module isn't executing at all (so nothing runs).\n\nIf it's #1 (build strips console), then the module IS executing, but the console output is stripped. In that case, the overlay will show the stages.\n\nIf it's #2 (module not executing), then the overlay won't show either (because the module code that creates the overlay doesn't run).\n\nHmm. But the user wants the overlay to show where it stops. If the module isn't executing, the overlay won't appear, and the screenshot will just show the spinner (no overlay). That itself is informative: \"no overlay = module didn't even start.\"\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. If the module IS executing but the console is stripped, the overlay will show. If the module is NOT executing, the overlay won't show. So the overlay is a good diagnostic either way.\n\nActually — let me reconsider whether the build strips console. Vite's default build does NOT strip console methods. But the lab's build config might have a plugin that strips them. Let me check.\n\nActually — the user's browser is the limiting factor. The user said \"My browser only gives me a screenshot and console lines.\" And \"the console is completely empty.\" So even if the build doesn't strip console, the user's browser might not be capturing console output properly (or the console is empty because the module isn't executing).\n\nThe overlay approach sidesteps all of this: it renders the progress as visible DOM text, which will appear in a screenshot regardless of console behavior.\n\nSo let me implement the overlay. This is the right approach.\n\nBut — I need to rebuild the production build after adding the overlay, and re-serve it. Let me:\n1. Edit scene252.ts to add the overlay.\n2. Rebuild (`vite build`).\n3. The preview server (bg_9133947c) serves `dist/` — but it serves the OLD dist (before my edit). I need to rebuild and the preview server will serve the new dist (since it reads from disk on each request? Or does it cache?).\n\nActually — `vite preview` serves static files from `dist/`. If I rebuild, the new files replace the old ones in `dist/`. The preview server should serve the new files (it reads from disk). But there might be caching. Let me rebuild and restart the preview server to be safe.\n\nWait — actually, the preview server serves static files. If I rebuild (which overwrites `dist/`), the preview server will serve the new files on the next request (it reads from disk). But the hashed asset names will change (e.g., `scene252-Q0CsJAaq.js` → `scene252-NEWHASH.js`), so the HTML will reference the new hash. So I need to rebuild and the preview will serve the new HTML + new assets.\n\nBut — the preview server might cache the HTML in memory. Let me restart the preview server after rebuilding to be safe.\n\nLet me plan:\n1. Edit scene252.ts to add the visible overlay.\n2. Rebuild (`vite build`).\n3. Restart the preview server (stop bg_9133947c, start a new one).\n4. Verify the overlay is in the served HTML/chunk.\n\nLet me first read the current scene252.ts to get the exact text of the `mark()` helper and the `catch` block, so I can edit precisely.\n\nLet me read the relevant parts of scene252.ts.\n\nActually — I have the full source from the sourcemap dump earlier. But to be safe (the stale-transform gotcha, and the file might have changed), let me read the current file.\n\nLet me read the main() function and the catch block.\n\nLet me read the file.\n","seq":149125,"t":9054102,"text":"\n\nGood diagnostic call — if your browser only yields a screenshot and an empty console, the progress has to be *on the page*. I'll render each init stage (and any error) as visible text over the spinner, then rebuild and re-serve. Let me read the current `main()`/`catch` to edit precisely.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":120064,"draft_n":4725,"draft_n_accepted":1780,"predicted_ms":42710.0,"predicted_n":2455,"prompt_ms":7470.0,"prompt_n":3799},"to_request":148460,"usage":{"cached_to