{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I have the exact text. Let me make the edits:\n\n1. Replace the `mark()` helper to also write to a visible overlay.\n2. Create the overlay element at the start of main().\n3. Update the catch block to show the error on the overlay.\n\nLet me write the new main() start:\n\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    // On-page init progress: the test browser only captures a screenshot (console is\n    // empty/stripped), so each stage is rendered as visible text over the spinner.\n    const overlay = document.createElement(\"div\");\n    overlay.id = \"fps252-init\";\n    overlay.style.cssText =\n        \"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:99999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.88);color:#8f8;font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:70vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\";\n    overlay.textContent = \"scene252 init: starting…\";\n    document.body.appendChild(overlay);\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart);\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`);\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nscene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`;\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n```\n\nAnd the catch block:\n\n```ts\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\");\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.style.color = \"#f88\";\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nERROR: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`;\n    }\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nWait — there's a concern. The overlay is created at the start of main(). But if the module fails to load/execute (before main() runs), the overlay won't appear. To handle that case, I could create the overlay at module level (top of the file, after imports). But module-level code runs before main(), so it would create the overlay even if main() fails to start.\n\nActually — the concern is: if the module doesn't execute at all (e.g., a transitive import fails), then NO code in the module runs, including the overlay creation. So the overlay won't appear. But that's actually a useful signal: \"no overlay = module didn't even start.\"\n\nBut — to maximize the chance of showing something, I could add a tiny inline script to the HTML that shows \"page loaded, waiting for module...\" and then the module's overlay takes over. But that requires editing the HTML, which is more invasive.\n\nActually — let me keep it simple. The overlay is created at the start of main(). If main() runs, the overlay shows the stages. If main() doesn't run (module failed to load), the overlay won't show, and the screenshot will just show the spinner — which tells us the module didn't execute.\n\nBut wait — there's an even better approach. Let me create the overlay at MODULE LEVEL (right after the imports, before main() is defined). This way, the overlay appears as soon as the module evaluates (before main() is called). If the module evaluates but main() hangs, the overlay shows \"starting…\" and then the stages. If the module doesn't evaluate, no overlay.\n\nActually — the module-level overlay would show \"module loaded\" which is useful. Let me do that.\n\nHmm, but module-level code that creates a DOM element is a side effect. The working set notes say \"All new scene files must have zero module-level side effects — no module-level new Map()/Set()/WeakMap(); caches must be lazy-init (GUIDANCE §4)\". But that's about caches (Map/Set), not DOM elements. Creating a DOM element at module level is a side effect, but it's a one-time UI setup, not a cache. I think it's acceptable for a diagnostic overlay. But to be safe and follow the convention, let me create it at the start of main() instead.\n\nActually — the key insight: if the module doesn't execute, main() won't run, and the overlay won't appear. So creating it at module level vs. main() start doesn't help with the \"module didn't execute\" case. In both cases, if the module doesn't execute, no overlay.\n\nWait — no. If I create the overlay at MODULE LEVEL (top of file), it runs when the module evaluates. If the module evaluates (even if main() then hangs or throws), the overlay appears. If the module doesn't evaluate (import failure), no overlay.\n\nIf I create the overlay at the START OF main(), it runs when main() is called. main() is called at the bottom of the module (`main().catch(...)`). So if the module evaluates, main() is called, and the overlay appears. Same as module level, effectively.\n\nThe difference: module-level overlay appears slightly earlier (before main() is called). But main() is called almost immediately after the module evaluates (it's the last statement). So the difference is negligible.\n\nLet me create it at the start of main() (cleaner, follows the convention of no module-level side effects).\n\nActually — wait. There's a subtle issue. If I create the overlay at the start of main(), and main() is `async`, then the overlay creation happens synchronously when main() is called (before the first await). So it appears immediately. Good.\n\nLet me go with creating it at the start of main().\n\nNow — one more consideration: the overlay should be visible in a screenshot. The spinner (from loader.js) is an overlay. My overlay needs to be ON TOP of the spinner (higher z-index) and visible. Let me make it prominent (large text, high contrast).\n\nLet me also make sure the overlay doesn't get removed by loader.js. loader.js dismisses the spinner on `data-ready` or `data-loaded`. My overlay is a separate element, so it won't be affected by loader.js.\n\nBut — once the scene is ready (data-ready set), the spinner disappears, but my overlay would still be there (showing all the stages). That's fine for diagnosis. Actually — I should hide the overlay once the scene is ready (so it doesn't clutter the game). But for now, since we're diagnosing, let me keep it visible. Actually — let me hide it when \"started\" is reached, so the game is visible. I'll add a line in mark() to hide the overlay when stage === \"started\".\n\nHmm, but that complicates the code. Let me keep it simple: the overlay stays visible. The user can see the game behind it (the overlay is top-left, max-width 70vw, so the game is still visible on the right). Actually — let me make the overlay smaller and position it top-left so it doesn't block the whole screen.\n\nActually — for diagnosis, the key is: the overlay shows the last stage reached. If it reaches \"started\", the game is running. The user can see the game (the overlay is top-left). If it stops at some stage, the overlay shows that stage.\n\nLet me make the overlay:\n- Top-left, fixed.\n- Small font (13px).\n- Semi-transparent black background, green text.\n- max-width 40vw (so the game is visible on the right).\n- pointer-events: none (so it doesn't block mouse input).\n\nLet me write the edits.\n\nEdit 1: the mark() helper + overlay creation.\n\nOld:\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart)}ms`);\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n```\n\nNew:\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    // On-page init progress: the test browser only captures a screenshot (its console\n    // is empty), so each stage is rendered as visible text over the spinner.\n    const overlay = document.createElement(\"div\");\n    overlay.id = \"fps252-init\";\n    overlay.style.cssText =\n        \"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:99999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.88);color:#8f8;font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:42vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\";\n    overlay.textContent = \"scene252 init: starting…\";\n    document.body.appendChild(overlay);\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart);\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`);\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nscene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`;\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n```\n\nEdit 2: the catch block.\n\nOld:\n```ts\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nNew:\n```ts\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\");\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.style.color = \"#f88\";\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nERROR: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`;\n    }\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nWait — there's a concern about the overlay not appearing if the module doesn't execute. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually — the user's report is that the console is EMPTY (no banner, no markers). This strongly suggests the module isn't executing (or the build strips console). If the module isn't executing, my overlay (created in main()) won't appear.\n\nTo handle this, I should ALSO add a fallback: a tiny inline script in the HTML that shows \"page loaded\" and sets a timeout that shows \"module not loaded\" if the module doesn't take over. But that's more invasive.\n\nAlternatively — I could add the overlay creation at MODULE LEVEL (top of file). If the module evaluates, the overlay appears (showing \"module loaded\"). If main() then hangs, the overlay shows the stages. If the module doesn't evaluate (import failure), no overlay.\n\nBut the \"module doesn't evaluate\" case is the one where I'd want a fallback. For that, I'd need an HTML-level script.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most likely. The user's browser is slow (the dev server was too slow to load in 8 seconds). Now with the production build, the page should load fast. But the user says \"scene252.html still sits on the spinner, and now the console is completely empty.\"\n\nWait — \"now the console is completely empty\" — before (dev server), the console showed the banner + markers. Now (production build), the console is empty. This is because the production build MINIFIES and the console.warn calls might be... no, minification doesn't strip console.warn.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. In the production build, the code is minified. The `console.warn` calls are preserved (minification doesn't remove them). So the markers should still print to the console. But the user says the console is empty.\n\nHmm. Unless the user's browser isn't capturing console output from the production build for some reason. Or the module isn't executing.\n\nActually — the most likely explanation: the user's browser is so slow/limited that it's not even finishing loading the page's modules within the window. The production build has a large bundle (the scene252 chunk is 13KB, but it imports many other chunks — the total could be several MB). If the browser is slow, it might not finish loading all the chunks.\n\nWait — but the production build is pre-bundled, so it should load fast. Unless the browser is genuinely slow (network or CPU).\n\nLet me reconsider. The user says \"the stock scene2.html on the same build also never gets past the spinner in my browser.\" So it's not scene252-specific — even scene2 (a simple scene) doesn't load. This suggests the browser is too slow to load ANY page's modules, even the pre-bundled production build.\n\nSo the issue is the browser's speed/limitations, not the code. The overlay approach will help: if the module loads, the overlay shows the stages. If the module doesn't load (browser too slow), the overlay won't show, and the screenshot will just show the spinner.\n\nBut — to maximize the diagnostic value, I should add an HTML-level fallback that shows \"page loaded, waiting for module\" and a timeout message. This way, even if the module doesn't load, the screenshot shows that the page HTML loaded but the module didn't.\n\nLet me add a small inline script to scene252.html that:\n1. Shows \"scene252.html loaded\" immediately.\n2. Sets a timeout (e.g., 10s) that shows \"module not loaded within 10s\" if the module hasn't taken over.\n\nBut — editing the HTML is more invasive. And the HTML is a template that Vite processes. Let me check if I can add an inline script to the HTML.\n\nActually — let me keep it simple first. Let me add the overlay in the module (main()). If the module loads, the overlay shows the stages. If not, the screenshot shows just the spinner (which tells us the module didn't load).\n\nBut — to distinguish \"module didn't load\" from \"module loaded but hangs before creating the overlay,\" I should create the overlay as EARLY as possible. The earliest point in the module is module-level (top of file). Let me create the overlay at module level.\n\nWait — but module-level DOM manipulation is a side effect. The convention says no module-level side effects (caches). But a one-time UI overlay is different from a cache. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually — the cleanest approach: create the overlay at the very start of main() (before the first await). This is the earliest point where I can create it without module-level side effects. If main() runs, the overlay appears immediately (before createEngine). If main() doesn't run (module failed to load), no overlay.\n\nBut — the \"module failed to load\" case is exactly what I need to diagnose. So I need an HTML-level fallback for that.\n\nLet me do BOTH:\n1. Module-level overlay (in main(), early) — shows stages if the module loads.\n2. HTML-level inline script — shows \"page loaded\" + timeout message if the module doesn't take over.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the HTML approach. The HTML is `lab/lite/scene252.html`. Let me look at it. It has:\n```html\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\"></script>\n```\n\nI can add an inline script BEFORE the module script that:\n1. Creates a \"loading\" indicator.\n2. Sets a timeout that shows an error if the module doesn't load.\n\nBut — the module script is `type=\"module\"`, which is deferred (executes after the HTML is parsed). The inline script (non-module) executes immediately. So the inline script can create the \"loading\" indicator, and the module script (when it loads) can replace it with the progress overlay.\n\nLet me design:\n- Inline script (in HTML): creates a div showing \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\". Sets a 12s timeout that changes it to \"module not loaded within 12s (check network)\".\n- Module script (scene252.ts): when it loads, it finds the div and updates it with the init stages.\n\nThis way:\n- If the HTML loads but the module doesn't: the screenshot shows \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\" or \"module not loaded within 12s\".\n- If the module loads: the screenshot shows the init stages.\n\nThis is a robust diagnostic. Let me implement it.\n\nBut — editing the HTML is a bit more work. Let me check the HTML structure first.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The user's request is specifically: \"make scene252 show its init progress and any error as text on the page itself (over the spinner), so I can see in a screenshot where it stops.\"\n\nSo the core request is: show init progress + error on the page. The module-level overlay (in main()) does this IF the module loads. The HTML-level fallback handles the \"module didn't load\" case.\n\nLet me implement both:\n1. Module overlay (in main()) — shows stages + error.\n2. HTML inline script — shows \"page loaded\" + timeout.\n\nLet me look at the HTML first.\n\nActually — let me be pragmatic. Let me implement the module overlay first (the core request), rebuild, and serve. If the user's screenshot shows the overlay with stages, we know where it stops. If the screenshot shows NO overlay (just the spinner), we know the module didn't load, and I'll add the HTML fallback.\n\nBut — to save a round-trip, let me implement BOTH now. The HTML fallback is cheap and covers the \"module didn't load\" case.\n\nLet me look at the HTML.\n\nLet me read scene252.html.\n","seq":151333,"t":9188098,"text":"\n\nThe build is pre-bundled and static, so the page should load fast — but your browser is clearly struggling to even finish loading modules (scene2 too). The on-page overlay is the right call. Let me also add a tiny HTML-level fallback so we can tell \"page loaded but module never arrived\" apart from \"module loaded but init hangs.\" Let me look at the HTML.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":126720,"draft_n":7728,"draft_n_accepted":3088,"predicted_ms":119420.0,"predicted_n":4192,"prompt_ms":3190.0,"prompt_n":662},"to_request":149141,"usage":{"cached_tokens":126720,"completion_tokens":4192,"prompt_tokens":127382}}
