fbaa23380998ebfb1","prompt_tokens":131765,"reason":"window","render":"# regime\narm: t1-phased-floor-r3\nsubstrate: accel24-tabbyapi-exl3-qwen38-27b-3p00\ndogma_version: 0\nphase: test\nframe_version: placeholder\n\n# working set\naudit-28769-1\tCCD/tunneling concern is moot — plan's bullets are instant rays (S4), not projec…\naudit-28769-2\tSettled — plan uses tile deletion via uploadMeshToGPU rebuild (S2), so in-place …\naudit-28769-3\tDebris functions confirmed but line numbers drift — setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity…\naudit-28769-30\tFPS-relevant references confirmed — scene18 (free camera), scene40 (physics), sc…\naudit-28769-36\tonTriggerCollisionObservable confirmed (character-controller.ts:306) for per-con…\naudit-28769-6\tResolved — lab/vite.config.ts:743 is `port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || …\naudit-28769-91\tAnchors (verified) — player/bot = createPhysicsCharacterController (moveWithColl…\ninterview-t10-0\tgotcha: `@recast-navigation/core`'s `init` is `Raw.Module = await impl()` (plain emscripten instantiation, no custom logic), so the stall is inside the recast wasm fetch/instantiate (or one of the three dynamic imports in `_ensureRecast`) — IMPLICATION: since scene170 loads recast fine on its own, the distinguishing factor is that scene252 instantiates Havok *before* recast (two emscripten modules on one page, no lab scene has done this), so I should test that interaction rather than assume the plugin is broken.\ninterview-t10-1\tgotcha: `createNavigationPluginAsync` doesn't load the wasm at call time — it funnels through `_ensureRecast` → `core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, {locateFile}))`, and the awaited value is the emscripten factory promise (fetch → `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`/`instantiateArrayBuffer` → `initRuntime`) — IMPLICATION: the stall is inside the emscripten instantiation path (fetch, streaming-compile fallback, or `initRuntime`), so I should inspect that path (and what the prior Havok load left behind) rather than the navigation.ts API layer.\ninterview-t10-10\tplan: Edit `scene252.ts` — `Promise.all([HavokPhysics(...), createNavigationPluginAsync(...)])` so both wasm loads start concurrently, and add a temp `mark(\"player\")` after controller creation to rule out a synchronous hang before the nav await.\ninterview-t10-11\tplan: Restart the dev server (bg_ec39a3f1) and curl-verify the served transform matches disk.\ninterview-t10-12\tplan: Ask you to hard-reload scene252.html and report the last `scene252 init:` marker.\ninterview-t10-13\tplan: If it still hangs — temporarily make `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` reject before the nav load (forces recast's ArrayBuffer fallback) and have you reload again to isolate streaming as the culprit.\ninterview-t10-14\tplan: Once init completes — delete the stage markers + `mark()` helper, run `pnpm run lint:fix` then `pnpm run lint`.\ninterview-t10-15\tplan: Restart the dev server and hand you the playtest checklist (ready dataset, move/slide/jump, mouse-look, shoot holes, fall-through, debris, bots, HUD, audio).\ninterview-t10-16\tplan: Check `lab/vite.config.ts` for rollup-input / gallery-card / thumbnail requirements for scene252 and apply whatever's needed.\ninterview-t10-17\tplan: Before commit — delete the scratch files and verify `git status --short`.\ninterview-t10-2\tgotcha: the recast glue (`recast-navigation.wasm.js`) has no `globalThis`/`window` mutations, so a plain global-symbol collision is not the mechanism — IMPLICATION: I can't conclude it's a shared-global clash; the collision (if any) is subtler (e.g. both emscripten builds touching the same emscripten `Module`/memory/streaming resource), so I need to read the Havok glue's load path before asserting a cause.\ninterview-t10-3\tpointer: `mark(\"terrain\")` printing at +90ms confirms the 1024-body `createFpsTerrain` is fast and synchronous — rules out init cost as the hang, keeping the diagnosis on the recast wasm load (scene252.ts, `main()`).\ninterview-t10-4\tpointer: the `cap()` end-cap helper + `tb`/`tt` block is the rewritten `rayVsCapsule` (cylindrical band + two end-cap spheres) — unrelated to the hang, but the place to look if bot hit-detection misbehaves later (scene252.ts, `rayVsCapsule`).\ninterview-t10-5\tpointer: `player.staticFriction = 0; player.dynamicFriction = 0.1;` — Quake slide tuned via the controller's public friction fields, not shape masks (scene252.ts, `main()`).\ninterview-t10-6\topen: Lint/typecheck hasn't been re-run since the marker + `maxObstacles` edit batch.\ninterview-t10-7\topen: Does scene252 need a rollup input / gallery card / thumbnail in `lab/vite.config.ts`, or is `getHtmlInputs()` auto-discovery sufficient — still unchecked.\ninterview-t10-8\topen: All runtime behavior remains unverified (mouse-look signs, HUD compositing, bot drift re-sync, debris node write-back, respawn, audio, crosshair centering) — blocked on a successful load + your playtest.\ninterview-t10-9\topen: The sandbox Vite watcher serves stale transforms — every edit needs a dev-server restart before you reload.\ninterview-t10-f119487-0\tgotcha: scene170/171 (which work) load *only* recast — they never load Havok — while scene252 loads Havok first (line 175) then recast (line 194), so the nav-plugin hang is specific to the Havok→recast ordering, not to the recast API itself — IMPLICATION: don't \"fix\" the `createNavigationPluginAsync` call or its `locateFile`; the bug is an interaction between the two wasm modules on one page, and the fix (if any) belongs in how/when the second wasm is loaded, or in isolating the two.\ninterview-t10-f120210-0\tgotcha: scene170/171 load *only* recast (no Havok anywhere in the file) — the only difference between the working nav scenes and scene252 is that scene252 loads `HavokPhysics` (line 175) *before* `createNavigationPluginAsync` (line 194) — IMPLICATION: the stall is specific to the Havok→recast load order on one page, not recast itself; don't debug recast in isolation.\ninterview-t10-f120210-1\tgotcha: the recast emscripten glue (`@recast-navigation/wasm/dist/recast-navigation.wasm.js`) performs **no** `globalThis`/`window` mutations, and its failure path (`abort` → `readyPromiseReject` + `console.error`) would produce a visible console error — the user reports none — IMPLICATION: this is a genuinely never-settling Promise, not a rejected/failed load; the culprit is upstream in the load order or a cross-module resource, not a glue-side error I can read off the stack.\ninterview-t10-f121598-1\tdecision: Stop reading emscripten glue code to hunt for a collision, and instead empirically test whether loading the recast wasm *after* the Havok wasm hangs (a node script that loads both in sequence, or a minimal test page) — WHY: inspecting minified glue is low-yield, and the one distinguishing fact (scene170 loads recast alone and works; scene252 loads Havok first and hangs) is best confirmed by direct reproduction.\ninterview-t10-f123557-0\tgotcha: The recast emscripten build (`@recast-navigation/wasm@0.43.0`, dist/recast-navigation.wasm.js) is structurally different from the Havok build — its factory is `async` and does `var wasmExports = await createWasm()` at the top of the factory body, so it only resolves after the wasm is *fully* instantiated; Havok's `createWasm()` is synchronous and loads in the background via a `readyPromise`. — IMPLICATION: the nav-plugin stall lives inside `createWasm` → `instantiateAsync` (which tries `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` first, falls back to `instantiateArrayBuffer`), and `Raw.Module`/`createNavigationPluginAsync` can only hang at that point — so the next step is to instrument/probe that fetch+instantiate, not the plugin wrapper.\ninterview-t10-f123557-1\tgotcha: The served `/recast-navigation.wasm` is byte-identical to `lab/public/recast-navigation.wasm` with `Content-Type: application/wasm` (valid magic `\\\\0asm`). — IMPLICATION: asset corruption/404 is ruled out; don't chase the file or Vite's wasm handling — the hang is in instantiation ordering/interaction, and the strongest lead remains that scene170 (recast alone) works while scene252 (Havok loaded first, then recast) hangs, i.e. a two-wasm-module interaction rather than a bad asset.\ninterview-t10-f124766-0\tgotcha: Bare-specifier imports (`@babylonjs/havok`, and by extension `@recast-navigation/*`) do not resolve from a scratch script at the repo root — pnpm isolates them in `node_modules/.pnpm` and only links them into the `node_modules` of the workspace package that declares them as a dependency (e.g. `packages/babylon-lite`), so a `load-order.mjs` sitting in `.smoke-tmp/` throws `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`. — IMPLICATION: to run the Node load-order test I must import via absolute `.pnpm` store paths (e.g. `node_modules/.pnpm/@babylonjs+havok@1.3.12/node_modules/@babylonjs/havok/lib/esm/HavokPhysics_es.js` and the matching `@recast-navigation/*` store paths), or place the script inside the depending package — not rely on bare-specifier resolution from the repo root.\ninterview-t10-f125598-0\tconstraint: The sandbox blocks writes to /tmp and the system temp dir — scratch files (including curl output and Node test scripts) must live in-repo (e.g. `.smoke-tmp/`) and be deleted before commit.\ninterview-t10-f125598-1\tconstraint: This is a pnpm-strict workspace — root-level Node scripts cannot resolve dependencies that only exist under a workspace sub-package (e.g. `@babylonjs/havok` is a dep of `packages/babylon-lite`, not the repo root), so any scratch load-order test must import via the `node_modules/.pnpm/...` path or be placed inside the depending package.\ninterview-t10-f126222-0\tgotcha: `@babylonjs/havok` and `@recast-navigation/*` are pnpm workspace deps linked only under `lab/node_modules`, not the repo-root `node_modules` — a Node script at the repo root fails with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` — IMPLICATION: any scratch Node script that imports these must live under `lab/` (e.g. `lab/.smoke-tmp/`), or it can't resolve the packages.\ninterview-t10-f126222-1\tgotcha: Loading Havok then recast in Node (with `wasmBinary` pre-seeded to bypass fetch) completes in ~64ms with no error — so the init hang is NOT a JS/wasm module-ordering collision in the emscripten glue — IMPLICATION: the stall is browser-specific (the `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`/fetch path or a WebGPU-context timing interaction); Node can't reproduce it, so diagnosis must continue via the in-browser stage markers I already added, not Node.\ninterview-t10-f129686-0\tevidence: ```ts\\n    // Physics world (Havok V2 wasm served from the lab root).\\n    const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\\n    const world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -GRAVITY, z: 0 });\\n    mark(\"havok\");\\n```\\nand, further down (the other half of the fix):\\n```ts\\n    // Navigation — navmesh from the terrain mesh; bots are NavCrowd agents driving controllers.\\n    const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\\n    mark(\"nav-plugin\");\\n```\\n\\nThese two `await`s are the target of my planned change: I intend to load Havok and the nav plugin concurrently (`Promise.all`) so the recast wasm — the second module, which is where the browser hangs — doesn't serialize behind the ~2MB Havok compile. The `world`/`terrain`/`player` lines in between depend on `hknp`, so the restructure has to keep those after the Havok leg resolves.\ninterview-t10-f129686-1\tpointer: `scene.fixedDeltaMs = 1000 / PHYSICS_FPS` — the fixed physics timestep; if player/bot movement ever stops stepping, this is the first thing to check (scene252.ts, `main()`).\ninterview-t10-f129686-2\tpointer: the `mark()` helper + `__initStart` + every `console.warn` stage marker are temporary instrumentation I added to diagnose this hang — they must be deleted before commit per GUIDANCE §0b (scene252.ts, `main()`).\ninterview-t10-f130738-0\topen: Is the hang really the browser's `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` for the second wasm (recast after Havok) — my Node test pre-seeded `wasmBinary`, so it bypassed the streaming path and doesn't rule out a streaming-specific never-settling hang.\ninterview-t10-f130738-1\topen: Could a synchronous hang between `mark(\"terrain\")` and the nav await (e.g. `createPhysicsCharacterController`) produce the identical symptom — not yet ruled out; needs a temp marker after player creation.\ninterview-t10-f130738-2\topen: Do the three dynamic imports inside `_ensureRecast` (core / generators / `wasm/wasm` subpath) resolve in scene252's much larger module graph — assumed fine because scene170 works, but unverified here.\ninterview-t10-f130738-3\topen: Will parallelizing the two wasm loads actually fix it — unverified, needs your reload.\ninterview-t10-f130738-4\topen: The `console.warn` stage markers + `mark()` helper in `main()` are temporary instrumentation that must be deleted before commit (GUIDANCE §0b).\ninterview-t10-f130738-5\topen: Scratch to delete before commit: `fps-smoke.cjs`, `.smoke-tmp/`, `lab/.smoke-tmp/`, `.smoke-home/`.\ninterview-t10-f133255-0\tgotcha: Loading Havok then recast in Node (with `wasmBinary` pre-seeded to bypass fetch) completes cleanly in ~64ms — the spinner hang is NOT a JS/wasm collision between the two emscripten modules, it's browser-specific — IMPLICATION: I can't reproduce or debug the hang in Node; the fix targets the browser path and must be verified in the user's browser, not by me.\ninterview-t10-f133255-1\tgotcha: The recast emscripten glue's `instantiateAsync` only falls back to the ArrayBuffer path (`WebAssembly.instantiate`) inside a `catch` — if `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` *hangs* (never settles) rather than rejects, that fallback is unreachable — IMPLICATION: to force the non-streaming path I'd have to make streaming actively *reject* (monkey-patch `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`), since a plain hang can't be caught; parallel-loading the two modules is the cleaner alternative.\ninterview-t10-f134181-0\tdecision: The scene252 init hang is the Recast wasm (the second wasm module) stalling in `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` after Havok has compiled — WHY: Node loads Havok→recast cleanly in 64ms (rules out a JS/wasm collision), the served recast wasm is valid (correct `application/wasm` MIME, identical bytes), scene252 is the only scene combining both modules (170–175 are recast-only), and terrain init was fast (<90ms), so the stall is the browser-side second streaming compile.\ninterview-t10-f134181-1\tdecision: Load the two independent wasm modules in parallel — start `createNavigationPluginAsync` (assign to `navPromise`) before awaiting `HavokPhysics`, then `await navPromise` later — WHY: it stops serializing the two streaming compiles, which is the likely trigger, and is also a genuine init-speed improvement.\ninterview-t10-f134181-2\tdecision: Restart the lab dev server (stop bg_ec39a3f1, relaunch as bg_08df777b on :5182) after editing scene252.ts — WHY: the long-running Vite watcher serves stale transforms in this sandbox, so the served code must match disk before the user reloads.\ninterview-t10-f134552-0\tconstraint: The two wasm modules (Havok + recast) must be loaded in parallel, not serially — a second `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` compile running after Havok stalls in some browsers (this is the compatibility rule behind the fix I just made; it's still unverified until you reload, so treat it as a working hypothesis rather than confirmed).\ninterview-t10-f135396-0\tgotcha: `@babylonjs/havok` and `@recast-navigation/*` resolve from `lab/node_modules`, not the repo-root `node_modules` — IMPLICATION: any Node-based repro script must live under `lab/` (e.g. `lab/.smoke-tmp/`) or the bare imports die with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`.\ninterview-t10-f135396-1\tgotcha: both emscripten loaders fetch the wasm binary via `fetch(locateFile(...))`, so a Node repro can't use the relative `/recast-navigation.wasm` path — IMPLICATION: pre-seed `Module.wasmBinary` with the on-disk bytes to exercise the `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) path in Node.\ninterview-t10-f135396-2\tgotcha: the recast glue's factory is `async` and does `var wasmExports = await createWasm()` (fully compiles+instantiates before resolving), whereas Havok's factory is synchronous and returns a `readyPromise` that resolves after background instantiation — IMPLICATION: a stall inside recast's `createWasm`/`instantiateStreaming` surfaces as a never-settling `createNavigationPluginAsync` (no rejection, no error box), which is exactly the spinner symptom.\ninterview-t10-f135800-0\tdecision: Load the Havok physics wasm and the Recast nav-plugin wasm in parallel in scene252 (start `navPromise` before the `HavokPhysics` await, `await` it after player setup) — WHY: the second wasm module's `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` compile stalls after the 2MB Havok compile in the browser, so compiling them concurrently avoids the serial stall.\ninterview-t10-f135800-1\tdecision: Prefer the parallel-load workaround over monkey-patching `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to force the ArrayBuffer path — WHY: parallel-load is high-level and fits the \"scene code reads like an API demo\" convention, whereas the monkey-patch is a low-level global mutation; parallel-load is the first attempt, with ArrayBuffer-forcing kept as the documented fallback if it still hangs.\ninterview-t10-f135800-2\tdecision: Keep the `console.warn` init-stage markers in scene252 for now and remove them only after the fix is confirmed — WHY: the next reload needs them to verify all stages (up to \"started\") now print, and GUIDANCE §0b requires deleting debug code before commit.\ninterview-t10-f136141-0\tconstraint: scene252 must load its two wasm modules (Havok + Recast) concurrently, not serially — a second wasm module compiled via `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` after the ~2MB Havok wasm stalls/hangs in the browser (Node's `WebAssembly.instantiate`/ArrayBuffer path handles the same Havok→recast sequence fine in 64ms, so the serial streaming compile is the culprit; the fix is the parallel `navPromise` load, and if that proves insufficient the fallback is forcing the ArrayBuffer path by making `instantiateStreaming` reject for the recast load).\ninterview-t11-0\tgotcha: I expected to find a broken/failing module in the served graph to explain \"no banner, no markers,\" but found none — the entry transform is complete, every module returns 200, bare imports are rewritten to resolvable `/@fs/...` paths, and `HavokPhysics_es.js` (served via `/@fs/`, not Vite dep-optimization) has no bare imports of its own — IMPLICATION: the regression is NOT server-side and NOT caused by my parallel-load edit (which sits after `createEngine` and can't stop the \"engine\" marker); the cause is a browser-side stale/failed module load (likely cached during the dev-server restart window), so I should stop editing code / restarting the server and instead have you hard-reload (Cmd+Shift+R) and check the Network tab for the `scene252.ts` module status plus any console error.\ninterview-t12-0\tevidence: ```\\n{\\n  \"dev\": \"vite\",\\n  \"build\": \"vite build\"\\n}\\n```\ninterview-t12-1\tpointer: `build.rollupOptions.input: getHtmlInputs()` — the production build bundles every HTML page, so `scene252.html` is included and will land in the build output (vite.config.ts:769)\ninterview-t12-2\tpointer: there is no `preview` section in the config — only `server.port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174` (the dev server, env-var driven) — so `vite preview` defaults to port 4173 and I must pass `--port 5182` explicitly (vite.config.ts:743)\ninterview-t12-3\tpointer: comment \"@recast-navigation/* ship a wasm binary the esbuild optimizer can't process; serve…\" — the dev config excludes it from `optimizeDeps`; the production build's bundler may hit the same wall on the recast/Havok wasm and needs the equivalent exclusion or it will fail (vite.config.ts:721-722)\ninterview-t12-4\tpointer: `pnpm build:bundle-scenes` / `build:bundle-demos` are separate from `vite build` and write into `lab/public/bundle/` — the gallery's Bundle tab depends on those artifacts, which a plain `vite build` won't produce (vite.config.ts:497-503)\ninterview-t12-5\tpointer: the compat plugin redirects bare `@babylonjs/core` imports to the compat barrel for the BJS oracle scenes under `lab/lite/src/bjs/` — only matters if the build pulls in those scenes (vite.config.ts:607-677)\ninterview-t12-f144194-0\tgotcha: `lab/vite.config.ts` configures only `server.port` (the dev server, via `LAB_DEV_PORT`) — there is no `preview` section — so `vite preview` will default to port 4173, not 5182 — IMPLICATION: I must pass `--port 5182` explicitly to `vite preview` (or the lab won't be where you expect it).\ninterview-t12-f144194-1\tgotcha: the lab's custom plugins (`pagesDemoPlugin`, `serveReferenceImages`, `apiDocsPlugin`, `tabContentPlugin`) hook `configureServer`, which only runs in dev mode — `vite preview` serves the static `dist/` and none of that middleware runs — IMPLICATION: dev-only lab features (the `/pages` demo index, reference images, API docs) won't be available on the preview server; scene252 itself is fine because its critical assets (HavokPhysics.wasm, recast-navigation.wasm, fonts) live in `public/` and get copied into `dist/`.\ninterview-t12-f144194-2\tgotcha: `build.rollupOptions.input` is `getHtmlInputs()`, which includes every `lite/*.html` (the `lite/` dir has ~734 entries) — the production build compiles the whole lab, not just scene252 — IMPLICATION: the build is large and may take a few minutes; I'm running it in the background and will confirm it finishes before starting preview.\ninterview-t12-f145486-0\tgotcha: The production build nests lite scenes under `dist/lite/` (e.g. `dist/lite/scene252.html` + `dist/assets/lite/scene252-*.js`), not at the dist root — I first looked for `dist/scene252.html` and it