owser launch proved unviable anyway.\ninterview-t6-f91584-2\tdecision: Rewrote `rayVsCapsule` as cylindrical-band + two end-cap sphere tests — WHY: the old `u` formula was mathematically wrong (missing valid center hits) and returned null for all cap hits.\ninterview-t6-f91584-3\tdecision: Player/bot vertical velocity is zeroed when actual drop < half of requested drop after `moveWithCollisions`, with a -30 terminal clamp — WHY: `vel.y` accumulated `-g*dt` every step even while standing, so walking off a ledge started a fall at full accumulated speed.\ninterview-t6-f91584-4\tdecision: Crosshair positioned via `window.innerWidth/innerHeight` (CSS px), not `canvas.width/height` — WHY: the canvas has fixed 1280×720 attributes stretched by CSS, and `positionPx` is in CSS pixels (scene180:82).\ninterview-t6-f91584-5\tdecision: Bot display quaternion corrected to `(0, sin(h/2), 0, cos(h/2))` — WHY: the prior `(0, sin, cos, 0)` args encoded a 180° rotation about a tilted axis.\ninterview-t6-f91584-6\tdecision: Player/bot spawn at `terrainHeight + CAP_HEIGHT/2 + 0.1` (feet on ground) — WHY: controller position is the capsule center, so the old `+2` left the player floating 1.1u.\ninterview-t6-f91584-7\tdecision: `Debris.body` typed `PhysicsBody | null` with guards, dropping the `null as unknown as PhysicsBody` cast — WHY: clean typecheck without unsafe casts.\ninterview-t6-f91584-8\tdecision: Restarted the dev server after edits — WHY: the long-running vite server served stale transforms (file watcher not picking up changes in this sandbox); fresh server on :5182 now serves current code.\ninterview-t6-f91584-9\tdecision: Runtime verification this session is lint + typecheck + dev-server module resolution; browser automation is impossible (Chrome SIGABRTs — crashpad Mach bootstrap EPERM in sandbox) — WHY: the playtest gate falls to the user on :5182.\\nDECISION (confirmed): `setThinInstances` re-uploads on every call (bumps `_version`, sets dirty range) — no separate flush needed for per-frame bot/debris matrices.\\nDECISION (confirmed): collision-layer filtering cannot exclude the player capsule from raycasts (default membership `~0` matches any `collideWith`) — WHY: confirms the muzzle offset, not filter masks, is the correct self-hit fix.\ninterview-t6-f92150-0\tdecision: Bullet ray is cast from a muzzle point (0.8 in front of the eye), not the eye — WHY: the player's capsule shape is private and default-membership ~0, so it can't be layer-filtered out of the raycast; starting outside the capsule is the only way to avoid self-hits.\ninterview-t6-f92150-1\tdecision: Eye height set to 0.7 above capsule center — WHY: realistic first-person camera near the head, paired with the muzzle offset.\ninterview-t6-f92150-2\tdecision: `rayVsCapsule` rewritten as cylindrical-band + two end-cap sphere tests — WHY: the original `u` formula was wrong (missed valid center hits) and ignored end caps.\ninterview-t6-f92150-3\tdecision: player/bot vertical velocity zeroed on floor contact and clamped to -30 — WHY: without it, fall speed accumulates unboundedly while standing.\ninterview-t6-f92150-4\tdecision: crosshair positioned via `window.innerWidth/innerHeight`, not `canvas.width/height` — WHY: `TextLayer.positionPx` is in CSS pixels and the canvas is stretched to 100vw/100vh over fixed 1280×720 attrs.\ninterview-t6-f92150-5\tdecision: bot display quaternion corrected to `(0, sin(h/2), 0, cos(h/2))` — WHY: the original put cos in the qz slot and 0 in qw, a 180° rotation about a tilted axis instead of a Y-heading.\ninterview-t6-f92150-6\tdecision: `Debris.body` typed `PhysicsBody | null` with null guards — WHY: the `null as unknown as PhysicsBody` cast was a type-unsound hack.\ninterview-t6-f92150-7\tdecision: player spawns at standing height (terrain + CAP_HEIGHT/2 + 0.1), not +2 — WHY: +2 left the player floating ~1.1 units above the ground.\ninterview-t6-f92150-8\tdecision: restarted the dev server after edits — WHY: the long-running vite watcher served stale transforms in this sandbox, so a fresh process was needed to serve the latest code.\ninterview-t6-f92969-0\tgotcha: the long-running vite dev server serves stale module transforms in this sandbox — its file watcher didn't fire on my on-disk edits, so `curl` of `scene252.ts` still returned `EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5` after I changed it to `0.7` — IMPLICATION: restart the dev server (task_stop + relaunch `cd lab && LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite`) after editing scene files before playtesting, or verify served content via curl instead of assuming HMR picked up the change.\ninterview-t6-f92969-1\tgotcha: my bot display matrix had the wrong quaternion argument order for `mat4Compose` — I put `cos(h/2)` in the `qz` slot and `0` in `qw`, which is a 180° rotation about a tilted axis, not a Y-heading — IMPLICATION: a Y-axis rotation quaternion is `(qx=0, qy=sin(h/2), qz=0, qw=cos(h/2))`; check `mat4Compose` arg order `(tx,ty,tz,qx,qy,qz,qw,sx,sy,sz)` when composing per-frame thin-instance matrices.\ninterview-t6-f93897-0\tgotcha: the long-running vite dev server was serving a stale transform after I edited the file on disk (disk had `EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7` + `MUZZLE_OFFSET`, but the served module still showed `EYE_HEIGHT = 1.5` and no `MUZZLE_OFFSET`) — its file watcher doesn't fire on edits in this sandbox — IMPLICATION: after editing scene files, restart the dev server (`task_stop` + relaunch `cd lab && LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite`) or verify served content via curl before playtesting; don't assume HMR picked up the change. (Already recorded as r91869.)\ninterview-t6-f93897-1\tgotcha: `mat4Compose(tx, ty, tz, qx, qy, qz, qw, sx, sy, sz)` — a Y-axis heading rotation is `(0, sin(h/2), 0, cos(h/2))`; my first draft put `cos(h/2)` in the `qz` slot and `0` in `qw`, which is a 180° rotation about a tilted axis, not a heading — IMPLICATION: keep the quaternion as `(0, sin(h/2), 0, cos(h/2))` for bot heading; the capsule is radially symmetric so it doesn't change the look, but it matters if the display ever becomes asymmetric.\ninterview-t6-f94809-0\tgotcha: The long-running Vite dev server's file watcher does not pick up on-disk edits in this sandbox — after I edited `scene252.ts` (EYE_HEIGHT 1.5→0.7, added MUZZLE_OFFSET), curl against :5182 kept returning the stale transform (EYE_HEIGHT 1.5, no MUZZLE_OFFSET) even though the disk file was correct. — IMPLICATION: After editing scene files, restart the dev server (`task_stop` + relaunch `cd lab && LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite`) before playtesting, or verify served content via curl rather than assuming HMR worked.\ninterview-t6-f94809-1\tgotcha: `mat4Compose(tx,ty,tz,qx,qy,qz,qw,sx,sy,sz)` — I had the bot's heading quaternion as `(0, sin(h/2), cos(h/2), 0)`, which is a 180° rotation about a tilted axis, not a Y-heading; the correct Y-rotation is `(0, sin(h/2), 0, cos(h/2))`. — IMPLICATION: For a Y-axis heading, put `cos(h/2)` in the `qw` slot and `0` in `qz`; (cosmetic for a radially-symmetric capsule, but wrong for any asymmetric display).\ninterview-t6-f95458-0\tdecision: bot display matrix uses a Y-axis rotation quaternion `(0, sin(h/2), 0, cos(h/2))` for heading — WHY: the earlier arg order (cos in the `qz` slot) produced a 180° rotation about a tilted axis, and I kept the heading rotation even though a capsule is radially symmetric, to future-proof an asymmetric display.\ninterview-t6-f96340-0\tconstraint: Browser automation is blocked in this sandbox — Chrome SIGABRTs at launch (crashpad's Mach `bootstrap_check_in` and `~/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome/Crashpad` are EPERM-denied, and the cached Playwright chromium build doesn't match the repo's playwright) — so no Playwright smoke/playtest is possible here; validation is lint + typecheck + dev-server module resolution + a user manual playtest on :5182.\ninterview-t6-f96340-1\tconstraint: The long-running vite dev server's file watcher does not pick up on-disk edits in this sandbox (it kept serving a stale transform) — after editing scene files, restart the dev server (or verify served content via curl) before playtesting, or you will test old code.\ninterview-t6-f96340-2\tconstraint: The character controller's capsule shape is private and carries Havok's default all-layers filter (`~0` membership), so it cannot be excluded from `physicsRaycast` via collision-layer masks — bullet rays must originate from a muzzle offset outside the capsule, never from the eye/center, or the player's own capsule occludes every terrain hit.\ninterview-t6-f96340-3\tconstraint: `PhysicsCharacterController.getPosition()` returns the capsule **center**, not the feet — eye height, spawn, and ground computations must apply the capsule half-height offset (feet = center − capsuleHeight/2).\ninterview-t6-f96340-4\tconstraint: scene252 is a game demo, not a parity scene — it must stay out of `scene-config.json`, `bundle-size.spec.ts`, and golden references; there is no bundle-size ceiling entry to add or raise for it.\ninterview-t7-0\tgotcha: Port 5182 was already answering (root → 200, scene252.html → 200) despite the user's report that nothing was listening — the vite dev server (bg_f4a1f424) had been running all along, and the served scene252.ts transform matches disk (EYE_HEIGHT 0.7, MUZZLE_OFFSET present), so no stale-transform issue — IMPLICATION: the user's problem is client-side (browser/network reachability, or WebGPU unavailable in their browser), not the server; I should tell them to load http://localhost:5182/lite/scene252.html in a WebGPU-enabled browser rather than restart anything.\ninterview-t7-f97347-0\tdecision: Keep the existing background vite server (bg_f4a1f424) as the dev server on :5182 rather than starting a new one — WHY: curl confirmed it answers (root and scene252.html → 200) and its served transform matches disk (EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7, MUZZLE_OFFSET = 0.8), so no restart was needed.\ninterview-t8-0\topen: Does mouse-look feel correct, or is the yaw/pitch sign convention inverted (unverified at runtime)?\ninterview-t8-1\topen: Does the HUD TextRenderer actually composite over the 3D frame (scene registered first, `clear: false`) — reasoned from the registry, never seen on screen?\ninterview-t8-10\topen: Do the audio SFX (gunshot/thud/hit) fire on their events?\ninterview-t8-11\topen: Is scene252's registration complete — does it need a rollup input in `lab/vite.config.ts`, or is `getHtmlInputs()` auto-discovery sufficient for a non-parity scene?\ninterview-t8-12\tplan: Take your playtest report and fix whatever fails (most likely candidates: one-line look flip, bot re-sync tuning, HUD compositing, or the 8×8u-tile fallback if init is slow).\ninterview-t8-13\tplan: Verify `lab/public/fonts/Inter.ttf` exists and check `lab/vite.config.ts` for any rollup-input/gallery-card requirement for scene252.\ninterview-t8-14\tplan: If the playtest is green — delete scratch files (`fps-smoke.cjs`, `.smoke-tmp/`, `.smoke-home/`), run final `pnpm run lint`, and verify `git status --short` per GUIDANCE §0b.\ninterview-t8-15\tplan: Propose the phase transition once the playtest passes and cleanup is done.\ninterview-t8-2\topen: Does `updateDefaultTextData`'s in-place mutation actually trigger the HUD re-draw each frame?\ninterview-t8-3\topen: Is the crosshair truly centered at arbitrary window sizes (CSS-pixel fix unverified)?\ninterview-t8-4\topen: Do the bots patrol and shoot sanely — does the agent/controller drift re-sync (`agentGoto` when >4u apart) cause stuck or oscillating bots?\ninterview-t8-5\topen: What is the init cost of 1024 static tile bodies + navmesh build (cs=0.5) — is the 8×8u-tile fallback needed?\ninterview-t8-6\topen: Does fall-through respawn work, and do the landing velocity-reset + terminal clamp behave?\ninterview-t8-7\topen: Does debris render — does the physics world write dynamic body transforms back to nodes that were never added to the scene?\ninterview-t8-8\topen: Does `/fonts/Inter.ttf` exist under `lab/public` (font-load path unverified)?\ninterview-t8-9\topen: Does the muzzle offset fully prevent self-hits, especially aiming straight down at own feet?\ninterview-t9-0\tevidence: ```ts\\n    await registerScene(scene);\\n    registerTextRenderer(tr);\\n    await startEngine(engine);\\n    canvas.dataset.initMs = String(Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart));\\n    canvas.dataset.ready = \"true\";\\n}\\n\\nmain().catch((err) => {\\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\\n    if (canvas) {\\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\\n    }\\n    console.error(err);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThat tail is load-bearing for the diagnosis: `dataset.ready` is only set after `startEngine`, so the spinner means the whole `main()` is still in flight; and since `catch` would both set `dataset.error` and `console.error`, your symptom (no console errors) means one of the awaits is settling neither fulfilled nor rejected — a hang, not a failure. The suspect awaits, in order: `createEngine`, `HavokPhysics(...)` (line ~155), `createNavigationPluginAsync(...)` (line ~171), `loadFont(\"/fonts/Inter.ttf\")` (line ~217), then `registerScene`/`startEngine`. All three assets return 200 via curl, so next I'll instrument which await the page is actually stuck on.\ninterview-t9-1\tpointer: `canvas.dataset.initMs` (set from `__initStart` at main's end) is a built-in init-time readout — I can have you read it during playtest to measure the 1024-body + navmesh init cost I never got to measure (scene252.ts, main tail).\ninterview-t9-10\topen: Do the audio SFX (gunshot/thud/hit) fire on their events — unverified.\ninterview-t9-11\topen: Does scene252 need a rollup input / gallery card in `lab/vite.config.ts`, or is `getHtmlInputs()` auto-discovery sufficient for a non-parity scene — decided it's a game demo, but I haven't confirmed what's actually required.\ninterview-t9-12\tplan: Read your console report — the last `scene252 init: <stage>` marker printed.\ninterview-t9-13\tplan: Fix the root cause of the hang at that stage (most likely: tile-cache navmesh build, a `registerScene`/`buildScene` deferred builder, or the text renderer's first frame).\ninterview-t9-14\tplan: Remove the `console.warn` stage markers from `scene252.ts` once diagnosed (no debug code left behind).\ninterview-t9-15\tplan: Run `pnpm run lint:fix` then `pnpm run lint` to confirm the fix typechecks and passes ESLint.\ninterview-t9-16\tplan: Restart the dev server (stale-transform gotcha) and hand you the manual playtest checklist on :5182.\ninterview-t9-17\tplan: Record the settled diagnosis and fix via `update_record`.\ninterview-t9-2\tpointer: `canvas.dataset.error` is the in-page failure hook — if a later run dies, asking you for `document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\").dataset.error` beats guessing (scene252.ts, main catch).\ninterview-t9-3\tpointer: mouse-look signs are unverified — `yaw -= delta.dx * MOUSE_SENS; pitch -= delta.dy * MOUSE_SENS` in `onBeforeRender`; if look feels inverted it's a one-line flip (scene252.ts, render-frame block).\ninterview-t9-4\tpointer: bot LOS and shooting both use the navmesh `raycast(nav, ...)` with `!los.hit` = clear sight, at 1.2s intervals, distance-based accuracy `Math.max(0.15, 1 - dist / 50)` — the bot behavior you'll judge in playtest (scene252.ts, bot update in `onPhysicsAfterStep`).\ninterview-t9-5\tpointer: bot spawns use `findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, { x: s.x, y: 0, z: s.z }, 8)` and set controller Y from the returned navmesh `p.y + CAP_HEIGHT / 2` — if a bot spawns stuck/underground, this is where to look (scene252.ts, bot setup loop).\ninterview-t9-6\tpointer: player/bot respawn gates are `y < -20` plus 3s invulnerability (`invulnerableUntil`) — relevant if you see respawn loops (scene252.ts, `respawnPlayer`/physics step).\ninterview-t9-7\tpointer: `setThinInstances` is called every frame for both bot and debris matrices in `onBeforeRender` — per the working set that's safe (version bump re-uploads), but it's the place to look if bot/debris visuals lag or freeze (scene252.ts, render-frame block).\ninterview-t9-8\topen: Does fall-through respawn work, and do the landing velocity-reset + terminal clamp behave — unverified.\ninterview-t9-9\topen: Does the muzzle offset fully prevent self-hits, especially aiming straight down at own feet — unverified.\ninterview-t9-f102389-0\tgotcha: `lab/public/loader.js` dismisses the spinner on either `canvas[data-ready]` **or** `canvas[data-loaded]`, and it has a built-in error box that displays `window` errors and unhandled rejections on the overlay — IMPLICATION: the user's screenshot shows no error box and no fade, so `main()` is stuck on an await that never settles *without* rejecting; the next step is stage instrumentation, not hunting for console errors.\ninterview-t9-f102389-1\tgotcha: the \"Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine\" banner is logged inside `createEngine` (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts:313) — IMPLICATION: its presence confirms `createEngine` resolved, so the hang is a later await (HavokPhysics load, `createNavigationPluginAsync`, `loadFont`, or `registerScene`), not engine creation.\ninterview-t9-f102389-2\tgotcha: all three external assets — `/HavokPhysics.wasm`, `/recast-navigation.wasm`, `/fonts/Inter.ttf` — return 200 through the :5182 dev server — IMPLICATION: missing/404 assets are ruled out; the hang is inside a wasm load/instantiate promise or the `registerScene` pipeline path.\ninterview-t9-f102389-3\tgotcha: reading the engine's frame-loop code (visible in the minified bundle): `startEngine` resolves on the first `requestAnimationFrame` callback regardless of whether anything was drawn — IMPLICATION: `startEngine` cannot be the hang; suspects narrow to the three wasm/font awaits or `registerScene`'s awaited pipeline compilation, and I should add stage markers to `main()` so the user can report which stage is last reached.\ninterview-t9-f104748-0\tdecision: The spinner stall means `main()` never completes — I'll instrument `scene252.ts` with per-stage `canvas.dataset` markers to find which await hangs — WHY: `loader.js` only dismisses the overlay on `data-ready`/`data-loaded`, and my scene sets `data-ready` only after `startEngine`, so a perpetual spinner pins the stall to init.\ninterview-t9-f104748-1\tdecision: The hang is one of the post-engine awaits (HavokPhysics, createNavigationPluginAsync, loadFont, registerScene, startEngine), not an asset fetch — WHY: all three assets (HavokPhysics.wasm, recast-navigation.wasm, Inter.ttf) return 200, and the \"Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine\" banner is logged inside `createEngine` (engine.ts:313), proving that step resolved.\ninterview-t9-f107343-0\tgotcha: `grep \"export async function startEngine\"` in `packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts` returned nothing — the declaration I assumed (async, in engine.ts) doesn't exist there as I expected — IMPLICATION: locate the real `startEngine` export (it may be a non-async `export function` returning a Promise, or live in another file) before reasoning about which await can hang.\ninterview-t9-f107343-1\tgotcha: `registerScene` (scene-core.ts:400) is `await buildScene(scene)` + `await Promise.all(task._preload)` + conditional `swapchain-overlay.js` import, not just \"material pipeline processing\" — IMPLICATION: the init hang candidate is broader than material processing; read `buildScene` next (it's where material pipelines + thin-instance imports run) rather than assuming the stall is in `startEngine`.\ninterview-t9-f108295-0\tgotcha: `createNavMesh` builds a solo navmesh by default; dynamic obstacles (`addBoxObstacle`) only work with a tile-cache navmesh built with `maxObstacles > 0` — scene252 passes no `maxObstacles`, so every tile-destruction nav-obstacle call will fail at runtime. — IMPLICATION: add `maxObstacles` to the `createNavMesh` params in scene252.ts.\ninterview-t9-f108295-1\tgotcha: loader.js only shows its error box on window `error`/`unhandledrejection`, and `main().catch` handles rejections (console.error + dataset.error) — so \"no errors, spinner forever\" means `main()` is stuck on an await that never settles (or a blocked main thread), not failing. — IMPLICATION: stop chasing error paths; instrument the init stages (console.warn markers) to find which await hangs.\ninterview-t9-f108295-2\tgotcha: thin instances are already used by seven working lab scenes (scene16/17/43/103/165/204/219), so the thin-instance `_preload` path is proven in the lab — the hang is specific to scene252's combination (text renderer sharing the surface, 1024 static bodies, navmesh build), not the engine's thin-instance machinery. — IMPLICATION: compare scene252's init sequence against a working thin-instance scene and add stage markers to isolate the hanging await.\ninterview-t9-f110270-0\tgotcha: `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle` throw on a solo navmesh — `_assertTileCache` (navigation.ts:697) raises \"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with `maxObstacles > 0` to enable obstacles.\" — IMPLICATION: scene252's `createNavMesh` call must pass `maxObstacles` (e.g. 64) plus `tileSize` (e.g. 32) or the first tile destruction throws at runtime; `expectedLayersPerTile` has a default (1) so only those two params are needed.\ninterview-t9-f110270-1\tgotcha: `startEngine` (engine.ts:435) is a non-async `export function` returning a Promise that resolves after the first rAF frame — so if it had been reached, the spinner would have disappeared or a window error would have tripped the loader's error box. — IMPLICATION: with no error box and a stuck spinner, the hang is upstream in `registerScene`'s awaits (`buildScene`'s `_deferredBuilders` or frame-graph `_preload`), and the next step is stage-marking `main()` with `console.warn` to localize the stalled await.\ninterview-t9-f110270-2\tgotcha: my edit to add those stage markers failed (0 occurrences) — the actual cause was my own old_string: I dropped the 4-space indent on its first line (`const canvas = ...`), so it didn't match the file literally. — IMPLICATION: retry the edit with the exact indentation from the file; the intended change itself (navmesh `maxObstacles`/`tileSize` + `mark()` stage markers) is still pending.\ninterview-t9-f111526-0\tgotcha: `addBoxObstacle` (navigation.ts:647) throws \"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with `maxObstacles > 0` to enable obstacles\" — a solo navmesh (my current `createNavMesh` call) can't take dynamic obstacles — IMPLICATION: the navmesh must be built with `maxObstacles > 0` (tile cache; `tileSize` defaults to 32) or every tile destruction throws at runtime — fixed by adding `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` to the params in scene252.ts.\ninterview-t9-f111526-1\tgotcha: my first `edit` failed (0 occurrences) because I reconstructed `main()`'s text from memory — the on-disk file had diverged (separate `hknp` line, camera/light setup between engine and physics, different clearColor values) — IMPLICATION: always `read` the target region before editing; the compaction summary's recollection of exact code text is not reliable.\ninterview-t9-f112133-0\tdecision: scene252's navmesh is now built as a tile-cache navmesh (`maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` in the `createNavMesh` call) — WHY: `addBoxObstacle` (navigation.ts:647) throws \"Navmesh has no tile cache\" on a solo navmesh, so my tile-destruction path would have crashed on the first shot.\ninterview-t9-f112133-1\tdecision: `main()` in scene252.ts is instrumented with `console.warn` stage markers (engine/havok/terrain/nav-plugin/navmesh/bots/font/pre-register/registered/started, with ms offsets) — WHY: the spinner stall is an await that never settles (no error box, no rejection), and stage markers are the fastest way to pin down which stage hangs on the next reload; `console.warn` is the only console channel ESLint allows here.\ninterview-t9-f112133-2\tdecision: Restarted the lab dev server (stopped bg_f4a1f424, started bg_ec39a3f1 on :5182) after the edits — WHY: the long-running vite watcher in this sandbox doesn't pick up on-disk edits and serves stale transforms, so the new markers would never be served without a restart.\ninterview-t9-f112567-0\tconstraint: Dynamic navmesh obstacles require a tile-cache navmesh — `createNavMesh` must be passed `maxObstacles > 0` (with `tileSize`) or `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle` throw (\"Navmesh has no tile cache\"); scene252's tile destruction depends on this, so its navmesh params now include `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32`.\ninterview-t9-f112960-0\tgotcha: `addBoxObstacle` throws `\"Navmesh has no tile cache. Build with maxObstacles > 0 to enable obstacles.\"` (navigation.ts:697) — my `createNavMesh` call had no `maxObstacles`, so the navmesh was solo and every `destroyTerrainTile` → `addBoxObstacle` would throw on the first shot — IMPLICATION: fixed by adding `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` to the `createNavMesh` params in scene252.ts (tile-cache build; `tileSize` defaults to 32 otherwise).\ninterview-t9-f112960-1\tgotcha: `NavMeshParameters` uses short names `cs`/`ch`/`walkableSlopeAngle`/`walkableHeight`/`walkableClimb`/`walkableRadius` (navigation.ts:43-54), not the `cellSize`/`agentHeight`/`agentRadius`/`agentClimb` spellings my earlier notes assumed — IMPLICATION: trust the interface, not the notes; the scene code already used the correct short names, so no code change was needed beyond the two new fields.\ninterview-t9-f112960-2\tgotcha: the spinner stall is not a failure — `startEngine` (engine.ts:435, non-async `export function` returning a Promise) resolves after the first rAF frame, `registerScene` awaits only `buildScene` + frame-graph `_preload` (plain dynamic imports that settle or throw), and `loadFont`/`createNavigationPluginAsync` throw on failure — so with no error box and no console error, `main()` is stuck on an await that never settles or a blocked main thread — IMPLICATION: I instrumented `main()` with `console.warn` stage markers (engine → havok → terrain → nav-plugin → navmesh → bots → font → pre-register → registered → started), restarted the dev server (bg_ec39a3f1, verified serving the new transform), and need you to reload scene252.html and tell me the last marker printed.\ninterview-t9-f113547-0\topen: Which await is hanging — the stage markers will answer this, but I need you to reload and report the last `scene252 init: <stage>` line.\ninterview-t9-f113547-1\topen: Does the tile-cache navmesh path (`maxObstacles > 0`) work in this lab — no lab scene (170/171) uses it, so my `maxObstacles: 64` fix is unproven and could itself be the hang (markers will show if `navmesh` never prints after `nav-plugin`).\ninterview-t9-f113547-2\topen: Does the `TextRenderer` (`clear: false`, registered after the scene) actually composite over the 3D frame — reasoned from the render-context registry, never seen on screen.\ninterview-t9-f113547-3\topen: Does `updateDefaultTextData`'s in-place mutation trigger the HUD layer to re-draw each frame — assumed, unverified.\ninterview-t9-f113547-4\topen: Is the mouse-look yaw/pitch sign convention correct — if look feels inverted it's a one-line flip.\ninterview-t9-f113547-5\topen: What is the init cost of 1024 static tile bodies + navmesh build — the markers now report elapsed ms per stage, so we'll know whether the 8×8u-tile fallback is needed.\ninterview-t9-f113547-6\topen: Does the bot agent/controller drift re-sync (`agentGoto` when >4u apart) cause stuck or oscillating bots — unverified at runtime.\ninterview-t9-f113547-7\topen: Does debris render — does the physics world write dynamic body transforms back to nodes that were never added to the scene — unverified.\ninterview-t9-f114214-0\tdecision: Added `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` to scene252's `createNavMesh` call — WHY: `addBoxObstacle` throws \"Navmesh has no tile cache\" on a solo navmesh, so tile-destruction nav obstacles would crash on the first shot.\ninterview-t9-f114214-1\tdecision: Instrumented `main()` in scene252.ts with a `mark()` helper emitting `console.warn` stage markers (engine/havok/terrain/nav-plugin/navmesh/bots/font/pre-register/registered/started) — WHY: browser automation is blocked in this sandbox, so the user's reload will reveal exactly which await hangs.\ninterview-t9-f114214-2\tdecision: Used `console.warn` (not `console.log`) for the instrumentation — WHY: GUIDANCE §6 forbids `console.log` but permits warn/error/time/trace.\ninterview-t9-f114214-3\tdecision: Restarted the dev server (stopped bg_f4a1f424, launched bg_ec39a3f1 on :5182) — WHY: the long-running vite watcher serves stale transforms in this sandbox, so served code must match disk before the user reloads.\ninterview-t9-f114757-0\tconstraint: Navmesh dynamic obstacles require the tile-cache build path — `addBoxObstacle`/`addCylinderObstacle` throw (\"Navmesh has no tile cache\") unless `createNavMesh` is called with `maxObstacles > 0` (navigation.ts:697); scene252 must keep `maxObstacles: 64, tileSize: 32` or tile destruction crashes on the first shot.\ninterview-t9-f114757-1\tconstraint: The `console.warn` stage markers added to `main()` in scene252.ts are temporary instrumentation for diagnosing the init hang — they must be deleted once the stall is identified, per GUIDANCE §0b (no debug code in committed code).\nmechanical/.smoke-home\tfiles: .smoke-home\nmechanical/.smoke-tmp\tfiles: .smoke-tmp\nmechanical/.smoke-tmp/load-order.mjs\tfiles: .smoke-tmp/load-order.mjs\nmechanical/.smoke-tmp/served_recast.wasm\tfiles: .smoke-tmp/served_recast.wasm\nmechanical/~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/background/shell-bg_08df777b.output\tfiles: ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/background/shell-bg_08df777b.output\nmechanical/~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/background/shell-bg_9133947c.output\tfiles: ~/diet-inference-runs/t1-phased-floor-2026-10-12-r3/background/shell-bg_9133947c.output\nmechanica