{"kind":"delta","request":151337,"seq":152734,"t":9323865,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/lab/lite/scene252.html\", \"old_string\": \"<canvas id=\\\"renderCanvas\\\" width=\\\"1280\\\" height=\\\"720\\\"></canvas>\\n  <script src=\\\"/loader.js\\\"></script>\\n  <script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\\\"></script>\", \"new_string\": \"<canvas id=\\\"renderCanvas\\\" width=\\\"1280\\\" height=\\\"720\\\"></canvas>\\n  <script src=\\\"/loader.js\\\"></script>\\n  <script>\\n    // On-page init indicator: the test browser only captures a screenshot (its console\\n    // is empty), so init progress must be visible text. This element is created before\\n    // the module loads; scene252.ts takes it over and updates it as init progresses.\\n    (function () {\\n      var el = document.createElement(\\\"div\\\");\\n      el.id = \\\"fps252-init\\\";\\n      el.style.cssText =\\n        \\\"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:99999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.88);color:#8f8;\\\" +\\n        \\\"font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:42vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\\\";\\n      el.textContent = \\\"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\\\";\\n      document.body.appendChild(el);\\n      window.addEventListener(\\\"error\\\", function (e) {\\n        el.textContent += \\\"\\\\nwindow error: \\\" + (e.message || String(e));\\n      });\\n      window.addEventListener(\\\"unhandledrejection\\\", function (e) {\\n        el.textContent += \\\"\\\\nunhandled rejection: \\\" + (e.reason instanceof Error ? e.reason.message : String(e.reason));\\n      });\\n    })();\\n  </script>\\n  <script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\\\"></script>\"}","id":"call_9439582aa8e44452848962d3","index":0,"name":"edit"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now let me design the approach:\n\n1. HTML inline script (before the module script): creates a \"loading\" indicator that shows \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\". Sets a timeout that changes it to \"module not loaded within Ns\" if the module hasn't taken over. The module script, when it loads, will find this element and repurpose it as the progress overlay.\n\n2. Module script (scene252.ts): in main(), find the existing element (created by the inline script) and update it with the init stages. If it doesn't exist (shouldn't happen), create it.\n\nActually — let me simplify. Let me have the inline script create the element with id `fps252-init` and set its initial text to \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\". Then the module's main() finds it by id and updates it with the stages. If the module never loads, the element stays with \"waiting for module…\" (and after the timeout, \"module not loaded within Ns\").\n\nThis is clean. Let me implement:\n\nHTML inline script:\n```html\n<script>\n  (function () {\n    var el = document.createElement(\"div\");\n    el.id = \"fps252-init\";\n    el.style.cssText = \"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:99999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.88);color:#8f8;font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:42vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\";\n    el.textContent = \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\";\n    document.body.appendChild(el);\n    setTimeout(function () {\n      el.textContent += \"\\n(still waiting — module may not have loaded; check network)\";\n    }, 12000);\n  })();\n</script>\n```\n\nWait — but the module script is `type=\"module\"`, which is deferred. The inline script (non-module) runs immediately (during HTML parsing). So the inline script creates the element first, then the module script (when it loads) updates it.\n\nBut — there's a timing issue. The inline script runs during HTML parsing (before the body is fully parsed). `document.body` might not exist yet if the script is in the `<head>`. But I'm putting the script in the `<body>` (after the canvas), so `document.body` exists. Good.\n\nActually — the inline script is in the `<body>`, after the canvas. So `document.body` exists. Good.\n\nNow — the module script, when it loads, needs to find the element and update it. Let me modify main() to find the element by id and update it.\n\nModule (scene252.ts) main():\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    // On-page init progress: the test browser only captures a screenshot (its console\n    // is empty), so each stage is rendered as visible text over the spinner. The\n    // element is created by an inline script in scene252.html (\"waiting for module\");\n    // we take it over here and update it as init progresses.\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\") as HTMLDivElement | null;\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.textContent = \"scene252 init: starting…\";\n    }\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart);\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`);\n        if (overlay) {\n            overlay.textContent += `\\nscene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`;\n        }\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd the catch block:\n```ts\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\") as HTMLDivElement | null;\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.style.color = \"#f88\";\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nERROR: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`;\n    }\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nThis is clean. The inline script creates the element, the module takes it over.\n\nBut — there's a concern: the inline script's `setTimeout` (12s) will fire even after the module has taken over (if the module is still loading). So if the module loads at 10s and reaches \"started\" at 15s, the timeout (at 12s) would append \"(still waiting…)\" to the overlay, which is wrong.\n\nTo avoid this, the module should clear the timeout when it takes over. But the timeout is in the inline script's closure, so the module can't access it directly.\n\nAlternative: the inline script sets a flag, and the module checks it. Or — the module clears the timeout by removing the element and recreating it. Or — the inline script's timeout checks if the module has taken over (e.g., by checking a data attribute).\n\nLet me simplify: the inline script's timeout only appends the message if the element's text still starts with \"waiting for module\" (i.e., the module hasn't taken over). \n\n```js\nsetTimeout(function () {\n  if (el.textContent.indexOf(\"waiting for module\") !== -1) {\n    el.textContent += \"\\n(module not loaded within 12s — check network)\";\n  }\n}, 12000);\n```\n\nThis way, if the module has taken over (changed the text), the timeout doesn't append the message. \n\nActually — let me reconsider. The module takes over by setting `overlay.textContent = \"scene252 init: starting…\"`. So after the module takes over, the text no longer contains \"waiting for module\". So the timeout check `indexOf(\"waiting for module\") !== -1` would be false, and the timeout wouldn't append. \n\nBut — what if the module takes over at 11s (text = \"scene252 init: starting…\"), and then hangs (no more stages). The timeout (at 12s) checks: does the text contain \"waiting for module\"? No (it's \"scene252 init: starting…\"). So the timeout doesn't append. But the overlay shows \"scene252 init: starting…\" with no stages — which tells us the module loaded but hung before the first stage (createEngine). That's useful.\n\nOK, this works. Let me implement it.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the timeout duration. The user's browser is slow. The module might take >12s to load. Let me set the timeout to 20s to be safe. Or — let me not set a timeout at all, and just show \"waiting for module…\" indefinitely. The user can see in the screenshot whether the module loaded (overlay shows stages) or not (overlay shows \"waiting for module…\").\n\nActually — a timeout is useful because it distinguishes \"module loading (slow)\" from \"module never loaded\". But if the browser is just slow, the timeout might fire before the module loads, giving a false \"module not loaded\" message.\n\nLet me set the timeout to 20s (generous). If the module loads within 20s, the timeout doesn't fire (or fires but doesn't append because the module took over). If the module doesn't load within 20s, the timeout appends \"module not loaded within 20s\".\n\nHmm — but the user's browser is SO slow that even the production build doesn't load. So 20s might not be enough. Let me set it to 30s. Or — let me not set a timeout, and just show \"waiting for module…\" indefinitely. The user can tell from the screenshot whether the module loaded (stages shown) or not (\"waiting for module…\" shown).\n\nActually — let me keep it simple: no timeout. The inline script shows \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\". If the module loads, it takes over and shows the stages. If not, the screenshot shows \"waiting for module…\". This is clear enough.\n\nBut — a timeout is useful for the \"module never loaded\" case. Let me add a timeout of 30s that appends a message only if the module hasn't taken over. This gives the user a clear signal.\n\nLet me go with a 30s timeout.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the whole approach. The user's browser is very limited (screenshot + console only, and the console is empty). The key question is: WHERE does it stop? \n\nIf the module loads, the overlay shows the stages, and the last stage tells us where it stops.\nIf the module doesn't load, the overlay shows \"waiting for module…\", which tells us the module didn't load.\n\nSo the overlay approach answers the question in both cases. Let me implement it.\n\nLet me write the HTML inline script and the module changes.\n\nHTML:\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n  <title>Scene 252 — Quake-style FPS (destructible terrain)</title>\n  <style>\n    body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; background: #000; }\n    canvas { display: block; width: 100vw; height: 100vh; }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <canvas id=\"renderCanvas\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\"></canvas>\n  <script src=\"/loader.js\"></script>\n  <script>\n    // On-page init indicator: the test browser only captures a screenshot (its console\n    // is empty), so init progress must be visible text. This element is created before\n    // the module loads; scene252.ts takes it over and updates it as init progresses.\n    (function () {\n      var el = document.createElement(\"div\");\n      el.id = \"fps252-init\";\n      el.style.cssText = \"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:99999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.88);color:#8f8;font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:42vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\";\n      el.textContent = \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\";\n      document.body.appendChild(el);\n      setTimeout(function () {\n        if (el.textContent.indexOf(\"waiting for module\") !== -1) {\n          el.textContent += \"\\n(module not loaded within 30s — check network/browser)\";\n        }\n      }, 30000);\n    })();\n  </script>\n  <script type=\"module\" src=\"/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\nWait — there's a concern. The inline script runs during HTML parsing. But `document.body` might not be fully parsed yet. Actually — the inline script is in the `<body>`, after the canvas. So `document.body` exists (the body tag is open). But the body isn't fully parsed (the rest of the body hasn't been parsed). However, `document.body.appendChild(el)` works because the body element exists (it's been created when the `<body>` tag was parsed).\n\nActually — in HTML parsing, when the parser encounters `<body>`, it creates the body element. Then it parses the contents. So when the inline script runs (inside the body), `document.body` exists. `appendChild` works. Good.\n\nBut — there's a subtlety: the inline script runs BEFORE the module script (which is deferred). So the inline script creates the element, then the module script (when it loads) takes it over. Good.\n\nNow — the module script. Let me modify main() to find the element and update it.\n\nActually — wait. There's a concern about the module script loading. The module script is `type=\"module\"`, which is deferred. It loads AFTER the HTML is fully parsed. So by the time the module script runs, the inline script has already created the element. Good.\n\nBut — what if the module script fails to load (e.g., network error)? Then the element stays with \"waiting for module…\" (and after 30s, \"module not loaded within 30s\"). Good.\n\nWhat if the module script loads but throws an error during evaluation (e.g., an import fails)? Then the module's top-level code throws, and main() is never called. The element stays with \"waiting for module…\". Hmm — that's not ideal. I'd want to show the error.\n\nTo handle this, I could add a `window.onerror` handler in the inline script that shows the error on the element. Let me add that.\n\n```js\nwindow.addEventListener(\"error\", function (e) {\n  el.textContent += \"\\nwindow error: \" + (e.message || String(e));\n});\nwindow.addEventListener(\"unhandledrejection\", function (e) {\n  el.textContent += \"\\nunhandled rejection: \" + (e.reason instanceof Error ? e.reason.message : String(e.reason));\n});\n```\n\nBut — this might be overkill. Let me keep it simple: the inline script shows \"waiting for module…\", and the module takes it over. If the module fails, the user sees \"waiting for module…\" (and the timeout message). That's enough to know the module didn't load.\n\nActually — let me add the error handlers. They're cheap and provide more diagnostic info.\n\nHmm — but the working set notes say \"loader.js has a built-in error box that displays window errors and unhandled rejections on the overlay\". So loader.js already shows window errors. But the user says the console is empty and there's no error box. So maybe loader.js's error box isn't working, or the errors aren't being captured.\n\nLet me add my own error handlers to the inline script, to be safe.\n\nActually — let me keep it focused. The core request is: show init progress + error on the page. Let me implement:\n1. Inline script: creates the element, shows \"waiting for module…\", adds error handlers.\n2. Module: takes over the element, shows the stages, shows errors in the catch block.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nActually — I realize I'm overcomplicating this. Let me step back.\n\nThe user's browser is slow/limited. The production build should load fast, but the browser is struggling. The key question is: WHERE does it stop?\n\nThe simplest, most robust approach:\n1. Inline script (in HTML): creates a visible element showing \"scene252.html loaded\". This proves the HTML loaded.\n2. Module script (scene252.ts): when it loads, updates the element with \"module loaded\" and then the init stages. If it hangs, the last stage shows where it stopped. If it throws, the catch block shows the error.\n\nThis answers the question:\n- If the screenshot shows \"scene252.html loaded\" (and nothing else): the HTML loaded but the module didn't.\n- If the screenshot shows \"module loaded\" + stages: the module loaded, and the last stage shows where it stopped.\n- If the screenshot shows \"module loaded\" + stages + \"ERROR: ...\": the module loaded, init failed, and the error is shown.\n\nLet me implement this.\n\nLet me write the HTML inline script:\n```html\n<script>\n  (function () {\n    var el = document.createElement(\"div\");\n    el.id = \"fps252-init\";\n    el.style.cssText = \"position:fixed;top:0;left:0;z-index:99999;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.88);color:#8f8;font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;padding:10px 14px;max-width:42vw;white-space:pre-wrap;pointer-events:none;\";\n    el.textContent = \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\";\n    document.body.appendChild(el);\n    window.addEventListener(\"error\", function (e) {\n      el.textContent += \"\\nwindow error: \" + (e.message || String(e));\n    });\n    window.addEventListener(\"unhandledrejection\", function (e) {\n      el.textContent += \"\\nunhandled rejection: \" + (e.reason instanceof Error ? e.reason.message : String(e.reason));\n    });\n  })();\n</script>\n```\n\nAnd the module (scene252.ts) main():\n```ts\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    // On-page init progress: the test browser only captures a screenshot (its console\n    // is empty), so each stage is rendered as visible text over the spinner. The\n    // element is created by an inline script in scene252.html; we take it over here.\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\") as HTMLDivElement | null;\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.textContent = \"scene252 module loaded — init starting…\";\n    }\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart);\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`);\n        if (overlay) {\n            overlay.textContent += `\\n  ${stage} +${ms}ms`;\n        }\n    };\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd the catch block:\n```ts\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\") as HTMLDivElement | null;\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.style.color = \"#f88\";\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nERROR: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`;\n    }\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});\n```\n\nThis is clean and robust. Let me implement it.\n\nWait — one concern: the `console.warn` calls. The user says the console is empty in the production build. So the `console.warn` calls won't show in the console. But that's fine — the overlay (DOM text) shows the stages, which is what matters. The `console.warn` calls are redundant (they won't show in the user's browser), but they don't hurt. Let me keep them (they're useful for debugging in a normal browser).\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The user's browser console is empty. So the `console.warn` calls won't show. But the overlay (DOM text) will show. So the overlay is the primary diagnostic channel. The `console.warn` calls are secondary (useful in a normal browser). Let me keep both.\n\nOK let me implement. Let me make the edits.\n\nEdit 1: HTML — add the inline script.\nEdit 2: scene252.ts — modify main() to take over the overlay.\nEdit 3: scene252.ts — modify the catch block to show the error on the overlay.\n\nLet me do these edits.\n\nActually — wait. I need to reconsider whether the inline script in the HTML will be preserved by Vite's build. Vite processes HTML files and can transform inline scripts. Let me check if Vite preserves inline scripts in the build output.\n\nVite's HTML processing: Vite parses the HTML, finds scripts, and processes them. Inline scripts (non-module) are generally preserved (Vite might extract them to separate files, but it should preserve them). Let me verify by checking the build output after I make the changes.\n\nActually — Vite might transform inline scripts. Let me check the current build output to see if the inline script (if any) is preserved. But the current HTML has no inline script (only the loader.js and module script). So I can't check.\n\nLet me just make the changes, rebuild, and verify the inline script is in the build output. If Vite strips it, I'll need a different approach (e.g., put the inline script in a separate file).\n\nActually — Vite's HTML processing: for inline scripts, Vite extracts them to separate JS files and replaces the inline script with a `<script src=\"...\">` tag. This is done for optimization. So the inline script will be preserved (as a separate file). Good.\n\nBut — there's a subtlety: Vite might not extract non-module inline scripts (it only extracts module scripts). Let me check.\n\nActually — Vite's HTML processing extracts BOTH module and non-module inline scripts to separate files. So the inline script will be preserved. Good.\n\nLet me make the changes and verify.\n\nLet me write the edits.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the approach once more. The user's browser is very limited. The production build is pre-bundled, so it should load fast. But the browser is struggling. The key question is: WHERE does it stop?\n\nThe overlay approach answers this. Let me implement it.\n\nBut — I'm worried about Vite's HTML processing. Let me verify by rebuilding and checking the output.\n\nLet me make the edits, rebuild, and verify.\n\nLet me start with the HTML edit.\n","seq":152735,"t":9323867,"text":"\n\nThe plan: an inline script in the HTML creates a visible overlay (\"page loaded — waiting for module\") *before* the module runs, and `scene252.ts` takes that element over and updates it with each init stage + any error. That way a screenshot tells us whether the HTML loaded, whether the module arrived, and exactly which await hangs. Let me make the edits.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":0,"draft_n":8848,"draft_n_accepted":4128,"predicted_ms":62600.0,"predicted_n":5392,"prompt_ms":61750.0,"prompt_n":61054},"to_request":151337,"usage":{"cached_tokens":0,"completion_tokens":5392,"prompt_tokens":61054}}
