{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The preview server is up and serving the new HTML with the overlay inline script, and the chunk has the take-over code. \n\nNow let me record what I did and explain to the user what to look for in the screenshot.\n\nLet me summarize the diagnostic setup:\n1. The HTML now has an inline script that creates a visible overlay (`#fps252-init`) showing \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\" as soon as the page's HTML is parsed (before the module loads).\n2. The module (scene252.ts) takes over that overlay when it loads, changing it to \"scene252 module loaded — init starting…\" and then appending each init stage (engine, havok, terrain, nav-plugin, navmesh, bots, font, pre-register, registered, started) with elapsed ms.\n3. If init throws, the catch block shows \"ERROR: <message>\" in red on the overlay.\n4. The inline script also has window error/unhandledrejection handlers that append to the overlay.\n\nSo in a screenshot, the user will see one of:\n- \"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\" → the HTML loaded but the module never arrived (network/browser issue).\n- \"scene252 module loaded — init starting…\" + some stages → the module loaded, and the LAST stage shown is where init stopped.\n- \"scene252 module loaded — init starting…\" + stages + \"ERROR: ...\" → init failed with that error.\n- Just the spinner (no overlay) → the HTML itself didn't load (or the inline script didn't run).\n\nThis is a robust diagnostic. Let me tell the user to reload and screenshot.\n\nLet me also note: the overlay is top-left, semi-transparent black with green text, max-width 42vw, so the game (if it loads) is still visible on the right.\n\nLet me record this and explain to the user.\n\nActually — let me reconsider one thing. The user's browser is very slow. The production build has a large module graph (scene252 imports many chunks). If the browser is slow, the module might take a long time to load. The overlay will show \"waiting for module…\" until the module loads. If the module loads but hangs on an await, the overlay shows the stages up to the hang.\n\nThis is exactly what we need. Let me tell the user to reload and screenshot.\n\nLet me record the change and explain.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should I keep the `console.warn` calls? They're redundant now (the overlay shows the stages), but they don't hurt. In a normal browser, the console would show the stages too. Let me keep them.\n\nActually — wait. The user's browser console is EMPTY. So the `console.warn` calls won't show. But the overlay (DOM text) will show. So the overlay is the primary channel. The `console.warn` calls are secondary. Let me keep them (they're useful for debugging in a normal browser, and they don't hurt).\n\nLet me record and explain.\n\nLet me write a clear explanation for the user:\n1. What I added (the on-page overlay).\n2. What to look for in the screenshot (the 4 cases).\n3. Ask them to reload and screenshot.\n\nLet me be concise.\n","seq":154432,"t":9420085,"text":"\n\nThe preview is serving the new build, and both pieces are confirmed in the served output:\n- The HTML's inline script creates the overlay (`#fps252-init`) showing **\"scene252.html loaded — waiting for module…\"** as soon as the page's HTML parses — *before* any module runs.\n- The scene chunk takes that element over when the module loads, changing it to **\"scene252 module loaded — init starting…\"** and appending each stage (`engine`, `havok`, `terrain`, `nav-plugin`, `navmesh`, `bots`, `font`, `pre-register`, `registered`, `started`) with elapsed ms.\n- If init throws, the `catch` writes **`ERROR: <message>`** in red on the same overlay; the inline script also catches window errors/unhandled rejections.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":69888,"draft_n":2149,"draft_n_accepted":846,"predicted_ms":24010.0,"predicted_n":1153,"prompt_ms":1240.0,"prompt_n":586},"to_request":153869,"usage":{"cached_tokens":69888,"completion_tokens":