{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me reflect on the step I just completed. I made several decisions in this step:\n\n1. I decided to render init progress as visible on-page text (a DOM overlay) instead of relying on console output — because the user's browser only yields a screenshot and an empty console.\n\n2. I decided to add an HTML-level inline script that creates the overlay element *before* the module loads, so we can distinguish \"page loaded but module never arrived\" from \"module loaded but init hangs.\"\n\n3. I decided to take over the same element from the module (scene252.ts) rather than creating a new one — so there's a single overlay that transitions from \"waiting for module\" to \"module loaded\" to the init stages.\n\n4. I decided to rebuild the production build and restart the preview server (rather than use the dev server) — because the production build is pre-bundled and loads fast, and the asset hash changed so the preview server needed a restart.\n\n5. I decided to add window error/unhandledrejection handlers to the inline script so errors show on-page — because the console is empty and the user can only see a screenshot.\n\nLet me list these as DECISION lines. These are decisions about how the project is built/tested (workarounds for the limited browser).\n\nLet me be precise about which are genuine decisions from THIS step:\n\n- DECISION: Show scene252 init progress + errors as visible on-page text (a fixed-position DOM overlay) instead of console output — WHY: the user's browser only captures a screenshot and its console is empty, so on-page text is the only observable channel.\n\n- DECISION: Create the overlay element via an inline script in scene252.html (before the module loads) and have scene252.ts take it over — WHY: this distinguishes \"HTML loaded but module never arrived\" from \"module loaded but init hangs,\" which a module-only overlay couldn't.\n\n- DECISION: Serve via the production build (vite build → vite preview) and restart the preview server after rebuilding — WHY: the pre-bundled static build loads within the browser's window (the dev server's on-demand transforms were too slow), and the asset hash changed so the preview needed a restart to serve the new dist.\n\n- DECISION: Add window error/unhandledrejection handlers to the inline overlay script — WHY: with an empty console, on-page error text is the only way to surface a load/runtime failure in a screenshot.\n\nThese are all from this step. Let me write them out.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — are these \"decisions about how this project is built\"? The instruction says \"an API choice, a convention, a structure, a tooling choice, a workaround.\" \n\n- The on-page overlay is a workaround (for the limited browser). Yes.\n- The HTML inline script + module take-over is a structure/workaround. Yes.\n- Serving via production build + restart is a tooling choice. Yes.\n- The error handlers are part of the workaround. Yes.\n\nLet me list them. I'll be concise.\n","seq":155133,"t":9452004,"text":"\n\nDECISION: Show scene252's init progress and any error as visible on-page text (a fixed-position DOM overlay) instead of console output — WHY: the user's browser only yields a screenshot and its console is empty, so on-page text is the only observable channel.\nDECISION: Create the overlay element via an inline script in `scene252.html` (before the module loads) and have `scene252.ts` take that same element over — WHY: it distinguishes \"HTML loaded but module never arrived\" from \"module loaded but init hangs,\" which a module-only overlay couldn't.\nDECISION: Add `window` error/unhandledrejection handlers to the inline overlay script — WHY: with an empty console, on-page error text is the only way to surface a load/runtime failure in a screenshot.\nDECISION: Serve via the production build (`vite build` → `vite preview`) and restart the preview server after rebuilding — WHY: the pre-bundled static build loads withi