ider. Is this really a \"discovery from this step\"? The step I just completed was:\n1. Checked the build output (clean, no errors).\n2. Stopped the preview server.\n3. Started the dev server.\n\nThe discovery that \"the production build hangs on createEngine for all scenes\" came from the user's screenshot report (the previous turn), not from this step. But it informed my decision in this step (to abandon the production build and go back to the dev server).\n\nHmm. The user asked \"Only discoveries from this step.\" So I should focus on what I discovered in THIS step (the step where I checked the build output, stopped preview, started dev server).\n\nIn this step, I:\n1. Checked the build output — it was clean (no errors, just chunk-size warnings). This confirmed the bundle isn't corrupted.\n2. Stopped the preview server.\n3. Started the dev server.\n\nDid anything behave differently than I expected in this step? \n\n- The build output was clean — I expected it might have errors, but it didn't. So the bundle isn't corrupted. This is a (mild) discovery: the production build's createEngine hang is NOT due to a corrupted/erroring build; it's due to the browser stalling on the large pre-bundled graph.\n\nActually — I think the most important gotcha from this whole exchange (which culminated in this step) is:\n\nGOTCHA: The production build (vite build → vite preview) is the wrong runtime for this constrained browser — it stalls inside `createEngine` for every scene (stock scene2 included), before any init stage prints, while the dev server let the same browser clear `createEngine` and reach terrain. — IMPLICATION: stop trying to serve a production build for runtime testing; go back to the dev server (where the browser actually makes progress) and attack the real hang (the recast wasm / nav-plugin load) there.\n\nThis is a genuine discovery that changes my approach. Let me record it.\n\nBut — is it \"from this step\"? The screenshot evidence came from the previous turn.