{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I have the full picture of the recast glue. The key function is:\n\n```js\nasync function instantiateAsync(binary, binaryFile, imports) {\n    if (!binary && typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming == \"function\") {\n        try {\n            var response = fetch(binaryFile, { credentials: \"same-origin\" });\n            var instantiationResult = await WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(response, imports);\n            return instantiationResult;\n        } catch (reason) {\n            err(`wasm streaming compile failed: ${reason}`);\n            err(\"falling back to ArrayBuffer instantiation\");\n        }\n    }\n    return instantiateArrayBuffer(binaryFile, imports);\n}\n```\n\nSo the glue:\n1. If `binary` is not set AND `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` is a function, it tries the streaming path.\n2. If the streaming path throws (rejects), it falls back to `instantiateArrayBuffer`.\n3. If `binary` IS set (i.e., `wasmBinary` is pre-seeded), it skips the streaming path and goes straight to `instantiateArrayBuffer`.\n\nSo there are TWO ways to force the ArrayBuffer path:\n1. Pre-seed `wasmBinary` (set `Module.wasmBinary` to the wasm bytes). But — the glue is a module (ESM), and `wasmBinary` is a local variable. I can't easily set it from outside.\n2. Make `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` reject (so the catch fires and falls back to `instantiateArrayBuffer`).\n\nOption 2 is the monkey-patch approach. Let me implement it.\n\nBut — wait. There's a subtlety. The glue checks `typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming == \"function\"`. If I monkey-patch `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to a function that rejects, the check passes (it's still a function), and the streaming path is tried. When it rejects, the catch fires, and it falls back to `instantiateArrayBuffer`. \n\nSo the monkey-patch approach works:\n```ts\nconst origInstantiateStreaming = WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\nWebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (async () => {\n    throw new Error(\"force ArrayBuffer path\");\n}) as typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\n```\n\nBut — this affects ALL wasm loads (including Havok). If Havok uses `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`, it would also fall back to the ArrayBuffer path. This might be fine (or it might break Havok).\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Actually — the Havok wasm is loaded via `HavokPhysics({ locateFile: ... })`. The Havok glue might use `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` or `WebAssembly.instantiate`. If I monkey-patch `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to reject, the Havok glue would also fall back to the ArrayBuffer path.\n\nBut — the ArrayBuffer path (`WebAssembly.instantiate`) is a standard Web API, and it should work for both the recast and Havok wasm. The streaming path (`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`) is the one that hangs (in this browser). So the ArrayBuffer path should be the fix for both.\n\nActually — wait. On the dev server, the Havok wasm loaded fine (havok +64ms). So the Havok wasm load works via the streaming path (or the ArrayBuffer path). If I monkey-patch `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to reject, the Havok wasm would fall back to the ArrayBuffer path. This should still work (the ArrayBuffer path is standard).\n\nSo the monkey-patch should be safe for both the recast and Havok wasm loads.\n\nLet me implement the monkey-patch. I'll put it at the start of `main()` (before the wasm loads).\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. The parallel-load fix (from the working set) loads the recast wasm in parallel with the Havok wasm. So the recast wasm might be loaded BEFORE the Havok wasm (or concurrently). In that case, the monkey-patch would affect both.\n\nBut — the monkey-patch is applied at the start of `main()`, before any wasm loads. So it affects both the recast and Havok wasm loads. If the ArrayBuffer path works for both, the monkey-patch is fine.\n\nLet me implement the monkey-patch.\n\nActually — let me reconsider wheth