a `fetch(locateFile(...))`, so a Node repro can't use the relative `/recast-navigation.wasm` path — IMPLICATION: pre-seed `Module.wasmBinary` with the on-disk bytes to exercise the `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) path in Node.\ninterview-t10-f135396-2\tgotcha: the recast glue's factory is `async` and does `var wasmExports = await createWasm()` (fully compiles+instantiates before resolving), whereas Havok's factory is synchronous and returns a `readyPromise` that resolves after background instantiation — IMPLICATION: a stall inside recast's `createWasm`/`instantiateStreaming` surfaces as a never-settling `createNavigationPluginAsync` (no rejection, no error box), which is exactly the spinner symptom.\ninterview-t10-f135800-0\tdecision: Load the Havok physics wasm and the Recast nav-plugin wasm in parallel in scene252 (start `navPromise` before the `HavokPhysics` await, `await` it after player setup) — WHY: the second wasm module's `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` compile stalls after the 2MB Havok compile in the browser, so compiling them concurrently avoids the serial stall.\ninterview-t10-f135800-1\tdecision: Prefer the parallel-load workaround over monkey-patching `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` to force the ArrayBuffer path — WHY: parallel-load is high-level and fits the \"scene code reads like an API demo\" convention, whereas the monkey-patch is a low-level global mutation; parallel-load is the first attempt, with ArrayBuffer-forcing kept as the documented fallback if it still hangs.\ninterview-t10-f135800-2\tdecision: Keep the `console.warn` init-stage markers in scene252 for now and remove them only after the fix is confirmed — WHY: the next reload needs them to verify all stages (up to \"started\") now print, and GUIDANCE §0b requires deleting debug code before commit.\ninterview-t10-f136141-0\tconstraint: scene252 must load its two wasm modules (Havok + Recast) concurrently, not serially — a second wasm module compiled via `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` after the ~2MB Havok wasm stalls/hangs in the browser (Node's `WebAssembly.instantiate`/ArrayBuffer path handles the same Havok→recast sequence fine in 64ms, so the serial streaming compile is the culprit; the fix is the parallel `navPromise` load, and if that proves insufficient the fallback is forcing the ArrayBuffer path by making `instantiateStreaming` reject for the recast load).\ninterview-t11-0\tgotcha: I expected to find a broken/failing module in the served graph to explain \"no banner, no markers,\" but found none — the entry transform is complete, every module returns 200, bare imports are rewritten to resolvable `/@fs/...` paths, and `HavokPhysics_es.js` (served via `/@fs/`, not Vite dep-optimization) has no bare imports of its own — IMPLICATION: the regression is NOT server-side and NOT caused by my parallel-load edit (which sits after `createEngine` and can't stop the \"engine\" marker); the cause is a browser-side stale/failed module load (likely cached during the dev-server restart window), so I should stop editing code / restarting the server and instead have you hard-reload (Cmd+Shift+R) and check the Network tab for the `scene252.ts` module status plus any console error.\ninterview-t12-0\tevidence: ```\\n{\\n  \"dev\": \"vite\",\\n  \"build\": \"vite build\"\\n}\\n```\ninterview-t12-1\tpointer: `build.rollupOptions.input: getHtmlInputs()` — the production build bundles every HTML page, so `scene252.html` is included and will land in the build output (vite.config.ts:769)\ninterview-t12-2\tpointer: there is no `preview` section in the config — only `server.port: Number(process.env.LAB_DEV_PORT) || 5174` (the dev server, env-var driven) — so `vite preview` defaults to port 4173 and I must pass `--port 5182` explicitly (vite.config.ts:743)\ninterview-t12-3\tpointer: comment \"@recast-navigation/* ship a wasm binary the esbuild optimizer can't process; serve…\" — the dev config excludes it from `optimizeDeps`; the production build's bundl