le the dev server gets past it (engine +22ms). — IMPLICATION: the `createEngine` stall is a runtime behavior difference between the dev server and the production bundle, not a build failure; I should stop hunting build output (there's nothing to find) and instead either diff how the bundle differs from the dev transform at runtime, or abandon the production-build path and go back to the dev server, which is the only path that reached terrain.\ninterview-t14-1\tdecision: Classify the build's `createEngine` stall as a build/preview infrastructure problem rather than a scene252 bug — WHY: stock scene2 hangs identically on the build, so the failure is shared across scenes and lives in the bundle/serve path, not in my code.\ninterview-t14-2\tdecision: Keep diagnosing the real hang (the recast wasm / `nav-plugin` stage) on the dev server — WHY: it is the only path where this browser made actual progress (engine +22ms → havok +64ms → terrain +90ms), so it is the viable surface for pinpointing the recast load stall.\ninterview-t14-f161153-0\tdecision: The production build (vite build + preview) is the regression, not scene252's code — WHY: `createEngine` never resolves on the build for every scene (stock scene2 hangs identically), while the same browser got past `createEngine` (engine +22ms) on the dev server, so the fault is in the built bundle/preview path, not the scene.\ninterview-t14-f162776-0\tgotcha: The production build (`vite build` → `vite preview`) is a dead end for this browser — it's clean (no build errors, bundle not corrupted) yet this browser stalls inside `createEngine` on it for *every* scene (stock scene2 included), before any init stage prints, while the same browser cleared `createEngine` on the dev server (engine +22ms → havok +64ms → terrain +90ms). So the stall is the browser choking on the large pre-bundled graph, not a build error — IMPLICATION: stop trying to serve a production build for runtime testing here; go back to the dev server (where the browser actually reaches terrain) and attack the real hang — the recast wasm / `createNavigationPluginAsync` (nav-plugin) load — on that server.\ninterview-t14-f163817-0\tdecision: Abandon the production build (`vite build` → `vite preview`) as the serving path and return to the Vite dev server — WHY: the build stalls inside `createEngine` for every scene (stock scene2 included) while the same browser cleared `createEngine` on the dev server, so the pre-bundled path is the regression, not my scene code.\ninterview-t14-f164216-0\tconstraint: The production build (`vite build` + `vite preview`) is not usable for runtime testing in this environment — it hangs inside `createEngine` for *all* scenes (even stock scene2) in the user's browser, while the same browser cleared `createEngine` on the dev server; runtime verification must use the dev server (`LAB_DEV_PORT=5182 pnpm exec vite` from `lab/`), not the build.\ninterview-t14-f164216-1\tconstraint: The user's browser only yields a screenshot plus console lines, and the console is empty/stripped on the build — so init progress and errors must be rendered as on-page DOM text (the `#fps252-init` overlay), never relied on via `console.*`.\ninterview-t14-f165659-0\tgotcha: The recast glue's `instantiateAsync` (recast-navigation.wasm.js) tries `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` first and only falls back to `instantiateArrayBuffer` (`WebAssembly.instantiate`) inside a `catch` — so if streaming *hangs* (never settles) rather than rejects, that fallback is unreachable — IMPLICATION: to force the ArrayBuffer path I must make `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` actively *reject* (monkey-patch it to throw), since a plain hang can't be caught by the glue's `catch`.\ninterview-t14-f165659-1\tgotcha: the glue's internal wasm filename is `recast-navigation.wasm.wasm` (double extension) — `findWasmBinary()` calls `locateFile(\"recast-navigation.wasm.wasm\")` — but scene252's `locateFile` ignores its argument and always returns `/recast-n