t: The scene must set `scene.fixedDeltaMs` (e.g. `1000/60`, scene40 pattern) — `moveWithCollisions`/`integrate` are no-ops when the world timestep is zero, so physics-driven player/bot movement silently dies without it.\ninterview-t6-f43708-0\tevidence: ```…\ninterview-t6-f43708-1\tpointer: the actual frame-loop ordering — i.e. where the engine consumes registered rendering contexts per surface and decides draw order (scene vs. text renderer) — is still unverified; I need to confirm the TextRenderer draws *after* the scene on the same canvas (engine render-context registry, referenced from `registerRenderingContext` in text-renderer.ts:437)\ninterview-t6-f43708-2\tpointer: `unregisterTextRenderer` / `disposeTextRenderer` exist for HUD teardown/disposal if the scene is ever re-registered or the device is lost (text-renderer.ts:441-456)\ninterview-t6-f43708-3\tpointer: `createTextRenderer` takes a `SurfaceContext`, not `EngineContext` — scene180 passes `engine` directly, so EngineContext evidently satisfies SurfaceContext; worth keeping in mind if I ever pass a non-engine surface (text-renderer.ts:289)\ninterview-t6-f44439-1\tevidence: ```\\nexport function registerRenderingContext(surface: SurfaceContext, context: RenderingContext): boolean {\\n    if (surface._renderingContexts.indexOf(context) !== -1) {\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n    surface._renderingContexts.push(context);\\n    return true;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOne caveat: this only proves coexistence, not order. Whether the scene draws first and the text (with `clear: false`) lands on top still hinges on the frame loop at `engine.ts:512` (`const ctxs = surface._renderingContexts;`) — I hadn't read that function yet.\\n\\n2. Not needed now, may matter later:\ninterview-t6-f44439-2\tpointer: `engine.ts:512` iterates `surface._renderingContexts` in the frame loop — read it before wiring the HUD to confirm contexts render in registration order (scene first, text after) (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44439-3\tpointer: `device-lost-recovery.ts:217` iterates all contexts on device loss — if the text renderer's per-layer GPU resources aren't in that recovery path, the HUD could break after a GPU device reset; check only if device loss comes up during playtest (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/device-lost-recovery.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44439-4\tpointer: `gpu-task-timer.ts:121` times per-context GPU work — useful only if I need to attribute frame-time cost between the 3D scene and the text pass (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/gpu-task-timer.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44439-5\tpointer: `surface.ts:241/256` clears `_renderingContexts` on surface teardown — only relevant if I add/stop the engine mid-session (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/surface.ts)\ninterview-t6-f44822-0\tgotcha: `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs?)` (mesh-factories.ts) is the dedicated in-place geometry-replacement seam — it bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record — IMPLICATION: tile removal must use it, not a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `mesh._gpu` swap (which would leave cached bundles binding the freed buffers).\ninterview-t6-f44822-1\tgotcha: FreeCamera's world matrix is built from `position` + `target` via `mat4LookAtLH`; `_yaw`/`_pitch` are internal and only consumed by `attachFreeControl` — IMPLICATION: the FPS camera must set `cam.position` and `cam.target` each frame from the player's eye + (yaw, pitch); setting `_yaw`/`_pitch` alone changes nothing.\ninterview-t6-f44822-2\tgotcha: NavCrowd has no agent reposition/teleport API — the agent's simulated position can drift from the character controller's real position — IMPLICATION: drive the bot controller with the agent's velocity and re-target via `agentGoto` periodically/on drift; fall back to `computePath` waypoint-following if drift gets bad.\ninterview-t6-f44822-3\tgotcha: A TextRenderer is a separate rendering context on the same surface, and render order = registration order in `surface._renderingContexts` — IMPLICATION: register the 3D scene first and the text renderer second, and create it with `clear: false` so it overlays instead of wiping the 3D frame.\ninterview-t6-f44822-4\tgotcha: `PhysicsWorld._timestep` is `@internal` — IMPLICATION: read dt from the `onPhysicsAfterStep(world, (timestep) => ...)` callback parameter instead.\ninterview-t6-f44822-5\tgotcha: The controller's friction is plain public fields (`staticFriction`, `dynamicFriction`) on `PhysicsCharacterController` — the plan's open question (\"does the controller expose its shape for friction?\") resolves as: no shape access needed, tune the fields directly — IMPLICATION: Quake slide is tuned via controller fields + `setPhysicsShapeMaterial` on the tile shapes, both already planned.\ninterview-t6-f45976-0\tgotcha: the plan's terrain-rebuild seam was `uploadMeshToGPU` + manual `_gpu` swap, but the correct API is `resizeMeshGeometry(engine, mesh, positions, normals, indices, uvs?)` (mesh-factories.ts) — it reallocates buffers in place AND bumps every registered scene's `_renderableVersion` so cached draw bundles re-record; a raw `uploadMeshToGPU` swap would keep stale buffer bindings — IMPLICATION: destroyTile must call `resizeMeshGeometry`, and must read fresh geometry from `mesh._cpuIndices`/`_cpuPositions` each time (resize updates those copies), not from a cached copy I own.\ninterview-t6-f45976-1\tgotcha: `createPhysicsAggregate(world, node, ...)` is typed `node: Mesh`, not `SceneNode` — a bare TransformNode per tile won't compile against it — IMPLICATION: per-tile static boxes must be built with `createPhysicsBody(world, node, PhysicsMotionType.STATIC)` + `createPhysicsShape(world, {type: BOX, parameters: {extents}})` + `setPhysicsBodyShape(world, body, shape)` (the character controller proves TransformNode + physics body works; `createPhysicsBody` reads `node.position`/`rotationQuaternion` at creation, so the node must be positioned before