e `canvas.dataset` markers to find which await hangs — WHY: `loader.js` only dismisses the overlay on `data-ready`/`data-loaded`, and my scene sets `data-ready` only after `startEngine`, so a perpetual spinner pins the stall to init.\ninterview-t9-f104748-1\tdecision: The hang is one of the post-engine awaits (HavokPhysics, createNavigationPluginAsync, loadFont, registerScene, startEngine), not an asset fetch — WHY: all three assets (HavokPhysics.wasm, recast-navigation.wasm, Inter.ttf) return 200, and the \"Babylon Lite v0.1.0 - WebGPU engine\" banner is logged inside `createEngine` (engine.ts:313), proving that step resolved.\ninterview-t9-f107343-0\tgotcha: `grep \"export async function startEngine\"` in `packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts` returned nothing — the declaration I assumed (async, in engine.ts) doesn't exist there as I expected — IMPLICATION: locate the real `startEngine` export (it may be a non-async `export function` returning a Promise, or live in another file) before reasoning about which await can hang.\ninterview-t9-f107343-1\tgotcha: `registerScene` (scene-core.ts:400) is `await buildScene(scene)` + `await Promise.all(task._preload)` + conditional `swapchain-overlay.js` import, not just \"material pipeline processing\" — IMPLICATION: the init hang candidate is broader than material processing; read `buildScene` next (it's where material pipelines + thin-instance imports run) rather than assuming the stall is in `startEngine`.\ninterview-t9-f108295-0\tgotcha: `createNavMesh` builds a solo navmesh by default; dynamic obstacles (`addBoxObstacle`) only work with a tile-cache navmesh built with `maxObstacles > 0` — scene252 passes no `maxObstacles`, so every tile-destruction nav-obstacle call will fail at runtime. — IMPLICATION: add `maxObstacles` to the `createNavMesh` params in scene252.ts.\ninterview-t9-f108295-1\tgotcha: loader.js only shows its error box on window `error`/`unhandledrejection`, and `main().catch` handles rejections (cons