w-t10-2).\n\nHmm, wait a moment. What if the issue is that recast's glue code module is a large minified file, and the dev server's transform (esbuild minify?) is slow, and the browser's 8-second window expires before it arrives? The glue code is probably ~500KB–1MB minified. Vite's dev transform for a .js file from node_modules — dep optimization! Vite pre-bundles deps in node_modules/.vite/deps. Is @recast-navigation excluded from optimizeDeps? Yes — interview-t12-3: \"The dev config excludes it from optimizeDeps\" (comment: \"@recast-navigation/* ships a wasm binary that esbuild's optimizer can't handle; serve it raw\"). So it's served raw via /@fs/ or the dep-optimized path… excluded from optimizeDeps means served as source via /node_modules/.vite/... or /@fs/. Serving raw is fast (no transform). So it should arrive quickly.\n\nUgh. OK, let me stop speculating and go look. Plan:\n\n1. Read the current scene252.ts (verify the actual code — where the patch is, where navPromise is started, what markers exist).\n2. Read navigation.ts's `_ensureRecast` to know exactly what it awaits (and where a hang could hide).\n3. curl the served recast glue code module and its dynamic imports, to confirm they're 200 and fast.\n4. Add sharper instrumentation to scene252.ts:\n   - Log when `createNavigationPluginAsync` is called (already marked? maybe)\n   - Wrap `fetch` to log start/end of wasm fetches\n   - Log calls to the patched instantiateStreaming\n   - Possibly: a `nav-promise-started` marker right after the call, and `nav-promise-settled` via `.then/.catch` on navPromise (to see if it ever settles, and with what error).\n5. Restart the dev server (stale transform gotcha), verify with curl that the served content matches disk, and ask the user to reload.\n\nActually — one more hypothesis worth checking first, because it's cheap: what if `navPromise` is actually being awaited before the patch is applied? That is, the order in the file is: engine → navPromise start → patch → Havok? No — the patch prints \"force-arraybuffer +0ms\" at +0ms, which is before engine +10ms. So the patch is before the engine. And navPromise is after the engine (to be parallel with Havok). So the patch is first. Confirmed by marker order.\n\nAnother cheap check: what if `createNavigationPluginAsync` is imported from the `babylon-lite` barrel, and the barrel's static import graph includes navigation.ts, which statically imports the recast glue code at module level (not dynamically)? Then the glue code module would be loaded at scene252's module load time — before main() even runs — and its factory wouldn't be called until createNavigationPluginAsync is called. Fine, either way.\n\nHmm, OK, yet another hypothesis: what if recast's `instantiateAsync` runs before the patch? Because the factory was called at module evaluation time of the glue code module (emscripten builds sometimes auto-run at load time! `Module[\"instantiateWasm\"]`… no. But some emscripten builds call `main()`/init at module scope). If the recast glue code auto-starts instantiation at module load (before scene252's main() runs), it would call the real `instantiateStreaming` (not patched), which… works in scene170. So it should resolve, not hang. Unless the streaming compile hangs when… no, scene170 works.\n\nOK, let me also reconsider: the user's browser \"only produces screenshots and console lines\" — the console is visible now (dev server). Fine.\n\nActually, you know what — let me look at the actual code first. Let me read scene252.ts and navigation.ts's _ensureRecast. And curl the served modules. That will ground everything.\n\nOne more important thing: the user's console shows lines attributed to \"HavokPhysics_es.js\" — so the console shows source attribution. If recast's err lines had run, they'd be attributed to the recast glue code module. Their absence is strong evidence that recast's catch block never ran.\n\nLet me also consider this: the recast glue code's `err` might be a no-op, or console.debug (filtered out)? Emscripten's default `err` is `console.error.bind(console)`. But some builds override it. If recast's err is console.debug, the user might not see it (console filtered to errors/warnings?). The user said \"full console\" — maybe all levels are shown. Can't be sure. But even if err is silent, the ArrayBuffer fallback should still run and resolve → nav-plugin marker should print. It didn't. So either way, recast's instantiate path did not complete.\n\nSo the remaining candidates, in order of likelihood:\n1. The recast glue code module or one of the three dynamic imports never arrived in the browser (dev server transform latency + browser window) → import Promise never settles → silence.\n2. fetch(\"/recast-navigation.wasm\") hangs in the browser (unlikely given scene170, but scene170 fetches it as the first wasm… hmm, actually same URL).\n3. recast's streaming path was taken without going through my patch (e.g., the glue code captured `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` at module scope before the patch ran — if the glue code's factory runs at module load time… but the factory is called at init time, after the patch).\n\nWait — actually, hmm, #3 variant: what if the recast glue code's `instantiateAsync` was called at module evaluation time (auto-run)? Emscripten's MODULARIZE builds don't auto-run; they export a factory. `@recast-navigation/wasm` — the factory is invoked via `core.init(impl)`. Not auto-run.\n\nAnd #1 — how do I test it? Instrument: wrap the dynamic import path? I can't modify navigation.ts (engine code — well, could I temporarily? GUIDANCE §0b says remove debug code before commit; temporarily instrumenting engine code and reverting is ugly but possible. Better: from scene252.ts, I can dynamically import the same subpaths that `_ensureRecast` imports, with timing markers! If I `await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\")` etc. in scene252.ts with markers, I can see if the imports resolve. But wait — do I know the exact subpaths? interview-t10-f130738-2: \"the three dynamic imports inside `_ensureRecast` (core / generators / wasm/wasm subpath)\". I need to read navigation.ts to get the exact specifiers. And whether pnpm allows importing @recast-navigation/* from lab's scene code — lab has @recast-navigation as a dependency (per \"listed:lab/node_modules/@recast-navigation\"). OK.\n\nHmm, but actually, simpler: I can add a marker before and after the `createNavigationPluginAsync` call (synchronous part), and attach `.then`/`.catch` to navPromise with markers (\"nav-resolved\"/\"nav-rejected: msg\"). If navPromise never settles, the absence of those markers proves it's pending forever. Plus the fetch/instantiateStreaming wrappers show how far it got.\n\nAlso: curl the served recast glue code module to measure the dev server's response time for it (server-side latency). If the server takes >8 seconds to serve it (cold transform), that's the smoking gun — the browser's window expires first. And the fix would be: warm the transform before asking the user to reload (curl it first), or pre-bundle… actually, the real fix if that's the cause: request the module once (curl) to warm Vite's transform cache, then the user's reload gets it fast. That's a band-aid; the real fix is to configure the recast glue code in optimizeDeps with exclude for wasm but include for the JS? Or `optimizeDeps.include`? Hmm, but the comment says esbuild can't handle the wasm binary — the JS glue code file references the .wasm by URL, not by import… actually, why is it excluded? \"@recast-navigation/* ships a wasm binary that esbuild's optimizer can't handle\" — esbuild follows imports; if the glue code imports the .wasm (some builds do `import wasmUrl from \"./recast-navigation.wasm\"`), esbuild chokes. Anyway.\n\nOK — enough. Let me act:\n\nStep 1: Read the current scene252.ts.\nStep 2: Read _ensureRecast in navigation.ts.\nStep 3: curl the served recast glue code module (and the wasm), measure latency.\nStep 4: Add instrumentation:\n   - Markers around the createNavigationPluginAsync call\n   - navPromise.then/catch markers\n   - Fetch wrapper for wasm files\n   - Log the instantiateStreaming patch call\nStep 5: Restart the dev server, verify with curl that the served content matches disk, and ask the user to reload.\n\nLet me start. Actually, the working directory: the session's cwd is `~/git/experiments/t1-floor-r3/babylon-lite-minecraft-fps`?? The task notification says cwd is `~/git/experiments/t1-floor-r3/babylon-lite-minecraft-fps`, but the repo is at `~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite` (interview-t2-8: \"The session's working directory is empty; the repo is at ~/git/referen…\"). The background command used `cd ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/lab`. So I'll cd explicitly in each bash call.\n\nLet me read the files first. The repo path: ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Read lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\n- Grep for _ensureRecast in packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts and read that section\n- Bash: curl the served recast glue code with timing\n\nThese are independent — let me run them in parallel.\n\nActually, one more reconsideration: is the dev server (bg_25106995) still running? Yes, per the background task list, it's running. Good — curl :5182.\n\nWhich URL is the recast glue code served at? navigation.ts (packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts) dynamically imports `@recast-navigation/core` etc. In Vite dev, bare imports from packages/babylon-lite resolve via its node_modules. The URL would be something like `/@fs~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/node_modules/.pnpm/@recast-navigation+core@0.43.0/node_modules/@recast-navigation/core/dist/...js` or `/node_modules/.vite/deps/...` (excluded, so /@fs/). To find the exact URL, I could curl the navigation.ts transform and grep for the import specifier — Vite rewrites bare imports to absolute URLs in the served transform.\n\nSo: curl -s \"http://localhost:5182/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\" → find the babylon-lite import → follow the chain. Simpler: curl the navigation.ts source via Vite: the module URL for packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts is probably `/@fs/.../packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts`. Let me just curl it and grep for \"recast\".\n\nLet me do it.\n\nAfter gathering evidence, I'll decide the instrumentation edits.\n\nOne more thought on the leading hypothesis (1): the browser's ~8-second window. Actually, I want to revisit what \"8-second load window\" means. From interview-t12-f147457-0: \"The Vite dev server's on-demand transforms are too slow to serve within the browser's ~8-second load window (scene2/scene40 also stall)\". I derived that earlier from observing scene2/scene40 stalling on the dev server and scene252 (at the time) stalling too. Hmm, but now scene252 gets to terrain in 106ms. The transforms for scene252's graph are warm (served many times). The recast glue code module — served before? scene170's test was in the user's browser; did I curl it? Probably not. So its transform might be cold → slow.\n\nBut wait — is the glue code even transformed in the first place? It's in node_modules, excluded from optimizeDeps → served via /@fs/ as-is (no transform, just file read + maybe import rewriting). Vite does import rewriting on /@fs/ files too. For a large minified JS file, the transform is an esbuild parse + rewrite — fast (sub-second). Hmm.\n\nSo maybe #1 isn't latency. Let me look at the code before I commit to a theory.\n\nActually, one more possibility I should take seriously: **the `locateFile` patch / the patched `instantiateStreaming` interacts with recast's `initRuntime`**. From interview-t10-1: \"The awaited value is the emscripten factory's Promise (fetch → WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming/instantiateArrayBuffer → initRuntime)\". The factory awaits createWasm, then runs initRuntime etc. Fine.\n\nAnd one more: **two emscripten modules both call `addOnInit`/`removeRunDependency` on a shared runtime?** No — each module has its own Module object; the runtime is per-module. interview-t10-2 says the recast glue code doesn't touch globalThis/window. So no shared global.\n\nAnd **memory pressure**: Havok's 2MB wasm compiles, then recast's ~1MB wasm compiles in parallel — could the browser be slow to compile both simultaneously (CPU)? That would be a delay, not a hang. The user waited… the screenshot is at some point; the console's last line is terrain +106ms. How long did they wait? Unknown. If it's just slow (say 30 seconds), they might call it \"stuck\". Hmm! That's a real possibility: the parallel ArrayBuffer compilation of two wasm modules could be slow in this browser (it's a test browser, maybe CPU-limited or a headless environment without a JIT warmup). The user's console shows Havok fell back to ArrayBuffer — the ArrayBuffer path compiles the wasm synchronously on the main thread! `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) is synchronous compilation — blocks the main thread for as long as the compile takes. Havok's 2MB wasm compiled in… Havok's marker is +75ms, which is after the fallback message. Recast's ArrayBuffer compile would block the main thread — during which no console lines can be printed (the main thread is blocked!). If recast's wasm is large and the browser's wasm compiler is slow (e.g., software fallback), the main thread could be blocked for many seconds — appearing as a hang with no console output!\n\nHmm wait, but the ArrayBuffer compile happens after the catch block prints \"wasm streaming compile failed\" + \"falling back to ArrayBuffer instantiation\" — those lines should print before the synchronous compile. We don't see them from recast. So recast didn't even reach the catch. So it's not (yet) at the ArrayBuffer compile.\n\nUnless recast's err is silent (console.debug or a no-op). Then: streaming rejects (silently caught) → err (silent) → instantiateArrayBuffer → synchronous compile blocks the main thread → during the block, no markers print → after the compile, initRuntime → factory resolves → navPromise resolves → but the main thread is blocked, so… no wait, after the compile completes, the Promise microtasks run, navPromise resolves, main() continues, mark(\"nav-plugin\") prints. So we'd eventually see nav-plugin. Unless the compile takes an extremely long time (minutes?) — the user's screenshot would show silence.\n\nHmm, that's actually a plausible story: **recast's ArrayBuffer instantiation is hanging/extremely slow on the main thread** — because `WebAssembly.instantiate` compiles synchronously, and this browser's compiler struggles (or the wasm binary is… wait, the served wasm is byte-identical to disk, valid magic — verified in interview-t10-f123557-1).\n\nBut in Node, recast's ArrayBuffer path completed in ~64ms total (interview-t10-f126222-1). So the wasm itself compiles fast in V8. The user's browser is probably also Chromium/V8. So a minutes-long compile is unlikely.\n\nOK, the point is — I have two competing stories:\nA) The recast glue code module/dynamic import never arrived (import Promise pending forever).\nB) recast reached the catch silently (err not visible), is in the synchronous ArrayBuffer compile, and it's very slow / blocked.\n\nBoth are consistent with \"no recast lines, no nav-plugin marker\". To distinguish: a fetch wrapper (prints when fetch starts/resolves) and a patched instantiateStreaming that logs the call, plus a navPromise.then/catch marker. If the fetch wrapper prints for the recast wasm → we're past the import → story B (or the import arrived). If nothing prints → story A.\n\nAlso, for story A, I can check the dev server's response time for the glue code module (curl timing). If it's fast, story A weakens (though the browser's window issue is client-side…).\n\nHmm, wait, actually — let me reconsider story A more carefully. What does `_ensureRecast` import exactly? Let me read navigation.ts. If it imports `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` (a subpath that exports the glue code factory), and that subpath's package.json \"exports\" maps to a file, and Vite handles it… in scene170 it works. Same specifier, same Vite. The only difference: scene252's page also loads the HavokPhysics glue code. Both glue code modules are independent files. Their import chains shouldn't collide.\n\nWhat if… the three dynamic imports include one that's not imported in scene170's path? For example, `generators` is only used by scene252? No — `_ensureRecast` is the same function for both scenes.\n\nOK, let me go read the code. Enough theory.\n\nPlan for this turn:\n1. Read scene252.ts (current).\n2. Read the _ensureRecast section of navigation.ts.\n3. curl the served navigation.ts transform → find the recast module URL → curl it with timing; also curl the wasm with timing.\n4. Based on the findings, add instrumentation (fetch wrapper, instantiateStreaming call log, navPromise settlement marker, marker around the createNavigationPluginAsync call).\n5. Restart the dev server (bg_25106995 → stop, relaunch), verify with curl that the served scene252.ts matches disk.\n6. Ask the user to reload and report the console lines.\n\nLet me start with the parallel reads/curls.\n\nActually, the dev server serves /lite/src/lite/scene252.ts — let me curl that too and compare with disk (to see if the watcher is stale — I'll restart anyway, but good to know).\n\nLet me write the commands:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite && sed -n '1,60p' lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\n```\n\nBetter to use the read tool for the file. For navigation.ts, grep for _ensureRecast with context.\n\nAnd bash:\n```bash\ncd ~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite && curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} %{time_total}s %{size_download}B\\n\" http://localhost:5182/recast-navigation.wasm\n```\n\nAnd find the glue code URL: curl the navigation.ts transform:\n```bash\ncurl -s \"http://localhost:5182/@fs/$PWD/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts\" | grep -o 'import[^;]*recast[^;]*' | head\n```\nHmm, the /@fs/ path must be absolute. Let me try. Actually, the exact URL Vite uses for a workspace package's source: since babylon-lite is a workspace dep of lab, Vite serves it via /@fs/... or via alias. The lab's vite config has aliases (directory alias for babylon-lite subpaths). The main barrel import `babylon-lite` might be aliased to packages/babylon-lite/src/index.ts. Then navigation.ts would be served at /@fs/.../packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts. Let me curl and grep.\n\nLet me do all of this now.\n\nThen, the instrumentation. Let me draft it now (to adjust after reading the file):\n\nInside main(), where the patch is, replace with something like:\n\n```ts\nconst __fetch = window.fetch.bind(window);\nwindow.fetch = ((input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => {\n    const url = typeof input === \"string\" ? input : input instanceof URL ? input.href : input.url;\n    if (url.endsWith(\".wasm\")) {\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url}`);\n        const p = __fetch(input, init);\n        p.then(\n            (r) => console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url} -> ${r.status}`),\n            (e) => console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url} -> REJECTED ${e}`),\n        );\n        return p;\n    }\n    return __fetch(input, init);\n}) as typeof window.fetch;\nWebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (() => {\n    console.warn(\"scene252 init: instantiateStreaming called -> rejecting (ArrayBuffer fallback)\");\n    return Promise.reject(new Error(\"scene252: forcing ArrayBuffer wasm path\"));\n}) as typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\nmark(\"force-arraybuffer\");\n```\n\nHmm wait, the current patch already rejects with that error; I just add the log line. Note: wrapping fetch — the emscripten glue code calls `fetch(binaryFile, {credentials:\"same-origin\"})` — a plain function call on global fetch. Since the glue code references global `fetch`, patching `window.fetch` works (in browsers, global fetch === window.fetch; assignment to window.fetch updates the global). Actually, `fetch` in a module scope resolves to globalThis.fetch — and `window.fetch = ...` sets the property on globalThis. OK.\n\nAnd around the nav call:\n\n```ts\nmark(\"nav-start\");\nconst navPromise = createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\nnavPromise.then(\n    () => console.warn(\"scene252 init: navPromise resolved\"),\n    (e) => console.warn(`scene252 init: navPromise rejected: ${e}`),\n);\n```\n\nHmm, but wait — if navPromise is already started before the patch… no, the patch is at +0ms, before the engine. Fine. But the fetch wrapper should also be installed before the nav call — it's in the same block. Good.\n\nAlso — I realize I should double-check: is `createNavigationPluginAsync` in the current file called before `HavokPhysics` (parallel), or is it sequential? The decision said parallel (navPromise before the HavokPhysics await). The user's console shows \"havok +75ms\" and \"terrain +106ms\" — with parallel, recast should have been loading during Havok's load. If recast's glue code module needed a cold transform, the browser would have fetched it during the Havok load window. Hmm.\n\nOK, let me also prep the \"warm the transform\" step: after restarting the dev server, curl the recast glue code module URL once to warm Vite's cache before asking the user to reload. Cheap insurance against story A (latency).\n\nLet me go. First batch (independent):\n- Read scene252.ts\n- Grep for _ensureRecast in navigation.ts\n- curl the timing for the wasm + the navigation.ts transform\n\nLet me do it.\n","seq":176149,"t":10720364,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":0,"draft_n":31829,"draft_n_accepted":8394,"predicted_ms":222590.0,"predicted_n":12941,"prompt_ms":54900.0,"prompt_n":55567},"to_request":171625,"usage":{"cached_tokens":0,"completion_tokens":12941,"prompt_tokens":55567}}
{"arguments":"{\"path\": \"~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/lab/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\"}","id":"call_3e830cf5820c4c1681589c55","kind":"tool_call","name":"read","outcome":"ran","request":171625,"seq":176150,"shown":"// Scene 252: Quake-style FPS — destructible noise terrain (tile removal), shooting,\n// navmesh bots, GPU-text HUD, Web Audio SFX. Built purely on the public Lite API:\n// character controllers, physics raycasts, navmesh crowds, thin instances, text layers.\n\nimport HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\";\nimport {\n    addAgent,\n    agentGoto,\n    addToScene,\n    createBox,\n    createCapsule,\n    createDefaultTextData,\n    createEngine,\n    createFreeCamera,\n    createHavokWorld,\n    createHemisphericLight,\n    createNavigationPluginAsync,\n    createNavCrowd,\n    createNavMesh,\n    createPhysicsBody,\n    createPhysicsCharacterController,\n    createPhysicsShape,\n    createSceneContext,\n    createStandardMaterial,\n    createTextLayer,\n    createTextRenderer,\n    createTransformNode,\n    findRandomPointAroundCircle,\n    getAgentPosition,\n    getAgentVelocity,\n    loadFont,\n    mat4Compose,\n    onBeforeRender,\n    onPhysicsAfterStep,\n    physicsRaycast,\n    raycast,\n    registerScene,\n    registerTextRenderer,\n    removePhysicsBody,\n    setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity,\n    setPhysicsBodyMass,\n    setPhysicsBodyShape,\n    setPhysicsShapeMaterial,\n    setThinInstances,\n    startEngine,\n    updateDefaultTextData,\n    updateNavCrowd,\n    CharacterSupportedState,\n    PhysicsMotionType,\n    PhysicsShapeType,\n} from \"babylon-lite\";\nimport type { PhysicsBody, PhysicsCharacterController, Vec3 } from \"babylon-lite\";\nimport { ARENA_SIZE, TERRAIN_LAYER, createFpsTerrain, destroyTerrainTile, terrainHeightAt, tileFromPoint } from \"./fps-terrain.js\";\nimport { createFpsAudio } from \"./fps-audio.js\";\nimport { createFpsInput } from \"./fps-input.js\";\n\nconst PHYSICS_FPS = 60;\nconst GRAVITY = 9.8;\nconst EYE_HEIGHT = 0.7; // above the capsule center (near the head)\nconst MUZZLE_OFFSET = 0.8; // ray origin in front of the eye, outside the player capsule\nconst CAP_HEIGHT = 1.8;\nconst CAP_RADIUS = 0.5;\nconst MAX_SPEED = 10;\nconst GROUND_ACCEL = 60;\nconst AIR_ACCEL = 18;\nconst JUMP_SPEED = 7;\nconst SLIDE_FRICTION = 1.5; // per-second horizontal decay while grounded with no input\nconst MOUSE_SENS = 0.002;\nconst FIRE_INTERVAL = 0.15;\nconst RAY_RANGE = 100;\nconst BOT_COUNT = 4;\nconst BOT_SPEED = 8;\nconst BOT_RANGE = 40;\nconst DEBRIS_COUNT = 32;\nconst DEBRIS_LIFE = 8; // seconds\nconst SPAWN = { x: 0, z: -40 };\n\ninterface Bot {\n    cc: PhysicsCharacterController;\n    agentIndex: number;\n    hp: number;\n    dead: boolean;\n    respawnAt: number;\n    nextRetarget: number;\n    nextShot: number;\n    velY: number;\n    heading: number;\n}\n\ninterface Debris {\n    body: PhysicsBody | null;\n    alive: boolean;\n    expiresAt: number;\n}\n\n/** Ray (origin + normalized dir, clamped to RAY_RANGE) vs a vertical capsule. Returns distance or null. */\nfunction rayVsCapsule(o: Vec3, d: Vec3, center: Vec3, halfH: number, r: number): number | null {\n    const ax = center.x;\n    const ay = center.y - halfH; // bottom cap center\n    const az = center.z;\n    const hh = halfH * 2;\n    const wx = o.x - ax;\n    const wy = o.y - ay;\n    const wz = o.z - az;\n    const b = d.y * hh;\n    const c = d.x * wx + d.y * wy + d.z * wz;\n    const e = hh * wy;\n    const det = hh * hh - b * b;\n    let best: number | null = null;\n    // Cylindrical band (axis between the two caps).\n    if (det > 1e-8) {\n        const t = (b * e - c * hh * hh) / det;\n        const u = (e - b * c) / det;\n        if (t >= 0 && t <= RAY_RANGE && u >= 0 && u <= 1) {\n            const px = o.x + d.x * t - ax;\n            const py = o.y + d.y * t - (ay + hh * u);\n            const pz = o.z + d.z * t - az;\n            if (px * px + py * py + pz * pz <= r * r) {\n                best = t;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    // End caps, treated as spheres.\n    const cap = (cy: number): number | null => {\n        const qx = o.x - ax;\n        const qy = o.y - cy;\n        const qz = o.z - az;\n        const t = -(d.x * qx + d.y * qy + d.z * qz);\n        if (t < 0 || t > RAY_RANGE) {\n            return null;\n        }\n        const px = o.x + d.x * t - ax;\n        const py = o.y + d.y * t - cy;\n        const pz = o.z + d.z * t - az;\n        if (px * px + py * py + pz * pz <= r * r) {\n            return t;\n        }\n        return null;\n    };\n    const tb = cap(ay);\n    if (tb !== null && (best === null || tb < best)) {\n        best = tb;\n    }\n    const tt = cap(ay + hh);\n    if (tt !== null && (best === null || tt < best)) {\n        best = tt;\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n    const __initStart = performance.now();\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement;\n    // On-page init progress: the test browser only captures a screenshot (its console is\n    // empty), so each stage is rendered as visible text over the spinner. The element is\n    // created by an inline script in scene252.html (\"waiting for module\"); we take it over.\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\") as HTMLDivElement | null;\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.textContent = \"scene252 module loaded — init starting…\";\n    }\n    const mark = (stage: string): void => {\n        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart);\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: ${stage} +${ms}ms`);\n        if (overlay) {\n            overlay.textContent += `\\n  ${stage} +${ms}ms`;\n        }\n    };\n    // This browser stalls on WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming for the second wasm\n    // module (recast after Havok) — the promise never settles, so the emscripten\n    // glue's ArrayBuffer fallback (which only runs on a rejection) is unreachable.\n    // Force streaming to reject so both wasm loads take the ArrayBuffer path.\n    WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (async () => {\n        throw new Error(\"scene252: forcing ArrayBuffer wasm path\");\n    }) as typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\n    mark(\"force-arraybuffer\");\n    const engine = await createEngine(canvas);\n    mark(\"engine\");\n    const scene = createSceneContext(engine);\n    scene.fixedDeltaMs = 1000 / PHYSICS_FPS;\n    scene.clearColor = { r: 0.04, g: 0.06, b: 0.09, a: 1 };\n\n    // Camera — driven per frame from the player's eye + yaw/pitch.\n    const cam = createFreeCamera({ x: SPAWN.x, y: 5, z: SPAWN.z }, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 });\n    cam.nearPlane = 0.1;\n    cam.farPlane = 500;\n    cam.fov = 1.1;\n    scene.camera = cam;\n\n    const light = createHemisphericLight([0, 1, 0]);\n    light.intensity = 0.7;\n    addToScene(scene, light);\n\n    // Physics world (Havok V2 wasm) and navigation plugin (Recast wasm) are\n    // independent loads — kick off the Recast load now so the two wasm modules\n    // compile in parallel instead of serially (a second streaming compile after\n    // Havok stalls in some browsers).\n    const navPromise = createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n    const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\n    const world = createHavokWorld(scene, hknp, { x: 0, y: -GRAVITY, z: 0 });\n    mark(\"havok\");\n\n    // Destructible terrain: one dense grid mesh + a static box per tile.\n    const terrain = createFpsTerrain(engine, world);\n    mark(\"terrain\");\n    const terrainMat = createStandardMaterial();\n    terrainMat.diffuseColor = [0.42, 0.48, 0.4];\n    terrainMat.specularColor = [0.05, 0.05, 0.05];\n    terrain.mesh.material = terrainMat;\n    addToScene(scene, terrain.mesh);\n\n    // Player — character controller with Quake-style slide (low dynamic friction).\n    const player = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: SPAWN.x, y: terrainHeightAt(terrain, SPAWN.x, SPAWN.z) + CAP_HEIGHT / 2 + 0.1, z: SPAWN.z }, { capsuleHeight: CAP_HEIGHT, capsuleRadius: CAP_RADIUS });\n    player.staticFriction = 0;\n    player.dynamicFriction = 0.1;\n\n    // Navigation — navmesh from the terrain mesh; bots are NavCrowd agents driving controllers.\n    const nav = await navPromise;\n    mark(\"nav-plugin\");\n    createNavMesh(nav, [terrain.mesh], {\n        cs: 0.5,\n        ch: 0.5,\n        walkableSlopeAngle: 45,\n        walkableHeight: 1,\n        walkableClimb: 1,\n        walkableRadius: 0.5,\n        maxEdgeLen: 12,\n        maxSimplificationError: 1.3,\n        minRegionArea: 8,\n        mergeRegionArea: 20,\n        maxVertsPerPoly: 6,\n        detailSampleDist: 6,\n        detailSampleMaxError: 1,\n        maxObstacles: 64,\n        tileSize: 32,\n    });\n    mark(\"navmesh\");\n    const crowd = createNavCrowd(nav, BOT_COUNT, 0.5);\n\n    // Bot display — one capsule mesh rendered as thin instances.\n    const botMesh = createCapsule(engine, { height: CAP_HEIGHT, radius: CAP_RADIUS, tessellation: 12 });\n    const botMat = createStandardMaterial();\n    botMat.diffuseColor = [0.9, 0.4, 0.2];\n    botMat.specularColor = [0.1, 0.05, 0.05];\n    botMesh.material = botMat;\n    addToScene(scene, botMesh);\n    const botMatrices = new Float32Array(BOT_COUNT * 16);\n    setThinInstances(botMesh, botMatrices, BOT_COUNT);\n\n    const bots: Bot[] = [];\n    const botSpawns = [\n        { x: -30, z: 30 },\n        { x: 30, z: 30 },\n        { x: -30, z: -30 },\n        { x: 30, z: -30 },\n    ];\n    for (let i = 0; i < BOT_COUNT; i++) {\n        const s = botSpawns[i]!;\n        const p = findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, { x: s.x, y: 0, z: s.z }, 8);\n        const cc = createPhysicsCharacterController(world, { x: p.x, y: p.y + CAP_HEIGHT / 2, z: p.z }, { capsuleHeight: CAP_HEIGHT, capsuleRadius: CAP_RADIUS });\n        cc.staticFriction = 0;\n        cc.dynamicFriction = 0.1;\n        const agentIndex = addAgent(crowd, p, {\n            radius: 0.5,\n            height: CAP_HEIGHT,\n            maxAcceleration: 20,\n            maxSpeed: BOT_SPEED,\n            collisionQueryRange: 4,\n            pathOptimizationRange: 0,\n            separationWeight: 1,\n        });\n        bots.push({ cc, agentIndex, hp: 100, dead: false, respawnAt: 0, nextRetarget: 0, nextShot: 1 + i * 0.3, velY: 0, heading: 0 });\n    }\n    mark(\"bots\");\n\n    // Debris — dynamic box bodies rendered as thin instances (ring buffer).\n    const debrisMesh = createBox(engine, 0.6);\n    const debrisMat = createStandardMaterial();\n    debrisMat.diffuseColor = [0.55, 0.5, 0.45];\n    debrisMat.specularColor = [0.05, 0.05, 0.05];\n    debrisMesh.material = debrisMat;\n    addToScene(scene, debrisMesh);\n    const debrisMatrices = new Float32Array(DEBRIS_COUNT * 16);\n    setThinInstances(debrisMesh, debrisMatrices, DEBRIS_COUNT);\n    const debris: Debris[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < DEBRIS_COUNT; i++) {\n        debris.push({ body: null, alive: false, expiresAt: 0 });\n    }\n    let debrisNext = 0;\n\n    // HUD — screen-space text layers composited over the 3D frame (clear: false).\n    const font = await loadFont(\"/fonts/Inter.ttf\");\n    mark(\"font\");\n    const hudData = createDefaultTextData(font, 28, \"HP 100   KILLS 0   TILES 0\", [1, 1, 1, 1], { maxWidth: 1200, align: \"left\" });\n    const hudLayer = createTextLayer(hudData, { positionPx: { x: 24, y: 24 }, scale: 1, rotationRad: 0, opacity: 1 });\n    const crossData = createDefaultTextData(font, 20, \"+\", [1, 1, 1, 1], {});\n    const crossLayer = createTextLayer(crossData, { positionPx: { x: window.innerWidth / 2 - 8, y: window.innerHeight / 2 - 10 }, scale: 1, rotationRad: 0, opacity: 0.9 });\n    const tr = createTextRenderer(engine, { layers: [hudLayer, crossLayer], clear: false });\n\n    // Input + audio (DOM side).\n    const input = createFpsInput(canvas);\n    const audio = createFpsAudio();\n    input.onLockChange((locked) => {\n        if (locked) {\n            audio.unlock();\n        }\n    });\n\n    // ─── Game state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    let gameTime = 0;\n    let yaw = 0;\n    let pitch = 0;\n    let playerHp = 100;\n    let kills = 0;\n    let invulnerableUntil = 3;\n    let lastShot = -1;\n    const playerVel: Vec3 = { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 };\n    let lastHudText = \"\";\n\n    const eyePos = (): Vec3 => {\n        const p = player.getPosition();\n        return { x: p.x, y: p.y + EYE_HEIGHT, z: p.z };\n    };\n    const forwardDir = (): Vec3 => {\n        const cp = Math.cos(pitch);\n        return { x: Math.sin(yaw) * cp, y: Math.sin(pitch), z: Math.cos(yaw) * cp };\n    };\n\n    const respawnPlayer = (): void => {\n        player.setPosition({ x: SPAWN.x, y: terrainHeightAt(terrain, SPAWN.x, SPAWN.z) + CAP_HEIGHT / 2 + 0.1, z: SPAWN.z });\n        playerVel.x = 0;\n        playerVel.y = 0;\n        playerVel.z = 0;\n        playerHp = 100;\n        invulnerableUntil = gameTime + 3;\n    };\n\n    const damagePlayer = (amount: number): void => {\n        if (gameTime < invulnerableUntil) {\n            return;\n        }\n        playerHp -= amount;\n        audio.damage();\n        if (playerHp <= 0) {\n            respawnPlayer();\n        }\n    };\n\n    const respawnBot = (bot: Bot): void => {\n        const p = findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }, ARENA_SIZE / 3);\n        bot.cc.setPosition({ x: p.x, y: p.y + CAP_HEIGHT / 2, z: p.z });\n        bot.hp = 100;\n        bot.dead = false;\n        bot.velY = 0;\n        bot.nextRetarget = gameTime;\n        bot.nextShot = gameTime + 1.5;\n    };\n\n    const damageBot = (bot: Bot, amount: number): void => {\n        if (bot.dead) {\n            return;\n        }\n        bot.hp -= amount;\n        if (bot.hp <= 0) {\n            bot.dead = true;\n            bot.respawnAt = gameTime + 3;\n            kills++;\n        }\n    };\n\n    const spawnDebris = (x: number, y: number, z: number): void => {\n        const slot = debris[debrisNext]!;\n        debrisNext = (debrisNext + 1) % DEBRIS_COUNT;\n        if (slot.alive && slot.body) {\n            removePhysicsBody(world, slot.body);\n        }\n        const node = createTransformNode(\"fpsDebris\", x, y, z);\n        const body = createPhysicsBody(world, node, PhysicsMotionType.DYNAMIC);\n        const shape = createPhysicsShape(world, { type: PhysicsShapeType.BOX, parameters: { extents: { x: 0.3, y: 0.3, z: 0.3 } } });\n        setPhysicsBodyShape(world, body, shape);\n        setPhysicsBodyMass(world, body, 1);\n        setPhysicsShapeMaterial(world, shape, 0.3, 0.2);\n        setPhysicsBodyLinearVelocity(world, body, { x: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 4, y: 3 + Math.random() * 3, z: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 4 });\n        slot.body = body;\n        slot.alive = true;\n        slot.expiresAt = gameTime + DEBRIS_LIFE;\n    };\n\n    const tryFire = (): void => {\n        if (gameTime - lastShot < FIRE_INTERVAL) {\n            return;\n        }\n        lastShot = gameTime;\n        audio.gunshot();\n        const eye = eyePos();\n        const fwd = forwardDir();\n        // Cast from a muzzle point in front of the eye so the ray never starts inside\n        // the player's own capsule (the controller's shape can't be filter-masked).\n        const from: Vec3 = { x: eye.x + fwd.x * MUZZLE_OFFSET, y: eye.y + fwd.y * MUZZLE_OFFSET, z: eye.z + fwd.z * MUZZLE_OFFSET };\n        const to: Vec3 = { x: from.x + fwd.x * RAY_RANGE, y: from.y + fwd.y * RAY_RANGE, z: from.z + fwd.z * RAY_RANGE };\n\n        const terr = physicsRaycast(world, from, to, { membership: TERRAIN_LAYER });\n        let bestDist = Infinity;\n        let hitBot: Bot | null = null;\n        for (const bot of bots) {\n            if (bot.dead) {\n                continue;\n            }\n            const bp = bot.cc.getPosition();\n            const d = rayVsCapsule(from, fwd, bp, CAP_HEIGHT / 2, CAP_RADIUS);\n            if (d !== null && d < bestDist) {\n                bestDist = d;\n                hitBot = bot;\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (terr.hasHit && (!hitBot || terr.hitDistance < bestDist)) {\n            const tile = tileFromPoint(terrain, terr.hitPoint.x, terr.hitPoint.z);\n            if (tile) {\n                destroyTerrainTile(terrain, engine, world, nav, tile);\n                spawnDebris(terr.hitPoint.x, terr.hitPoint.y, terr.hitPoint.z);\n                audio.thud();\n            }\n        } else if (hitBot) {\n            damageBot(hitBot, 25);\n            audio.hit();\n        }\n    };\n    input.onFire(tryFire);\n\n    // ─── Physics-step logic (fixed 60 Hz) ────────────────────────────\n    onPhysicsAfterStep(world, (dt) => {\n        gameTime += dt;\n\n        // Player — Quake-style: slide on ground, weak air control, jump gated by support.\n        const support = player.checkSupport(dt, { x: 0, y: -1, z: 0 });\n        const grounded = support.supportedState === CharacterSupportedState.SUPPORTED || support.supportedState === CharacterSupportedState.SLIDING;\n        const cy = Math.cos(yaw);\n        const sy = Math.sin(yaw);\n        let ix = 0;\n        let iz = 0;\n        if (input.forward) {\n            ix += sy;\n            iz += cy;\n        }\n        if (input.back) {\n            ix -= sy;\n            iz -= cy;\n        }\n        if (input.right) {\n            ix += cy;\n            iz -= sy;\n        }\n        if (input.left) {\n            ix -= cy;\n            iz += sy;\n        }\n        const il = Math.hypot(ix, iz);\n        if (il > 0) {\n            ix /= il;\n            iz /= il;\n            playerVel.x += ix * (grounded ? GROUND_ACCEL : AIR_ACCEL) * dt;\n            playerVel.z += iz * (grounded ? GROUND_ACCEL : AIR_ACCEL) * dt;\n        }\n        if (input.jump && grounded) {\n            playerVel.y = JUMP_SPEED;\n        }\n        playerVel.y -= GRAVITY * dt;\n        if (playerVel.y < -30) {\n            playerVel.y = -30;\n        }\n        if (grounded && il === 0) {\n            const damp = Math.max(0, 1 - SLIDE_FRICTION * dt);\n            playerVel.x *= damp;\n            playerVel.z *= damp;\n        }\n        const hs = Math.hypot(playerVel.x, playerVel.z);\n        if (hs > MAX_SPEED) {\n            const s = MAX_SPEED / hs;\n            playerVel.x *= s;\n            playerVel.z *= s;\n        }\n        const preY = player.getPosition().y;\n        player.moveWithCollisions({ x: playerVel.x * dt, y: playerVel.y * dt, z: playerVel.z * dt });\n        const postY = player.getPosition().y;\n        if (playerVel.y < 0 && preY - postY < Math.abs(playerVel.y * dt) * 0.5) {\n            playerVel.y = 0; // floor contact — stop accumulating fall speed\n        }\n        const pp = player.getPosition();\n        if (pp.y < -20) {\n            respawnPlayer();\n        }\n\n        // Bots — NavCrowd steering + controller collisions.\n        updateNavCrowd(crowd, dt);\n        for (let i = 0; i < bots.length; i++) {\n            const bot = bots[i]!;\n            if (bot.dead) {\n                if (gameTime >= bot.respawnAt) {\n                    respawnBot(bot);\n                }\n                continue;\n            }\n            const bp = bot.cc.getPosition();\n            const pp2 = player.getPosition();\n            const dist = Math.hypot(pp2.x - bp.x, pp2.z - bp.z);\n            if (gameTime >= bot.nextRetarget) {\n                bot.nextRetarget = gameTime + 2 + Math.random() * 2;\n                const los = raycast(nav, { x: bp.x, y: bp.y - CAP_HEIGHT / 2, z: bp.z }, { x: pp2.x, y: pp2.y - CAP_HEIGHT / 2, z: pp2.z });\n                if (dist < BOT_RANGE && !los.hit) {\n                    agentGoto(crowd, bot.agentIndex, pp2);\n                } else {\n                    agentGoto(crowd, bot.agentIndex, findRandomPointAroundCircle(nav, bp, 20));\n                }\n            }\n            // Keep the nav agent glued to the controller (collisions cause drift).\n            const ap = getAgentPosition(crowd, bot.agentIndex);\n            if (Math.hypot(ap.x - bp.x, ap.z - bp.z) > 4) {\n                agentGoto(crowd, bot.agentIndex, bp);\n            }\n            const av = getAgentVelocity(crowd, bot.agentIndex);\n            if (av.x !== 0 || av.z !== 0) {\n                bot.heading = Math.atan2(av.x, av.z);\n            }\n            bot.velY -= GRAVITY * dt;\n            if (bot.velY < -30) {\n                bot.velY = -30;\n            }\n            const bPreY = bot.cc.getPosition().y;\n            bot.cc.moveWithCollisions({ x: av.x * dt, y: bot.velY * dt, z: av.z * dt });\n            const bPostY = bot.cc.getPosition().y;\n            if (bot.velY < 0 && bPreY - bPostY < Math.abs(bot.velY * dt) * 0.5) {\n                bot.velY = 0;\n            }\n            if (bot.cc.getPosition().y < -20) {\n                respawnBot(bot);\n                continue;\n            }\n            // Bot shooting — LOS-gated, distance-based accuracy.\n            if (gameTime >= bot.nextShot) {\n                bot.nextShot = gameTime + 1.2;\n                const los = raycast(nav, { x: bp.x, y: bp.y - CAP_HEIGHT / 2, z: bp.z }, { x: pp2.x, y: pp2.y - CAP_HEIGHT / 2, z: pp2.z });\n                if (dist < BOT_RANGE && !los.hit) {\n                    const accuracy = Math.max(0.15, 1 - dist / 50);\n                    if (Math.random() < accuracy) {\n                        damagePlayer(10);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Debris expiry.\n        for (const d of debris) {\n            if (d.alive && d.body && gameTime >= d.expiresAt) {\n                removePhysicsBody(world, d.body);\n                d.alive = false;\n            }\n        }\n    });\n\n    // ─── Render-frame logic (visual only) ────────────────────────────\n    onBeforeRender(scene, () => {\n        // Mouse look (applied once per frame from accumulated DOM deltas).\n        const delta = input.consumeLookDelta();\n        yaw -= delta.dx * MOUSE_SENS;\n        pitch -= delta.dy * MOUSE_SENS;\n        if (pitch > 1.5) {\n            pitch = 1.5;\n        }\n        if (pitch < -1.5) {\n            pitch = -1.5;\n        }\n        const eye = eyePos();\n        const fwd = forwardDir();\n        cam.position.set(eye.x, eye.y, eye.z);\n        cam.target.set(eye.x + fwd.x * 10, eye.y + fwd.y * 10, eye.z + fwd.z * 10);\n\n        // Bot display matrices.\n        for (let i = 0; i < bots.length; i++) {\n            const bot = bots[i]!;\n            const m = botMatrices.subarray(i * 16, i * 16 + 16);\n            if (bot.dead) {\n                m.fill(0);\n                m[15] = 1;\n            } else {\n                const p = bot.cc.getPosition();\n                const hm = mat4Compose(p.x, p.y, p.z, 0, Math.sin(bot.heading / 2), 0, Math.cos(bot.heading / 2), 1, 1, 1);\n                m.set(hm);\n            }\n        }\n        setThinInstances(botMesh, botMatrices, BOT_COUNT);\n\n        // Debris display matrices (node transform tracks the physics body).\n        for (let i = 0; i < debris.length; i++) {\n            const d = debris[i]!;\n            const m = debrisMatrices.subarray(i * 16, i * 16 + 16);\n            if (!d.alive || !d.body) {\n                m.fill(0);\n                m[15] = 1;\n                continue;\n            }\n            const n = d.body.node;\n            const q = n.rotationQuaternion;\n            const hm = mat4Compose(n.position.x, n.position.y, n.position.z, q.x, q.y, q.z, q.w, 1, 1, 1);\n            m.set(hm);\n        }\n        setThinInstances(debrisMesh, debrisMatrices, DEBRIS_COUNT);\n\n        // HUD text (only when the string changes).\n        const hudText = `HP ${Math.max(0, Math.round(playerHp))}   KILLS ${kills}   TILES ${terrain.destroyedCount}`;\n        if (hudText !== lastHudText) {\n            lastHudText = hudText;\n            updateDefaultTextData(hudData, hudText);\n        }\n    });\n\n    mark(\"pre-register\");\n    await registerScene(scene);\n    mark(\"registered\");\n    registerTextRenderer(tr);\n    await startEngine(engine);\n    mark(\"started\");\n    canvas.dataset.initMs = String(Math.round(performance.now() - __initStart));\n    canvas.dataset.ready = \"true\";\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n    const overlay = document.getElementById(\"fps252-init\") as HTMLDivElement | null;\n    if (overlay) {\n        overlay.style.color = \"#f88\";\n        overlay.textContent += `\\nERROR: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`;\n    }\n    const canvas = document.getElementById(\"renderCanvas\") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;\n    if (canvas) {\n        canvas.dataset.error = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n    }\n    console.error(err);\n});","t":10720389,"turn":15}
