 does import analysis + rewriting. For a 1MB minified file, that might take a bit, but usually <1s.\n\nHmm, but here's the point — the browser loads these when `createNavigationPluginAsync` is called (during init, right after engine +10ms). The browser's load window… I previously estimated ~8s. The wasm file itself: 2ms. The glue JS: let me curl to check.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety with Vite + /@fs/ + `?v=` hash: if the dev server restarted (I restarted it: bg_25106995), the dep-scan version might change (the hash in ?v=). The transform I curled was from the current server (bg_25106995) — good, that's what the browser will request.\n\nLet me curl the three module URLs with timing and status. And check their sizes.\n\nAlso — a key alternative hypothesis I should verify: **`core.init` inside `_ensureRecast`**. Let me read lines 136-175 of navigation.ts to see exactly what `_ensureRecast` does after the imports, and what `createNavigationPluginAsync` awaits. From memory: `Raw.Module = await impl()`. Let me read.\n\nAlso, let me think about what could cause one of these imports to never settle without rejecting in the browser:\n- Vite serves the module, but the response hangs (server-side transform deadlock?) — I can verify with curl.\n- The browser's fetch to that URL never completes — e.g., the URL 404s? No, 404s reject the import.\n- **Circular dependency**: if the module graph has a cycle involving these imports, the import could deadlock? Vite/esbuild handle cycles with live bindings; they don't hang.\n\nHmm, wait, actually — I should reconsider. Let me look at the three imports again: `@recast-navigation/core/dist/index.mjs`. In scene170, the same three imports work. The only difference in scene252: the page also loads `@babylonjs/havok` (HavokPhysics_es.js).\n\nIs there any chance the two glue modules share a URL or cache key in Vite? No, they're different files.\n\nCould the browser's WebGPU context creation (createEngine) interfere with the fetch? No.\n\nOK, let me curl the three URLs. And read lines 130-180 of navigation.ts.\n\nActually — wait. I just noticed something. Look at the curl output for the navigation.ts transform: the imports have `?v=ec16f4b0`. That version hash is tied to the optimizer state. But these are /@fs/ paths — for files excluded from optimizeDeps, Vite appends the version hash from the dep scanner. When the browser requests them, Vite serves them after transform. If the transform is slow on first request (cold), the browser waits. My curl just warmed them up (well, I curled navigation.ts, not the three recast modules).\n\nLet me curl the three recast module URLs now (which also warms the cache — good for the user's reload anyway) and time them.\n\nAlso let me read navigation.ts:130-180.\n\nAnd another thought — maybe I should test whether `createNavigationPluginAsync`'s Promise hangs even without Havok on the same page… I can't easily do that.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what the most likely cause is, given all the evidence:\n\n1. Node: Havok→recast sequential load with wasmBinary pre-seeded: OK in 64ms. (ArrayBuffer path in Node, no fetch.)\n2. Browser, scene170 (recast only): works. (Streaming path, first wasm on the page.)\n3. Browser, scene252, without patch (original): Havok loads (streaming), recast's streaming hangs forever.\n4. Browser, scene252, with patch: Havok's streaming rejects → ArrayBuffer → OK. Recast: no lines at all — not even the catch's err line.\n\nIn case 4, recast's load should: dynamic import (glue module) → factory → createWasm → instantiateAsync → fetch → patched instantiateStreaming (rejects) → catch → err line → ArrayBuffer → resolve.\n\nNo err line means we didn't reach the catch. So we're stuck at one of:\n(a) one of the three dynamic imports,\n(b) the factory's code before instantiateAsync (e.g., an `addOnInit` dependency loop?),\n(c) the fetch.\n\nFor (c): the wasm is served in 2ms. The fetch inside the glue: `fetch(binaryFile, {credentials:\"same-origin\"})`. binaryFile = locateFile(\"recast-navigation.wasm.wasm\") = \"/recast-navigation.wasm\". Should be fine…\n\nHmm wait, is that really so? `findWasmBinary()` → `locateFile(\"recast-navigation.wasm.wasm\")`. Our locateFile ignores its argument and returns \"/recast-navigation.wasm\". Fine.\n\nFor (a): let me curl the three modules. If they're all fast and 200, (a) is unlikely server-side, but a client-side stall is still possible (though a stall on a 200 response is bizarre).\n\nFor (b): let me read the glue's factory code. From memory: the factory is `async` and does `var wasmExports = await createWasm()` at the top. Before that — the MODULARIZE factory's body starts with `var Module = ...` merging options, then… hmm, in an emscripten MODULARIZE build, the factory is: `var Module = /** @type {EMU} */(Module || {}); ...` then `var wasmExports = await createWasm();` then init. Before createWasm, it might call `preRun` etc. Nothing that would hang.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider (a) more carefully. The browser's module graph for scene252 on the dev server:\n\nscene252.ts statically imports the `babylon-lite` barrel → which statically imports navigation.ts (among many other things). So navigation.ts is loaded at page load (before main() runs). But `_ensureRecast`'s dynamic imports only fire when `createNavigationPluginAsync` is called.\n\nNow — here's a thought — the three recast modules: do they import each other? core/dist/index.mjs might import from ../wasm/…? Hmm. If `@recast-navigation/core`'s index.mjs statically imports the wasm glue, and the glue statically imports something from core → cycle. But that would be the same in scene170.\n\nOK, let me also seriously consider a completely different angle: **the browser might not be executing `createNavigationPluginAsync` at all**. If… `navPromise` is created at step 6, but the console shows \"havok +75ms\" — HavokPhysics's call is at step 7, right after. Both are sequential statements. If step 6 threw synchronously, main() would reject → the overlay would show ERROR. It didn't. So step 6 ran and returned a pending Promise.\n\nSo the hang is inside `createNavigationPluginAsync`'s Promise chain: `_ensureRecast` → imports → `core.init(wasmFactory)` → factory → createWasm → instantiateAsync → fetch/streaming.\n\nHmm, OK wait, let me look at `_ensureRecast`'s actual code (lines 136-175) — I need to see what happens after the imports. There might be something like `await new Promise(...)` or a callback wait. Let me read.\n\nAlso — one more idea. `core.init` — from interview-t10-0: \"`Raw.Module = await impl()`\". So `core.init(impl)` = `Raw.Module = await impl()`. impl = `wasmFactory.bind(null, {locateFile})`. So it awaits the factory. Factory = the default export of `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` — the emscripten MODULARIZE factory. OK.\n\nNow, in the factory, `createWasm()` → `instantiateAsync(wasmBinary, wasmBinaryFile, info)`:\n\n```js\nif(!binary && binaryFile && typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming==\"function\"){\n  try{\n    var response=fetch(binaryFile,{credentials:\"same-origin\"});\n    var instantiationResult=await WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(response,imports);\n    return instantiationResult\n  }catch(reason){\n    err(`wasm streaming compile failed: ${reason}`);\n    err(\"falling back to ArrayBuffer instantiation\")\n  }\n}\nreturn instantiateArrayBuffer(binaryFile,imports)\n```\n\nWait — from the memory excerpt (message-19), the condition starts with `[…] !binary&&typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming==\"function\"`. The `[…]` hides the beginning — probably `if(` and maybe `binaryFile &&`. OK.\n\n`instantiateArrayBuffer(binaryFile, imports)`:\n```js\nfunction instantiateArrayBuffer(binaryFile, imports, callback){\n  return fetch(binaryFile,{credentials:\"same-origin\"}).then(response=>{\n    if(response.ok){return response.arrayBuffer()}else{throw...}\n  }).then(buffer=>WebAssembly.instantiate(buffer,imports).then(result=>receiveInstantiationResult(result)))\n}\n```\nSomething like that.\n\nSo if the catch runs, err prints, then instantiateArrayBuffer fetches again and instantiates. The err line should be visible. It's not. So the catch didn't run. So either the streaming call never happened, or the fetch before it never resolved.\n\nHmm, hmm. OK, one more wild idea: **`fetch` inside the glue module resolves to something else**. The glue is served via /@fs/ as a classic module… it uses bare `fetch` → global fetch. I'm not patching fetch (yet). No issue.\n\nAnd **`WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` — my patch is `(async () => { throw ... })`**. That's a function. `typeof` → \"function\". Called with (response, imports) → returns a rejected Promise. The `await` inside try → catch → err. It should work. For Havok it worked (Havok's glue has a different shape: `.then(callback, onRejected)` — also handled).\n\nSo the only remaining explanations:\nA) Stuck at one of the three dynamic imports (module never arrived in the browser).\nB) Stuck at the factory's code before instantiateAsync.\nC) Stuck at the fetch.\n\nTo distinguish: instrument. Since I can't easily instrument navigation.ts (engine code) — well, actually, I can add a temporary console.warn to navigation.ts and remove it later… but GUIDANCE says scene code is the user-facing reference; temporarily modifying engine code for debugging and reverting is acceptable as long as I revert. Hmm, but there's a risk of forgetting to revert. Alternatively, instrument from scene252.ts:\n\n1. In scene252.ts, before calling createNavigationPluginAsync, do the same three dynamic imports myself with markers:\n```ts\nmark(\"nav-imports-start\");\nconst [core, gens, wasmMod] = await Promise.all([\n    import(\"@recast-navigation/core\"),\n    import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\"),\n    import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\"),\n]);\nmark(\"nav-imports-done\");\n```\nBut wait — can scene252.ts import `@recast-navigation/*` directly? Lab's package.json — does lab declare @recast-navigation as a dependency? The \"listed:lab/node_modules/@recast-navigation\" entry suggests yes (it's linked under lab/node_modules). And TypeScript: will tsc resolve it? For lint/typecheck to pass, the import has to resolve. It's in lab's node_modules, so yes.\n\nBut actually — if I import them in scene252.ts, the modules get cached by the browser's module loader, and `_ensureRecast`'s imports will resolve from the cache instantly. So if my `await Promise.all` succeeds with a marker, `_ensureRecast`'s imports can't be the hang. And if it hangs, I've proven it's the imports. Either way, I get the answer!\n\nBut hmm — one risk: my imports load the modules, but `_ensureRecast` also does `core.init(wasmFactory...)` — the factory is called only once (via _ensureRecast). My imports don't call the factory. Good — the module is loaded, but the wasm isn't instantiated until _ensureRecast runs. So no double-instantiation.\n\nWait, actually — does the glue module's top-level code auto-instantiate? In a MODULARIZE build, no — it just defines the factory. OK.\n\n2. Wrap fetch to log wasm fetches:\n```ts\nconst __fetch = window.fetch;\nwindow.fetch = (input, init) => {\n    const url = typeof input === \"string\" ? input : input instanceof URL ? input.toString() : input.url;\n    if (url.includes(\".wasm\")) {\n        console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url}`);\n        const p = __fetch(input, init);\n        p.then(r => console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url} → ${r.status}`), e => console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url} → REJECTED: ${e}`));\n        return p;\n    }\n    return __fetch(input, init);\n};\n```\nTypeScript: window.fetch's type — the wrapper needs to match. `__fetch(input, init)` with input: RequestInfo | URL. Fine.\n\n3. Log the patched instantiateStreaming call:\n```ts\nWebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (() => {\n    console.warn(\"scene252 init: instantiateStreaming called → rejecting\");\n    return Promise.reject(new Error(\"scene252: forcing ArrayBuffer wasm path\"));\n}) as typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\n```\n\n4. Mark navPromise's resolution:\n```ts\nnavPromise.then(() => console.warn(\"scene252 init: navPromise settled (ok)\"), (e) => console.warn(`scene252 init: navPromise settled (error): ${e}`));\n```\nHmm — but if navPromise rejects, main()'s `await navPromise` throws → the overlay's ERROR. The then handler just adds info. Actually, if it rejects, the overlay shows the error, so the console line is redundant but harmless. Let me keep it simple: only add a marker for when the navPromise is created (\"nav-kickoff\"), plus the three-import preflight with markers.\n\nActually, let me simplify to the most decisive instrumentation:\n\n- mark(\"nav-kickoff\") right before `createNavigationPluginAsync`.\n- The fetch wrapper (logs every .wasm fetch start + settle).\n- The instantiateStreaming patch that logs calls.\n- The three-import preflight with markers, right before the nav kickoff (so the glue module is definitely loaded before Havok/terrain run).\n\nWait, order matters: if I do the preflight imports before `createEngine`, they run in parallel with everything. Sequence: patch → mark → preflight imports (marker start/done) → engine → navPromise → Havok → …\n\nHmm, but if the preflight imports hang, we never reach the engine marker — which is exactly the diagnostic info I want (the last marker will tell me).\n\nBut — TypeScript concern: `import(\"@recast-navigation/core\")` in scene252.ts — tsc will type-check it. @recast-navigation/core ships types (probably). `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")` — subpath with types? If tsc complains about missing types, lint fails. The dynamic import of a JS module without types → TS7016 (implicit any) error? In strict mode, `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")` where the package's \"exports\" don't map types → error TS2307 or implicit any. Hmm. That could break `pnpm run lint` (tsc --noEmit).\n\nAlternative: skip the preflight imports in scene252.ts, and instead temporarily instrument navigation.ts with console.warn markers, run lint… no, navigation.ts is engine code — adding console.warn there and then removing it is fine, but there's a risk of forgetting.\n\nAlternative: use `import(/* @vite-ignore */ specifier)`? No — that disables Vite's rewriting; the browser would try to resolve the bare specifier and fail.\n\nAlternative: construct the URL directly! I know the exact served URLs from the navigation.ts transform:\n- `/@fs~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/node_modules/.pnpm/@recast-navigation+core@0.43.0/node_modules/@recast-navigation/core/dist/index.mjs?v=ec16f4b0`\n- etc.\n\nBut the `?v=ec16f4b0` hash might change on dev server restart. I could fetch those URLs via `fetch()` (not dynamic import) and see if they arrive — but a fetch 200 doesn't prove the browser's module import will settle (though it's strong evidence). Hmm, and using hardcoded absolute paths in scene code is ugly.\n\nCleaner: **temporarily instrument navigation.ts** with two console.warn lines (before/after the three imports, and around core.init). It's engine code, but a temporary debug marker I'll remove later — the same kind of instrumentation I already did in scene252.ts. Risk: forgetting to remove → mitigated by recording it clearly and lint… lint won't catch console.warn (warn is allowed per GUIDANCE §6). I'll have to remove it manually. OK.\n\nActually wait — even better: what if I instrument navigation.ts to make `_ensureRecast` log each stage. That directly answers where in the chain it hangs:\n\n```ts\nasync function _ensureRecast(locateFile?: (url: string) => string): Promise<{ core: any; gens: any }> {\n    if (!_recastReady) {\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-imports-start\");\n        const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-import-core\");\n        const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-import-gens\");\n        if (...) {\n            const wasmFactory = (await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")).default;\n            console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-import-wasm\");\n            console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-init-start\");\n            Raw.Module = await wasmFactory({ locateFile });\n            console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-init-done\");\n        }\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, I need to read the actual code first. Let me read lines 130-180 of navigation.ts.\n\nBut hold on — modifying packages/babylon-lite means lint will type-check it (fine, console.warn is OK) — and per the constraint \"zero changes to packages/babylon-lite\" was a project decision (interview-t5-f27685-2: \"zero changes to packages/babylon-lite; the FPS is built purely on top of existing features\"). Temporary debug instrumentation that I fully revert is different from a permanent change. I've already been working around the scene-side instrumentation. Let me prefer scene-side if possible.\n\nScene-side preflight via `import()` — the TS problem. Let me check: does `@recast-navigation/wasm`'s package.json have types for the `/wasm` subpath? Unknown. I could try it and run tsc. If it fails, fallback: `import(/* @vite-ignore */ \"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")`… no.\n\nHmm, actually — the simplest, most robust scene-side approach: use `fetch()` to probe the three module URLs. But I'd need the URLs with the correct ?v= hash. I could discover them at runtime: fetch the navigation.ts transform and parse the import URLs? Overkill.\n\nOK, decision: **temporarily instrument navigation.ts** (a few console.warn lines in `_ensureRecast`), plus scene-side fetch wrapper + instantiateStreaming call log. Both removed before commit (recorded). This gives the most direct answer:\n\n- If \"recast-imports-start\" never prints → the hang is before _ensureRecast (inside createNavigationPluginAsync's earlier code — maybe something else I'm not seeing; let me read lines 160-180).\n- If \"recast-imports-start\" prints but not \"recast-import-core\" → the first dynamic import (core) never settles → module delivery problem → check served URL/curl timing.\n- If core/gens print but not \"recast-import-wasm\" → the glue module import never settles → the big file; check curl timing for that URL.\n- If \"recast-init-start\" prints but not \"recast-init-done\" → the factory is hanging → then the fetch wrapper + instantiateStreaming log will show whether fetch/instantiateStreaming ran.\n\nThat's a complete decision tree.\n\nPlus: curl the three module URLs with timing right now (server-side latency check + cache warm).\n\nLet me also read navigation.ts lines 130-180 to see the exact code I'm instrumenting.\n\nOne more consideration: after the dev server restart, the ?v= hash might change — irrelevant to curl timing (I'll curl the current hash), and my instrumentation doesn't depend on the hash.\n\nAnd — importantly — the fetch wrapper should also catch the ArrayBuffer fallback's second fetch (instantiateArrayBuffer fetches again). It will log that too — good.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider whether the `?v=ec16f4b0` modules might be served slowly due to Vite's \"module invalidation\" behavior… curl will tell me.\n\nPlan for this turn:\n1. Read navigation.ts:130-180.\n2. Curl the three recast module URLs with timing (also warms the cache).\n3. Edit navigation.ts: add temporary console.warn markers in _ensureRecast (record for removal).\n4. Edit scene252.ts: add fetch wrapper + instantiateStreaming call log (temp; record for removal).\n5. Restart the dev server (stop bg_25106995, start a new one), verify the served scene252.ts transform matches disk (grep for the fetch wrapper), and re-curl the three modules (new hash) to warm them.\n6. Ask the user to reload and report the console.\n\nWait — actually, about restarting: the three module URLs I curled (with ?v=ec16f4b0) belong to the current server instance (bg_25106995). If I restart, the hash might change and the warm cache is lost. So: first do the edits, then restart, then curl the new URLs to warm them, then ask the user to reload. The user's browser will request the modules fresh; the warmed cache makes them fast.\n\nHmm, but actually — is the cache warming even necessary? If the modules are served in <1s (curl), the browser's window is fine and the hang is somewhere else. Curl timing will tell me. If curl is fast, no restart is needed… but I do need a restart to serve the edited scene252.ts + navigation.ts (stale transform gotcha). So restart anyway, then re-curl (fast, warms the new cache).\n\nOK, let me execute. First: read navigation.ts 130-180 and curl the three URLs.\n","seq":180379,"t":10921796,"text":"\n\nI've confirmed the parallel load and patch are working, but the recast load is stuck before it even reaches the glue's catch block. I'll instrument `_ensureRecast` in navigation.ts to track which of the three dynamic imports or the factory call is hanging, and log the fetch and instantiateStreaming calls to see what's happening.\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":67584,"draft_n":14931,"draft_n_accepted":4011,"predicted_ms":175670.0,"predicted_n":6144,"prompt_ms":22120.0,"prompt_n":8766},"to_request":176156,"usage":{"cached_tokens":67584,"completion_tokens":6144,"prompt_tokens":76350}}
{"arguments":"{\"limit\": 55, \"offset\": 128, \"path\": \"~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite/src/navigation/navigation.ts\"}","id":"call_51b6ed108a1141e8b8830928","kind":"tool_call","name":"read","outcome":"ran","request":176156,"seq":180380,"shown":"Showing lines 128-182 of 699 total lines.\n\n---\n\n}\n\n// ─── Factory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nlet _coreModule: any = null;\nlet _generatorsModule: any = null;\nlet _initPromise: Promise<void> | null = null;\n\nasync function _ensureRecast(locateFile?: (url: string) => string): Promise<{ core: any; gens: any }> {\n    if (!_coreModule || !_generatorsModule) {\n        if (!_initPromise) {\n            _initPromise = (async () => {\n                const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n                const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n                if (locateFile) {\n                    const wasmFactory = (await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")).default;\n                    // core.init types impl as typeof Recast but calls it as impl() at runtime;\n                    // bind pre-fills locateFile and cast to satisfy the declaration.\n                    await core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }) as typeof wasmFactory);\n                } else {\n                    await core.init();\n                }\n                _coreModule = core;\n                _generatorsModule = gens;\n            })();\n        }\n        await _initPromise;\n    }\n    return { core: _coreModule, gens: _generatorsModule };\n}\n\n/**\n * Create a navigation plugin. Loads the Recast wasm internally on first call;\n * subsequent calls reuse the loaded module.\n *\n * Pass `locateFile` to serve the wasm from a public path instead of bundling\n * it inline — same pattern as `HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" })`.\n *\n * @example\n * ```ts\n *   const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n * ```\n */\nexport async function createNavigationPluginAsync(options?: { locateFile?: (url: string) => string }): Promise<NavigationPlugin> {\n    const { core, gens } = await _ensureRecast(options?.locateFile);\n    return {\n        _recast: core,\n        _generators: gens,\n    };\n}\n\n// ─── NavMesh ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * Build a navmesh from one or more meshes. Each mesh's CPU positions are\n","t":10921822,"turn":15}
