{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"All three recast modules are being served fast (max 54ms, the 2MB glue). So server-side delivery is not the issue. The hang is client-side inside the chain:\n\n`createNavigationPluginAsync` → `_ensureRecast` → imports (core, gens, wasm glue) → `core.init(wasmFactory)` → factory → `createWasm` → `instantiateAsync` → fetch → instantiateStreaming (patched) → catch → err → ArrayBuffer.\n\nSince there are no lines from recast at all, we haven't reached the catch. Add temporary markers to `_ensureRecast` to identify the stage:\n\n```ts\n_initPromise = (async () => {\n    console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-imports-start\");\n    const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n    console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-import-core\");\n    const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n    console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-import-gens\");\n    if (locateFile) {\n        const wasmFactory = (await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")).default;\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-import-wasm\");\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-init-start\");\n        await core.init(wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile }) as typeof wasmFactory);\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: recast-init-done\");\n    } else {\n        await core.init();\n    }\n    _coreModule = core;\n    _generatorsModule = gens;\n})();\n```\n\nWait — these markers in navigation.ts will fire for every scene that loads recast (scene170/171). That's fine — temporary instrumentation, and scene170 should print the full sequence (which would be a useful control!). Actually, this is a good control: if the user loads scene170, they should see the complete marker sequence through recast-init-done. That confirms the instrumentation works and isolates the failure to scene252's page context.\n\nAlso, in scene252.ts, add:\n1. A fetch wrapper that logs .wasm fetches (start + resolve/reject).\n2. An instantiateStreaming patch that logs the call.\n\nLet me write the scene252.ts edit. Current patch block:\n\n```ts\n    // This browser stalls on WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming for the second wasm\n    // module (recast after Havok) — the promise never settles, so the emscripten\n    // glue's ArrayBuffer fallback (which only runs on a rejection) is unreachable.\n    // Force streaming to reject so both wasm loads take the ArrayBuffer path.\n    WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (async () => {\n        throw new Error(\"scene252: forcing ArrayBuffer wasm path\");\n    }) as typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\n    mark(\"force-arraybuffer\");\n```\n\nReplace with:\n\n```ts\n    // This browser stalls on WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming for the second wasm\n    // module (recast after Havok) — the promise never settles, so the emscripten\n    // glue's ArrayBuffer fallback (which only runs on a rejection) is unreachable.\n    // Force streaming to reject so both wasm loads take the ArrayBuffer path.\n    const __fetch = window.fetch;\n    window.fetch = (input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => {\n        const url = typeof input === \"string\" ? input : input instanceof URL ? input.href : input.url;\n        if (url.includes(\".wasm\")) {\n            console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url}`);\n            const p = __fetch(input, init);\n            p.then(\n                (r) => console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url} -> ${r.status}`),\n                (e) => console.warn(`scene252 init: fetch ${url} -> rejected: ${e}`),\n            );\n            return p;\n        }\n        return __fetch(input, init);\n    };\n    WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming = (() => {\n        console.warn(\"scene252 init: instantiateStreaming called -> rejecting (forcing ArrayBuffer)\");\n        return Promise.reject(new Error(\"scene252: forcing ArrayBuffer wasm path\"));\n    }) as typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming;\n    mark(\"force-arraybuffer\");\n```\n\nTypeScript: `window.fetch`'s type is `(input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>`. My wrapper matches. `__fetch(input, init)` — init could be undefined, but that's fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: the emscripten glue calls `fetch(binaryFile, {credentials:\"same-origin\"})` — bare `fetch`, which in the browser resolves to `window.fetch` at call time (global lookup). Patching `window.fetch` will intercept it. Good.\n\nAlso: `p.then(...)` — this attaches a handler, but doesn't swallow the rejection. The original promise returned to the caller still rejects as before. Good.\n\nNow — the instantiateStreaming patch: `(async () => { throw ... })` vs `(() => Promise.reject(...))` — behavior is equivalent (rejected promise). Add a console.warn at the top. Note: the glue checks `typeof WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming==\"function\"` — mine is a function. Good.\n\nNext, navigation.ts edit. The markers use the \"scene252 init:\" prefix — since this is engine code, the prefix is a bit off, but it keeps the console grep simple. It's temporary and will be removed. Use the same prefix so the user can just report \"the last scene252 init: line.\"\n\nAlso — I should add a marker right before `createNavigationPluginAsync` in scene252 to show the ordering relative to the recast markers:\n\nActually, the sequence should be:\n- force-arraybuffer +0ms\n- engine +10ms\n- (navPromise kickoff — no marker... should I add mark(\"nav-kickoff\")? The recast-imports-start marker will come right after, so it's not strictly needed. But a marker before the call would confirm the call was made. Since recast-imports-start proves it, skip.)\n\nOK. One more thought — should I also instrument the `core.init` path? `core.init` is inside @recast-navigation/core (node_modules) — I'm not editing node_modules. The recast-init-start/done markers bracket it. If it hangs inside, the fetch/instantiateStreaming logs will show how far it got.\n\nNow — the key question: where is it actually hanging? Let me think about the most likely candidates again:\n\nIf the markers show \"recast-import-wasm\" printed and \"recast-init-start\" printed but nothing after — the factory is hanging before/at instantiateStreaming. Then the fetch wrapper tells us whether fetch ran. If fetch ran and resolved (200) but \"instantiateStreaming called\" never prints... that's impossible — instantiateStreaming is called right after fetch in the same try block. If fetch resolved and instantiateStreaming was called, my log prints. So the decision tree works.\n\nIf the markers stop at \"recast-imports-start\" (no recast-import-core) — the core module import is hanging client-side even though curl gets it in 7ms. That would point to something about the browser's module fetch... hmm, what could cause a module import to hang but not reject? If the response never completes on the client side. curl gets it in 7ms. Unless the browser is waiting on something else first — like, module scripts execute in order and something earlier in the graph is blocking the main thread? No — imports are fetched in parallel and execution order doesn't block fetch.\n\nWait. Actually — hmm, let me reconsider. What if the issue is **main-thread starvation**: the browser is stuck executing a synchronous wasm compile (Havok's ArrayBuffer fallback) for a very long time? No — the Havok marker printed at +75ms, and terrain at +106ms. After that, the main thread is free (no markers print until navPromise resolves, but that's a pending promise, not a blocked thread). If the main thread were blocked, the screenshot's spinner wouldn't animate either... the screenshot shows the spinner mid-rotation (arc partially drawn) — it's animating. So the main thread is free. The recast promise is simply pending.\n\nSo: either the glue module import is pending, or the factory is pending before fetch/instantiateStreaming, or fetch is pending. The instrumentation will tell us.\n\nOne more scenario to prepare for: if the markers show everything up to \"recast-init-start\" and the fetch wrapper shows `fetch /recast-navigation.wasm -> 200` and \"instantiateStreaming called -> rejecting\" — but no \"falling back\" line and no nav-plugin... that would mean the catch's err isn't printing (err is silent in the recast glue) and `instantiateArrayBuffer` is hanging — i.e., **the synchronous ArrayBuffer instantiation is hanging the main thread**... but then the spinner would freeze. Actually, `WebAssembly.instantiate` (ArrayBuffer) compiles asynchronously but returns a promise — compilation happens off-thread, promise resolves when done. Not a main-thread block. If it never resolves... hmm, that would be a browser bug in wasm compilation. But Node compiled it in 64ms. Unlikely, but the markers will show it (we'd see \"instantiateStreaming called\" + fetch 200, and silence after — with the main thread free, since the spinner animates).\n\nWait, actually — one more important thing: in the current console, we can see the \"falling back to ArrayBuffer instantiation\" line from Havok — so Havok's ArrayBuffer path did work (instantiate resolved, Havok marker at +75ms). So `WebAssembly.instantiate` works in this browser. If recast's ArrayBuffer path also hangs, that would be bizarre (same API, valid wasm).\n\nMost likely, given all the evidence, is: **the recast glue module import or the factory call is deadlocked** — and the markers will pinpoint it.\n\nHmm, one more possibility to consider: **the `?v=ec16f4b0` hash**. When the browser loaded scene252.ts's transform (from the current server), it got the navigation.ts import URLs with ?v=ec16f4b0. Fine. But wait — what if the browser had cached an old navigation.ts transform (from a previous server instance with a different ?v= hash) and the glue module URL 404s on the new server?? A 404 would reject the import → unhandled rejection → overlay ERROR. The user didn't see an error. And they're hard-reloading... actually, did they hard-reload, or a normal reload? The console shows the new markers (force-arraybuffer) — so they're running the new scene252.ts. Module cache: a normal reload re-fetches. OK, no issue.\n\nSo let me write the edits.\n\nEdit 1 — scene252.ts patch block (replace with fetch wrapper + logging patch).\n\nEdit 2 — navigation.ts _ensureRecast (add markers).\n\nThen:\n- Stop bg_25106995, restart the dev server.\n- Verify the served scene252.ts transform matches disk (grep for \"fetch /recast\" or \"instantiateStreaming called\").\n- Verify the served navigation.ts transform has the markers.\n- Re-curl the three recast modules (new hash) to warm the cache.\n- Ask the user to reload and report the last `scene252 init:` line.\n\nAlso, I should record the temporary instrumentation in navigation.ts for removal (update_record) — GUIDANCE §0b.\n\nNow let me make the edits.\n","seq":182032,"t":11006271,"text":"\n\nAll three recast modules load quickly on the server side, so the issue is client-side—likely in the dynamic imports or factory setup. I'll add stage markers to `_ensureRecast` and log the fetch/instantiateStreaming calls to pinpoint where it's stalling.\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":81920,"draft_n":7308,"draft_n_accepted":2618,"predicted_ms":81100.0,"predicted_n":3662,"prompt_ms":2390.0,"prompt_n":1368},"to_request":180386,"usage":{"cached_tokens":81920,"completion_tokens":3662,"prompt_tokens":83288}}
