n.ts transform had it), and on the second curl I omitted it and it still worked. Not really unexpected — Vite generally accepts URLs without ?v= (the hash is a cache-buster). Not a gotcha worth reporting.\n\n- Another: the served navigation.ts transform resolved the bare imports to /@fs/... .pnpm paths — as expected.\n\n- The fetch wrapper approach: I assumed patching `window.fetch` would intercept the emscripten glue's bare `fetch` calls. That's an assumption, not yet verified (waiting on the user's reload). Not a discovery from this step.\n\n- One thing that actually might be a gotcha: the `?v=ec16f4b0` hash on the served navigation.ts transform's imports — when I restarted the dev server, the hash could change, and the browser's module cache… but I haven't observed anything about that yet.\n\n- Also: all six markers in navigation.ts appeared in the served transform — as expected.\n\nHonestly, this step was mechanical: read, curl, edit, restart, verify. Everything behaved as expected. The fast curl results ruled out one hypothesis, but that's a confirmation, not a \"behaved differently than expected\" discovery.\n\nWait — one candidate: on the new server, the first curl of the recast glue took 193ms (versus 54ms on the old server). That's warm-cache variance, not a discovery.\n\nAnother candidate: I curled the module URLs without ?v= and got 200 — meaning the served navigation.ts transform's ?v= hash is not required for the browser to fetch them. Hmm, but the browser uses the exact URL with ?v= from the transform. So this is irrelevant.\n\nWhat about the fact that the `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` subpath module is 2MB of JS, and the wasm is a separate 339KB file? I already knew that.\n\nI think the honest answer is NONE — this step was all as expected. The instructions say \"only discoveries from this step. If none, reply NONE.\"\n\nLet me double-check one more potential gotcha: `curl -s \"http://localhost:5182/lite/src/lite/scene252.ts\" | grep -c \"instantiate