tch that never completes, or top-level eval that never ends (though the top level is just function definitions + `export default`, so eval is trivially fast) — which narrows the hang to the network/transfer side of that specific 2MB response.\n\nHmm wait — actually, let me double-check that the top level is trivial. From the tail: `...if(runtimeInitialized){moduleRtn=Module}else{moduleRtn=new Promise(...)} return moduleRtn; });  })(); export default Recast;` — so the factory is defined, and the module just exports it. Top-level eval = defining functions. Fast. So if the import is pending, it's the transfer.\n\nActually, wait — one more thing to reconsider: the first 300 bytes show it starts with a comment. And `head -c 300` is fine. OK.\n\nGOTCHA 4 (maybe): The dev server log doesn't record requests (plain Vite), so I can't tell from the log whether the browser's glue request arrived. IMPLICATION: to check whether the request arrives, I need to add a logging middleware or use a proxy; or just rely on client-side markers. Hmm — is this \"different from what I expected\"? I did cat the log expecting evidence. The absence of request logging is a known Vite behavior (no request log by default). Borderline. I'll include it briefly.\n\nAlso: the disk size (559KB) vs. what I remembered from before — the working set had no size recorded. The curl size was 2MB. So the \"2MB glue\" belief was wrong; the code is 559KB.\n\nLet me also note: the served size was 2,074,603 (old server) vs. 2,074,587 (new server) — 16 bytes different. Consistent with a stable transform (probably a different ?v= hash length… whatever). Not a discovery.\n\nNow — is the source map hypothesis correct? Let me think about Vite's behavior: in dev, `vite:import-analysis` and the transform… Vite appends `//# sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;charset=utf-8;base64,...` to served modules when `server` transform is on… actually, I recall Vite dev adds inline source maps to transformed modules by default (the `map` in the transform result is inlined as a data URL by the server). The size of a source map for a 559KB file: the \"sourcesContent\" field contains the entire original source (559KB), plus \"mappings\" (VLQ, could be large for minified code — minified code has long lines, and the mappings table can be big), plus names, etc. Base64 adds 33%. So 559KB → source map ~750KB–1.5MB. Total served ~1.3MB–2.1MB. Observed 2.07MB. Plausible.\n\nBut I haven't verified — I should verify in the next step (curl the tail of the served content and look for `sourceMappingURL`). I'll phrase the GOTCHA as \"the served size is 3.7x the disk size — probably inline source map, unverified.\"\n\nThe question says \"only discoveries from this step\" — so I'll list:\n\n1. GOTCHA: the recast glue file on disk is 559,543 bytes, but Vite dev serves it as ~2.07MB — the dev transform inflates it ~3.7x (probably an inline source map data URL, unverified). IMPLICATION: the browser is downloading ~2MB for this module (not 559KB of code); before blaming \"the 2MB module,\" I should check the tail of the served content to confirm what the extra bytes are — and this inflates the payload through the constrained test browser.\n\n2. GOTCHA: my grep for the glue import URL used the literal `wasm/wasm` (the bare specifier), but the served transform rewrites it to a /@fs/ URL that doesn't contain that substring — the grep returned empty, so I haven't actually verified the glue URL on the new server. IMPLICATION: re-grep with the package name/filename (\"recast-navigation.wasm.js\") to extract the exact served URL.\n\n3. GOTCHA: the glue module has zero import statements (self-contained ESM, ends with `export default Recast`) — top-level eval is just function definitions. IMPLICATION: the hanging `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")` can't be a nested module load or a slow top-level eval; the stall must be in the transfer/eval of that specific response, which narrows the next test to fetching that one URL in isolation.\n\n4. GOTCHA: plain Vite dev server logs don't record incoming requests (the log only shows npmrc EPERM warnings + \"ready\"). IMPLICATION: I can't tell from the server log whether the browser's glue request arrived; I'll need a client-side marker (test page) or a logging middleware to check.\n\nShould I include the npmrc EPERM warning? It's a known sandbox issue, not new. No.\n\nThe user asked for a specific format, one per line, only discoveries from this step. I'll keep it concise. 4 items feels right. Let me write them.\n","seq":191547,"t":11550908,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: the recast glue file on disk is 559,543 bytes, but Vite dev serves it as ~2.07 MB — the dev transform inflates it ~3.7× (likely an inline source-map data URL, not yet verified) — IMPLICATION: my \"2 MB glue module\" assumption was wrong (it's ~546 KB of code); the browser is still downloading ~2 MB for it, and I should verify what the extra bytes are (tail of the served content) before blaming module size for the hang.\nGOTCHA: my grep for the glue import URL in the served navigation.ts transform used the literal `wasm/wasm` (the bare specifier), but the rewritten URL is a `/@fs/.../wasm/dist/recast-navigation.wasm.js?v=...` path that never contains that substring — the grep returned empty, so I have NOT actually verified the glue URL on the restarted server — IMPLICATION: re-grep with the package name or file name (\"recast-navigation.wasm.js\") to extract the exact served URL and confirm the transform is intact.\nGOTCHA: the glue module has zero import statements (self-contained ESM; top level is function definitions ending in `export default Recast`) — IMPLICATION: the never-settling `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")` cannot be a nested module load or a slow top-level evaluation; the stall must be in the transfer or parse of that one response, so the next test should fetch exactly that URL in isolation.\nGOTCHA: plain Vite dev server logs record no incoming requests 