{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"This makes it clear now! The delivered response is the glue file, with a **base64-encoded inline source map** appended at the end (the tail is base64: `LENBQUMsR0FBRy...In0=` — that's the base64 of the source map JSON `...;AAA;;...}`). The 2.07MB is 559KB of JS + about 1.5MB of base64 source map.\n\nSo Vite is serving the glue with an inline source map. That in itself is normal for dev mode (source maps help debugging). The browser downloads 2.07MB, executes the 559KB module — the source map is just appended after the code, in a `//# sourceMappingURL=data:...` comment... wait, is it in a comment? Let me check: the tail is raw base64 ending in `In0=`. Normally it's in the form `//# sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;base64,...`. Let me check the boundary between the JS and the base64.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, does this matter? The browser just needs to download the full response (2.07MB) and evaluate the module. The source map doesn't affect evaluation.\n\nBut — wait. What if the response is being served chunked, and something is getting stuck? curl got the full 2.07MB just fine.\n\nHmm, OK. So the server side is totally fine. Let me step back and think about the client side.\n\nNo, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing with this new fact: the response is 2.07MB. Earlier I found that \"the Vite dev server's on-demand transform is too slow to serve within the browser's ~8-second load window (scene2/scene40 also stall).\" What if the real mechanism isn't the transform but **the browser/harness has a cap on response size or total bytes, and 2.07MB responses get stalled**?\n\nEvidence:\n- core (506KB delivered — wait, what's the delivered size for core? curl said 506840B for core, 205989B for gens, 2074587B for glue. The file on disk for core — let me guess it's similar; if core's delivered size is also file + inline source map... core delivered 506KB — what's core's file size on disk? Unknown. But core's import settled in the browser.\n- glue (2.07MB delivered) — import never settles.\n- HavokPhysics.wasm (339KB? no wait — the earlier curl: 339385B for /recast-navigation.wasm... hmm, that was recast's wasm. HavokPhysics.wasm is about 2MB? The fetch log showed it fetched twice and got 200. Size unknown, about 2MB.)\n- HavokPhysics_es.js (static import, part of the initial graph) — settled (the scene got to the engine). What's its delivered size? Probably about 1MB + source map.\n\nSo the browser settled modules of unknown delivered size, but the 2.07MB glue never settled. If there's a ~1-2MB cap per response in the harness browser... HavokPhysics_es.js — if its delivered size is over 2MB, it should have stalled too, but it didn't (the engine ran). Let me check the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js. If it's under 2MB and works, and the glue at 2.07MB stalls, the cap hypothesis gets stronger.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Is the inline source map really a Vite default? Vite dev serves JS with source maps — by default external (a separate .map file referenced via a comment), not inline. An inline base64 source map in the response is unusual... unless Vite is inlining it for files served via /@fs/ (which can't have a separate .map URL? no, they can).\n\nWait, let me actually look at the boundary in the delivered file to confirm the format. Let me grep for \"sourceMappingURL\" in the delivered file.\n\nActually — hold on. I have a better idea. What if the base64 tail isn't a source map but **Vite is serving the file with an inline source map because of a config** (lab/vite.config.ts might set `build.assetsInlineLimit` or `css.inlineSources`... no). Let me check vite.config.ts for source map settings: `server` options... Vite's `server` has no inline source map option; `build` has `sourcemap: 'inline'`. In dev, Vite always generates source maps (external).\n\nHmm, let me just look at the boundary. If it's `//# sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;base64,...` — it's an inline source map. Why would Vite inline it?\n\nOh, wait. I think I get it! **Vite's `optimizeDeps` excluded @recast-navigation — and for excluded deps, Vite serves them via /@fs/ with... no.**\n\nHmm, let me think about `vite.config.ts` — maybe the lab config has a custom plugin that inlines source maps, or `server.cors`... I need to look at the config. Actually — interview-t12-3 mentioned a comment around lines 721-722 of the config: \"@recast-navigation/* ship a wasm binary the esbuild optimizer can't process; serve…\" — let me read that section of the config. There might be a custom plugin serving these files specially.\n\nActually, the most important question is: **is the inline source map the problem for the browser?** A 1.5MB base64 source map appended to a 559KB module... the browser downloads 2.07MB total. Evaluation uses only the JS part. The source map is parsed lazily (on debugger attach). So it shouldn't block module evaluation.\n\nUnless the harness browser's network layer stalls on responses over ~2MB. That's a harness limitation I can't directly observe... but I can test it: **shrink the delivered response**. How? If the inline source map is the bloat, disable source maps for these files, or pre-bundle them some other way.\n\nWait wait wait. Hold on. Let me reconsider. What if the bloat isn't a source map? Let me actually examine the content between the JS end and the base64. The JS ends with `export default Recast;` (confirmed at the tail of the file on disk). In the delivered file, the tail is base64. Let me find where `export default Recast;` appears in the delivered file and what follows it.\n\nLet me also check: is the base64 decodable as a source map? `In0=` ends with `}` — typical JSON ending. Let me decode the tail.\n\nAnd crucially — compare: what's the delivered size of core (506840B)? What's core's file size on disk? If core's file on disk is about 500KB and delivered is 506KB (no source map bloat), then only the glue got the inline source map... that would be strange. Or maybe core's file on disk is 150KB and delivered is 506KB (with source map). Let me check the sizes on disk for all three files.\n\nLet me gather:\n1. File sizes on disk: core/dist/index.mjs, generators/dist/index.mjs, wasm/dist/recast-navigation.wasm.js.\n2. Delivered sizes (from earlier curl): core 506840, gens 205989, glue 2074587.\n3. Look for \"sourceMappingURL\" in the delivered glue, and at the boundary.\n4. Check lab/vite.config.ts for source map / inline settings and the @recast-navigation exclude comment section.\n\nIf core on disk is about 500KB and delivered is 506KB (no bloat), but glue on disk is 559KB and delivered is 2.07MB (3.7x bloat) — the difference is the source map size: the glue's source map is huge (because it's minified emscripten output — the source map for minified code is large, about 1.5MB). core's source map might be small (unminified source → small map). That would be consistent: Vite inlines source maps for these /@fs/ files (or generates them and appends them), and the glue's map is the big one.\n\nBut why does Vite inline the source map in the first place? Let me check the config.\n\nActually — I recall now! Vite's behavior: for files served via `/@fs/` (outside the workspace root), Vite can't serve a separate `.map` file... no, it can (the .map URL would be /@fs/...map). Hmm.\n\nActually, the more likely explanation: **the lab's vite.config.ts has `server` or a plugin that does this**, or Vite's default `transformWithEsbuild`... no.\n\nLet me just look at the config and the boundary. Also — let me decode the base64 tail to confirm it's a source map.\n\nNo, actually, wait — I should focus. The actionable question is: **why does the browser stall on this 2.07MB response when it settles the 506KB one?** Two sub-hypotheses:\n(H1) The harness browser has a per-response or total byte cap, and the glue's response (2.07MB) exceeds it → the download stalls mid-body → the import never settles (and never rejects — the harness just... stops? that's weird; a stalled TCP connection should eventually time out and reject).\n(H2) Something in the content of the delivered glue response breaks the browser's module parser/loader — e.g., the inline source map isn't properly in a comment, and the browser chokes on the base64 tail. But curl got a complete response; if the content were malformed, the browser would fail evaluation → the import would reject (SyntaxError) → unhandled rejection → error box. The user saw no error. Hmm. But wait — what if the harness browser swallows module evaluation errors?? The earlier \"empty console\" behavior on the build suggests the harness browser might swallow errors! If the harness swallows module load errors, a failed glue import would... no — a rejected import still rejects the Promise; the rejection goes to `await _initPromise` → `createNavigationPluginAsync` rejects → `navPromise` rejects → `await navPromise` in main() throws → main().catch → overlay ERROR. The user saw no ERROR in the overlay. So the import really is pending, not rejected.\n\nSo H1 (the download stalls and never completes, and never times out) is the leading hypothesis. What causes a download to stall forever with no timeout? If the harness's network proxy silently drops the connection... or — **HTTP/1.1 keep-alive + Vite's chunked response + the harness reusing a dead connection**?\n\nHmm, OK — actually, let me test H1 directly with a size experiment: make the delivered glue response smaller. If the bloat is an inline source map, I can disable source maps (or the plugin inlining them) for these files → the response becomes 559KB → if the browser then settles the import, the size cap is confirmed.\n\nHow to shrink it:\n- Option A: check the config for what's inlining the source map and disable it.\n- Option B: add a temporary Vite plugin/middleware that strips the sourceMappingURL tail for these specific files.\n- Option C: copy the glue file to lab/public/ (e.g., /recast-glue.js) and temporarily change navigation.ts's import to a public URL... that's a bigger change.\n\nLet me first look at the config and the boundary — the answer might be right there.\n\nOne more thing to check: does the delivered response end properly? curl got 2074587 bytes — but is that the complete response, or did curl just grab what arrived? curl completed (it printed the status), so the response completed for curl.\n\nLet me also check the dev server log again after the user's load — did the browser's request for the glue appear? Vite doesn't log requests by default... actually, Vite doesn't log individual requests. Hmm.\n\nOK, let me run the inspection commands.\n","seq":192253,"t":11588889,"text":"\n\nI found that the served glue file is 2.07 MB with a base64 source map appended, while the other modules settled at 506 KB and 206 KB. Now I'll check the disk sizes, map boundary, and config to understand why only the glue module has this bloated map.\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":101120,"draft_n":7917,"draft_n_accepted":1997,"predicted_ms":107520.0,"predicted_n":3128,"prompt_ms":4550.0,"prompt_n":2601},"to_request":190054,"usage":{"cached_tokens":101120,"completion_tokens":3128,"prompt_tokens":103721}}
