{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I have the full picture:\n\nSizes on disk:\n- core: 101,997 B → delivered 506,840 B (about 5x — source map inlined)\n- generators: 42,989 B → delivered 205,989 B (about 4.8x — source map inlined)\n- glue: 559,543 B → delivered 2,074,587 B (about 3.7x — source map inlined)\n\nSo all three get an inline base64 source map appended. Vite dev is inlining source maps for these files (that's just Vite's behavior for some files — actually, does Vite dev inline source maps when serving files that don't have an adjacent .map file? Hmm, actually I recall Vite dev generates source maps and serves them inline as a data URL when the file is in node_modules and... whatever the reason, empirically: an inline map is being appended).\n\nSo the delivered sizes are: core 507KB, gens 206KB, glue 2.07MB. All three are module imports, and in the browser core and gens settled while glue didn't. The distinguishing factor is still SIZE (2.07MB vs 507KB).\n\nWait — hmm, but is it really size? Let me check what else the browser is loading on the scene252 page:\n- The whole babylon-lite barrel graph (dozens of modules, source maps inlined? — for files in packages/babylon-lite/src (aliased as first-party), Vite serves them with source maps... external or inline? If first-party TS files, Vite dev serves them with inline source maps by default (the transform includes `//# sourceMappingURL=data:...` inline — yes! In dev, Vite's esbuild transform for TS adds an inline source map to the transformed JS — that's standard Vite dev behavior).\n- HavokPhysics_es.js (@babylonjs/havok — excluded from optimizeDeps → served raw via /@fs/ with an inline map) — what's the size on disk? Probably about 1MB → delivered about 2-3MB? And that settled (static import, engine ran).\n\nHmm! If HavokPhysics_es.js is a delivered 2-3MB response and it settled, then the \"response size cap\" hypothesis weakens. Let me check the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js.\n\nWhere is it? node_modules/.pnpm/@babylonjs+havok@1.3.12/node_modules/@babylonjs/havok/lib/esm/HavokPhysics_es.js (from interview-t10-f124766-0). Let me curl the delivered version and get the size.\n\nActually — wait. Let me think about this from a different angle. What if it's not size but something specific to the glue's delivered content? The glue's inline source map is 1.5MB of base64. The browser has to parse the whole response as a module script. Module parsing is fast even for 2MB. But — the source map is a data: URL in a comment, and the browser's module loader... no, comments get ignored.\n\nHmm, what if the problem is **response delivery under concurrent load**: when the browser requests the glue, the dev server is simultaneously... no, curl got it in 190ms.\n\nOK, let me try a completely different approach — **stop trying to explain the browser and make the response smaller**. If I can strip the inline source map from the delivered recast modules (or serve them without a map), the responses become 102KB/43KB/560KB. If the browser then settles the glue import → size/bloat is the cause. If it still hangs → content-independent; the problem is elsewhere (e.g., the import itself under page load).\n\nHow to strip the inline map: a temporary Vite plugin with a middleware... but the module transform happens inside Vite's transform pipeline, not in middleware. A plugin's `transform` hook runs after load; I can strip `//# sourceMappingURL=data:...` from the output for these files. That's a config change — temporary, revert before commit. Alternatively — simpler: **copy the three recast dist files into lab/public/ and change navigation.ts's imports to those public URLs** (temporary edit to navigation.ts). The public files are served raw (no transform, no source map). This also eliminates the /@fs/ transform entirely.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, there's an even simpler zero-config test: **`?v=` and cache-busting aside, can I check whether the glue import settles when requested as the first thing on a nearly-empty page?** An isolation test page (recast-test.html) does exactly that. If the glue import settles on the isolation page → the problem is the page's load context (concurrency/budget). If it hangs on the isolation page → the problem is the response itself (size/bloat) → then strip the map and retest.\n\nActually, I realize I should also check the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js — it's a critical data point for the size-cap hypothesis. If it's delivered at over 2MB and settled, size-cap is dead and the isolation page becomes the key experiment.\n\nTo do:\n1. curl the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js (need the URL — find it from the delivered transform of scene252.ts or from node_modules path + /@fs/).\n2. Create recast-test.html (isolation page, minimal: only the three recast imports + factory call).\n3. Ask the user to load it.\n\nAbout the URL for HavokPhysics_es.js: `import HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\"` — from scene252.ts (lab code). Vite resolves bare specifiers from lab's node_modules: lab/node_modules/@babylonjs/havok → symlink to .pnpm/@babylonjs+havok@1.3.12/... Let me find the actual file and curl it via /@fs/.\n\nPath: node_modules/.pnpm/@babylonjs+havok@1.3.12/node_modules/@babylonjs/havok/lib/esm/HavokPhysics_es.js (per the earlier note). Let me verify and curl it.\n\nNow, designing the isolation page. Requirements:\n- Temporary file under lab/lite/ (served at /lite/recast-test.html).\n- No scene, no barrel, no Havok — only the three recast imports + factory init with locateFile → reports stage + elapsed time on-page.\n- On-page text (the overlay approach works — the user reads the screenshot; console is also visible on the dev server).\n- Window error/unhandledrejection handlers to catch errors on-page.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n    <title>recast-test</title>\n    <style>\n      body { background: #111; color: #0f0; font: 14px monospace; margin: 0; }\n      #t { white-space: pre-wrap; padding: 8px; }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"t\">recast-test.html loaded — waiting for module…</div>\n    <script>\n      const t = document.getElementById(\"t\");\n      const log = (s) => { t.textContent += \"\\n\" + s; console.warn(s); };\n      window.addEventListener(\"error\", (e) => log(\"ERROR: \" + (e.message || e)));\n      window.addEventListener(\"unhandledrejection\", (e) => log(\"REJECTION: \" + String(e.reason)));\n    </script>\n    <script type=\"module\">\n      const t = document.getElementById(\"t\");\n      const log = (s) => { t.textContent += \"\\n\" + s; console.warn(s); };\n      const t0 = performance.now();\n      log(\"module start\");\n      const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n      log(\"core +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n      const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n      log(\"gens +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n      const wasmMod = await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\");\n      log(\"glue +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n      const Recast = wasmMod.default;\n      await Recast({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n      log(\"recast init done +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nWait — `Recast({locateFile})` — the factory signature: `wasmFactory.bind(null, { locateFile })` — so the factory takes an options object. The default export is the factory (`export default Recast`, where Recast is the factory function). `core.init(impl)` calls `impl()`. So I can just call `Recast({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" })` directly — that's exactly what the bound call does.\n\nNote: this page imports the same bare specifiers from lab code — Vite resolves them the same way as from navigation.ts (lab has @recast-navigation as a dependency).\n\nOne concern: does `import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\")` resolve in lab's Vite? The `wasm/wasm` subpath — package.json exports map. navigation.ts (in packages/babylon-lite) uses it, and it resolved (the delivered transform has the /@fs/ URL). From lab/lite/*.html module scripts — same Vite resolution. Should work.\n\nShould I also make a second page (two-wasm-test.html) that loads Havok first? Let me think about what's most informative per round trip.\n\nCurrent state of knowledge: the glue import hangs on the scene252 page. Two candidate causes:\n(A) The glue response itself (2.07MB with inline map) stalls in this browser.\n(B) The glue import stalls only under scene252's page load context (concurrency/budget/race).\n\nThe isolation page distinguishes A from B:\n- Hangs on isolation page → A (response-level problem) → next: strip the source map / shrink the response and retest.\n- Completes on isolation page → B (context-level problem) → next: bisect the context (two-wasm page: Havok + recast without the scene).\n\nI could build both pages now and have the user load both in one round trip: first recast-test.html, then two-wasm-test.html. That gives both data points in one exchange. Let's do that.\n\ntwo-wasm-test.html:\n```html\n<script type=\"module\">\n  import HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\";\n  const t = document.getElementById(\"t\");\n  const log = (s) => { t.textContent += \"\\n\" + s; console.warn(s); };\n  const t0 = performance.now();\n  log(\"module start\");\n  const navPromise = (async () => {\n    const core = await import(\"@recast-navigation/core\");\n    log(\"core +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n    const gens = await import(\"@recast-navigation/generators\");\n    log(\"gens +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n    const wasmMod = await import(\"@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm\");\n    log(\"glue +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n    await wasmMod.default({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\n    log(\"recast init done +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n  })();\n  const hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\n  log(\"havok +\" + Math.round(performance.now() - t0) + \"ms\");\n  await navPromise;\n  log(\"all done\");\n</script>\n```\n\nThis reproduces scene252's load profile (barrel not needed — scene252 imports the barrel, but this page skips it; if B is \"two wasm on one page,\" this reproduces it; if B is \"huge barrel graph + two wasm,\" it might not reproduce — acceptable, the next bisection step will handle that).\n\nHmm — wait, should two-wasm-test also import the babylon-lite barrel to fully reproduce? The barrel graph is big (engine, text, physics, navigation...). Adding `import { createNavigationPluginAsync } from \"babylon-lite\"` instead of manual imports would reproduce the barrel + navigation path exactly. But then I lose the per-import markers (they're in navigation.ts — oh wait, navigation.ts has my temporary markers! recast-imports-start/core/gens/wasm/init-start/init-done — they'll print on any page that goes through createNavigationPluginAsync!).\n\nSo two-wasm-test.html can just use the real API:\n```js\nimport HavokPhysics from \"@babylonjs/havok\";\nimport { createNavigationPluginAsync } from \"babylon-lite\";\nconst navPromise = createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => \"/recast-navigation.wasm\" });\nconst hknp = await HavokPhysics({ locateFile: () => \"/HavokPhysics.wasm\" });\nlog(\"havok loaded\");\nconst nav = await navPromise;\nlog(\"nav loaded\");\n```\nThe temporary markers in navigation.ts will show the stages. And the fetch wrapper... no, the fetch wrapper is in scene252.ts, not on this page. That's fine — the nav markers + on-page log are enough.\n\nSo:\n- recast-test.html: bare recast imports only (no barrel, no Havok) — isolates the glue response.\n- two-wasm-test.html: barrel + Havok + createNavigationPluginAsync — reproduces scene252's load minus terrain/scene.\n\nBoth temporary, deleted before commit. Record it.\n\nAlso — let me quickly check the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js for the size-cap data point.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether the inline source map is really Vite's doing... it doesn't matter for the experiment — the experiment (isolation page) will tell me if the response is the problem, and then I can strip the map as the fix.\n\nOne more thing to verify about the isolation page: will Vite serve lab/lite/recast-test.html? The dev server root is lab/ (vite.config.ts is in lab/). lab/lite/scene252.html is served at /lite/scene252.html. So lab/lite/recast-test.html → /lite/recast-test.html. Yes.\n\nAnd the pagesDemoPlugin — it intercepts some HTML URLs (for compat scenes with ?compat, etc.) — my page has no ?compat and isn't a compat scene; it should be served as a static file. No problem.\n\nLet me create both pages and check the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js. Then restart the dev server? No — new HTML files don't need a restart (HTML is served from disk on each request; the stale-transform problem was about module transforms, and HTML is static). Actually — is HTML served fresh? Vite serves HTML files via transformIndexHtml on each request — fresh from disk. Yes, no restart needed. But the module transforms for the new pages: the bare imports resolve to the same /@fs/ URLs (already warm). OK, no restart needed.\n\nWait — one thing: the `?v=ec16f4b0` hash in the navigation.ts transform — stable as long as the server is running. The new pages import the same modules → same URLs → warm. Good.\n\nTo do:\n1. Write lab/lite/recast-test.html\n2. Write lab/lite/two-wasm-test.html\n3. curl the delivered size of HavokPhysics_es.js (bash)\n4. update_record (temporary files to delete)\n5. Ask the user to load both pages and send both screenshots.\n\nAbout the URL for HavokPhysics_es.js: let me find it first. `ls node_modules/.pnpm | grep havok` then curl via /@fs/. Let me write the command.\n","seq":194822,"t":11726365,"text":"\n\nI've identified that all three recast modules use inline base64 source maps, with the glue being the largest at 2.07 MB, while Havok's glue loads fine as a static import. I'll create two test pages to isolate whether the issue is the glue response itself or the page context, and check Havok's served size.\n\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":106496,"draft_n":9604,"draft_n_accepted":3502,"predicted_ms":133490.0,"predicted_n":4874,"prompt_ms":2890.0,"prompt_n":1558},"to_request":192262,"usage":{"cached_tokens":106496,"completion_tokens":4874,"prompt_tokens":108054}}
