{"kind":"delta","request":194832,"seq":195815,"t":11783746,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"content\": \"TEMP diagnostic pages to delete before commit (GUIDANCE §0b): lab/lite/recast-test.html (bare 3-module recast import + factory init, on-page markers) and lab/lite/two-wasm-test.html (barrel + Havok + createNavigationPluginAsync, scene252 load profile minus scene). Dev server bg_20d6e132 serves both (200, no restart needed — HTML served fresh, module URLs warm). Data so far: served sizes with inline base64 source maps — core 507KB (settled), gens 206KB (settled), HavokPhysics_es.js 283KB (settled), recast glue 2.07MB (import never settles); plain fetch of 2MB HavokPhysics.wasm completed twice. So module-pipeline stall on the big module response, not a size cap on fetches.\", \"field\": \"followup\"}","id":"call_316186a5323e46e585690b2e","index":1,"name":"update_record"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"HavokPhysics_es.js: 67KB on disk, 283KB when served (with inline map). Small! So the size-cap hypothesis is now pretty clear:\n\n- core: 507KB when served → settled\n- gens: 206KB when served → settled\n- HavokPhysics_es.js: 283KB when served → settled\n- glue: 2.07MB when served → didn't settle\n\nEverything under ~500KB settles, and the 2.07MB one doesn't. The glue's inline source map (1.5MB of base64) is the bloat factor. If the harness browser (or its network path) is stalling on responses above ~1MB... but wait, what about the HavokPhysics.wasm fetch (2MB)? The fetch log showed `fetch /HavokPhysics.wasm -> 200` twice! So a 2MB response did complete via fetch()!\n\nHmm! So a 2MB fetch completed (HavokPhysics.wasm), but a 2.07MB module import didn't complete. So it's not a simple response-size cap!\n\nWhat's the difference between the two?\n1. `fetch()` vs module import — different request paths in the browser.\n2. HavokPhysics.wasm is a plain asset (Content-Type: application/wasm), the glue is a JS module (Content-Type: text/javascript, evaluated as a module).\n3. Timing: the wasm fetch happened early (before the recast import settled); the glue import was requested after the core/gens import.\n\nHmm, what else is different... module preloading? The browser preloads module dependencies. For the glue import, the browser requests the URL, receives the response, parses it as a module. For the wasm fetch, it just downloads bytes.\n\nSo the hypothesis narrows to: **the module pipeline (fetch → parse → evaluate) is stalling on the 2.07MB module in this browser**, while a plain fetch of a 2MB blob completes. Or the stall is coincidental (a race) and the size is just correlated.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. What if the response the browser receives for the glue module is different from what curl receives? For example, Vite's response to the browser includes... hmm, no, same URL, same server.\n\nWhat if the browser's request to the glue URL is never sent, because the module loader is waiting for something else first? The glue import is the third sequential await in _ensureRecast. core and gens settled, so the code reached the glue import. The import fires a fetch. The fetch... hangs.\n\nOr — what if the browser has already fetched the glue module earlier (during dep scanning or a previous page load) and is using a cached/stale entry that never completes? A hard reload should clear that...\n\nAlright — the isolation experiment will disambiguate. If recast-test.html (bare page, glue as the third import, same conditions minus the barrel/Havok) completes → the stall requires scene252's context → two-wasm-test will pin down which part (Havok? barrel?). If recast-test.html hangs on the glue → the module response itself is the problem in this browser → next step: shrink the response (strip the inline source map) and retest.\n\nBoth pages are created. No dev server restart needed (HTML is served fresh; module URLs are warm).\n\nWait — one issue: two-wasm-test.html imports `babylon-lite` (the barrel). The barrel's transform — is it warm? scene252.ts imports the same barrel, so yes, warm. And @babylonjs/havok — also warm (scene252 imports it).\n\nAnother consideration for recast-test.html: top-level await in module scripts — supported in all modern browsers. No problem.\n\nAlso — both pages use `console.warn` — allowed by GUIDANCE §6 (but these are temporary files, deleted before commit anyway).\n\nSo let me record the temp files and ask the user to load both pages in order:\n1. http://localhost:5182/lite/recast-test.html\n2. http://localhost:5182/lite/two-wasm-test.html\n\nAnd send both screenshots (the on-page text is the channel; the console is also visible on the dev server).\n\nInterpretation matrix:\n- recast-test completes → the glue loads fine on its own → the stall requires scene252's context → look at two-wasm-test:\n  - two-wasm completes → the context needs more (barrel graph size? terrain? something else) → next bisection.\n  - two-wasm hangs on the glue → the two-wasm-on-one-page condition is reproduced → next: force both onto the ArrayBuffer path here (a patch in the test page) or isolate further.\n- recast-test hangs on the glue → the 2.07MB module response is the problem in this browser → strip the inline source map (shrink to 560KB) and retest.\n\nLet me record the temp files (update_record) and ask the user.\n\nActually — one more cheap check I can do right now: verify that both test pages are actually being served (200). A quick curl.\n","seq":195816,"t":11783749,"text":"\n\nI've found that responses under ~500 KB load fine, but the 2.07 MB glue module fails while a plain fetch of the 2 MB HavokPhysics.wasm succeeds—so the issue is specific to the module pipeline, not response size. I'll test this with two scenarios: a minimal page loading only the three recast modules, and a page replicating scene252's load order with the barrel and Havok.\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":110592,"draft_n":3654,"draft_n_accepted":1059,"predicted_ms":51130.0,"predicted_n":1581,"prompt_ms":5170.0,"prompt_n":2473},"to_request":194832,"usage":{"cached_tokens":110592,"completion_tokens":1581,"prompt_tokens":113065}}
