{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"User's question: In the step I just completed (the step where I created two test pages and verified them with curl), was there anything that behaved differently from what I expected? List each item as GOTCHA: ... — IMPLICATION: ...\n\nLet me review what I actually discovered in this step:\n\n1. **The glue file on disk is 559 KB, but the response Vite serves is 2.07 MB** — Vite's dev server is appending an inline base64 source map (`sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;base64,...`) to the end of the served JS module. I expected the served size to be roughly the same as the size on disk. This is the big discovery. — IMPLICATION: When diagnosing browser stalls, the response size that the browser actually downloads ≠ the file size on disk. To shrink the response, the inline map needs to be stripped (or the file served raw). Also, when curl-checking the \"served size,\" always compare against the disk size.\n\n2. **All three recast modules have inline maps appended** (core 102KB→507KB, gens 43KB→206KB, glue 559KB→2.07MB) — meaning the bloat isn't specific to the glue, but the glue's map is the largest. — IMPLICATION: A size-based stall hypothesis needs to account for the post-map-applied size. Core at 507KB succeeded, and the glue at 2.07MB stalled.\n\n3. **HavokPhysics_es.js is only 67 KB on disk / 283 KB when served** — I had been assuming it was a ~2MB glue file (I was conflating it with the 2MB HavokPhysics.wasm binary). In reality the JS glue is small. — IMPLICATION: The \"two 2MB JS modules\" framing was wrong. The only large JS module response is the recast glue (2.07MB when served). The 2MB Havok load is the .wasm asset, and that fetch completed without issue.\n\n4. **The 2MB HavokPhysics.wasm plain fetch completed (200, twice), but the 2.07MB glue module import never settled** — meaning it's not a simple response-size cap. The stall is specific to the module pipeline (or a race). — IMPLICATION: A size-cap 