ctly with \"=== recast exclude section:\" — meaning `grep -n \"sourcemap\\|sourceMap\\|inline\" lab/vite.config.ts | head -20` output nothing (no matches). So the lab config isn't explicitly setting an inline source map — this is Vite's default dev behavior for these files. This is a sub-discovery: the inline map isn't coming from lab config, it's coming from Vite's default. IMPLICATION: The fix has to go through Vite's options (e.g., `server` config, or a plugin that strips the map), not through changing an existing lab setting.\n\nHmm, wait, let me double-check: the output of the second command:\n```\n=== recast exclude section:\n                    \"<script>(function(){...\n```\nYes — the grep produced no output, and then the sed output followed. So no sourcemap/inline setting in the config. Confirmed.\n\nSo the discoveries from this step:\n\n1. GOTCHA: Vite's dev server is appending an inline base64 source map to the end of the served JS module (the recast glue is 559KB on disk but 2.07MB when served. core 102KB→507KB, gens 43KB→206KB) — IMPLICATION: The response size the browser downloads ≠ the disk size. To shrink the glue response, the inline map has to be stripped (there's no source map setting in the lab config that controls this — it's Vite's default dev behavior, so the fix is either a temporary plugin/middleware or serving the file raw).\n\n2. GOTCHA: HavokPhysics_es.js (the JS glue) is only 67KB on disk / 283KB when served — the ~2MB Havok load is the .wasm asset, not the JS module — IMPLICATION: The only large JS module response on the page is the recast glue. The \"two large wasm JS modules\" framing was wrong.\n\n3. GOTCHA: A plain `fetch()` of the 2MB HavokPhysics.wasm completed (200 twice) in the browser, but the 2.07MB glue module import never settled — IMPLICATION: This is not a generic response-size cap. The stall is specific to the module-import path (or a page-context race), so a size experiment has to target the module response (e.g., stripping the map) rather than assuming fetches and imports share a limit.\n\n4. GOTCHA: The recast glue module has zero imports (self-contained IIFE, `export default Recast;`) — IMPLICATION: Hangs can't be attributed to nested module loads. The suspect list narrows to the response itself or the page context, which is exactly what the two test pages are separating.\n\nIs #4 really a \"discovery that it behaves differently from what I expected\"? I had expected there might be nested imports. Yes, it's a discovery that narrows the hypothesis space. Include it.\n\nAnything else? The first reload \"only reached force-arraybuffer\" — that was user-reported, not from my step. Skip.\n\nKeep it concise — list format only.\n","seq":197349,"t":11865978,"text":"\n\nGOTCHA: Vite's dev server appends an inline base64 source map to the served JS modules — the recast glue is 559 KB on disk but 2.07 MB served (core 102 KB→507 KB, generators 43 KB→206 KB), and the lab config has no sourcemap setting controlling this (it's Vite default dev behavior) — IMPLICATION: browser-downloaded response size ≠ disk file size; any size-based diagnosis or fix must account for the map, and shrinking the glue response means stripping the map (temporary plugin/middleware or serving the file raw), not a config toggle that already exists.\nGOTCHA: `HavokPhysics_es.js` (the Havok JS glue) is only 67 KB on disk / 283 KB served — the ~2 MB Havok load is the `.wasm` asset, not the JS module — IMPLICATION: my \"two big wasm JS modules on one page\" framing was wrong; the recast glue is the only large JS module response on the page, and it's the lone >1 MB module.\nGOTCHA: the browser completed plain `fetch()` of the 2 MB `HavokPhysics.wasm` (200, twice) yet the 2.07 MB glue *module import* never settles — IMPLICATION: this is not a generic response-size cap; the stall is specific to the module-import path or a page-context race, so the size experiment must target the module response itself (strip the inline map) 