ponse,\" we now know the bloat is the inline source map.\n\nHmm, but I need to be careful: the prompt asks for constraints the rest of the project must respect. Let me think about what's genuinely constraint-like that surfaced in this step:\n\n1. CONSTRAINT: The `optimizeDeps.exclude` entries in `lab/vite.config.ts` for `@babylonjs/core`, `@babylonjs/loaders`, `@babylonjs/havok`, `@recast-navigation/core`, `@recast-navigation/generators`, `@recast-navigation/wasm` must not be removed — esbuild's optimizer can't process the wasm binaries / ?raw WGSL imports these packages ship (a comment in the config documents \"without a hung pre-bundle\"). This is a compatibility rule that surfaced in this step (I read it directly this time).\n\n2. CONSTRAINT: The Vite dev server serves these excluded node_modules modules with an inline base64 source map appended, so the served response is 3.7–5x the file size on disk (glue: 559KB on disk → 2.07MB served). This is an environmental limit that surfaced in this step — any hypothesis about browser response-size limits must use served size, not disk size; and it means I can test the size-cap theory by stripping the inline map.\n\nHmm, is #2 a \"constraint the rest of the project must respect\"? It's more of a fact. But it does constrain the diagnostic/fix approach. The prompt says \"requirements, environmental limits, compatibility rules, things that must never be done.\" Environmental limits — yes, the inlined source map bloat is an environmental limit of the dev-serving path.\n\n3. Also: `resolve.alias` maps `babylon-lite` to `packages/babylon-lite/src` — the engine is served as TS source. That means my temporary edits to navigation.ts are served as source transforms (which is why the markers appeared). Constraint-like: edits to packages/babylon-lite/src are picked up by the dev server immediately (via transform, no build needed). That's a fact, not a constraint.\n\n4. `server.watch.ignored` — the lab config ignores build-artifact paths from Vite's watcher. Environmental fact.\n\nI think the honest answer is: the main constraint that surfaced in this step is the optimizeDeps.exclude rule (things that must never be done: don't un-exclude these packages). The second is the served-size bloat due to inline source maps (an environmental limit to respect in diagnosis).\n\nActually wait — let me re-read the prompt: \"In the step you just completed, did I learn or commit to any constraint that the rest of the project must respect?\" The step I just completed was the two bash calls that revealed: (a) the file sizes on disk vs. served, (b) the inline source map, (c) the contents of vite.config.ts (optimizeDeps.exclude, alias, port, watch.ignored, compat plugin).\n\nConstraints from that step:\n- The exclude list must stay (compatibility rule — removing it will break esbuild's pre-bundling with a hung pre-bundle).\n- The dev server inlines source maps into these module responses → served size is much larger than disk size (environmental limit — relevant to the size-cap hypothesis).\n\nAnything else? The alias (babylon-lite → src) means the lab consumes the engine from source — so lint/typecheck covers engine changes, and dev-server edits to engine files are served without a build. Not really a \"must respect\" constraint for the rest of the project… well, it implies that any temporary instrumentation I put in navigation.ts will be served and must be removed (already recorded).\n\nI'll list the two genuine ones. Concise, one per line, prefixed with CONSTRAINT:.\n","seq":197941,"t":11900577,"text":"\n\nCONSTRAINT: `lab/vite.config.ts` `optimizeDeps.exclude` must keep `@babylonjs/core`, `@babylonjs/loaders`, `@babylonjs/havok`, `@recast-navigation/core`, `@recast-navigation/generators`, `@recast-navigation/wasm` excluded — esbuild's dep optimizer can't process the wasm binaries / `?raw` WGSL imports these ship, and the config comment says un-excluding them causes a hung pre-bundle.\nCONSTRAINT: The Vite dev 