{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Major new information:\n\n1. **recast-test.html hangs at `glue`** — even on a bare page with no Havok and no barrel. The glue module import (a 2.07MB response including an inline source map) never completes in this browser.\n2. **two-wasm-test.html** also hangs at the glue (after core/gens, same as scene252).\n3. **scene170.html (the standard recast demo) also hangs on the spinner!**\n\nPoint 3 is a big deal. Previously I was operating under the assumption that \"scene170 works in the user's browser\" — that was the premise of the entire \"two-wasm interaction\" hypothesis. But now the user is saying scene170 also hangs. So recast glue import is broken in this browser across all pages. The distinguishing factor was never Havok — it's simply that **this browser cannot load the 2.07MB recast glue module response within the ~8-second window**.\n\nWait — but did scene170 work before? Let me check the working set: interview-t10-f120210-0 says \"scene170/171 load only recast (there's no Havok anywhere in the file) — the only difference between the working nav scene and scene252 is that scene252 loads HavokPhysics first.\" Hmm — \"working nav scene\" — where did that come from? Maybe the user said the other gallery scenes rendered fine — the user's original report: \"other gallery scenes render fine in the same browser.\" But scene170 specifically may never have been tested! The user just tested it now, and it hangs. So the premise was wrong — scene170 was never confirmed working. The \"other gallery scenes\" that render fine are probably non-nav scenes (scene2, scene18, etc.).\n\nSo the true situation is: **in this browser, the recast glue module import never completes, on any page.** Everything else (Havok, the barrel, the engine) loads in under 100ms.\n\nNow, why does a 2.07MB module response stall while 507KB (core) and 283KB (Havok glue) complete? And a plain fetch of a 2MB wasm completes?\n\nThe user says: \"In my browser, the recast glue import never completes within the ~8 seconds I get per page\" — so the browser/harness has an ~8-second per-page window. Within that window, the glue import doesn't complete. And \"I can't make the browser wait any longer.\"\n\nSo the constraint is: **the game must start up without that import stalling it.** The user is asking for a solution, not more diagnostics.\n\nWhat can I do?\n\nThe glue module is 559KB of JS + a 1.5MB inline base64 source map = 2.07MB when served. The JS itself is fine (it loads in Node; core/gens are fine in the browser). The stall is probably caused by the 2.07MB response (mostly source map) not completing within the window — or the browser's module pipeline choking on it.\n\nWait — but why would the response not complete? The server serves it in 190ms (curl). The browser's window is ~8 seconds. 190ms << 8 seconds. So if the server is fast, the response should complete well within the window… unless the browser's request isn't being processed. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, let me reconsider. What if the problem is that the browser's module loader is requesting the glue module, but the response is being delivered slowly to the browser because… the dev server is single-threaded and is busy transforming other modules at the same time? In recast-test.html, the graph is tiny (three recast modules). The glue request comes right after core/gens. The server has time. curl got it in 190ms.\n\nSo why does the browser stall?! Let me think…\n\nOh, wait. I think I finally get it. **`?v=ec16f4b0` — the optimizer version hash.** Let me think about what happens when the browser requests `/@fs/.../recast-navigation.wasm.js?v=ec16f4b0`:\n\nVite dev server: for files in node_modules excluded from optimizeDeps, the import analysis rewrites bare specifiers to `/@fs/...?v=<hash>`. When the browser requests that URL, Vite transforms the file and serves it.\n\nBut — what if the transform for this specific file hangs on the server side for the browser's request? I curled it and got a response in 190ms… but that was after my first curl had warmed the cache. The first request (cold) might be slow. How slow? Transforming a 559KB file with esbuild + generating a source map… maybe 1–2 seconds? Not 8+ seconds.\n\nHmm, wait — but actually, what about the inline source map generation? The source map for the glue file is 1.5MB. Generating it requires esbuild to map the minified code back to the original source — that requires loading the original source file… the glue is already minified (dist), so esbuild's map generation is nearly identity — fast.\n\nOK, another thought — **what if the stall is on the browser side: the harness browser's network stack has a limit on response size for module requests, or stalls on chunked responses above a certain size?** The user says the window is ~8 seconds and the glue import \"never completes within ~8 seconds.\" The other modules (507KB) complete quickly. A 2.07MB response should complete in under a second on localhost… unless the harness is simulating a slow network. If the harness simulates, say, 256Kbps, then 2.07MB takes ~65 seconds — way past the 8-second window! And 507KB takes ~16 seconds — hmm, that would stall too. If 1Mbps: 2.07MB = 16.5 seconds (stall), 507KB = 4 seconds (complete), 283KB = 2.3 seconds (complete), 2MB wasm fetch = 16 seconds — but the wasm fetch completed (200 logged twice)! Hmm, that breaks the theory — unless the wasm fetch was served from cache (the browser had already fetched it? no, it's the first load…).\n\nHmm, wait — actually, the wasm fetch log: `fetch /HavokPhysics.wasm` → 200, then again (ArrayBuffer fallback) → 200. Both completed within the window (havok +82ms). So a 2MB asset fetch completed fast. But a 2.07MB module import didn't complete. Same server, same machine, similar size. The difference is: fetch() vs module import.\n\nWhat's different in the browser between fetch() and module import?\n- Module imports go through the module loader: fetch → parse as module → link → evaluate.\n- Module requests can be preloaded (the browser discovers nested module imports and prefetches them).\n- **Module requests may be subject to different concurrency limits.**\n\nOr — the difference might not be fetch vs import, but timing/order: the wasm fetch happened first (early in the window), the glue import happened later (after the core/gens imports + Havok load). If the harness browser has a total per-page byte budget (say ~4MB) and the glue request (2.07MB) would exceed it, the harness might stall/discard it. Total bytes before the glue request in scene252: barrel graph (~2MB?) + Havok glue (283KB) + core (507KB) + gens (206KB) + HavokPhysics.wasm (2MB) ≈ 5MB. Hmm, the budget would have been exceeded before the glue request.\n\nIn recast-test.html: bytes before the glue request: core (507KB) + gens (206KB) ≈ 700KB. The glue request (2.07MB) would bring the total to ~2.8MB. If the budget is ~2MB… core+gens (700KB) fit, and the glue doesn't. That fits! And in two-wasm-test: barrel (~2MB?) + Havok glue (283KB) + core (507KB) + gens (206KB) + HavokPhysics.wasm (2MB) ≈ 5MB — well over any budget, yet the page got to \"havok loaded +56ms\"… hmm, that contradicts a strict byte budget — unless the budget only applies to module responses and not fetch() responses (the wasm is a fetch, not a module). Let me recompute the module-only bytes for two-wasm-test: barrel graph modules — how big? The babylon-lite barrel pulls in engine, math, physics, navigation, text, etc. — could be 1–3MB when served (with inline maps). Plus HavokPhysics_es.js (283KB) + core (507KB) + gens (206KB) ≈ 2–4MB of modules. Then the glue (2.07MB) → total 4–6MB. If the module byte budget is ~2–3MB, the glue request would stall. In recast-test: 700KB + 2.07MB = 2.8MB — if the budget is ~2MB, this also stalls. Consistent!\n\nBut honestly — I can't verify the harness's internals. What I can do is: **make the glue response small enough to fit.** The bloat is the inline source map (1.5MB of base64). If I strip it, the glue response becomes 559KB — same order as core (507KB), which completes. Then the total module bytes for recast-test: 507 + 206 + 559 = 1.27MB. For scene252: barrel + 283 + 507 + 206 + 559 ≈ 2.5–4MB. Hmm — if the budget is ~2MB, scene252 might still stall. But wait — scene252 got through the barrel + Havok glue + core + gens without stalling… so whatever the budget is, it tolerated ~2–4MB of modules and only stalled on the +2.07MB glue. If I shrink the glue to 559KB, the total is within what was already tolerated.\n\nSo the plan: **strip the inline source map from the served recast glue module** (and ideally from all the big served modules — but the glue is the only one that stalls; core at 507KB is fine).\n\nHow to strip it? Options:\n\n**Option A: A temporary Vite plugin** with a `transform` hook that strips `//# sourceMappingURL=data:...` from the output of the recast modules (or all files above a certain size). Add it to lab/vite.config.ts — but vite.config.ts is shared infrastructure; a temporary edit is fine if I revert it before commit. But wait — the final fix has to stay (the game must start up for the user). Is a permanent config change acceptable? The user said \"whatever you do, the game must start up without that import stalling it.\" A permanent change to lab/vite.config.ts (stripping inline maps for large files, or specifically for @recast-navigation) is a legitimate infrastructure fix… but is it within the scope of my authority? The lab config is shared with all scenes. Stripping inline source maps for large files would slightly affect dev debugging (no source maps for those files in the debugger). Hmm. Actually — wait, why does Vite inline the source map for these files in the first place? Let me think…\n\nActually — I just realized I should check: is the inline source map a Vite default for /@fs/ files, or is it due to the lab config? Vite dev default: source maps are generated and served as separate .map files, referenced by a `//# sourceMappingURL=/@fs/...js.map?v=...` comment. Inline (data: URL) maps in dev are not the default… or are they? Hmm. Actually, I recall that Vite dev does use inline source maps for some cases: when serving files via /@fs/ (outside the project root), Vite can't reliably serve the .map file, so it inlines it? I'm not sure.\n\nLet me test: curl the .map URL: `/@fs/.../recast-navigation.wasm.js.map?v=ec16f4b0` — if it 404s, that explains the inlining. And check what a normal first-party module (say packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts) looks like when served — inline or external map? That would tell me whether the inlining is special to /@fs/ files.\n\n**Option B: Copy the three recast dist files into lab/public/** (say, as a `recast/` directory) and temporarily point navigation.ts's imports at the public URLs. Public files are served raw — no transform, no source map. This completely bypasses Vite's transform pipeline for these files. But it means modifying navigation.ts (engine code) — the imports would become absolute URLs like \"/recast-core.mjs\" — which breaks the package's portability (the imports must work in other contexts too — e.g., the bundle build, the published package). That's a real change to engine code — bad. Unless done conditionally… ugly.\n\n**Option C: A Vite plugin that serves these specific files raw** (middleware that intercepts the /@fs/ URL and sends the file from disk). A permanent, small, well-commented plugin addition to lab/vite.config.ts: \"serve @recast-navigation dist files raw — Vite's transform + inline source map bloats the 560KB glue to 2MB, which stalls the lab's constrained test browser.\" That's a legitimate infrastructure fix, scoped, and commented. And it's the kind of thing this config already does (it has several custom plugins).\n\nHmm, wait — but actually, let me reconsider. Is the source map really the cause? The evidence is correlational: 2.07MB stalls, ≤507KB completes. The map is 72% of the response. Stripping it is a cheap test with a high probability of success. If stripping the map doesn't fix it (the 559KB module still stalls — maybe the harness caps module responses at ~500KB?), the next step is to split/serve the glue differently… but 559KB vs core's 507KB — close enough that it probably fits.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider the mechanism once more, because there's a subtlety: **maybe it's not a byte budget — maybe the harness browser stalls on responses above ~1MB for module requests specifically.** The wasm fetch (2MB) completed — that's a fetch() request, not a module. The core module (507KB) completed. So: module responses above ~1MB stall; fetch responses above 1MB are fine. Stripping the map → 559KB module → completes. Same prediction. Good — either mechanism, the fix is the same: shrink the module response.\n\nNow — how to implement it cleanly?\n\nFirst, let me understand why the map is inlined. Let me check:\n1. Is there a .map URL that 404s?\n2. How does Vite serve the maps for first-party modules (packages/babylon-lite/src/...)?\n\nActually — you know what, I just remembered! Vite's dev server inlines source maps when the source map file can't be served separately — specifically for files served via `/@fs/`? No… let me just check empirically.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe the lab config sets `build.sourcemap: true` or similar… the grep for \"sourcemap|sourceMap|inline\" in vite.config.ts returned nothing (the first command's output showed the grep result — wait, it showed nothing before \"=== recast exclude section:\" — right, no sourcemap setting in the config). So the inlining is Vite's default behavior for these files.\n\nPlan:\n1. Verify: curl the .map URL (expect 404 or 200), curl a first-party module's transform (check the form of its sourceMappingURL).\n2. Implement the fix: a small Vite plugin (temporary at first, made permanent if it works) that strips the inline source map from served JS responses for the @recast-navigation modules (or, more generally, for files above a size threshold — but let's keep it scoped: just the recast files, or all /@fs/ node_modules JS… hmm).\n\nActually — the simplest, most robust approach: a `transform` hook plugin:\n\n```ts\n// Vite's dev transform appends an inline base64 source map to node_modules files\n// served via /@fs/ (no separate .map is served). For the 560KB recast glue that\n// bloats the response to 2MB, which the lab's constrained test browser cannot\n// finish within its load window. Strip the inline map for @recast-navigation files.\n{\n    name: \"strip-recast-inline-source-map\",\n    enforce: \"post\",\n    transform(code, id) {\n        if (id.includes(\"@recast-navigation\") && id.endsWith(\".js\") || id.includes(\"@recast-navigation\") && id.endsWith(\".mjs\")) {\n            return code.replace(/\\n?\\/\\/[#@]\\s*sourceMappingURL=data:[^\\n]*\\n?$/, \"\");\n        }\n    },\n}\n```\n\nHmm — wait, but the transform hook receives the post-load code (the raw file). The inline map is added by Vite's… where? If the map is added after the transform hook (in the serve step), my plugin can't strip it. I need to know where the inline map comes from.\n\nAlternative: a middleware plugin (configureServer) that intercepts the response — but the response is assembled by Vite's internal serve pipeline; middleware runs before Vite's handler… I could use `server.middlewares.use` with a wrapper that post-processes the response — hacky.\n\nBetter: check whether the inline map is part of the transform output. Let me look at the served content of a first-party module and see if it also has an inline map. Actually — I already have data: the served glue has an inline map. Let me check a first-party file (say, the served version of scene252.ts — I curled it earlier; did it have an inline map? I didn't check). Let me check now.\n\nActually — a cleaner idea: **Vite's `optimizeDeps` exclude + `optimizeDeps.esbuildOptions`**… no.\n\nWhat about **`server` config: `server.warmup`**? No.\n\nHmm, let me look at how Vite does it. In Vite 6, the dev transform pipeline: `pluginContainer.transform` → then the server adds the source map. For files with `?v=` (optimized deps) vs /@fs/… the inline map: I think Vite inlines the source map when the file is outside the root (served via /@fs/) — because the .map URL would need the same /@fs/ path, which actually works… I'm not sure. Empirical check will tell me.\n\nActually — you know what, let me step back and think about the simplest thing that definitely works:\n\n**Copy the three recast dist files into lab/public/recast/ and change navigation.ts's dynamic imports to use the public URLs when running in the lab.** No — that changes engine code semantics. Bad.\n\n**A middleware that serves the raw file for the three specific /@fs/ URLs:**\n\n```ts\n{\n    name: \"serve-recast-raw\",\n    configureServer(server) {\n        server.middlewares.use((req, res, next) => {\n            const url = req.url?.split(\"?\")[0];\n            if (url && url.includes(\"@recast-navigation\") && (url.endsWith(\".js\") || url.endsWith(\".mjs\"))) {\n                // find the file path from the /@fs/ URL\n                const fsPath = url.replace(/^\\/@fs\\//, \"\");\n                fs.readFile(fsPath, (err, data) => {\n                    if (err) return next();\n                    res.statusCode = 200;\n                    res.setHeader(\"Content-Type\", \"application/javascript\");\n                    res.setHeader(\"Cache-Control\", \"no-cache\");\n                    res.end(data);\n                });\n                return;\n            }\n            next();\n        });\n    },\n}\n```\n\nThis bypasses Vite's transform entirely for these files — raw bytes, no source map, no bloat. The files are native ESM (index.mjs, the glue ends with `export default Recast`) — they can be served as modules without import rewriting (no imports inside — verified: the glue has zero imports; core/gens — do they have imports? They're the dist/index.mjs of a workspace package — they might import each other or from ../wasm/…! If core/dist/index.mjs has a bare import like `import ... from \"@recast-navigation/wasm\"`, serving it raw would break in the browser (bare specifiers don't resolve).\n\nLet me check the imports in core and generators dist files. Earlier I only grepped the glue. Let me grep all three.\n\nIf core/gens have no bare imports (self-contained), raw serving works for all three. If they do, I need Vite's import rewriting — in which case the transform approach (stripping the map after the transform) is needed.\n\nLet me check now. Also check where the inline map is added (first-party module comparison).\n\nActually — the simplest diagnostic that also fixes: **strip the inline map with a post-transform plugin.** If the map is added by Vite after the transform hook, this won't work — in that case use the middleware. Let me first understand the pipeline empirically:\n\n1. Grep the imports in core/gens dist files.\n2. Curl the served version of a first-party module (packages/babylon-lite/src/engine/engine.ts) — check the form of the sourceMappingURL (inline data: or external /@fs/...map).\n3. Curl the .map URL for the glue — 200 or 404?\n\nThen implement the appropriate fix, restart the server, verify with curl that the served glue is now ~559KB (and still valid — ends with `export default Recast;`), and ask the user to reload recast-test.html (the fastest confirmation), then scene252.html.\n\nOne more consideration — the user said \"the game must start up without that import stalling it.\" If stripping the map works for recast-test, scene252 should also work (its module byte total: barrel + 283 + 507 + 206 + 559 — the barrel+Havok+core+gens part already loaded fine in scene252; adding a 559KB glue instead of a 2.07MB one should fit in the same envelope).\n\nAlso — what about the `?v=` cache? After the fix, the served content changes; the browser might have a cached old response. The user should hard-reload. And the server restart clears Vite's transform cache anyway.\n\nLet me also prepare for the possibility that stripping the map isn't enough (if the harness caps module responses at ~500KB and 559KB still stalls). Fallback in that case: the glue has zero imports and is self-contained — I could serve it as a **compressed** response… the middleware can gzip it (the browser sends Accept-Encoding: gzip; a 560KB minified JS compresses to ~150KB!). Actually — wait, does Vite already gzip? Vite dev doesn't gzip by default (compression middleware is a separate plugin). If the harness's network path handles gzip fine… the middleware can manually gzip. But let's try the simple path first.\n\nLet me run the checks.\n","seq":199864,"t":12056143,"text":"\n\nThe recast glue import fails consistently across all test cases, including the stock scene170, so the issue isn't the two-wasm interaction—it's that the 2.07 MB module response won't complete in this browser while smaller modules and the 2 MB wasm fetch succeed. I'll strip the inline source map from the recast modules to shrink the response to 560 KB and verify the fix works.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":114944,"draft_n":13930,"draft_n_accepted":3741,"predicted_ms":122910.0,"predicted_n":5731,"prompt_ms":2090.0,"prompt_n":1046},"to_request":197949,"usage":{"cached_tokens":114944,"completion_tokens":5731,"prompt_tokens":115990}}
