{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Key findings:\n\n1. **core and generators dist files have zero imports** — self-contained. The glue is also self-contained (confirmed earlier). Therefore, raw delivery of all three files will work (no import rewriting needed).\n\n2. **The .map URL returns 200** — Vite is serving the .map file separately AND inlining it into the JS response?! Wait — the delivered glue JS has an inline `sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;base64,...`, and the .map URL also returns 200. So Vite is doing both? That's weird. Actually — hmm, maybe the 200 on the .map URL is just Vite serving the map that exists... no wait, is there a .map file on disk? Let me check. If there's no .map file on disk, Vite generates it on the fly.\n\n3. **First-party modules (engine.ts) also have inline base64 source maps** — so inlining is Vite's dev default here (for everything). Interesting. So all modules in this lab are being delivered with inline maps. Which means the barrel graph modules are also bloated by maps... and yet the barrel graph loaded fine (in under 100ms). The only thing that stalled was the glue (2.07MB).\n\nSo the size story holds: all modules are delivered with inline maps; the glue is simply the largest (2.07MB); everything else is small enough.\n\nNow — the fix. Since all three recast files are self-contained (no imports), the cleanest fix is: **a small dev-server middleware that serves the three @recast-navigation dist files raw (as bytes from disk, no transform, no inline map)**. This shrinks the glue delivery from 2.07MB → 559KB.\n\nBut wait — should I worry that raw delivery skips Vite's ESM checks? These files are native ESM (imported as modules; end with `export default Recast;` / export statements). Content-Type: text/javascript (or application/javascript). The browser will parse them as modules just fine. No bare imports to rewrite.\n\nOne concern: the `?v=ec16f4b0` query string — my middleware has to handle URLs with query strings (strip the query before matching). Also cache headers: no-cache (the user will hard-reload anyway).\n\nWhere to add it: lab/vite.config.ts — as a plugin with configureServer. This is a permanent infrastructure change (not temporary) — justified by a comment. It's scoped to @recast-navigation dist files.\n\nHmm — wait, actually, let me reconsider a less invasive alternative: **strip the inline map only from the transform output of these files** via a transform hook. Problem: the inline map is added by Vite after the transform hook (it's part of the final response assembly — evidence: first-party engine.ts also has an inline map, and there's no plugin stripping/adding it). So a transform hook can't strip it (it's added later). Middleware is the right tool.\n\nActually — hmm, another alternative: **`optimizeDeps.include`** — if I pre-bundle @recast-navigation with esbuild, Vite will serve a single optimized chunk from /node_modules/.vite/deps/ — optimized deps are minified... and esbuild \"can't handle the wasm binary\" (per the config comment) — that's why they're excluded. What exactly does that mean? If the glue JS references the .wasm via `new URL(\"recast-navigation.wasm.wasm\", import.meta.url)` (not an import), esbuild can process the JS fine — the wasm file itself is never imported (locateFile provides the URL at runtime). The exclusion comment says \"ships a wasm binary that the esbuild optimizer can't handle\" — maybe the package.json or build causes esbuild to try to bundle the .wasm. Risky; the middleware approach is more predictable.\n\nLet me also double-check: are there other @recast-navigation files that might be imported (subpaths)? navigation.ts imports: `@recast-navigation/core` → dist/index.mjs, `@recast-navigation/generators` → dist/index.mjs, `@recast-navigation/wasm/wasm` → dist/recast-navigation.wasm.js. That's all (grep found only these three). The middleware should match exactly these three files (or all files under @recast-navigation's dist — safer to match the pattern).\n\nMiddleware design:\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Serve @recast-navigation dist files raw, bypassing Vite's transform.\n * Vite dev appends an inline base64 source map to every served module; for the\n * 560 KB recast glue that bloats the response to ~2 MB, which the lab's\n * constrained test browser cannot finish within its load window (the import\n * never settles). The three dist files are self-contained native ESM (no\n * imports to rewrite), so raw bytes are a drop-in replacement.\n */\n{\n    name: \"serve-recast-raw\",\n    configureServer(server) {\n        server.middlewares.use((req, res, next) => {\n            const url = (req.url ?? \"\").split(\"?\")[0];\n            if (!url.startsWith(\"/@fs/\") || !url.includes(\"/@recast-navigation/\")) {\n                next();\n                return;\n            }\n            const fsPath = url.slice(\"/@fs/\".length);\n            fs.readFile(fsPath, (err, data) => {\n                if (err) {\n                    next();\n                    return;\n                }\n                res.statusCode = 200;\n                res.setHeader(\"Content-Type\", \"text/javascript; charset=utf-8\");\n                res.setHeader(\"Cache-Control\", \"no-cache\");\n                res.end(data);\n            });\n        });\n    },\n},\n```\n\nWait — one gotcha: URL decoding. The /@fs/ path contains `+` characters (pnpm directory names: `@recast-navigation+core@0.43.0`). In URLs, `+` in the path is literal (not decoded to space — that's only in query strings/form data). req.url gives the raw path; `+` stays as `+`. The fs path on disk also has `+`. So a direct slice works. But what about URL-encoded characters (%20 etc.)? None exist in these paths. OK.\n\nAlso — I need to import `fs` in vite.config.ts — check whether it's already imported (it is — the config uses fs heavily; it probably imports node:fs). Let me check the imports at the top of vite.config.ts.\n\nAlso — middleware ordering: `server.middlewares.use` inside configureServer adds after Vite's internal middlewares? Actually — in Vite, plugins' configureServer can add middlewares before Vite's own (if you return a function, it runs after). By default (no return), middlewares added in configureServer run before Vite's internal middleware — which is what I want (intercept before Vite handles it). Yes: the default configureServer middlewares run first.\n\nBut wait — the /@fs/ URL: Vite has a built-in /@fs/ middleware that validates the path is allowed (fs.allow). My middleware intercepts before that — fine, I'm just reading a file I know exists.\n\nOne more consideration: HMR / invalidation — raw delivery means these files won't get HMR. That's fine (they're node_modules).\n\nAnother: does anything else request these URLs expecting Vite's transform (e.g., the ?compat plugin, the production build)? The production build doesn't use dev middleware — the build bundles via rollup; these files get bundled as regular deps (with optimizeDeps.exclude affecting only dev). So the middleware is dev-only.\n\nNow — is this change acceptable as permanent infrastructure? The user's requirement: \"the game must come up without that import stalling it.\" This is the minimal change that achieves that. It's in lab/vite.config.ts (infrastructure, allowed — \"Vite is the foundation for infrastructure\"). I'll add a clear comment. I'll also run lint on the config file afterward (lint:fix + lint covers lab).\n\nAlternative — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more whether stripping the map (rather than raw delivery) is better. Raw delivery also skips Vite's security check on /@fs/ and the ?v= cache-busting. If the recast package is updated (pnpm install), the ?v= hash should change... the version hash comes from the optimizer's state, which doesn't track excluded files — hmm, actually, for excluded files, does the ?v= hash change on file change? The hash I saw (ec16f4b0) — if it doesn't change on file change, the browser cache could serve stale content after a package update. With Cache-Control: no-cache, the browser revalidates every time — no staleness. Good enough.\n\nOK — one more thing to handle: the user's browser may have the old 2.07MB response cached (from the earlier reload). Cache-Control: no-cache on the new response + a hard reload should resolve it. I'll ask for a hard reload.\n\nAlso — should I keep the force-arraybuffer patch? With the glue loading, the streaming path will run for recast. Remember the original diagnosis: recast's streaming stalled after Havok (before I added the patch). Was that real, or was it also a delivery stall misattributed? The original stall was \"no lines from recast at all\" — consistent with the glue module import stalling (never reaching instantiateStreaming). So the original hang was probably always the glue module delivery, not instantiateStreaming! The \"force-arraybuffer\" patch was a red herring (it worked for Havok, but recast never got to the wasm load stage).\n\nSo: once the glue loads, I should remove the instantiateStreaming patch (it's a global mutation, and it's no longer needed). But — careful: don't remove it before confirming the scene loads without it. Plan:\n1. Add the raw delivery middleware.\n2. Restart the server.\n3. Verify: the delivered glue is now 559KB (curl).\n4. Have the user load recast-test.html (should finish with \"recast init done\") — this also tests the streaming path without the patch (recast-test doesn't patch instantiateStreaming — it uses the real one!).\n\nWait — recast-test.html has no patch — it uses the real WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming. If the glue loads and the wasm instantiation via streaming works, recast-test will complete. If streaming itself stalls (the original hypothesis), recast-test will hang after \"glue\" — and then I'll know streaming is also a problem and need to keep the patch. Either way, recast-test is the decisive test.\n\n5. If recast-test passes → have the user load scene252.html. If it hangs at nav-plugin (streaming after Havok), re-add the patch only for the recast load... the patch is global (both modules). Havok with the patch works (ArrayBuffer). So if streaming stalls in scene252, keep the patch. Decide after the user's report.\n\nActually — to minimize round trips: I could preemptively keep the patch in scene252 for now (it's harmless — forces ArrayBuffer on both, proven to work for Havok; recast's ArrayBuffer path is proven in Node). If the scene loads with the patch, great; then in a final cleanup pass, remove the patch and re-test to see if streaming works without it (nice-to-have; the patch is a global mutation and per my earlier decision interview-t10-f135800-1, \"prefer parallel-load workaround over monkey-patching\" — but that was when I thought parallel-load was the fix).\n\nHmm — wait, let me reconsider: what's the cleanest final state?\n- If streaming works fine once the glue loads: final state = no patch, no parallel-load needed (parallel-load is still a nice perf improvement, but not required).\n- If streaming stalls in scene252 (two-wasm interaction): final state = keep the patch (or better: keep the patch but scope it... it's global; Havok also takes the ArrayBuffer path — proven fine).\n\nI'll decide after the user's test. For now: keep the patch in scene252 (it's already there and proven harmless).\n\nAlso — what about the temporary markers (navigation.ts recast-* markers, scene252 fetch wrapper)? Keep them for one more round (they'll confirm the stage), then remove in the cleanup pass.\n\nSo now:\n1. Read the top of vite.config.ts (imports, plugin list location).\n2. Add the plugin.\n3. Run lint:fix + lint (config file).\n4. Restart the dev server.\n5. Verify the delivered glue size (should be 559543) and tail (ends with `export default Recast;`).\n6. Ask the user: hard-reload recast-test.html first, then scene252.html; send both screenshots.\n\nLet me read the top of the config and the plugins array.\n","seq":200949,"t":12128354,"text":"\n\nAll three recast dist files are self-contained with no imports, so they can be served raw without needing Vite's transform. I'll add a small dev-server plugin to serve these files directly from disk, which will reduce the glue size from 2.07 MB to 560 KB.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":120832,"draft_n":7686,"draft_n_accepted":1878,"predicted_ms":69040.0,"predicted_n":2976,"prompt_ms":2540.0,"prompt_n":1005},"to_request":199866,"usage":{"cached_tokens":120832,"completion_tokens":2976,"prompt_tokens":121837}}
